   Pneumonia, hospital-acquired or ventilator-associated; urinary tract infection, compli-
   cated; intraabdominal infection, complicated: In a randomized, open-label trial,
   imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam was compared with an active control (investigator''s choice of
   specified comparators) in hospitalized pediatric patients with hospital-acquired bacterial pneu-
   monia (HABP), ventilator-associated bacterial pneumonia (VABP), complicated urinary tract in-
   fections (cUTI), or complicated intra-abdominal infections (cIAI). Clinical cure rates for cIAI were 2% versus 9% in the imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam and active control groups, respec-
   tively. Clinical cure rates for HABP/VAPB were 100% in both groups. For cUTI, the clinical cure
   rates were 7% versus 70%, and microbiological response was 7% versus 80% in the
   imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam and active control groups, respectively. Efficacy assessments
   were not powered for formal hypothesis testing between treatment groups. The modified in-
   tention-to-treat population included 54 patients (48%) with cUTI, 53 (47%) with cIAI, and 6 (5%)
   with HABP/VABP. Median age was 5 years (range, 21 days to 17 years) and age groups treated
   with imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam were as follows: 12 to less than 18 years (n=10), 6 to less
   than 12 years (n=31), 2 to less than 6 years (n=21), 3 months to less than 2 years (n=15), and
   birth to less than 3 months (n=8) FDA Approved Pediatric Indications Imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam is indicated in pediatric
   patients weighing at least 2 kg with limited or no alternative treatment options for the follow-
   ing infections 2 :.

   • Hospital acquired bacterial pneumonia and ventilator-associated bacterial pneumonia
   caused by the following susceptible gram-negative microorganisms: Acinetobacter calcoaceti-
   cus-baumannii complex, Enterobacter cloacae, Escherichia coli, Haemophilus influenzae, Klebsiella
   aerogenes, Klebsiella oxytoca, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Pseudomonas aeruginosa, and Serratia
   marcescens • Complicated urinary tract infections (cUTI), including pyelonephritis, caused by the following
   susceptible gram-negative microorganisms: Enterobacter cloacae, Escherichia coli, Klebsiella
   aerogenes, Klebsiella pneumoniae, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa • Complicated intra-abdominal infections (cIAI) caused by the following susceptible gram-nega-
   tive microorganisms: Bacteroides caccae, Bacteroides fragilis, Bacteroides ovatus, Bacteroides ster-

coris, Bacteroides thetaiotaomicron, Bacteroides uniformis, Bacteroides vulgatus, Citrobacter fre-
   undii, Enterobacter cloacae, Escherichia coli, Fusobacterium nucleatum, Klebsiella aerogenes,
   Klebsiella oxytoca, Klebsiella pneumoniae, Parabacteroides distasonis, and Pseudomonas
   aeruginosa Limitations of use

   • Not recommended in pediatric patients less than 37 weeks post-menstrual age • Not recommended in pediatric patients weighing less than 30 kg with renal impairment Reduction of drug-resistant bacteria
   • To reduce the development of drug-resistant bacteria and maintain the effectiveness of
   imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam and other antibacterial drugs, imipenem/cilastatin/relebactam
   should be used only to treat or prevent infections that are proven or strongly suspected to be
   caused by susceptible bacteria. When culture and susceptibility information are available, they
   should be considered in selecting or modifying antibacterial therapy. In the absence of such
   data, local epidemiology and susceptibility patterns may contribute to the empiric selection of
   therapy Administration
   • Infuse over 30 minutes (weight 30 kg or greater) or 60 minutes (weight less than 30 kg) Some experts recommend infusion over 30 minutes for the treatment of resistant gram-nega-
   tive infections Preparation

   • Dilute with NS, D5W, D5-0.9% NaCl, D5-0.45% NaCl, or D5-0.225% NaCl • For diluents available in 100-mL prefilled infusion bags, proceed to the next step. For diluents
   unavailable in 100-mL prefilled infusion bags, withdraw 100 mL of desired diluent and transfer
   to an empty infusion bag prior to constituting the vial • Withdraw 20 mL of chosen diluent (as two 10-mL aliquots) from the infusion bag; constitute
   the vial with one 10-mL aliquot. Shake vial well and transfer vial contents to the infusion bag • Add the second 10-mL aliquot of diluent to the vial and shake well to ensure complete trans-
   fer of vial contents. Transfer the contents to the infusion bag for a final concentration of 25
   g/100 mL. Agitate the infusion bag until contents are clear (no visible particles) • For patients weighing at least 30 kg with normal renal function, the prepared 25 g/100
   mL solution is ready for administration 2

• For patients weighing 2 to less than 30 kg, calculate the infusion volume to withdraw from
   the prepared 100 mL solution bag as follows 2 :
    ●   Infusion volume = (weight [kg] x dose [mg/kg]) divided by 5 mg/mL.
   • To achieve this infusion volume, remove and discard the excess solution from the prepared
   100 mL solution bag (100 mL - calculated infusion volume). The remaining solution in the infu-
   sion bag will be the appropriate calculated infusion volume • For patients with renal impairment weighing 30 kg or greater, prepare solution as above
   to a final concentration of 25 g/100 mL. To prepare the reduced dose, follow the below in-
   structions 2 :

