ance of trimethoprim, which is a substrate of P-gp', 'Pharmacology
  Mechanism of action: Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole is a combination antibacterial with activity against E. coli, H. influenzae, Klebsiella
  species, Enterobacter species, M. morganii, P. mirabilis, and indole-positive Proteus species including P. vulgaris, Streptococcus pneumoniae,
  Shigella flexneri, Shigella sonnei, and Pneumocystis jiroveci. Trimethoprim is a synthetic antibiotic that interferes with the production of folic
  acid. It inhibits the production of tetrahydrofolic acid from dihydrofolic acid by binding to dihydrofolate reductase in the folic acid synthesis
  pathway. Sulfamethoxazole is an antibacterial sulfonamide that prevents the formation of dihydrofolic acid, a bacterial compound neces-
  sary for survival. It exerts its effect by competing with para-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) thereby blocking bacterial synthesis of dihydrofolic
  acid Absorption: Rapidly absorbed after oral administration.

  Distribution: Both trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole exist in the blood as unbound, protein-bound, and metabolized forms; sul-
  famethoxazole also exists as the conjugated form. Plasma protein binding is approximately 44% and 70% for trimethoprim and sul-
  famethoxazole, respectively.
  Metabolism: In vitro studies suggest that trimethoprim is a substrate of P-glycoprotein, organic cation transporter 1 and 2 (OCT1 and
  OCT2), and that sulfamethoxazole is not a substrate of P-glycoprotein. Trimethoprim is an inhibitor of CYP2C8 as well as OCT2 transporter,
  and sulfamethoxazole is an inhibitor of CYP2C9. Sulfamethoxazole is extensively metabolized to at least 5 different metabolites: N4-acetyl-,
  N4-hydroxy-, 5-methylhydroxy-, N4-acetyl-5-methylhydroxy-sulfamethoxazole metabolites, and an N-glucuronide conjugate. Trimethoprim
  is metabolized in vitro to 11 different metabolites, 5 of which are glutathione adducts and 6 are oxidative metabolites, including the major
  metabolites 1- and 3-oxides and the 3- and 4-hydroxy derivatives. The free forms of sulfamethoxazole and trimethoprim are considered to
  be the therapeutically active forms.

  Excretion: Trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole are primarily excreted by the kidneys through glomerular filtration and tubular secretion
  61,3 ,23
             .
  Half-life: Elimination half-life of trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole is approximately 11 and 5 hours in patients less than 3 months; 6
  (+/- 9) and 9 (+/- 9) hours in patients 3 months to less than 10 years; and 5 hours (+/- 2) and 1 (+/- 6) hours in patients 10 years
  and older, respectively. The half-life of trimethoprim is significantly prolonged in patients with renal impairment Optimized Dose- Bacteria: A pharmacokinetic model was developed from 153 children (median age 9 years (range, 1 to 2 years)),
  then dose simulations were performed to determine optimal dose regimens based on pharmacodynamic (PD) target. PD target was free

