  Cystic Fibrosis

  Drug disposition is altered in patients with cystic fibrosis (CF), similar to reports for other drugs used in this population Higher total body
  clearance (trimethoprim 179+/-87 mL/min vs 1+/-31.2 mL/min and sulfamethoxazole 1+/-13 mL/min vs 4+/-4.4 mL/min, a reduc-
  tion in half-life (trimethoprim 68+/-1.78 hours vs 7+/-7.5 hours and sulfamethoxazole 04+/-2.08 hours vs 11+/-3 hours), and a smaller
  Vd (trimethoprim 8+/-33.2 liters vs 5+/-31.8 liters and sulfamethoxazole 5+/-4 liters vs 3+/-1.7 liters) were evident in adolescent
  and adult CF patients as compared to normal controls following a single oral dose. Compared with values obtained following a single dose,
  patients with CF also demonstrated a reduction in clearance for both trimethoprim and sulfamethoxazole at steady-state. In addition, was
  an increase in AUC for sulfamethoxazole, bot not trimethoprim, with multiple dosing (n=14; age 13 to 29 years) Similar results were ob-
  tained in a second study which compared the pharmacokinetic parameters of adult patients with CF to those of normal controls (n=7). The
  increase in total body clearance seen with CF was primarily associated with a 5-fold increase in metabolic clearance of sulfamethoxazole
  and an increase in renal clearance of trimethoprim, likely related to greater tubular secretion. The rate of elimination by these routes was
  not correlated with the patient''s NIH clinical severity score P-glycoprotein (P-gp) transport may play a role in the increased renal clear-
  ance of trimethoprim, which is a substrate of P-gp'),
