   Oral or Rectal Administration: Treatment may take hours to days to lower serum potassium;
   therefore, monotherapy with sodium polystyrene sulfonate may be insufficient for severe hy-
   perkalemia Hyperkalemia

Pretreatment of Milk: A 24% reduction in serum potassium concentrations was demon-
   strated in 13 infants (1.4 to 33 weeks of age) with non-oliguric hyperkalemia, 48 hours after
   feeding with formula and/or expressed breast-milk that was pretreated with sodium poly-
   styrene sulfonate powder. Infants had either acute kidney injury or chronic kidney disease. The
   average amount of sodium polystyrene sulfonate used was 9 g per 100 mL (range, 4 to 5
   g/100mL) of prepared formula/milk. The treated formula/milk was tolerated without any ob-
   servable side effects Caution must be exercised because significant increases in sodium con-
   tent of sodium polystyrene sulfonate-treated formula have been demonstrated 8 ,9 . Although,
   the average serum sodium increased from 6 mEq/L to 7 mEq/L in the study with pre-
   treated milk. The average serum calcium decreased from 7 mEq/L to 10 mEq/L Pediatric FDA Approved Indications: Indicated for treatment of hyperkalemia in adults. The
   effectiveness in children has not been established Should not be used for emergency treat-
   ment of life-threatening hyperkalemia due to its delayed onset of action Administration
   Oral: Shake well before use. Sodium polystyrene sulfonate suspension may be delivered into
   the stomach through a plastic tube and, if desired, given with a diet appropriate for a patient
   in renal failure When using the powder, mix in a small quantity of water or syrup. Mix with 3
   to 4 mL per gram of resin Separate dosing of other oral medications by at least 3 hours (before or after other oral medi-
   cations). Patients with gastroparesis or other conditions resulting in delayed emptying of food
   from the stomach into the small intestine should increase the time to a 6-hour separation Enema: May also give as an enema. The enema should be retained as long as possible and fol-
   lowed by a cleansing enema. After the initial cleansing enema, a soft, rubber tube is inserted
   into the rectum, with the tip well into the sigmoid colon, and taped in place. Introduce suspen-
   sion at body temperature by gravity. Flush with fluid, clamp tube and leave in place. If back
   leakage occurs, elevate hips on pillows or assume a knee-chest position. Suspension is kept in
   the sigmoid colon for several hours, if possible. Then, irrigate colon with adequate volumes of
   sodium-free cleansing enema at body temperature in order to remove the resin. Particular at-
   tention should be paid to the cleansing enema if sorbitol has been used When using the
   powder, mix in a small quantity of water to make a suspension, not a paste. Mix with 3 to 4 mL
   per gram of resin Pretreat Formula or Expressed Breast-milk: Add sodium polystyrene sulfonate powder to
   the prepared formula or expressed breast milk, vigorously shake 2 minutes, refrigerate for 60

to 120 minutes to allow precipitate to settle, then decant', 'Adverse effects associated with sodium polystyrene sulfonate include anorexia, nausea, gastric
   irritation, diarrhea, and vomiting. Constipation may occur, especially with high doses.
   Hypokalemia, hypocalcemia, hypomagnesemia and significant sodium retention may occur.
   Intestinal obstruction due to concretions of aluminum hydroxide, when used in combination
   with sodium polystyrene sulfonate, have been reported. Rare instances of intestinal necrosis
   have been reported Post-marketing events include fecal impaction following rectal administration, particularly in
   children, and gastrointestinal concretions (bezoars) following oral administration. Other re-
   ports were ischemic colitis and gastrointestinal tract ulceration or necrosis which could lead to
   intestinal perforation. Rare cases of acute bronchitis and/or bronchopneumonia associated
   with inhalation of particles of polystyrene sulfonate Monitoring
   Monitor for electrolyte disturbances including potassium, calcium, and magnesium. Especially
   monitor serum potassium levels every 24 hours to avoid hypokalemia; early signs of severe hy-
   pokalemia include irritability, confusion, and delayed thought processes. Late signs include se-
   vere muscle weakness, which may progress to paralysis', 'Monitor for electrolyte disturbances including potassium, calcium, and magnesium. Especially', 'Pharmacology
   A cation exchange resin which releases sodium ion in exchange for potassium ion as it passes
   along the intestine or is retained in the colon after administration by enema. For the most
   part, this action occurs in the large intestine, which excretes potassium ions to a greater de-
   gree than does the small intestine. The efficiency of this process is limited and unpredictably
   variable. Exchange approximates 33%, but the range is so large that electrolytes must be mon-
   itored'),
