   Specific monitoring has not been determined', 'Mechanism of action: Beremagene geperpavec-svdt can induce both keratinocytes and fi-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Beremagene geperpavec-svdt can induce both keratinocytes and fi-
   broblasts. Following entry into cells, the vector genome is deposited into the nucleus. Once in
   the nucleus, transcription of the encoded human COL7A1 is initiated. The resulting transcripts
   allow for production and secretion of COL7 by the cell. These COL7 molecules arrange them-
   selves into long, thin bundles that form anchoring fibrils. The anchoring fibrils hold the epider-
   mis and dermis together and are essential for maintaining the integrity of the skin. Patients
   with autosomal dominant dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa have lower than normal functional
   anchoring fibrils, and patients with autosomal recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa have
   no functioning anchoring fibrils Immunogenicity: In a randomized, intra-subject placebo-controlled clinical trial, antibodies
   against the viral vector (HSV-1) and transgene protein (COL7) were evaluated in a subset of
   subjects. A total of 64% of evaluated subjects (n=22) were anti-HSV-1 antibody positive at base-
   line. Six of the 8 anti-HSV-1 seronegative subjects seroconverted by week 26 following treat-
   ment with beremagene geperpavec-svdt. For subjects with available matched baseline and
   end-of-study serum samples, anti-drug antibodies to COL7 were detected in 72% (n=18) of
   subjects treated with beremagene geperpavec-svdt for up to 26 weeks Therapeutic Drug Concentrations

   Viral vector DNA levels

   Topical: 1 x 104 to 1 x 108 vector genomes 2

   In a clinical study (N=9), viral vector DNA was detected in the skin swab samples in all 9 treated
   patients, with maximum level ranging from 1 x 104 to 1 x 108 vector genomes. No viral vec-
   tor DNA was detected in blood or urine In a small, randomized, intra-subject, placebo-controlled clinical trial (N=31), systemic and po-
   tential environmental exposure assessments were conducted at weekly intervals via quantifi-

cation of beremagene geperpavec-svdt genomes in blood, urine, skin swabs, and bandage
   samples. All blood samples and all but 1 urine sample were below the limit of detection. Skin
   swabs of 61% of patients were positive for viral vector DNA following beremagene geperpavec-
   svdt treatment. No extracellular infectious particles were detected on the skin surface of any
   patient at any time point tested after topical beremagene geperpavec-svdt application'),
