   lowing: Staphylococcus aureus, Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae, Moraxella
   catarrhalis, and Pseudomonas aeruginosa Fluocinolone acetonide, a corticosteroid, has antiinflammatory properties partially due to its
   inhibition of local prostaglandin biosynthesis. Lipocortins, which are peptides that inhibit phos-
   pholipase A2, are induced by corticosteroids. Phospholipase A2 is an enzyme that releases
   arachidonic acid by breaking down leukocyte lysosomal membranes, and inhibition of arachi-
   donic acid release prevents inflammatory mediators (eg, prostaglandins, histamine, the com-
   plement system) from forming 2/3
Plasma levels of both ciprofloxacin and fluocinolone acetonide were undetectable 1 to 2 hours
   after the seventh dose on the last day of treatment in 2 studies of children 6 months to 12
   years of age (N=30). After visit 3 (within 1 and 2 hours after the 7th dose), 1 plasma concentra-
   tion sample contained 3 mcg/mL of ciprofloxacin. This patient was receiving 3% ciprofloxacin
   otic solution (the active comparator) in both ears compared with all other subjects who had
   unilateral AOMT'),
