   Intravenous
   U-100 Only

   Infuse at a concentration of 1 to 1 unit/mL; individualize per patient needs 3 , 5

   Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA)

When continuous IV administration not available, and with uncomplicated mild to mod-
   erate DKA: 15 units/kg subQ every 2 hours; initiate 1 hour after the start of fluid replace-
   ment. The dose may be reduced to 1 unit/kg subQ every 2 hours if blood glucose continues
   to decrease by more than 5 mmol/L (90 mg/dL) even after adding dextrose 6

   Transition from IV to subQ:

   Table 1: Transitioning from IV to subQ insulin

      Begin subQ insulin once ketoacidosis has resolved and oral intake is tolerated.

      Long-acting insulin should be administered in addition to rapid- or short-acting insulin.

      The most convenient time to change to subQ insulin is just before a mealtime.

      Administer the first subQ injection 15 to 30 minutes (for rapid-acting insulin) before stop-
      ping the insulin infusion to allow sufficient absorption. With long-acting insulin, the over-
      lap should be longer and the rate of IV administration decreased gradually (eg, administer
      basal dose in evening, then stop infusion in morning)

      The regimen, dose and type of subQ insulin should be according to local preference or
      circumstances.

      After transitioning to subQ insulin, frequent blood glucose monitoring is required to avoid
      marked hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia.

      [6]

   Dosage Adjustment

   Renal impairment: More frequent dosage adjustment and blood glucose monitoring may be
   necessary 3 ,2 .

   Hepatic impairment: More frequent dosage adjustment and blood glucose monitoring may
   be necessary 3 ,', 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus: Intensive insulin therapy, either by multiple subQ injections of
   prandial and basal insulin or continuous subQ infusion, is the mainstay of treatment. Prandial
   insulin daily requirements are approximately 55% to 70% of the total daily dose, while basal in-
   sulin accounts for 30% to 45% of the total daily dose. Prepubertal children typically require 7
   to 1 units/kg/day and during puberty, the insulin requirements may rise to 1 to 2 units/kg/day.
   Rapid acting insulins such as insulin lispro should ideally be given 10 to 15 minutes before
   meals or immediately before meals to help reduce postprandial hyperglycemia and nocturnal
   hypoglycemia 8 ,9 .

   Type 2 diabetes mellitus: Insulin therapy should be initiated in pediatric patients presenting
   with ketosis/ketoacidosis and/or the patients HbA1c is greater than 5%. After initial treat-
   ment, insulin may be weaned off over 2 to 6 weeks by decreasing the insulin dose 30% to 50%
   with each increase in the metformin dose. If glycemic goals are not met after initial treatment
   and the addition of a glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) agonist or empagliflozin, consider adding
   an intermediate/long-acting insulin. The starting dose of basal insulin is typically 25 to 5
   units/kg and the dose is titrated based on results of self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG)
   8 ,9
          .

   Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA): The standard of care for DKA is continuous IV regular insulin. In
   cases when continuous IV administration is not available and in children with uncomplicated
   mild to moderate DKA, hourly or 2-hourly subcutaneous rapid-acting insulin is safe and may
   be as effective IV regular insulin infusion. Do not use this method in children whose peripheral
   circulation is impaired Pediatric FDA Approved Indication:
   Insulin lispro is a rapid acting human insulin analog indicated to improve glycemic control in
   pediatric patient with diabetes mellitus 3 ,4 .

   Administration
   Continuous Subcutaneous Infusion

   Admelog® 3
    ●   Do not dilute or mix with other insulins.
    ●   Change the insulin reservoir at least every 7 days and change the infusion sets and the infu-
        sion set insertion site at least every 3 days.
    ●   Do not expose insulin in pump reservoir to temperatures greater than 37° C (98.6° F).

●   Verify that the infusion pump system is compatible with Admelog®.

   HumaLOG® 4
    ●   Do not dilute or mix U-100 with any other insulin when used in an external insulin pump; do
        not use U-200 in an external pump.
    ●   Prior to use in an external insulin pump, ensure that the pump is indicated for continuous
        delivery of fast-acting insulin, such as the MiniMed, Disetronic, and other equivalent pumps.
        Insulin lispro U-100 can be used in any reservoir and infusion sets that are compatible with
        insulin and the specific pump.
    ●   Change the insulin reservoir at least every 7 days and change the infusion sets and the infu-
        sion set insertion site at least every 3 days.
    ●   Do not expose insulin in pump reservoir to temperatures greater than 37° C (98.6° F).

   Intermittent Subcutaneous Injection

   Admelog® 3
    ●   May dilute with NS for subQ injection in a 1:1 ratio to create a final concentration of U-50.
    ●   Administer subQ in abdominal wall, thigh, upper arm, or buttocks. Rotate injection sites to
        reduce risk of lipodystrophy and localized cutaneous amyloidosis. Do not inject into areas of
        lipodystrophy or localized cutaneous amyloidosis

   HumaLOG® 7
    ●   Insulin lispro U-100 may be mixed with NPH insulin only. Draw insulin lispro U-100 into sy-
        ringe first if mixing with NPH insulin. Use immediately following mixing.
    ●   Do not transfer U-200 from the KwikPen to a syringe as dosage will not be measured
        correctly.
    ●   Do not convert dose when using U-100 or U-200 KwikPens as the dose window displays the
        number of units to be delivered; no conversion is necessary.
    ●   Do not mix U-200 with any other insulin.
    ●   Administer subQ in abdominal wall, thigh, upper arm, or buttocks. Rotate injection sites to
        reduce risk of lipodystrophy and localized cutaneous amyloidosis. Do not inject into areas of
        lipodystrophy or localized cutaneous amyloidosis.

   Intravenous Infusion (Admelog® Only) 3
    ●   Dilute in NS to a concentration of 1 to 1 unit/mL.', 'The most commonly reported adverse effects in patients with type-1 diabetes mellitus were flu
   syndrome (34.6%), pharyngitis (33.3%), headache (29.6%), rhinitis (24.7%), pain (19.8%), in-
   creased cough (17.3%), infection (13.6%) 2 , nasopharyngitis (13.1%), and upper respiratory
   tract infection (6%) Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus
   •Routinely assess glycemic control to determine treatment effectiveness. Monitor HbA1c at
   least twice yearly in a patient who is well-controlled, every 3 months if not meeting treatment
   goals or when there is a change of therapy, or more frequently as clinically warranted. An
   HbA1c goal of less than 7% is appropriate for many children and adolescents, but HbA1c goals
   may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to each patient •Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) multiple times a day (6 to 10 times a day via
   meter or continuous glucose monitoring [CGM]), including prior to meals, snacks, at bedtime,
   and as needed to assist in meeting goals of therapy. Real-time or intermittently scanned CGM
   should be offered as soon as possible to children on multiple daily injections or insulin pump
   therapy Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus

   •Monitor HbA1c every 3 months if possible. A reasonable HbA1c goal for most patients is less
   than 5%, but HbA1c goals may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to
   each patient 10,9
   •Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) as needed to assist in meeting goals of ther-
   apy; SMBG should be individualized to each patient 10

   Toxic Monitoring

   •Monitor blood potassium levels in patients at risk for hypokalemia 6/11
•Observe patient for signs or symptoms of heart failure when receiving concomitant peroxi-
