the anticoagulant used) to avoid excessive bleeding or', 'Pharmacology
   Adult data unless otherwise specified.

   Mechanism of Action

   Antithrombin (AT), an alpha2-glycoprotein of molecular weight 58,000, is normally present in
   human plasma at a concentration of approximately 5 mg/dL and is the major plasma in-
   hibitor of thrombin. Inactivation of thrombin by AT occurs by formation of a covalent bond re-
   sulting in an inactive 1:1 stoichiometric complex between the two, involving an interaction of
   the active serine of thrombin and an ', 'Antithrombin deficiency, hereditary: Treatment and prophylaxis of thromboembolism and
   prophylaxis of perioperative and peripartum thromboembolism

   • Individualize the exact loading and maintenance dose and/or dose intervals for each patient
   based on the individual clinical conditions, response to therapy, and actual plasma antithrom-
   bin (AT) levels achieved. Recovery of antithrombin III may vary by patient (eg, half-life may be
   shortened following surgery, hemorrhage or acute thrombosis, and during IV heparin or low
   molecular weight heparin administration; monitor plasma AT levels more frequently and ad-
   just dosing as needed) • When used for a patient with hereditary deficiency to control an acute thrombotic episode or
   prevent thrombosis during or following surgical or obstetrical procedures, raise the AT level to
   normal and maintain this level for 2 to 8 days, depending on the indication for treatment, type
   and extent of surgery, patient’s medical condition, past history and physician’s judgment.
   Concomitant use of heparin in these situations should be based on clinical judgment • Dosage guidance is provided in the following table 1 :

