repository corticotropin induces hyperplasia and hypertrophy of the adrenal cortex and con-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Repository corticotropin is an adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)
   analogue. Repository and endogenous corticotropin stimulate the adrenal cortex to secrete
   cortisol, corticosterone, aldosterone, and other weak androgenic substances. Elevated cortisol
   levels in the plasma suppresses ACTH release. Prolonged administration of large doses of
   repository corticotropin induces hyperplasia and hypertrophy of the adrenal cortex and con-
   tinuous high output of cortisol, corticosterone, and weak androgens. The release of endoge-
   nous ACTH is influenced by the nervous system via regulatory hormone released from the hy-
   pothalamus and by a negative corticosteroid feedback mechanism. The trophic effects of ACTH
   and repository corticotropin appear to be mediated by cyclic adenosine monophosphate.
   Repository corticotropin also binds to melanocortin receptors 1

   Half-life: 15 minutes after IV administration 1'),
