   Mechanism of Action

   Etuvetidigene autotemcel adds full-length copies of the human Wiskott-Aldrich Syndrome
   (WAS) complementary deoxyribonucleic acid into patients’ hematopoietic stem cells through

transduction with WAS lentiviral vector. After infusion, the genetically modified cells engraft in
   the bone marrow, repopulate the hematopoietic compartment, and produce biologically active
   lymphoid and myeloid progenitors whose progeny express WAS protein (WASP). WASP regu-
   lates the structural protein actin in blood cells'),
