   Administer with or without food. Mix one-half (2.5 g) powder packet in at least 2 ounces (60
   mL) of water or other liquid. Stir to mix. Do not shake to dissolve the powder. Alternatively,
   blend the powder with foods such as pudding or yogurt. Use immediately after mixing or
   blending; do not store mixed in liquids as product may degrade over time 1/3', 'The most common adverse effects are nausea, constipation, abdominal cramps, headache,
   and increased urination', '', 'Pharmacology
   Serum-derived bovine immunoglobulin/protein isolate is useful in the management of en-
   teropathy due to its ability to bind microbial components, manage gastrointestinal immune
   balance, maintain gut barrier function, and improve nutrient utilization. It also increases lean
   body mass, protein utilization, and nutrient absorption while decreasing protein catabolism
   and fecal fat and energy loss Intact bovine immunoglobulin/protein isolate is not absorbed from the gastrointestinal tract,
   though some proteins in the formulation are absorbed leading to increases in plasma amino
   acid (ie, leucine) levels, particularly 60 to 120 minutes following administration Approximately 5% to 12% of bovine immunoglobulin is excreted in the feces of infants with di-
   arrhea, while 10% to 20% is excreted in the feces of adults with compromised gastrointestinal
   tracts. In healthy adults, almost no orally administered immunoglobulins are excreted fecally,
   indicating that most of ingested dose is digested as protein. Gastrointestinal transit time after
   oral ingestion is 6 to 72 hours in infants Up to 50% of bovine immunoglobulin survive stomach digestion, and it is only detected in the
   gastrointestinal tract following oral administration'),
