  been established, but some experts recommend a maximum of 5 g amoxicillin/day Duration of therapy: Total duration of oral plus IV therapy is 7 to 14 days 20,21 . A 5-day course of therapy has been studied in a clinical
  trial of patients 2 months to 10 years old; consider only in patients showing clinical improvement after the first 5 days', 'Suspension, immediate-release tablets, and chewable tablets 2
   ●   Treatment of lower respiratory tract infections, otitis media, and sinusitis caused by beta-lactamase producing strains of H influenzae and
       M catarrhalis.
   ●   Treatment of skin and skin structure infections caused by beta-lactamase producing strains of S aureus, E coli, and Klebsiella species.
   ●   Treatment of urinary tract infections caused by beta-lactamase producing strains of E coli, Klebsiella species, and Enterobacter species.

  Extra-strength suspension: 10
   ●   Treatment of pediatric patients 3 months to 12 years old with recurrent or persistent acute otitis media due to S pneumoniae (penicillin
       MICs 2 mcg/mL or less), H influenzae or M catarrhalis, including beta-lactamase producing strains, characterized by antibiotic exposure for
       acute otitis media within the preceding 3 months, and either age 2 years and younger or daycare attendance.

  Extended-release tablets 23
   ●   Treatment of community-acquired pneumonia or acute bacterial sinusitis due to confirmed or suspected beta-lactamase producing
       pathogens (eg, H influenzae, M catarrhalis, H parainfluenzae, K pneumoniae, or methicillin-susceptible S aureus) and S pneumoniae with re-
       duced susceptibility to penicillin (eg, penicillin MICs equals 2 mcg/mL) in children 7 years and older and weighing at least 40 kg .

  Limitations of Use

• Amoxicillin-clavulanate extended-release tablets (Augmentin XR®) and extra-strength suspension (Augmentin ES-600®) are not FDA ap-
  proved for the treatment of infections due to S. pneumoniae with penicillin mean inhibitory concentrations (MICs) 4 mcg/mL or greater due
  to limited data • When susceptibility test results show susceptibility to amoxicillin, indicating no beta-lactamase production, amoxicillin-clavulanate should
  not be used Administration
  • Give at start of a meal 23,25,24,10 .

  • Shake oral suspension well before using 25,10 .

  • Store reconstituted suspension under refrigeration. Discard unused portion after 10 days 2 ,4 .', 'Common adverse effects include diarrhea/loose stools, nausea, skin rashes and urticaria, vomiting, and vaginitis. In a clinical trial compar-
  ing twice daily (amoxicillin/clavulanate 45/6.4 mg/day) to three times daily dosing (amoxicillin/clavulanate 40/10 mg/kg/day), the incidence
  of diarrhea was 3% compared with 3%, respectively, and incidence of patients with severe diarrhea or discontinuing due to diarrhea
  were 1% compared with 6% for the twice daily and three times daily, respectively 23,24,25,10 . In another clinical trial comparing the toler-
  ability of amoxicillin/clavulanate 90/6.4 mg/kg/day to amoxicillin/clavulanate 45/6.4 mg/kg/day (each in two divided doses), the incidence of
  protocol-defined diarrhea was 11% and 8%, respectively 11/16
