   therapy with itraconazole for at least 12 months and until histoplasma antigen is no longer de-
   tected in CSF', 'Aspergillosis, Invasive

   Voriconazole IV or oral is the first-line agent and alternative agents are liposomal amphotericin
   B or isavuconazole. Salvage therapy may be considered with the following agents: ampho-

tericin B lipid complex, caspofungin, micafungin, posaconazole, or itraconazole suspension for
   the following aspergillosis infections 14 :
   ●   CNS aspergillosis
   ●   Cutaneous aspergillosis
   ●   Ear, invasive infections
   ●   Esophageal
   ●   Eye (endophthalmitis) (additionally treat with intravitreal amphotericin B or intravitreal
       voriconazole)
   ●   Gastrointestinal
   ●   Invasive pulmonary aspergillosis
   ●   Invasive sinus aspergillosis
   ●   Osteomyelitis and septic arthritis
   ●   Peritonitis
   ●   Tracheobronchial aspergillosis (adjunct-inhaled amphotericin B)

   Allergic bronchopulmonary aspergillosis: The first-line agent is oral itraconazole. Alternative
   agents are oral voriconazole or posaconazole Aspergilloma (saprophytic or colonizing syndrome): Itraconazole or voriconazole are alter-
   native agents to no therapy or surgical resection for symptomatic fungal ball of the lung Chronic cavitary pulmonary aspergillosis (saprophytic or colonizing syndrome of as-
   pergillus): Oral itraconazole or voriconazole are the preferred oral antifungal agents.
   Posaconazole is a third-line agent for those intolerant of the other agents or those who experi-
   ence clinical failure. For those who fail therapy, develop triazole resistance, and/or have ad-
   verse effects, then IV micafungin, caspofungin, or amphotericin B may be tried Heart (endocarditis, pericarditis, and myocarditis): First-line agent is either voriconazole or
   a lipid formulation of amphotericin B Hepatic: First-line agent is either voriconazole or a lipid formulation of amphotericin B Keratitis: Topical natamycin 5% or topical voriconazole is recommended Prophylaxis, Aspergillosis
   In patients with prolonged neutropenia for those who are at high risk of invasive aspergillosis,
   then prophylaxis with posaconazole, voriconazole, and/or micafungin are recommended.
   Caspofungin is probably effective. Itraconazole is effective but limited usefulness due to its ab-
   sorption and tolerability 4/23
Empiric or Preemptive, Aspergillosis

   Allogeneic Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplant recipients and Acute Myelogenous
   Leukemia patients: Recommended for those high-risk patients with potential for prolonged
   neutropenia who are persistently febrile despite broad-spectrum antibiotic therapy. Choices
   for first-line agents: ●   Liposomal amphotericin B
   ●   Caspofungin
   ●   Micafungin
   ●   Voriconazole

