   Comparison with Echinocandins: There was no difference in clinical response between
   echinocandins and amphotericin B (OR 38; 95% CI, 68 to 8) for the treatment of sus-
   pected or confirmed invasive candidiasis in a meta-analysis (n=5; 354 neonates and children).
   Antifungals included were micafungin, caspofungin, amphotericin B deoxycholate, and liposo-

mal amphotericin B. Subanalysis demonstrated no difference in other comparisons including
   mycological response, mortality, recurrence of candida infection, type of echinocandin, differ-
   ent risk groups (high-risk, low-risk, or neutropenic groups), and type of use (targeted or empiri-
   cal). Discontinuation due to adverse effects were higher with amphotericin B than the
   echinocandins (OR 3; 95% CI, 12 to 76) Coccidioidomycosis: An oral azole antifungal agent, particularly fluconazole, is the primary
   agent used to treat coccidioidomycosis. Itraconazole is another option. Amphotericin B (spe-
   cific formulation not given) is an alternative agent in certain circumstances Empiric treatment of febrile neutropenia: The International Pediatric Fever and
   Neutropenia Guideline Panel provides guidelines for the empiric management of febrile neu-
   tropenia in pediatric cancer and hematopoietic stem-cell transplant patients. The guidelines
   are endorsed by the American Society of Clinical Oncology and should be adapted to meet the
   individual needs of the institution. The following table provides the recommendations 17 :

      Summary of Recommendations for Febrile Neutropenia[17]

      Initial Presentation of Febrile Neutropenia

      Treatment

      High-Risk FN
      Use monotherapy with antipseudomonal beta-lactam, a fourth-generation cephalosporin
      or carbapenem as empiric therapy (1A).

      Reserve addition of a second Gram-negative agent or a glycopeptide for patients who are
      clinically unstable, when a resistant infection is suspected, or in centers with a high rate of
      resistant pathogens (1B).

      Low-Risk FN
      Consider initial or step-down outpatient management if infrastructure is in place to en-
      sure careful monitoring and follow-up (2B).

      Consider oral antibiotic administration if child able to tolerate route reliably (2B).

