   Mechanism of Action:

Recombinant coagulation factor XIII A-subunit is a recombinant human factor XIII-A(2) homod-
   imer composed of 2 factor XIII A-subunits. Factor XIII is activated by thrombin at the site of ves-
   sel wall injury and plays a substantial role in maintaining hemostasis by cross-linking fibrin and
   other proteins in the fibrin clot. Recombinant coagulation factor XIII A-subunit binds to free hu-
   man factor XIII B-subunit and is activated by thrombin in the presence of calcium; once active,
   it increases the mechanical strength of fibrin clots, retards fibrinolysis, and enhances platelet
   adhesion to the site of injury in a dose-dependent manner. Recombinant coagulation factor
   XIII A-subunit, after combining with available plasma B-subunits, has been shown to have the
   same pharmacodynamic properties in plasma as endogenous factor XIII Pharmacokinetic parameters are not influenced by age (1 year or older) 1 ,2 .

   In a pharmacokinetic analysis of patients 1 to 5 years of age (n=6), 6 to 11 years of age (n=9),
   12 to 17 years of age (n=6), and 18 years or older (n=26), the AUCs were 6 international
   units/hr/mL, 7 international units/hr/mL, 1 international units/hr/mL, and 2 inter-
   national units/hr/mL; the Cmax were 67 international units/mL, 75 international units/mL, 67 international units/mL, and 76 international units/mL; the troughs were 21 interna-
   tional units/mL, 2 international units/mL, 17 international units/mL, and 18 international
   units/mL; and the half-lives were 1 days, 38 days, 91 days, and 58 days, respec-
   tively The Cmax, AUC, clearance, and half-life were 48 +/- 14 international units/mL, 8 +/- 2 international units x hr/mL, 41 +/- 2 mL/hr/kg, and 1 +/- 9 days, respectively, in 6
   pediatric patients 1 to younger than 6 years administered a single-dose of recombinant coagu-
   lation factor XIII A-subunit 35 international units/kg IV'),
