   Preparation
   • Prademagene zamikeracel is prepared by the manufacturer in an appropriate healthcare set-
   ting for surgical application by a qualified healthcare provider • Hold prademagene zamikeracel at room temperature (15° to 25° C) until application Administration
   Prademagene zamikeracel is for autologous topical application on wounds only. The sheets
   are administered surgically by a qualified healthcare provider using the following steps 1

   • Verify patient’s identity.

• Prepare wound by debridement under general or other appropriate anesthesia.
   • Before surgical application, pick up prademagene zamikeracel sheet with forceps by grasping
   the titanium ligating clips. Handle sheets only with forceps and only by the titanium ligating
   clips.
   • Do not trim sheets.
   • Apply unaltered prademagene zamikeracel sheets onto the wound bed only (nylon suture on
   the prademagene zamikeracel sheet facing away from wound) by affixing via resorbable su-
   tures. Do not suture prademagene zamikeracel onto healthy intact skin. Do not overlap sheets
   on wounds.

   • Apply all selected sheets in a single surgical session.
   • Cover area of application of prademagene zamikeracel sheets with non-adhesive dressings
   and topical antibiotic ointment.
   • Instruct patient to leave the treated area undisturbed for 5 to 10 days at the discretion of the
   physician based on individual needs for immobilization of treated areas and post-surgical
   recovery.
   • Instruct patient to keep dressings dry and not submerge the treated area in water until the
   gauze of the prademagene zamikeracel product falls off the treatment site. Gauze backing
   should fall off within 2 to 3 weeks of surgical application.', 'Common: Procedural pain (27%) and pruritus (9%) 1

   Monitoring
   Toxic Physical Monitoring

   • Monitor for signs and symptoms of hypersensitivity reactions (eg, itching, swelling, hives, dif-
   ficulty breathing, runny nose, watery eyes, nausea) and in severe cases, anaphylaxis, during
   application • Monitor lifelong after treatment for the development of malignancies. In the event that a ma-
   lignancy occurs, contact Abeona Therapeutics Inc. at 1-844-888-2236 to obtain instructions on
   collecting patient samples for testing', '• Monitor for signs and symptoms of hypersensitivity reactions (eg, itching, swelling, hives, dif-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: In patients with recessive dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa (RDEB),
   both copies of the COL7A1 gene are mutated, resulting in the absence or low levels of biologi-
   cally active C7 protein which form anchoring fibrils (AFs). The lack of AFs disrupts the connec-
   tion between the epidermis and the dermis and causes skin fragility and other signs and symp-
   toms of RDEB. Prademagene zamikeracel consists of a patient''s own cells that have been
   gene-modified through RVV transduction to express the COL7A1 gene to produce the C7 pro-
   tein. These cells are formed into cellular sheets for topical application onto wounds Immunogenicity: In a single-arm clinical study, anti-C7 antibodies against Type VII collagen
   were assessed in 7 patients at baseline, 1, 3, and 6 months and then annually for 5 years at
   physician’s discretion after treatment with prademagene zamikeracel. Out of 7 patients, circu-
   lating anti-C7 antibodies were detected in 2 patients which resolved at 1 year follow-up.
   Tissue-bound anti-C7 antibodies were detected in 4 patients which resolved in 3 patients at 1
   year follow-up. In 1 patient with positive baseline anti-C7 antibodies, localized immunoreac-
   tants were observed at treated sites for up to 2 years after treatment. In the VIITAL study, anti-
   C7 antibodies were assessed at baseline and at physician’s discretion at months 3 and Eight
   patients were biopsied at either month 3 or 6, and none of those patients tested positive for
   C7 immune complexes. Because of the small sample size, there is limited data to determine

the effect of anti-C7 antibodies on the pharmacodynamics, safety, and/or effectiveness of
   prademagene zamikeracel. The incidence of anti-drug antibodies is highly dependent on the
   sensitivity and specificity of the assay Pharmacodynamics
   Pharmacodynamics Duration

   Single dose: Anchoring fibrils: In a single-arm clinical study (n=7), C7 was assessed by both im-
   munofluorescence and the presence of anchoring fibrils (AFs) at months 3, 6, and The NC2
   domain of C7 was assessed using the LH24 antibody, and the presence of AFs was assessed by
   immunoelectron microscopy. The NC2 domain of C7 and AFs were detected in 6 patients at 3
   months and in 5 patients at 6 months. One year after treatment with prademagene zamikera-
   cel, 3 patients were positive for NC2 or AFs. At Year 2, 2 out of 3 patients with biopsies were
   positive for NC2 or AFs (1 patient was positive for both AFs and NC2; the second patient was
   positive only for NC2, as an additional biopsy for AF assessment was not obtained)'),
