   aureus Treatment of skin/skin structure infections caused by beta-lactamase producing strains of S.
   aureus, Klebsiella species, or E. coli Treatment of complicated and uncomplicated urinary tract infections caused by beta-lacta-
   mase-producing strains of E. coli, Klebsiella species, P. aeruginosa or other Pseudomonas
   species, Citrobacter species, Enterobacter cloacae, S. marcescens, or S. aureus Treatment of endometritis caused by beta-lactamase-producing strains of P. melaninogenicus,
   Enterobacter species including E. cloacae, E. coli, K. pneumonia, S. aureus, or S. epidermidis Treatment of infections of the abdomen (peritonitis) caused by beta-lactamase-producing
   strains of E. coli, K. pneumonia, or B. fragilis group 15,2 . Ticarcillin-clavulanate is considered an
   appropriate single agent for pediatric patients with a complicated extra-biliary intra-abdominal
   infection Administration
   Dilute in NS, D5W, or LR to a final concentration of 10 to 100 mg/mL and administer IV over 30
   minutes', 'Most common adverse effects reported in clinical studies include diarrhea (1.4% to 3%) and
   nausea (1.2%) 16,3 ,4 .

   Solution Compatibility
   D5W, LR, and NS.

   Terminal Injection Site Compatibility
   Aztreonam, cefepime, famotidine, fluconazole, heparin, insulin, milrinone, morphine, propofol,
   remifentanil, and theophylline.

   Terminal Injection Site Incompatibility
   Amikacin, azithromycin, gentamicin, netilmicin, sodium bicarbonate, tobramycin, and
   vancomycin.

   Monitoring
   Periodic monitoring for hypokalemia in patients with fluid/electrolyte imbalances or in patients
   receiving prolonged therapy. Perform coagulation tests and monitor bleeding time in patients
   with renal failure. Periodic assessment of renal, hepatic, and hematopoietic function should be
   considered with prolonged therapy of 21 days or greater', 'mase inhibitor clavulanic acid in a 30:1 ratio. Clavulanic acid effective for inhibiting beta-lacta-
tive inhibitor of staphylococcal beta-lactamase. Less effective for inhibiting Citrobacter species,', 'Pharmacology

Ticarcillin/clavulanate combines the extended-spectrum penicillin (ticarcillin) with the β-lacta-
   mase inhibitor clavulanic acid in a 30:1 ratio. Clavulanic acid effective for inhibiting beta-lacta-
   mases from K. pneumoniae, P. mirabilis, Proteus vulgaris, B. fragilis, and M. catarrhalis; very effec-
   tive inhibitor of staphylococcal beta-lactamase. Less effective for inhibiting Citrobacter species,
   Enterobacter species, Serratia marcescens, and indole-positive Proteus species Ticarcillin is
   excreted 60% to 70% unchanged by the kidney, and clavulanate is excreted 35% to 45% un-
   changed. The mean half-life of both ticarcillin and clavulanate in infants and children is ap-
   proximately 1 hour Based on adult data, the half-life of ticarcillin in patients with renal fail-
   ure is approximately 13 hours'),
