   Azelastine is phthalazinone derivative H1-receptor blocking agent effective for the treatment of
   allergic rhinitis. The primary metabolite of azelastine, desmethylazelastine, also has H1 antihis-
   tamine activity. Fluticasone propionate, a potent synthetic trifluorinated corticosteroid, has ac-
   tivity against various proinflammatory mediators (eg, histamine, leukotrienes, cytokines, and
   eicosanoids) and cell types (eg, mast cells, eosinophils, lymphocytes, macrophages, and neu-
   trophils), although its exact mechanism in allergic rhinitis is unknown With the combination azelastine hydrochloride/fluticasone propionate nasal spray,
   bioavailability of azelastine is approximately 40% (comparable with azelastine monotherapy
   nasal spray) and fluticasone is approximately 44% to 61% greater than fluticasone monother-
   apy nasal spray (which is less than 2%). Elimination half-life of azelastine is approximately 25
   hours Azelastine hydrochloride: Following oral and IV administration, steady-state Vd of azelastine
   is 5 L/kg. Plasma protein binding is 88%. Metabolized via CYP450 enzyme system to the ac-
   tive metabolite desmethylazelastine; specific isoenzymes have not been identified. Total clear-
   ance is approximately 5 L/kg/hr. Following oral administration, approximately 75% of radiola-
   beled azelastine hydrochloride dose was excreted in feces (less than 10% as unchanged drug) Fluticasone propionate: Plasma protein binding is 91%. Following IV administration, average
   steady-state Vd of fluticasone is 2 L/kg. Metabolized by CYP3A4 to the inactive 17-beta-car-
   boxylic acid derivative. Average total clearance is approximately 66 L/hr. Following oral dosing,
   less than 5% of the radiolabeled fluticasone propionate dose was excreted in the urine as me-
   tabolites. Following IV administration, elimination half-life of fluticasone is approximately 8
   hours'),
