   Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP). Penicillin VK (or amoxicillin) is the preferred oral an-
   tibiotic for mild-to-moderate CAP or for step-down therapy after initial intravenous antibiotics
   in patients 3 months and older with suspected or confirmed group A Streptococcus pneumonia Prevention of recurrent rheumatic fever. Penicillin V is the recommended oral agent; how-
   ever, intramuscular benzathine penicillin G every 4 weeks is the preferred regimen. The risk for
   rheumatic fever recurrence is higher in patients receiving oral therapy, even with good patient
   adherence Prophylaxis of pneumococcal infections in functionally or anatomically asplenic pa-
   tients with asplenia or hyposplenia, including sickle cell disease. Daily prophylaxis is rec-
   ommended regardless of immunization status. Appropriate duration of prophylaxis children
   with asplenia is not known. Some authors have recommended considering prophylaxis for all
   asplenic patients up to 5 years of age and at least 1 to 2 years after splenectomy 16,17,4 .
   Sickle cell disease. Begin prophylaxis in early infancy and continue through at least 5 years of
   age in all children with homozygous hemoglobin SS (HbSS). Prophylaxis may be discontinued
   at 5 years of age in children with HbSS. However, continue prophylaxis in children who had a
   splenectomy or an invasive pneumococcal infection. Pneumococcal vaccination series must be
   completed when discontinuing penicillin prophylaxis. In children with hemoglobin sickle cell
   disease (HbSC) and hemoglobin sickle β+-thalassemia (HbSβ+-thalassemia), penicillin prophy-
   laxis may be withheld. However, if they had a splenectomy then provide prophylaxis. These
   guidelines do not apply to patients with sickle cell trait (carrier state) 3 ,4 .

   Streptococcal Pharyngitis: Amoxicillin or penicillin is recommended as a drug of choice for
   Group A Streptococcal pharyngitis. For penicillin-allergic patients, a first-generation
   cephalosporin (no immediate-type penicillin hypersensitivity), clindamycin, azithromycin or
   clarithromycin are alternatives . A meta-analysis including 35 clinical trials found clinical and
   bacteriologic failure was less likely when a cephalosporin was used to treat group A strepto-
   coccal tonsillopharyngitis compared with penicillin Pediatric FDA-Approved Indications - 12 years of age and older

