   induration (6.8%) 3

   Monitoring
   No specific monitoring has been determined.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Protection against invasive disease is conferred mainly by IgG antibod-
   ies directed against capsular polysaccharides and opsonophagocytic activity (OPA) against
   Streptococcus pneumoniae (serotypes 1, 3, 4, 5, 6A, 6B, 7F, 9V, 14, 18C, 19A, 19F, 22F, 23F, and
   33F)'),
(119, 'Rabies Immune Globulin', 'vaccine', TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, 100, 'Rabies Prophylaxis, Postexposure

   Not previously vaccinated, usual dosage: 20 international units/kg IM given at the same
   time as the first dose of rabies vaccine; if able, infiltrate as much of the dose as possible into
   and around the bite wound and administer remaining amount IM. Give as soon as possible af-
   ter exposure; in case of delay, may administer at any time up to and including the seventh day
   after the first rabies vaccination', 'Human rabies immune globulin is indicated for postexposure prophylaxis, along with rabies
   vaccine for all persons suspected of exposure to rabies Administration
   Infiltration
   • Infiltrate as much of the dose as possible in and around the wound(s) if feasible; administer
   any remaining amount IM at a site distant from rabies vaccination site Intramuscular
   • Administer immediately after exposure or up to or on the seventh day after the first rabies
   vaccine dose; do not give additio', 'Common, >5%, pediatric: Injection site pain, headache, pyrexia, pain in extremity, bruising,
   fatigue, and vomiting 1

   Monitoring
   No specific monitoring has been determined.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Infiltration of human rabies immune globulin provides neutralizing an-
   tibody protection until the unvaccinated patient''s immune system begins producing antibodies', 'Rabies Prophylaxis, Postexposure

   Not previously vaccinated, usual dosage: 20 international units/kg IM given at the same
   time as the first dose of rabies vaccine; if able, infiltrate as much of the dose as possible into
   and around the bite wound and administer remaining amount IM. Give as soon as possible af-
   ter exposure; in case of delay, may administer at any time up to and including the seventh day
   after the first rabies vaccination', 'Human rabies immune globulin is indicated for postexposure prophylaxis, along with rabies
   vaccine for all persons suspected of exposure to rabies Administration
   Infiltration
   • Infiltrate as much of the dose as possible in and around the wound(s) if feasible; administer
   any remaining amount IM at a site distant from rabies vaccination site Intramuscular
   • Administer immediately after exposure or up to or on the seventh day after the first rabies
   vaccine dose; do not give additional doses as vaccine efficacy may be affected • Do not mix with rabies vaccine or administer in same syringe as vaccine • Administer IM at a site distant from rabies vaccination site. Do not administer in the gluteal
   region; if gluteal region is the wound site, may administer via infiltration there and give re-
   maining dose IM elsewhere 1/3
• May split large volumes (greater than 5 mL for adults or 2 mL for pediatric patients) and give
   at different sites', 'Common, >5%, pediatric: Injection site pain, headache, pyrexia, pain in extremity, bruising,
   fatigue, and vomiting 1

   Monitoring
   No specific monitoring has been determined.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Infiltration of human rabies immune globulin provides neutralizing an-
   tibody protection until the unvaccinated patient''s immune system begins producing antibodies'),
(120, 'Riboflavin 5-Phosphate', 'antibiotic', TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, 90, 'Corneal Ectasia
   14 years and older

   Following epithelial debridement, administer 1 drop of Photrexa® Viscous ophthalmically ev-
   ery 2 minutes for 30 minutes. If yellow flare in the anterior chamber is not observed via slit
   lamp examination at the end of this period, administer 1 drop every 2 minutes for an addi-
   tional 2 to 3 drops. May repeat as necessary until yellow flare is observed 1 ,2 .
   Once yellow flare is observed, perform ultrasound pachymetry, and if corneal thicknes', 'Pediatric FDA Approved Indication:
   Indicated in patients 14 years and older for use in corneal collagen cross-linking in combina-
   tion with the KXL™ System for the treatment of progressive keratoconus and corneal ectasia
   following refractive surgery 1 ,2 .

   Administration
   Do not inject; for topical ophthalmic use only 3 ,4 .

   For use with the KXL™ System only 3 , 4

   Syringes are single use; after use, discard syringes 3 ,4', 'Common: Corneal opacity (64%), punctate keratitis (25%), corneal striae (24%), corneal epithe-
   lium defect (24%), eye pain (17%), vision blurred (16%), foreign body sensation in eyes (15%),
   ocular hyperaemia (14%), photophobia (11%), and reduction in visual acuity (10%) 3 ,4

   Monitoring
   Monitor for resolution of epithelial defects 3 ,4 .', 'Monitor for resolution of epithelial defects 3 ,4 . Pharmacology', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of Action

Riboflavin 5''-phosphate sodium is the precursor of two coenzymes, flavin adenine dinucleotide
   and flavin mononucleotide, which catalyze oxidation and reduction reactions in a number of
   metabolic pathways. When used for corneal collagen cross-linking, riboflavin 5''-phosphate
   functions as a photo-enhancer and generates singlet oxygen which is responsible for the
   cross-linking 3 ,4', 'Corneal Ectasia
   14 years and older

   Following epithelial debridement, administer 1 drop of Photrexa® Viscous ophthalmically ev-
   ery 2 minutes for 30 minutes. If yellow flare in the anterior chamber is not observed via slit
   lamp examination at the end of this period, administer 1 drop every 2 minutes for an addi-
   tional 2 to 3 drops. May repeat as necessary until yellow flare is observed 1 ,2 .
   Once yellow flare is observed, perform ultrasound pachymetry, and if corneal thickness is less
