proton pump inhibitors as increased pH may decrease atazanavir absorption Common, greater than or equal to 5%: Jaundice and rash 2
adults Dyslipidemia: Dyslipidemia has been reported with the use of protease inhibitors. Assess all
changes, especially if the patient is receiving an older protease inhibitor (eg, lopinavir/ritonavir)', 'Renal excretion

   • Atazanavir: 7% unchanged 2

   • Cobicistat: 2% 2

   Feces

   • Cobicistat: 2% 2
   Hemodialysis removal

   • Atazanavir, severe renal impairment: 1% of dose removed by hemodialysis Elimination Half-Life: Atazanavir, 5 hours; cobicistat, 3 to 4 hours 2', 'KIDs List: Use caution in infants younger than 3 months (unless pharmacogenetic testing is
   used) due to risk of kernicterus Critical Dosing Notes

   Perform renal laboratory testing prior to initiation and during treatment in all patients, including es-
   timated CrCl, SCr, and urinalysis with microscopic examination When coadministering with tenofovir disoproxil fumarate, assess estimated CrCl, urine glucose, and
   urine protein at baseline and during treatment; additionally, monitor serum phosphorus in patients
   with chronic kidney disease In patients with underlying liver disease, perform hepatic laboratory testing prior to initiation and
   during treatment Do not initiate in pregnant individuals; use alternative regimen in patients who become pregnant
   during therapy HIV infection, treatment
   35 kg or greater: 1 tablet (atazanavir 300 mg/cobicistat 150 mg) orally once daily with food in
   combination with other antiretroviral agents 2', '• Atazanavir/cobicistat is indicated for the treatment of HIV-1 infection in combination with
   other antiretroviral agents in pediatric patients weighing at least 35 kg Limitations of use

   • Use in treatment-experienced patients should be guided by the number of baseline primary
   protease inhibitor resistance substitutions Administration
   • Administer with food • Dose separation may be necessary when coadministered with H2-receptor antagonists or
   proton pump inhibitors as increased pH may decrease atazanavir absorption', 'Common, greater than or equal to 5%: Jaundice and rash 2
   Results form a safety profile of atazanavir and cobicistat coadministered were similar to that in
   adults Dyslipidemia: Dyslipidemia has been reported with the use of protease inhibitors. Assess all
   patients with dyslipidemia for additional atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) risk
   factors (patients with HIV are considered at moderate risk for ASCVD). Consider ARV regimen
   changes, especially if the patient is receiving an older protease inhibitor (eg, lopinavir/ritonavir)
   and/or ritonavir-boosting. If LDL-C is 130 mg/dL or more but less than 250 mg/dL, refer to the
   NHLBI guidelines on a treatment approach with lifestyle modifications and drug therapy.
   Persistent dyslipidemia may lead to premature ASCVD Osteopenia and osteoporosis: Osteopenia and osteoporosis has been reported with
   atazanavir. To help prevent osteopenia or osteoporosis from occurring, ensure that the patient
   has sufficient intake and levels of calcium and vitamin D, encourage weight-bearing exercise,
   minimize modifiable risk factors (eg, smoking, low BMI, use of steroids or medroxyproges-
   terone), and consider switching from atazanavir to rilpivirine or an unboosted INSTI. If os-

teopenia or osteoporosis does occur, consider switching the ARV regimen and supplementing
   with vitamin D3 to raise serum 25-OH-vitamin D concentrations to 30 nanograms (ng)/mL Monitoring
