   cell types and mediators involving allergic and nonallergic/irritant mediated inflammation.
   Peak concentration is achieved in the lungs within 20 minutes. Protein binding is approxi-
   mately 85% to 90%. Rapidly and extensively metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 enzymes to 2
   major metabolites (less than 1% activity of parent compound). Renal elimination accounts for
   60% of the dose. Elimination half-life is approximately 2 to 3 hours Formoterol fumarate is a long-acting selective beta-2 adrenoceptor agonist which acts locally
   in the lung as a bronchodilator. Rapidly absorbed in the plasma reaching a peak concentration
   within 5 to 10 minutes after dosing Because of the increased lipophilicity of LABAs, the du-
   ration of action of bronchodilation is approximately 12 hours after a single dose; however, for
   chronic administration, the effect lasts for approximately 5 hours Plasma protein binding is
   approximately 61% to 64%, with 31% to 38% bound to albumin. Metabolized mainly by direct
   glucuronidation and O-demethylation, with the P450 enzyme subsystem (CYP2D6, CYP2C19,
   CYP2C9, and CYP2A6) involved in the O-demethylation. Renal elimination is approximately
   60%, with 6% as unchanged drug and 5% to 9% as conjugated formoterol. Fecal elimination
   accounts for approximately 32% of the drug. The mean elimination half-life is 10 hours'),
