   Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS)

   DRESS is a rare but serious adverse effect that has been reported with rilpivirine use.
   Discontinue all ARV drugs and other possible causative agents if confirmed. Consult with a spe-
   cialist to determine if corticosteroids and/or IVIG is appropriate. Provide supportive care of
   end-organ disease and do not reintroduce the offending agent Endocrine and metabolic
   Dyslipidemia has been reported with the use of tenofovir alafenamide. Assess all patients with
   dyslipidemia for additional atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease (ASCVD) risk factors (patients
   with HIV are considered at moderate risk for ASCVD). If LDL-C is 130 mg/dL or more but less
   than 250 mg/dL, refer to the NHLBI guidelines on a treatment approach with lifestyle modifica-
   tions and drug therapy. Persistent dyslipidemia may lead to premature ASCVD Gastrointestinal
   Pancreatitis has been reported with the use of NRTIs and ritonavir-boosted PIs; discontinue
   the offending agent and avoid reintroduction 7/20
Lactic acidosis

   Lactic acidosis has been reported with emtricitabine and tenofovir alafenamide use. Consider
   discontinuing ARV drugs temporarily in patients with a lactate 1 to 5 mmol/L (confirmed with
   second test) while conducting additional diagnostic work-up. In patients with a lactate 5
   mmol/L or greater (confirmed with second test) or 10 mmol/L (any one test), discontinue all
   ARV drugs and provide supportive therapy (eg, IV fluids, sedation, respiratory support).
   Following resolution of clinical and laboratory abnormalities, resume therapy with either an
   NRTI-sparing regimen or a revised NRTI-containing regimen. Monitor lactate monthly for 3
   months or more Psychiatric
   Neuropsychiatric symptoms (eg, abnormal dreams, depressive disorders, suicidal ideation)
   and other CNS manifestations (eg, dizziness, somnolence, insomnia, headache) have been re-
   ported with rilpivirine use. Depressive disorders of all grades were reported in 4% of chil-
   dren aged 12 to 17, with 7% of patients reporting severe depressive disorders. Consider drug
   substitution in cases of severe symptoms Black Box Warning
   Post Treatment Acute Exacerbation of Hepatitis B

   Severe acute exacerbations of hepatitis B have been reported in patients with HIV-1 and HBV
   who have discontinued products containing emtricitabine (FTC) and/or tenofovir disoproxil fu-
   marate (TDF), and may occur with discontinuation of emtricitabine/rilpivirine/tenofovir
   alafenamide.
   Hepatic function should be monitored closely with both clinical and laboratory follow-up for at
   least several months in patients with HIV-1 and HBV who discontinue
   emtricitabine/rilpivirine/tenofovir alafenamide. If appropriate, anti-hepatitis B therapy may be
   warranted Monitoring
