   mula) at least 8 weeks after the most recent dose • Previously vaccinated with any COVID-19 vaccine, Mnexspike®: Give 1 dose (0.2 mL) IM of
   Mnexspike® (2025 to 2026 formula) at least 12 weeks after the most recent dose Immunocompromised patients
   • 6 months or older: Patients who are moderately or severely immunocompromised are eligible
   to receive additional doses at the discretion of the provider. Complete guidance on treating
   moderately or severely immunocompromised patients 6 months or older is available at', 'Spikevax® (2025 to 2026 formula) is indicated for active immunization to prevent COVID-19 in
   pediatric patients aged 6 months or older with at least one underlying condition that puts
   them at high risk for severe outcomes from COVID-19 2
   Mnexspike® (2025 to 2026 formula) is indicated for active immunization to prevent COVID-19
   caused by SARS-CoV-2 in patients 12 years or older with at least one underlying condition that
   puts them at high risk for severe outcomes from COVID-19 Limitations of Effectiveness

   The Moderna COVID-19 vaccine (Spikevax® or Mnexspike®) may not protect all vaccine recipi-
   ents 3 ,2 .

   Administration
   • Inspect each dose in the dosing syringe prior to administration; the vaccine is a white to off-
   white suspension which may contain white or translucent product-related particulates.
   Confirm there are no particulates or discoloration; do not administer if either is observed 3 , • Administer via IM injection 3 ,2 . Preferred administration site is the deltoid muscle; alternate
   site is anterolateral thigh • For administration errors, please visit the following CDC website for details:

   appendix.html#appendix-b 6

   Preparation

   • Thaw suspension prior to administration 3 ,2 .
   • Thaw a carton of 1 single-dose pre-filled syringe (Mnexspike) or 1 single-dose pre-filled sy-
   ringe removed from a larger carton (Mnexspike or Spikevax) in refrigerator between 2° to 8° C
   (36° to 46° F) for 1 hour and 40 minutes. Alternatively, may thaw at room temperature be-
   tween 15° to 25° C (59° to 77° F) for 40 minutes 3 ,2 .

   • Thaw a carton of 2 single-dose pre-filled syringes in refrigerator between 2° to 8° C (36° to
   46° F) for 1 hour and 40 minutes. Alternatively, may thaw carton of 2 single-dose pre-filled sy-
   ringes at room temperature between 15° to 25° C (59° to 77° F) for 40 minutes 3 , 4/9
• Thaw a carton of 10 single-dose pre-filled syringes in refrigerator between 2° to 8° C (36° to
   46° F) for 2 hours and 40 minutes. Alternatively, may thaw carton of 10 single-dose pre-filled
   syringes at room temperature between 15° to 25° C (59° to 77° F) for 1 hour and 20 minutes
   3 ,2
          .

   • After thawing, do not refreeze 3 ,2 .

   • Do not shake 3 , • Attach appropriate size needle for intramuscular injection 3 ,2 .
   • After thawing, may store refrigerated between 2° to 8° C (36° to 46° F) for up to 60 days
   (Spikevax) or up to 90 days (Mnexspike) or up to expiration date printed on the carton, which-
   ever comes first . 3 ,2 .

   • After thawing, may store between 8° and 25° C (46° to 77° F) for up to 12 hours (Spikevax) or
   up to 24 hours (Mnexspike) 3 ,', 'Common, 6 months to 4 years of age: Injection site pain, swelling, or erythema; axillary
   swelling/tenderness; irritability/crying; loss of appetite; fever Common, greater than 4 years of age: Injection site pain, swelling, or erythema; axillary
   swelling/tenderness; fatigue; headache; myalgia; arthralgia; chills; nausea/vomiting; fever.
   Rates of adverse events were generally higher with the second dose compared with first dose
   3,2
         .

   Myocarditis and pericarditis:
   • Estimated incidence following administration of 2023-2024 formula was 8 cases per million
   doses in those 6 months to 64 years; in the subset of males 12 to 25 years, the incidence was
   25 to 27 cases per million doses 3 ,2 .

   • Onset typically occurs in first week after vaccination 3 ,2 .

   • Although limited, observational analysis of postmarketing data suggests lower myocarditis
   risk following booster dose relative to risk following primary series second dose • Most cases resolve within a few days of symptom occurrence with conservative manage-
   ment, though some may require intensive care support 3 ,2 .
   • Abnormal cardiac magnetic resonance imaging findings were persistent at 5 months post-
   vaccination in an observational study of those hospitalized for COVID-19 vaccine-associated
   myocarditis; significance of these findings is not known 3 ,2 .

   Monitoring
   No specific monitoring has been determined.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: The nucleoside-modified mRNA in the Moderna COVID-19 vaccine is
   formulated in lipid particles, which enable delivery of the mRNA into host cells to allow expres-

sion of the SARS-CoV-2 spike (S) antigen. The vaccine elicits an immune response to the S anti-
   gen, which protects against COVID-19 3 ,2 .'),
(143, 'Sodium Phenylbutyrate', 'antibiotic', TRUE, TRUE, FALSE, 100, 'Urea Cycle Metabolism Disorder; Adjunct

   NOTE: Sodium phenylbutyrate must be used in conjunction with a protein-restricted diet.
   Supplementation of essential amino acids may also be necessary. Powder or pellets; less than 20 kg: 450 to 600 mg/kg/day orally (powder or pellets) in equally
   divided doses with each meal or feeding (3 to 6 doses per day); MAX dose: 20 g/day 2 ,3 .

   20 kg or greater: 9 to 13 g/m2/day orally in equally divided doses with each meal or feeding
   (3 to 6 doses', 'Management of urea cycle metabolism disorders (UCD): Long-term treatment of patients
   (UCD) involves the restriction of dietary protein, supplementation with essential amino acids,
   and administration of compounds to augment excretion of waste nitrogen Sodium phenyl-
   butyrate has been used successfully for long-term management of hyperammonemia in a vari-
   ety of urea cycle disorders, including arginase deficiency, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase de-
   ficiency, N-acetyl-glutamate synth', 'When used for urea cycle disorders (UCD), it may be difficult to distinguish between the ad-
   verse effects of sodium phenylbutyrate and the effects of the disease or dietary restrictions
   1 ,9
          . Decreased appetite (4%), body odor (3%), and bad taste/ taste aversion (3%) have been re-
   ported by the parents and/or guardians of patients with UCD. In post-pubertal females, the
   most common adverse event (23%) was amenorrhea/menstrual dysfunction Monitoring
   Therapeutic Laborato', 'Dose Adjustment
• Monitor for signs and symptoms of neurotoxicity •Kidney: 80% to 100% 4 ,2 ,3', '•Kidney: 80% to 100% 4 ,2 ,3

   Elimination Half-Life

   •Parent compound: 5 to 8 4 ,2 ,3

   •Elimination metabolites: 15 to 29 hours (phenylacetate) 4 , 21', 'Urea Cycle Metabolism Disorder; Adjunct

   NOTE: Sodium phenylbutyrate must be used in conjunction with a protein-restricted diet.
   Supplementation of essential amino acids may also be necessary. Powder or pellets; less than 20 kg: 450 to 600 mg/kg/day orally (powder or pellets) in equally
   divided doses with each meal or feeding (3 to 6 doses per day); MAX dose: 20 g/day 2 ,3 .

   20 kg or greater: 9 to 13 g/m2/day orally in equally divided doses with each meal or feeding
   (3 to 6 doses per day) MAX dosing: 20 g/day 2 , NOTE: Adjust dose to maintain plasma ammonia level within normal range for patient''s age consid-
   ering clinical conditions 4
   Suspension, 20 kg or more and body surface area [BSA] of 2 m2or greater: 9 to 13
   g/m2/day orally divided into 3 to 6 doses in combination with dietary protein restriction and in
   some cases amino acid supplementation (eg, essential amino acids, arginine, citrulline, and
   protein-free caloric supplements); round each individual dose to the nearest available dosage
   strength MAX, 20 g/day 4

   Dose Adjustment

   Renal impairment: Monitor plasma ammonia levels when initiating therapy Hepatic impairment: Start at the lower end of the recommended dosing range and maintain
   patients on the lowest dose necessary to control plasma ammonia levels Neurotoxicity
   If symptoms of vomiting, nausea, headache, somnolence, or confusion are present in
   the absence of high ammonia levels or other concomitant illness: Consider dose reduc-
   tion 4', 'Management of urea cycle metabolism disorders (UCD): Long-term treatment of patients
   (UCD) involves the restriction of dietary protein, supplementation with essential amino acids,
   and administration of compounds to augment excretion of waste nitrogen Sodium phenyl-
   butyrate has been used successfully for long-term management of hyperammonemia in a vari-
   ety of urea cycle disorders, including arginase deficiency, carbamoyl phosphate synthetase de-
   ficiency, N-acetyl-glutamate synthase deficiency 7 ,6 , argininosuccinic acid synthetase deficiency
   6,8
      , and ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency 7 , 6 , 9 , According to a cross-sectional study
   from the UCD Consortium of the Rare Diseases Clinical Research Network, sodium phenylbu-
   tyrate is used in 37% of patients in the United States having UCD Another product, glycerol
   phenylbutyrate, was approved in 2013 for management of UCD. In two open-label, cross-over,
   comparative studies (total n=26; age 2 months to 17 years), glycerol phenylbutyrate was found
   to be non-inferior to sodium phenylbutyrate with respect to ammonia control when used at
   equivalent doses 12,13 . Signs and symptoms associated with UCD or its treatment either de-
   creased or resolved after 10 days of treatment with glycerol phenylbutyrate following a switch
   from the sodium product When asked, study patients expressed a preference for the glyc-
   erol product A further pooled analysis of 77 patients, which compared glycerol and sodium
   phenylbutyrate (n=77; 51 adults and 26 children age 6 to 17 years), also demonstrated non-in-
   feriority of the glycerol product. Analysis of non-inferiority was consistent among the subpopu-
   lations examined, including age, gender, age at onset, and UCD type. In addition, significantly
   lower serum ammonia and glutamine levels were obtained with the glycerol as compared with
   the sodium product. Of particular note, improvements in executive function were seen among
   the pediatric patients Induction of fetal hemoglobin synthesis in sickle cell disease: Increased levels of fetal he-
   moglobin (HbF) was associated with less severe disease and increased survival in patients with
   sickle cell disease (HbSS) In a pilot study of children with HbSS (n=8; age 4 to 13 years), sodium phenylbutyrate doubled
   the percentage of fetal reticulocytes in 75% of patients given daily doses ranging from 9 to 7 gm/m2/day; an apparent linear dose-response relationship was noted. After an initial rise,
   however, reticulocyte levels fell in 63% of patients after 3 to 11 weeks of therapy. Alterations in
   gene expression or tachyphylaxis with daily dosing were suggested as possible causes The
   same investigative group later reported that pulsed dosing, rather than daily use, may over-
   come the subsequent fall in reticulocyte levels seen in the first study. Weekly pulses consisting
   of 5 g/day, given for 4 out of 7 days each week, produced a sustained elevation of fetal reticu-
   locyte and HbF levels in a child from the pilot study who had then switched to pulsed dose
   therapy. Increases were maintained over a period of 3 years, when the patient opted to dis-
   continue therapy due to pill burden 2/11
Pediatric FDA Approved Indication

   Oral Tablet and Powder
   Sodium phenylbutyrate oral tablet and oral powder is indicated as adjunct therapy to dietary
   protein restriction and occasionally essential amino acid supplementation in urea cycle disor-
   ders with neonatal-onset (complete enzyme deficiency and presents within the first 28 days of
   life) or late-onset (partial deficiency occurring after the first month of life) involving deficiencies
   of carbamylphosphate synthetase, argininosuccinic acid synthetase, or ornithine transcar-
   bamylase Oral Suspension

   Sodium phenylbutyrate oral suspension is indicated as adjunctive therapy to standard of care,
   which includes dietary management, for the chronic management of pediatric patients weigh-
   ing 20 kg or greater and with a body surface area (BSA) of 2 m2 or greater, with urea cycle
   disorders (UCDs) involving deficiencies of carbamylphosphate synthetase (CPS), ornithine tran-
   scarbamylase (OTC), or argininosuccinic acid synthetase (AS) Limitation of Use
   • Episodes of acute hyperammonemia may occur in patients while on sodium phenylbutyrate.
   Sodium phenylbutyrate is not indicated for the treatment of acute hyperammonemia, which
   can be a life-threatening medical emergency that requires rapid acting interventions to reduce
   plasma ammonia levels 4 , Administration
   Powder
   • Each level teaspoon (included) dispenses 2 g of powder and 3 g sodium phenylbutyrate;
   each level tablespoon (included) dispenses 1 g of powder and 6 g sodium phenylbutyrate • Mix powder with water, solid food, or liquid food prior to administration. Administer immedi-
   ately after mixture with food. If mixed with water, suspension may be stored up to 1 week at
   room temperature or refrigerated. May be administered via mouth, gastrostomy, or nasogas-
   tric tube. Lightly shake before use Tablets

   • Tablets are for use in patients 20 kg or greater 3

Pellets

   • Administer orally; administration via gastrostomy or nasogastric tubes has not been evalu-
   ated • Schedule dosages at the same time as food consumption (meal or snack) • Use the calibrated dosing spoon to measure oral pellets. The dosing spoon is directly cali-
   brated in grams of sodium phenylbutyrate • Swallow the coated oral pellets with a drink (eg, water, fruit juices, protein-free infant formu-
   las) or sprinkle onto spoonful of apple sauce or carrot puree. Do not chew oral pellets directly
   or mix into liquids • Swallow immediately to minimize dissolution of coating • Missed dose: Take missed dose as soon as possible. There should be at least 3 hours be-
   tween 2 doses and doses should not be doubled to make up for the missed dose Suspension
   • Pour the entire contents of the Mix-Aid packet into approximately 4 oz of water in a cup and
   stir, forming a suspension 4
   • Pour the entire content of the sodium phenylbutyrate packet(s) into the suspension and stir;
   the entire packet should be administered within 5 minutes after stirring to minimize dissolu-
   tion of the coating • After 30 minutes, the suspension should be discarded • Pour another 4 oz of water into the cup and administer to make sure that any sodium
   phenylbutyrate remaining in the cup is consumed • Sodium phenylbutyrate oral suspension is for oral administration only; do not administer via
   gastrostomy or nasogastric tubes. Take with food • Missed dose: Take the missed dose as soon as possible on the same day 4

   Preparation

   Extemporaneous oral compound

   Note: Pellets for reconstitution to an oral suspension are commercially available.

   200 mg/mL Suspension (60 mL) 5
   • Triturate 12 g of sodium phenylbutyrate powder to a fine powder using a glass mortar and
   pestle.

• Geometrically add 30 mL of vehicle until a smooth suspension is formed. Vehicle options are
   a 1:1 mixture of Ora-Plus and Ora-Sweet or a 1:1 mixture of Ora-Plus and Ora-Sweet SF.

   • Transfer mixture into a 2 ounce amber plastic bottle.

   • Rinse mortar with enough vehicle to bring final volume to 60 mL.

   • Label the container with "Shake well before using."
   • Stable for 90 days at room temperature (23 to 25C). Use of a masking agent (chocolate syrup
   or peanut butter) before and after administration is recommended.', 'When used for urea cycle disorders (UCD), it may be difficult to distinguish between the ad-
   verse effects of sodium phenylbutyrate and the effects of the disease or dietary restrictions
   1 ,9
          . Decreased appetite (4%), body odor (3%), and bad taste/ taste aversion (3%) have been re-
   ported by the parents and/or guardians of patients with UCD. In post-pubertal females, the
   most common adverse event (23%) was amenorrhea/menstrual dysfunction Monitoring
   Therapeutic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Plasma levels of ammonia, arginine, branched-chain amino acids, and serum proteins should
   be maintained within normal limits 3
   • Plasma glutamine should be maintained below 1000 micromol/L; these levels are unlikely to
   be associated with hyperammonemia 3

   • Periodically monitor serum level of phenylbutyrate and its metabolites 3 , phenylacetate, and
   phenylacetylglutamine 2 ,3 . Consider monitoring urinary phenylacetylglutamine levels, if avail-
   able, to assist with dosage adjustment Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Monitor serum potassium during treatment Toxic Physical Monitoring

   • Monitor for signs and symptoms of neurotoxicity', 'Dose Adjustment
• Monitor for signs and symptoms of neurotoxicity •Kidney: 80% to 100% 4 ,2 ,3', '•Kidney: 80% to 100% 4 ,2 ,3

   Elimination Half-Life

   •Parent compound: 5 to 8 4 ,2 ,3

   •Elimination metabolites: 15 to 29 hours (phenylacetate) 4 , 21'),
