   Mechanism of Action
   In patients with von Willebrand disease, von Willebrand factor recombinant acts to promote
   hemostasis by mediating platelet adhesion to damaged vascular sub-endothelial matrix (eg,
   collagen) and platelet aggregation, and as a carrier protein for factor VIII, protecting it from
   rapid proteolysis Immunogenicity
   Of the 132 patients who received von Willebrand factor (VWF) recombinant in clinical trials, 1
   patient who was treated with VWF recombinant perioperatively developed treatment-emer-
   gent binding antibodies against VWF following a surgery (no adverse events or lack of hemo-
   static efficacy was reported). No binding antibodies against potential impurities such as rFurin,
   CHO-protein or mouse IgG developed after treatment with VWF recombinant. Two patients in-
   cluded in the surgery study had pre-existing high-titer specific binding antibodies against VWF
   which were associated with a significantly decreased VWF:Ag activity post-infusion of either
   plasma derived VWF (pdVWF) or VWF recombinant leading to decreased activity of VWF:RCo,
   VWF:CB and FVIII:C. This finding indicates the potential clinical significance of pre-existing bind-
   ing (non-neutralizing) antibodies: patients with von Willebrand disease previously treated with
   pdVWF concentrates may be at risk to express a pre-existing binding antibody against VWF
   prior to first exposure to VWF recombinant which might result in a decreased hemostatic re-
   sponse. Such patients could be managed clinically by administration of higher doses of von
   Willebrand factor recombinant based on the pharmacokinetic data for each individual patient 11/13
