  infuse each dose over 4 hours; total daily dose is 9 g sulbactam/day 12 years or older, continuous infusion: 27 g ampicillin/sulbactam (18 g ampicillin plus 9 g sulbactam) as a continuous
  IV infusion. Suggested for CRAB isolates with sulbactam mean inhibitory concentrations (MICs) of 8 mcg/mL or greater CNS infections: Infuse doses over 1 hour or less Concomitant medications: Administer with at least 1 other agent (polymyxin B, minocycline, tigecycline, or cefidero-
  col); may discontinue this agent once clinical improvement occurs if a long duration of treatment is indicated (eg, os-
  teomyelitis) 2 ,3 .

  Anthrax, systemic; penicillin-susceptible

  1 month or older: 50 mg/kg/dose ampicillin component IV every 6 hours; maximum 2 grams ampicillin/dose Duration of therapy: At least 2 weeks; initiate with IV therapy and switch to oral based on clinical improvement. If
  aerosol exposure occurred or is suspected and patient is immunocompromised, transition to a postexposure prophy-
  laxis regimen after completion of the treatment regimen for a total duration of 60 days. Immunocompetent patients
  do not need further prophylaxis after completing treatment Infective endocarditis

  Children and adolescents: 200 mg/kg/day of ampicillin component (300 mg/kg/day ampicillin plus sulbactam compo-
  nent) IV divided every 4 to 6 hours (maximum 12 g/day ampicillin/sulbactam (8 g/day ampicillin component)).
  Duration is generally 4 to 6 weeks, longer duration may be required 5 ,6 .
  Initial empirical therapy or culture-negative endocarditis, native valve or late (greater than 1 year) prosthetic valve: Add gen-
  tamicin 3 to 6 mg/kg/day IV divided every 8 hours and with or without vancomycin 60 mg/kg/day IV divided every 6
  hours (maximum 2 g/day). When prosthetic valve is present, add rifAMPin 15 to 20 mg/kg/day IV/orally divided every
  12 hours (maximum 600 mg/day) 1/15
