mation, especially with regard to interactions with a strong CYP3A inhibitor such as ritonavir COVID-19, mild to moderate, in patients at high risk for progression to severe COVID-19
potential drug interaction • Modification of other medications is needed due to a potential drug interaction • Nirmatrelvir and ', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of Action: Nirmatrelvir is a peptidomimetic inhibitor of the SARS-CoV-2 main pro-
   tease (Mpro), also referred to as 3C-like protease (3CLpro) or nsp5 protease. Inhibition of
   SARS-CoV-2 Mpro renders it incapable of processing polyprotein precursors, preventing viral
   replication. Nirmatrelvir was found to bind directly to the SARS-CoV-2 Mpro active site by X-ray
   crystallography. Ritonavir is an HIV-1 protease inhibitor but is not active against SARS-CoV-2
   M', 'Nirmatrelvir with ritonavir is an unapproved product as limited clinical data are available. Serious
   and unexpected adverse events may occur that have not been previously reported with use Consider the potential for drug interactions prior to and during nirmatrelvir with ritonavir therapy
   and review concomitant medications during therapy; consult the Fact Sheet as well as other appro-
   priate resources including prescribing information for the interacting drug for comprehensive infor-
   mation, especially with regard to interactions with a strong CYP3A inhibitor such as ritonavir COVID-19, mild to moderate, in patients at high risk for progression to severe COVID-19
   12 years or older AND at least 40 kg: Nirmatrelvir 300 mg (two 150-mg tablets) with ritonavir
   100 mg (one 100-mg tablet); administer all three tablets together orally twice daily for 5 days;
   initiate as soon as possible after COVID-19 diagnosis and within 5 days of symptom onset 1

   • Dosage Adjustments
   • Renal impairment (mild; estimated GFR 60 to less than 90 mL/min): No adjustment nec-
   essary 1
   • Renal impairment (moderate; estimated GFR 30 to less than 60 mL/min): Nirmatrelvir
   150 mg and ritonavir 100 mg twice daily for 5 days 1

   • Renal impairment (severe; estimated GFR less than 30 mL/min): Use not recommended;
   appropriate dosage for this population has not yet been determined. Systemic exposure of nir-
   matrelvir increases in renally impaired patients 1
   • Hepatic impairment (mild to moderate; Child-Pugh Class A or B): No adjustment neces-
   sary 1
   • Hepatic impairment (severe; Child-Pugh Class C): Use not recommended; no pharmacoki-
   netic or safety data are available for this population 1', 'Evidence
   The safety and effectiveness of nirmatrelvir and ritonavir have not been established in pedi-
   atric patients. The authorized adult dosing regimen is expected to result in comparable serum
   exposures of nirmatrelvir and ritonavir in patients aged 12 years or older and weighing at least
   40 kg as observed in adults, and adults with similar body weight were included in the trial EPIC-
   HR COVID-19 Infection - HIV Co-infection
   Guidelines: Nirmatrelvir/ritonavir should be considered in children with HIV in the outpatient
   setting for treatment of laboratory-confirmed or clinically suspected mild to moderate COVID-
   19, aged 12 years or more, weighing 40 kg or more, and are at high risk of progressing to se-
   vere COVID-19 due to advanced or untreated HIV, or another high risk condition defined by
   the CDC. Ritonavir-boosted nirmatrelvir may be administered with antiretrovirals (ARV), includ-
   ing those that contain cobicistat or ritonavir, without any interruption of or modification to ARV
   therapy. For patients 12 years or older, nirmatrelvir/ritonavir is the preferred treatment option Emergency Use Authorization
   • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir administered together are authorized for emergency use by the US
   Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for the treatment of adult and pediatric patients (age 12
   years or older and 40 kg or greater) with a current diagnosis of mild to moderate COVID-19
   and who are at high risk for progression to severe COVID-19, including hospitalization or death • See the CDC website for more information on medical conditions and factors associated with
   increased risk for progression to severe COVID-19:
   ncov/need-extra-precautions/people-with-medical-conditions.html Limitations of Authorized Use

   • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are not authorized for initiation of treatment in patients requiring
   hospitalization due to severe or critical COVID-19 • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are not authorized for use as pre-exposure or post-exposure pro-
   phylaxis for prevention of COVID-19 • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are not authorized for use for longer than 5 consecutive days • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are not approved for any use, including for use for the treatment
   of COVID-19 2/8
• This emergency use authorization (EUA) for nirmatrelvir and ritonavir is valid only for the du-
   ration of the declaration that circumstances exist justifying the authorization of the emergency
   use of nirmatrelvir and ritonavir, unless the authorization is terminated or revoked sooner Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir may be prescribed for an individual patients by a state-li-
   censed pharmacist under the following conditions:
   • Sufficient information is available, such as through access to health records less than 12
   months old or consultation with a health care provider in an established provider-patient rela-
   tionship with the individual patient, to assess renal and hepatic function; and 2

   • Sufficient information is available, such as through access to health records, patient reporting
   of medical history, or consultation with a health care provider in an established provider-pa-
   tient relationship with the individual patient, to obtain a comprehensive list of medications
   (prescribed and non-prescribed) that the patient is taking to assess for potential drug interac-
   tions The state-licensed pharmacist should refer an individual patient for clinical evaluation
   (eg, telehealth, in-person visit) with a physician, advanced practice registered nurse, or
   physician assistant licensed or authorized under state law to prescribe if any of the fol-
   lowing apply:
   • Sufficient information is not available to assess renal and hepatic function or to assess for a
   potential drug interaction • Modification of other medications is needed due to a potential drug interaction • Nirmatrelvir and ritonavir are not an appropriate therapeutic option based on the authorized
   Fact Sheet for Healthcare Providers or due to potential drug interactions for which recom-
   mended monitoring would not be feasible Administration
   • Both nirmatrelvir and ritonavir tablets can be taken with or without food; swallow the tablets
   whole and do not chew, break, or crush • If a dose is missed within 8 hours of the time it is usually taken, administer as soon as possi-
   ble and resume the normal dosing schedule; if a dose is missed by more than 8 hours, do not
   administer the missed dose and instead administer the next dose at the regularly scheduled
   time. Do not double the dose to make up for a missed dose • Use of ritonavir may reduce the efficacy of combined hormonal contraceptives. Advise pa-
   tients using combined hormonal contraceptives to use an effective alternative contraceptive

method or an additional barrier method of contraception', 'Common (adults): Dysgeusia (6% vs less than 1% with placebo), diarrhea (3% vs 2%), hyper-
