inhibitor, PrEP = pre-exposure prophylaxis', 'Pharmacology
   Tenofovir disoproxil fumarate is a nucleotide analog HIV-1 and HBV reverse transcriptase in-
   hibitor and is a prodrug that is rapidly hydrolyzed to tenofovir via plasma esterases following
   oral absorption Tenofovir is structurally, and has actions similar to adefovir 17,18 . Steady-

state pharmacokinetics of HIV-1-infected pediatric patients ages 2 years to less than 18 years
   (n=31) were similar to exposures achieved in adults receiving tenofovir 300 mg once daily 5 ,19 .
   Relative to the tablet formulation, the Cmax was 26% lower for the oral powder; however, the
   AUC was similar for both formulations following a single dose of tenofovir in a bioequivalence
   study conducted under non-fasted conditions. Oral bioavailability in fasted patients is approxi-
   mately 25%, and is increased following administration with a high fat meal. Administration
   with a light meal does not alter the bioavailability. Peak concentration achieved within 1 hour
   and food delays Tmax by one hour. Tenofovir may be given without regard to meals. Protein
   binding to human plasma is less than 7% and serum protein binding is approximately 2%.
   Eliminated primarily by the renal system. Following administration of multiple doses of teno-
   fovir 300 mg once daily, 32% +/- 10% of the administered dose is recovered in the urine over
   24 hours. Both glomerular filtration and active tubular secretion are involved in the renal ex-
   cretion Tenofovir clearance increases with body weight and decreases with the coadminis-
   tration of lopinavir/ritonavir Half-life in children is approximately 5 hours (range, 8 to 18
   hours)'),
