   the deltoid for children greater than 2 years of age 3 , Administration in the gluteal region is
   not recommended as this may result in a suboptimal response Vaqta® may be given concurrently with ', 'Most common adverse events include injection site pain/tenderness, fever, and headache 2 , Monitoring
   Monitor injection site for local reactions.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Hepatitis A vaccine contains inactivated whole virus vaccine derived from hepatitis A vaccine
   grown in MRC-5 human diploid cells. Havrix® contains 720 enzyme linked immunosorbent as-
   say units in each 5-mL pediatric dose, adsorbed onto 25 mg of aluminum as aluminum hy-
   droxide. Vaqta® contains 25 units of hepatitis A virus antigen in each 5-mL pediatric dose,
   adsorbed onto 225 mg of aluminum as amorphous aluminum hydroxyphosphate sulfate.
   Seroconversion rates are a', 'Routine vaccination; hepatitis A
   12 months or older: 5 mL IM x 1 dose, followed by a booster dose of 5 mL 6 to 12 months
   later for Havrix® 2 or 6 to 18 months later for Vaqta® A booster dose of Vaqta® may be
   given at 6 to 12 months following a primary dose of Havrix® Catch-up vaccination; hepatitis A 1
   Unvaccinated, up to 18 years: Complete 2-dose series; administer doses at least 6 months
   apart
   Previously given 1 dose at 12 months or older: Give second dose at least 6 months after
   first dose

   Vaccination, international travel 1
   6 to 11 months: Administer 1 dose before departure; revaccinate with 2 doses given at least 6
   months apart between 12 and 23 months of age
   12 months or older, unvaccinated: Administer dose 1 as soon as travel is considered
   Vaccination, postexposure prophylaxis

   12 months and older
   Healthy persons, unvaccinated: Single, age-appropriate vaccine dose as soon as possible af-
   ter hepatitis A exposure Immunocompromised or chronic liver disease, unvaccinated: Single age-appropriate vac-
   cine dose AND immune globulin IM simultaneously in different anatomic sites as soon as pos-
   sible after hepatitis A exposure 4', 'The hepatitis A vaccine is indicated for prevention of hepatitis A in persons aged 12 months
   and older 2 ,6 .

   Administration
   For IM use only; do not inject IV, subQ, or intradermally 3 ,2 .

   The preferred injection site is the anterolateral thigh in children 12 to 23 months of age and
   the deltoid for children greater than 2 years of age 3 , Administration in the gluteal region is
   not recommended as this may result in a suboptimal response Vaqta® may be given concurrently with measles, mumps, rubella vaccine; varicella vaccine;
   diphtheria and tetanus toxoids and acellular pertussis vaccine; measles, mumps, rubella, and
   varicella vaccine; pneumococcal 7-valent conjugate vaccine; Haemophilus B conjugate vaccine
   (meningococcal protein conjugate); and immune globulin, human; administer with different sy-
   ringes at different injection sites Havrix® may be given concurrently with DTaP (Infanrix®), Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib)
   conjugate vaccine (PRP-T), MMR vaccine, varicella vaccine, and immune globulin; administer
   with different syringes at different injection sites 5 ,', 'Most common adverse events include injection site pain/tenderness, fever, and headache 2 , Monitoring
   Monitor injection site for local reactions.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Hepatitis A vaccine contains inactivated whole virus vaccine derived from hepatitis A vaccine
   grown in MRC-5 human diploid cells. Havrix® contains 720 enzyme linked immunosorbent as-
   say units in each 5-mL pediatric dose, adsorbed onto 25 mg of aluminum as aluminum hy-
   droxide. Vaqta® contains 25 units of hepatitis A virus antigen in each 5-mL pediatric dose,
   adsorbed onto 225 mg of aluminum as amorphous aluminum hydroxyphosphate sulfate.
   Seroconversion rates are approximately 96% to 100% after the first dose and 99% to 100% af-
   ter the second dose 2 ,6 .'),
(175, 'Hepatitis B Vaccine', 'vaccine', TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, 100, 'Routine vaccination, maternal HBsAg-negative: 3-dose series of Engerix-B® 10 mcg (0.5
   mL) or Recombivax HB® 5 mcg (0.5 mL) IM 1 ,2 ,3
   • Birth dose: Administer first dose within 24 hours of birth for medically stable infants 2000 g or
   more. For infants less than 2000 g, administer 1 dose at chronological age 1 month or hospital
   discharge (whichever is earlier, and even if weight is still less than 2000 g)
   • Second dose: 1 dose at 1 to 2 months of age (minimum interval of 4 weeks be', 'Immunoprophylaxis against infection caused by all known subtypes of hepatitis B virus in chil-
   dren of all ages 2 ,3 .

   Administration
   Administration

   Administer IM in the anterolateral thigh (preferred in infants and young children) or deltoid
   (older children; preferred in adolescents 2 , The vaccine may be administered subcutaneously in patients at risk for hemorrhage following
   IM injection, but the immune response may be lower 2 ,3 ,5 .
   A 22- to 25-gauge needle should be ', 'Fever, fatigue, and injection site soreness are common. Rare anaphylactic reactions (ie, hives,
   swelling of the mouth, hypotension, breathing difficulty, and shock) have been reported 2 , Monitoring
   Testing for immunity after completion of at least 3 doses of the vaccination series (at age 9
   through 18 months) is recommended for infants born to HBsAg-positive mothers', '', 'Pharmacology
   Recombinant hepatitis B vaccines are produced by Saccharomyces cerevisiae (common baker''s
   yeast) that has been genetically modified to synthesize HBsAg. Both vaccines are inactivated
   (noninfective) products that contain HBsAg protein adsorbed to aluminum hydroxide, and may
   be interchanged with comparable efficacy 2 ,3 .', 'Routine vaccination, maternal HBsAg-negative: 3-dose series of Engerix-B® 10 mcg (0.5
   mL) or Recombivax HB® 5 mcg (0.5 mL) IM 1 ,2 ,3
   • Birth dose: Administer first dose within 24 hours of birth for medically stable infants 2000 g or
   more. For infants less than 2000 g, administer 1 dose at chronological age 1 month or hospital
   discharge (whichever is earlier, and even if weight is still less than 2000 g)
   • Second dose: 1 dose at 1 to 2 months of age (minimum interval of 4 weeks between dose 1
   and 2). Use monovalent hepatitis B vaccine if doses administered before 6 weeks of age
   • Third dose: 1 dose at 6 to 18 months of age (no earlier than 24 weeks of age; minimum inter-
   val of at least 8 weeks after dose 2 and 16 weeks after dose 1). A total of 4 doses of hepatitis B
   vaccine is permitted when a combination vaccine containing hepatitis B is used after the birth
   dose

   Routine vaccination, maternal HBsAg-positive: 3- or 4-dose series of Engerix-B® 10 mcg
   (0.5 mL) or Recombivax HB® 5 mcg (0.5 mL) IM. Test for HBsAg and anti-HBs at age 9 to 12
   months, or if hepatitis B series is delayed, test 1 to 2 months after final dose 1 ,2 ,3 .

   • Birth dose: Administer first dose before 12 hours of age regardless of birth weight. Administer
   with 5 mL of hepatitis B immune globulin in a separate limb
   • Second dose: For infants with birthweight 2000 g or more, 1 dose at 1 to 2 months of age
   (minimum interval of 4 weeks between dose 1 and 2). For infants with birthweight less than
   2000 g, 1 dose at 1 month of age
   • Third dose: For infants with birthweight 2000 g or more, final dose at 6 months of age (no ear-
   lier than 24 weeks of age; minimum interval of at least 8 weeks after dose 2 and 16 weeks after
   dose 1). For infants with birthweight less than 2000 grams, 1 dose at 2 to 3 months of age
   • Fourth dose: For infants with birthweight less than 2000 g only, final dose at 6 months of age

Routine vaccination, maternal HBsAg-unknown: 3- or 4-dose series of Engerix-B® 10 mcg
   (0.5 mL) or Recombivax HB® 5 mcg (0.5 mL) IM. If mother is determined to be HBsAg-positive
   or status remains unknown, test for HBsAg and anti-HBs at age 9 to 12 months, or if hepatitis
   B series is delayed, test 1 to 2 months after final dose 1 ,2 ,3 .
   • Birth dose: For infants with birthweight 2000 g or more, administer first dose before 12 hours
   of age. Determine mother''s HBsAg status as soon as possible. If positive, administer 5 mL of
   hepatitis B immune globulin by 7 days of age. For infants with birthweight less than 2000 g, ad-
   minister first dose with 5 mL of hepatitis B immune globulin in separate limbs before 12
   hours of age
   • Second dose: For infants with birthweight 2000 g or more, 1 dose at 1 to 2 months of age
   (minimum interval of 4 weeks between dose 1 and 2). For infants with birthweight less than
   2000 g, 1 dose at 1 month of age
   • Third dose: For infants with birthweight 2000 g or more, final dose at 6 months of age (no ear-
   lier than 24 weeks of age; minimum interval of at least 8 weeks after dose 2 and 16 weeks after
   dose 1). For infants with birthweight less than 2000 grams, 1 dose at 2 to 3 months of age
   • Fourth dose: For infants with birthweight less than 2000 g only, final dose at 6 months of age

   Catch-up vaccination, hepatitis B 1 ,2 ,3

   • Engergix-B® or Recombivax HB® (pediatric/adolescent formulation: 3-dose series of
   Engerix-B® 10 mcg (0.5 mL) or Recombivax HB® 5 mcg (0.5 mL) IM given at intervals of 0, 1 to
   2 months, and 6 months for any unvaccinated patient

   • Recombivax HB® (adult formulation): Alternative 2-dose series of Recombivax HB 10 mcg
   (1 mL) given at least 4 months apart for unvaccinated adolescents 11 to 15 years of age

   Engerix-B® alternative regimens

   • 10 mcg (0.5 mL) IM given at 0, 1, 2, and 12 months for children who are at high risk (ie,
   neonates born of HBsAg-positive mothers, children recently exposed to the virus, and travel-
   ers to high-risk areas) • 10 mcg (0.5 mL) IM given at 0, 12, and 24 months for children 5 to 16 years of age in whom an
   extended schedule is acceptable based on low risk of exposure • 20 mcg (1 mL) IM given at 0, 1, and 6 months in adolescents 11 years of age and older • 20 mcg (1 mL) IM given at 0, 1, 2, and 12 months in adolescents 11 years of age and older
   who are at high risk (ie, recently exposed to virus, travelers to high-risk areas) 2/8
Revaccination; hepatitis B: 1 dose of age-specific hepatitis B vaccine if anti-HBs is less than
   10 international units/mL in certain populations, including infants born to HBsAg-positive
   mothers, persons on predialysis or maintenance dialysis, and other immunocompromised per-
   sons 1

