   Dosage: Individualize the dosage based on the patient’s metabolic needs, blood glucose moni-
   toring results and glycemic control goal Concomitant medication: In general, use should be accompanied by intermediate- or long-
   acting insulin', 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus
   Guidelines: Intensive insulin therapy, either by multiple subQ injections of prandial and basal
   insulin or continuous subQ infusion, is the mainstay of treatment. Prandial insulin daily re-
   quirements are approximately 55% to 70% of the total daily dose, while basal insulin accounts
   for 30% to 45% of the total daily dose. Prepubertal children typically require 7 to 1
   units/kg/day and during puberty, the insulin requirements may rise to 1 to 2 units/kg/day.
   Rapid acting insulins such as insulin lispro should ideally be given 10 to 15 minutes before
   meals or immediately before meals to help reduce postprandial hyperglycemia and nocturnal
   hypoglycemia 2 , 1/7
In a 24-week study in children and adolescents aged 6 to 18 years (N=283), subQ insulin aspart
   was comparable to regular human insulin in achieving glycemic control in pediatric patients
   with type 1 diabetes mellitus who were also receiving NPH insulin. HbA1c results were also
   found to be similar between regular insulin and insulin aspart in 26 patients aged 2 to 6 years Type 2 diabetes mellitus
   Guidelines: Insulin therapy should be initiated in pediatric patients presenting with
   ketosis/ketoacidosis and/or the patients HbA1c is greater than 5%. After initial treatment, in-
   sulin may be weaned off over 2 to 6 weeks by decreasing the insulin dose 30% to 50% with
   each increase in the metformin dose. If glycemic goals are not met after initial treatment and
   the addition of a glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) agonist or empagliflozin, consider adding an
   intermediate/long-acting insulin. The starting dose of basal insulin is typically 25 to 5 units/kg
   and the dose is titrated based on results of self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) 2 ,3 .

   FDA Approved Pediatric Uses
   Insulin aspart-szjj is indicated to improve glycemic control in pediatric patients with diabetes
   mellitus Administration
   • Inject subQ within 5 to 10 minutes before a meal into the abdominal area, thigh, buttocks or
   upper arm • Rotate injection sites within the same region from one injection to the next to reduce the risk
   of lipodystrophy and localized cutaneous amyloidosis. Do not inject into areas of lipodystrophy
   or localized cutaneous amyloidosis • Do not mix with any other insulin • Patients with visual impairment who may rely on audible clicks to dial their dose should use
   Merilog™ SoloStar prefilled pen with caution', 'Adverse reactions to insulin aspart include hypoglycemia, allergic reactions, injection site reac-
   tions, lipodystrophy, rash, and pruritus 3/7
Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus
   •Routinely assess glycemic control to determine treatment effectiveness. Monitor HbA1c at
   least twice yearly in a patient who is well-controlled, every 3 months if not meeting treatment
   goals or when there is a change of therapy, or more frequently as clinically warranted. An
   HbA1c goal of less than 7% is appropriate for many children and adolescents, but HbA1c goals
   may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to each patient •Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) multiple times a day (6 to 10 times a day via
   meter or continuous glucose monitoring [CGM]), including prior to meals, snacks, at bedtime,
   and as needed to assist in meeting goals of therapy. Real-time or intermittently scanned CGM
   should be offered as soon as possible to children on multiple daily injections or insulin pump
   therapy Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus

   •Monitor HbA1c every 3 months if possible. A reasonable HbA1c goal for most patients is less
   than 5%, but HbA1c goals may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to
   each patient 4 , 3
   •Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) as needed to assist in meeting goals of ther-
   apy; SMBG should be individualized to each patient 4

   Toxic Monitoring

   • Increase the frequency of blood glucose monitoring with changes to insulin dosage (including
   strength, manufacturer, type or method of administration), meal pattern, physical activity, and
   in patients with renal or hepatic impairment and hypoglycemia unawareness • Carefully monitor blood glucose during pregnancy • Monitor potassium in patients at risk for hypokalemia such as patients taking potassium-low-
   ering medications and medications that are sensitive to serum potassium concentrations • Observe for signs and symptoms of heart failure', '', '• Protein binding: Insulin aspart is similar to regular human insulin and has a low binding
   affinity to plasma proteins (less than 10%) Half-life: 81 minutes after subQ administration'),
