   blood glucose closely during times of stress, illness, or changes in insulin regimen (insulin
   strength, manufacturer, type, injection site or method of administration), coadministered med-
   ications, changes in level of physical activity, or meal patterns (macronutrient content or timing
   of meals) • Closely monitor blood potassium levels in patients at risk for hypokalemia (eg, concomitant
   use of potassium-lowering medications, or medications sensitive to serum potassium concen-
   trations) • Monitor for signs and symptoms of hyperglycemia (eg, polyuria, polyphagia, polydipsia,
   blurred vision, fatigue) or hypoglycemia (sweating, trembling, tachycardia, hunger, anxiety,
   dizziness, headache, clouding of vision, loss of fine motor skills, combativeness, seizures, men-
   tal confusion, and loss of consciousness) • Monitor for symptoms of hypoglycemia, with increased monitoring in patients with higher
