   In a randomized study (N=151), pediatric patients who received peginterferon alfa-2a therapy
   were significantly more likely to have HBeAg seroconversion compared with those receiving
   observation alone by week 48 (26% vs 6%; odds ratio, 43; 95% CI, 54 to 2). Compared
   with observation, the percentage of patients achieving HBV DNA less than 20,000 international
   units/mL and less than 2000 international units/mL, respectively, was greater with peginter-
   feron alfa-2a therapy (34% and 29% vs 4% and 2%). ALT normalization occurred in 52% of
   peginterferon alfa-2a patients and 12% of patients who were observed. Patients (3 to less than
   18 years of age) with previously untreated HBeAg-positive chronic hepatitis B in the immune-
   active phase in the therapy group (n=111) received peginterferon alfa-2a once weekly for 48
   weeks according to body surface area (BSA) or were observed (n=50) for 48 weeks Chronic Hepatitis C Virus (HCV) Infection:
   In a retrospective study (n=157), 28% of children (2 years or older) infected with chronic hepati-
   tis C cleared the infection spontaneously. Younger age at follow-up and normal ALT levels fa-

vored clearance In a prospective randomized controlled trial (n=114), sustained viral re-
   sponse (SVR) at 72 weeks was achieved in 53% of the combination therapy arm and 21% of
   monotherapy group Peginterferon alfa 2a has been used in children younger than 5 years.
   In an open-label study (n=14), peginterferon alfa 2a monotherapy was effective in children 2 to
   8 years of age (mean age, 4 years) with chronic hepatitis C with compensated liver disease
   and interferon alpha-naive; SVR at 72 weeks was 43% patients A meta-analysis, which examined the efficacy and safety of ribavirin combined with peginter-
   feron alfa-2a or alfa-2b in children (n=8 studies; age 3 to 18 years), revealed an early virologic
   response (EVR) in 70% of patients, a sustained viral response (SVR) in 58%, and a relapse rate
   of 7%. EVR and SVR were higher for genotypes 2/3 (87% and 89%, respectively) than for geno-
   types 1/4 (61% and 52%, respectively). Treatment discontinuations were primarily due to lack
   of virologic response (15%), while 4% were due to virologic breakthrough and another 4% due
   to adverse events. Comparable efficacy and safety were determined for ribavirin when com-
   bined with either peginterferon product Newer therapies, protease inhibitor (eg, boceprevir, telaprevir) added to combination therapy
   with ribavirin and peginterferon alfa, are being used in adults and are under investigation for
   pediatric patients Guideline Recommendations: Direct-acting antiviral (DAA) treatment with an approved regi-
   men is recommended for all children and adolescents with HCV infection 3 years or older, as
   they will benefit from antiviral therapy, regardless of disease severity. The presence of extra-
   hepatic manifestations (eg, cryoglobulinemia, rash, and glomerulonephritis) or advanced fibro-
   sis should lead to early antiviral therapy to minimize future morbidity and mortality. Selection
   of DAA and duration of therapy should take into account the patient''s viral genotype, presence
   and grading of cirrhosis, and any prior treatments. Current recommendations are found in the
