   Administration
   • Administer by IV injection through a 24-gauge or larger angiocatheter into a peripheral vein
   at a rate not exceeding 05 mL/second • Suggested methods of administration include: a short extension tubing, heparin lock, or in-
   travenous line, all with a 3-way stopcock • Do not administer by intra-arterial injection • Do not aspirate blood back into the administration syringe before administration 2/6
• For short extension tubing or heparin lock, fill one syringe with 5 mL of NS and flush the line
   after administration of the drug • For a continuous IV line, open an IV line with NS or D5W to maintain vascular patency. Flush
   the line immediately after administration of the drug Preparation

   • Do not use if the container has been damaged, protective seal and/or rubber cap have been
   entered, or the upper white layer is missing • Allow the product to come to room temperature by inverting the vial and gently rotating to
   resuspend the microspheres. Do not use if the suspension appears clear instead of opaque
   and milky-white • Vent the vial with a sterile vent spike or 18-gauge needle before withdrawing the suspension
   into the injection syringe. Do not inject air into the vial • Draw up an equal volume of NS and add into the injection syringe to form a 1:1 dilution • Mix gently by holding the syringe horizontally between the palms and rolling the syringe gen-
   tly back and forth for at least 10 seconds • If not used within 10 minutes after dilution, discard the diluted product', 'Common (0.5% or greater incidence): Headache, nausea and/or vomiting, warm sensation
   or flushing, dizziness, dysgeusia, chills or fever, flu-like symptoms, malaise/weakness/fatigue,
