   with tedizolid compared with 1% with a comparator treatment in a study of pediatric pa-
   tients 12 to less than 18 years with clinically documented acute bacterial skin and skin struc-
   ture infection. Among pediatric patients 4 months to less than 12 years, clinical success at test
   of cure was 3% with tedizolid compared with 3% with a comparator treatment FDA Pediatric Approved Indications
   Tedizolid phosphate injection is indicated in pediatric patients at least 26 weeks gestational
   age and weighing at least 1 kg, and tedizolid phosphate oral tablets are indicated for pediatric
   patients weighing at least 35 kg, for the treatment of acute bacterial skin and skin structure in-
   fections caused by susceptible isolates of the following gram-positive microorganisms 1 :

   • Staphylococcus aureus (including methicillin-resistant and susceptible isolates)
   • Streptococcus pyogenes
   • Streptococcus agalactiae
   • Streptococcus anginosus group (including S. anginosus, S. intermedius, and S. constellatus)
   • Enterococcus faecalis

   Administration
   IV

   • For IV use only; do not administer via intra-arterial, IM, intrathecal, intraperitoneal, or subQ
   routes • Infuse over 1 hour; do not administer via IV push or bolus • Do not mix or infuse simultaneously with other drugs or additives during administration • If the same IV line is used for sequential infusion of several different drugs, flush the line with
   NS before and after tedizolid administration • Total time from reconstitution of the vial until the end of administration is not to exceed 24
   hours at either room temperature or under refrigeration Oral

   • Administer with or without food • Missed Dose: Administer as soon as possible up to 8 hours before next scheduled dose; if
   less than 8 hours before next dose, wait until next scheduled dose 1

