   • In pediatric patients, inject close to the center of the gland using a sterile needle (eg, 27 to 30
   gauge [0.3 to 4 mm diameter], 5 mm length) Intramuscular

   • Use reconstituted solution for only 1 injection session and 1 patient only • If pen marks are used to mark injection sites, do not inject product into pen marks or a per-
   manent tattooing effect may occur • Use caution when injecting product near sites close to sensitive structures (ie, carotid artery,
   lung apices, and esophagus) • Administer using appropriate needle size (eg, 30-gauge [0.3 mm diameter], 25 mm length [su-
   perficial muscles] or 27-gauge [0.4 mm diameter], 37 mm length [deeper musculature]) 1', 'Common, 2 to 5 years old, chronic Sialorrhea: Nasopharyngitis (6%) 2
   Common, 6 to 17 years old, chronic Sialorrhea: Bronchitis (1%), headache (1%), and
   nausea/vomiting (1%) 2

   Common, 2 to 17 years old, upper limb spasticity: Nasopharyngitis (3% to 6%) and bronchi-
   tis (2% to 3%) 1

   Black Box Warning
   Distant Spread of Toxin Effect
   Postmarketing reports indicate that the effects of incobotulinumtoxinA and all botulinum toxin
   products may spread from the area of injection to produce symptoms consistent with bo-
   tulinum toxin effects. These may include asthenia, generalized muscle weakness, diplopia,

blurred vision, ptosis, dysphagia, dysphonia, dysarthria, urinary incontinence, and breathing
   difficulties. These symptoms have been reported hours to weeks after injection. Swallowing
   and breathing difficulties can be life threatening and there have been reports of death. The
   risk of symptoms is probably greatest in children treated for spasticity but symptoms can also
   occur in adults treated for spasticity and other conditions, particularly in those patients who
   have underlying conditions that would predispose them to these symptoms. In unapproved
   uses, including lower limb spasticity in children and in approved indications, cases of spread of
   effect have been reported at doses comparable to those used to treat cervical dystonia and at
   lower doses Monitoring
   Therapeutic Physical Monitoring

   • Median first onset of therapeutic effect appears within 7 days postinjection. Usual duration
   of effect is up to 12 to 16 weeks, but the effect may be shorter or longer in some patients', 'nerve terminals, entering the nerve terminals, and inhibiting the release of acetylcholine. The
inhibition occurs in the following steps: neurotoxin binding to cholinergic nerve terminals, in-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: IncobotulinumtoxinA, a neurotoxin formed from Clostridium bo-
   tulinum serotype A, blocks neuromuscular conduction by binding to receptor sites on motor
   nerve terminals, entering the nerve terminals, and inhibiting the release of acetylcholine. The
   inhibition occurs in the following steps: neurotoxin binding to cholinergic nerve terminals, in-
   ternalization of the neurotoxin into the nerve terminal, translocation of the light-chain part of
   the molecule into the cytosol of the nerve terminal, and cleavage of SNAP25, a presynaptic tar-
   get protein necessary for the release of acetylcholine. The formation of new nerve endings
   leads to restoration of impulse transmission Pharmacodynamics

   Pharmacodynamics onset, initial response: The median first onset of a clinical response oc-
   curs within 7 days post-injection Pharmacodynamics duration, single dose: Usual duration of effect is up to 12 to 16 weeks ,
   but the effect may be shorter or longer in some patients'),
