(211, 'Potassium Chloride', 'fluid_electrolyte', TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, 100, 'Hypokalemia

   Intravenous: 5 to 75 mEq/kg IV infused over 1 to 2 hours, then reassess 1 ,2 ,3 . Maximum
   40 mEq/dose.
   In diabetic ketoacidosis: consider use of potassium chloride 20 mEq/L in combination with
   potassium phosphate 20 mEq/L in maintenance fluids 4 ,5 ,6 ,1 .

   Oral: 3 to 8 mEq/kg/day orally 7 , 8 , 9 , 10 divided 1 to 5 times per day depending on tolerability
   and dose. Start at lower dose and adjust based on serum potassium concentrations. Adult
   doses 40 to 100 mEq', 'Treatment of hypokalemia 7 ,1 ,2 ,3 .

Administration
   Intravenous: Preferred concentration for peripheral infusion is 40 mEq/L; maximum concen-
   tration for peripheral infusion 60 to 80 mEq/L 16,9 ,10,3 .

   A maximum concentration of 200 mEq/L for central line infusion has been suggested 17 ,
   and concentrations up to 400 mEq/L have been used safely in critically ill adult patients Maximum infusion rate 1 mEq/kg/hour 16,1 or 40 mEq/hour Oral Solutions: Mix 15 mL of solution with at lea', 'Gastrointestinal (GI) irritation is common, most commonly diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal
   pain. Intestinal and gastric ulceration and bleeding have been reported with extended-release
   potassium formulations. Minimize the GI and bleeding effects by the use of liquid or efferves-
   cent preparations, administering with food, or reducing the amount given at one time. Use
   with caution (if at all) in patients receiving potassium-sparing diuretics (eg, spironolactone)
   11,15
           .', 'Oral: 1 to 3 mEq/kg/day orally 13,14 . Maximum 20 mEq/day Dose Adjustment', 'Pharmacology
   Potassium is the major intracellular cation. Hypokalemia in critically ill pediatric patients is usu-
   ally the result of decreased intake or increased renal (diuretics, hypomagnesemia, glucocorti-
   coids, aminoglycosides) or gastrointestinal (vomiting, diarrhea) losses of potassium. Alkalosis,
   as well as insulin infusions, will lower serum potassium concentrations by driving the ion intra-
   cellularly. Other causes include hyperaldosteronism and renal disorders. Symptom', 'Hypokalemia

   Intravenous: 5 to 75 mEq/kg IV infused over 1 to 2 hours, then reassess 1 ,2 ,3 . Maximum
   40 mEq/dose.
   In diabetic ketoacidosis: consider use of potassium chloride 20 mEq/L in combination with
   potassium phosphate 20 mEq/L in maintenance fluids 4 ,5 ,6 ,1 .

   Oral: 3 to 8 mEq/kg/day orally 7 , 8 , 9 , 10 divided 1 to 5 times per day depending on tolerability
   and dose. Start at lower dose and adjust based on serum potassium concentrations. Adult
   doses 40 to 100 mEq/day Daily Requirement
   Intravenous

   Infants/children: 2 to 4 mEq/kg/day IV of potassium Adolescents/children (greater than 50 kg): 1 to 2 mEq/kg/day IV of potassium Supplementation with Diuretics

   Oral: 1 to 3 mEq/kg/day orally 13,14 . Maximum 20 mEq/day Dose Adjustment

   Renal failure: Contraindicated Adjust dosage based on monitoring of serum potassium concentrations', 'Treatment of hypokalemia 7 ,1 ,2 ,3 .

Administration
   Intravenous: Preferred concentration for peripheral infusion is 40 mEq/L; maximum concen-
   tration for peripheral infusion 60 to 80 mEq/L 16,9 ,10,3 .

   A maximum concentration of 200 mEq/L for central line infusion has been suggested 17 ,
   and concentrations up to 400 mEq/L have been used safely in critically ill adult patients Maximum infusion rate 1 mEq/kg/hour 16,1 or 40 mEq/hour Oral Solutions: Mix 15 mL of solution with at least 6 fluid ounces of water or juice and admin-
   ister with a meal Dissolve 20 mEq or 25 mEq potassium chloride for oral solution in at least 4 or 5 ounces, re-
   spectively, of cold water or other beverage. Administer with or after a meal', 'Gastrointestinal (GI) irritation is common, most commonly diarrhea, vomiting, and abdominal
   pain. Intestinal and gastric ulceration and bleeding have been reported with extended-release
   potassium formulations. Minimize the GI and bleeding effects by the use of liquid or efferves-
   cent preparations, administering with food, or reducing the amount given at one time. Use
   with caution (if at all) in patients receiving potassium-sparing diuretics (eg, spironolactone)
   11,15
           .

   Solution Compatibility
   Most standard IV solutions.

Terminal Injection Site Compatibility
   Most drugs.

   Terminal Injection Site Incompatibility
   Amphotericin B, diazepam, and phenytoin.

   Monitoring
   Continuous electrocardiogram monitoring is mandatory when administering highly con-
   centrated IV solutions or when using higher doses and administration rates (greater
   than 3 mEq/kg/hour). Observe IV site closely for signs of extravasation, especially when us-
   ing concentrated solutions or infusing through peripheral line. Monitor serum potassium con-
   centrations and acid-base status frequently 16,15,1 ,2 . Assess for gastrointestinal intolerance.', 'Oral: 1 to 3 mEq/kg/day orally 13,14 . Maximum 20 mEq/day Dose Adjustment', 'Pharmacology
   Potassium is the major intracellular cation. Hypokalemia in critically ill pediatric patients is usu-
   ally the result of decreased intake or increased renal (diuretics, hypomagnesemia, glucocorti-
   coids, aminoglycosides) or gastrointestinal (vomiting, diarrhea) losses of potassium. Alkalosis,
   as well as insulin infusions, will lower serum potassium concentrations by driving the ion intra-
   cellularly. Other causes include hyperaldosteronism and renal disorders. Symptoms of hy-
   pokalemia include neuromuscular weakness and paralysis, ileus, urine retention, respiratory
   depression, and EKG changes (ST segment depression, low-voltage T wave, and appearance of
   U wave). Hypokalemia increases digitalis toxicity 20,15,21,1 . Oral potassium preparations are
   completely absorbed'),
