   Guidelines: In preschool-aged children (4 to 5 years of age), recommended first-line treatment
   for ADHD is evidence-based behavioral therapy; may consider methylphenidate if no signifi-
   cant improvement with behavioral interventions occur and moderate-to-severe disturbance in
   the child''s functioning continue. For school-aged children (6 to 11 years of age), recommended
   treatment includes an FDA-approved medication and evidence-based behavioral therapy. The
   evidence is strongest for use of stimulant medications, followed by atomoxetine, extended-re-
   lease guanfacine, and then extended-release clonidine. For adolescents (12 to 18 years of age),
   recommended first-line treatment is an FDA-approved ADHD medication and evidenced-based
   behavioral therapy FDA Approved Pediatric Indication
   Serdexmethylphenidate/dexmethylphenidate is indicated for the treatment of attention deficit
   hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) in patients 6 years of age and older Limitations of Use
   Not recommended in pediatric patients younger than 6 years due to greater plasma exposure
   and higher incidence of adverse reactions (eg, weight loss) compared with patients 6 years and
   older at the same dosage Administration
   • Administer in the morning with or without food • Capsules may be taken whole, or opened and the entire contents sprinkled into 50 mL of wa-
