   Velaglucerase alfa is an enzyme created by gene activation technology in human fibroblast
   cells. Velaglucerase alfa catalyzes hydrolysis of glucocerebroside normally accomplished by the
   enzyme beta-glucocerebrosidase. In patients deficient in lysosomal enzyme beta-glucocere-
   brosidase (Gaucher disease), velaglucerase alfa will catalyze conversion of sphingolipid gluco-
   cerebroside into glucose and ceramide, thereby reducing the accumulation of glucocerebro-
   side in the lysosomal compartment of macrophages (Gaucher cells) and subsequent accumu-
   lation of Gaucher cells in the liver, spleen, bone marrow and other organs. Accumulation of
   Gaucher cells in the liver and spleen causes organomegaly. In a multicenter study that involved
   7 pediatric patients (4 to 17 years of age) with type 1 Gaucher disease who received a 60-
   minute infusion of velaglucerase alfa 60 units/kg every other week, the mean steady state Vd
   (evaluated at weeks 1 and 37) was 82 to 108 mL/kg (8.2% to 8% of body weight), mean total
   body clearance (evaluated at weeks 1 and 37) was 72 to 56 mL/min/kg, and serum concen-
   trations (evaluated at weeks 1 and 37) declined rapidly with a mean half-life of 11 to 12 min-
   utes. No accumulation or change was observed in pharmacokinetics over time from weeks 1 to
   37 with multiple-dosing 60 units/kg every other week'),
