Codeine metabolism is subject to genetic polymorphism of the CYP2D6 enzyme. Patients with', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action
   The exact mechanism for the analgesic action of codeine is unknown, but is thought to come
   from the conversion of codeine to morphine. The precise mechanism of the analgesic proper-
   ties of acetaminophen is not well established, but is thought to involve central actions Acetaminophen and codeine phosphate is a combination of a peripherally acting analgesic, ac-
   etaminophen, and a centrally acting analgesic, codeine. Acetaminophen is a non-opiate an-
   tipyretic and a non-salicylate analgesic. Codeine phosphate is a narcotic analgesic.
   Acetaminophen: Rapidly absorbed from gastrointestinal (GI) tract and distributed throughout
   most body tissues. Plasma half-life: 25 to 3 hours. Principally metabolized in liver (conjuga-
   tion), with renal excretion of metabolites. Following oral dose, approximately 85% appears in
   urine within 24 hours, most as glucuronide conjugate. Codeine: Readily absorbed from GI
   tract. Not bound to plasma proteins and does not accumulate in body tissues. Peak effect
   within 2 hours, persists for 4 to 6 hours. Converted to active metabolite, morphine, by CYP2D6
   isoenzymes. Plasma half-life: about 9 hours. Primarily renal elimination; approximately 90%
   of oral dose excreted within 24 hours. Remainder excreted in feces 20,21 .

   Codeine metabolism is subject to genetic polymorphism of the CYP2D6 enzyme. Patients with
   the ultra-rapid metabolizer phenotype (UM) rapidly produce higher levels of morphine than
   other phenotypes. The prevalence of the UM phenotype varies widely and has been estimated
   a 5 to 1% in Chinese, Japanese, and Hispanics, 1 to 10% in Caucasians, 3% in African
   Americans, and 16% to 28% in North Africans, Ethiopians, and Arabs 20 , 21 ,'),
