   Aminocaproic acid inhibits fibrinolysis through plasminogen activator inhibition (principal) and
   antiplasmin activity (lesser extent). Apparent Vd (mean +/- SD), 1 +/- 6 L (oral) and 30 +/- 2 L (IV). Distributes throughout extravascular and intravascular compartments, penetrating
   RBCs and other tissues with prolonged administration. Primarily excreted renally as un-
   changed drug (65%) and the metabolite adipic acid (11%). Renal clearance, 116 mL/min. Total
   body clearance, 169 mL/min. Terminal elimination half-life, approximately 2 hours 25,10 .

   Pediatric-Specific Pharmacokinetics

   Based upon a population pharmacokinetic model, a steady-state plasma concentration of 130
   mcg/mL was predicted with use of a 100 mg/kg loading dose followed by 40 mg/kg/hr. Plasma
   samples from 18 infants with a median age of 39 weeks (27 to 107 weeks) and weight 8 kg
   (6.7 to 8 kg) who underwent craniofacial reconstruction surgery were utilized to develop the
   model. Plasma drug clearance was 6 mL/kg/min and central Vd was 14 L/kg (peripheral Vd
   was 29 L/kg) . Aminocaproic acid concentrations of 130 mcg/mL inhibit fibrinolysis in adults
   (in vitro) 28 ; however, in neonates (in vitro) the minimal effective concentration was 2
   mcg/mL Based on a population pharmacokinetic model, a bolus dose of 75 mg/kg over 10 minutes fol-
   lowed by 75 mg/kg/hr and an additional 75 mg/kg placed in the pump (venous reservoir)
   would produce an aminocaproic acid plasma concentration of 260 mcg/mL in pediatric pa-
   tients undergoing surgery on cardiopulmonary bypass. Plasma samples from 8 pediatric pa-
   tients (9 months to 4 years of age) with congenital heart defects undergoing CPB were utilized
   to develop the model. A concentration of 260 mg/L, 2 times the concentration needed to in-
   hibit fibrinolysis (adult in vitro data), was the target to minimize the potential for any patient to
   have ineffective concentrations. Pharmacokinetic variables pre-CBP, during CPB, and post-CBP
   were: Vd 23 L/kg, 36 L/kg, and 43 L/kg; Cl 4 mL/kg/min, 1 mL/kg/min, and 2
   mL/kg/min; and terminal half-life 100 minutes, 213 minutes, and 100 minutes, respectively 6/10'),
