   Monitoring
   When used by nebulization, monitor for bronchospasm, changes in pulmonary function, and
   oxygen saturation 5 , When used by the IV route, periodically monitor for changes in fluid balance, electrolyte con-
   centrations, and acid-base balance, especially during prolonged parenteral therapy and when
   clinically warranted 15,31 .
   When used for elevated intracranial pressure (ICP) associated with traumatic brain injury, sug-
   gested targets for treatment (any modality) are to maintain ICP less than 20 mmHg and cere-
   bral perfusion pressure greater than 40 mmHg', 'When used by nebulization, monitor for bronchospasm, changes in pulmonary function, and
oxygen saturation 5 , When used by the IV route, periodically monitor for changes in fluid balance, electrolyte con-
Hypertonic 3% and 5% sodium chloride solution induces diuresis through supplementation of
molarity and acid-base balance. Inhibition of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion occurs', 'Pharmacology

Hypertonic 3% and 5% sodium chloride solution induces diuresis through supplementation of
   water and electrolytes Sodium is the principle cation of extracellular fluid. Sodium content
   normally determines the volume of extracellular fluid, and is important in the regulation of os-
   molarity and acid-base balance. Inhibition of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) secretion occurs
   when serum sodium concentration is low, which prevents water reabsorption by the distal re-
   nal tubules When used by inhalation, hypertonic saline improves mucociliary clearance by
   reducing the viscosity and elasticity of mucus secretions, increasing sputum production, and
   inducing cough. In addition, it reduces edema in the airways 5 ,'),
