Insulin stimulates the uptake of glucose by skeletal tissue and fat, and inhibits hepatic glucose
production. Insulin also enhances protein synthesis and inhibits lipolysis and proteolysis Cmax: 83 microunits/mL', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Insulin glulisine is a rapid-acting insulin analog produced by recombinant DNA technology. Like
   other insulins and insulin analogs, the primary activity is regulation of glucose metabolism.
   Insulin stimulates the uptake of glucose by skeletal tissue and fat, and inhibits hepatic glucose
   production. Insulin also enhances protein synthesis and inhibits lipolysis and proteolysis Cmax: 83 microunits/mL
   Following subQ administration of insulin gluli', 'Diabetes Mellitus Type 1
   4 years or older

   IV Infusion: Closely monitor blood glucose and serum potassium SubQ Intermittent: Administer within 15 minutes before a meal or within 20 minutes after
   starting a meal. Use in a regimen with an intermediate or long-acting insulin. Individualize per
   patient needs. Total daily insulin requirement is usually between 5 and 1 unit/kg/day Continuous SubQ Infusion Pump: Individualize per patient needs. Initial dosage based on the
   total daily insulin dose of the previous regimen. Total daily insulin requirement is usually be-
   tween 5 and 1 unit/kg/day Transition from IV to subQ:

   Table 1: Transitioning from IV to subQ insulin

      Begin subQ insulin once ketoacidosis has resolved and oral intake is tolerated.

      Long-acting insulin should be administered in addition to rapid- or short-acting insulin.

      The most convenient time to change to subQ insulin is just before a mealtime.

      Administer the first subQ injection 15 to 30 minutes (for rapid-acting insulin) before stop-
      ping the insulin infusion to allow sufficient absorption. With long-acting insulin, the over-
      lap should be longer and the rate of IV administration decreased gradually (eg, administer
      basal dose in evening, then stop infusion in morning)

      The regimen, dose and type of subQ insulin should be according to local preference or
      circumstances.

After transitioning to subQ insulin, frequent blood glucose monitoring is required to avoid
        marked hyperglycemia and hypoglycemia.

        [2]', 'Type 1 diabetes mellitus: Intensive insulin therapy, either by multiple subQ injections of
   prandial and basal insulin or continuous subQ infusion, is the mainstay of treatment. Prandial
   insulin daily requirements are approximately 55% to 70% of the total daily dose, while basal in-
   sulin accounts for 30% to 45% of the total daily dose. Prepubertal children typically require 7
   to 1 units/kg/day and during puberty, the insulin requirements may rise to 1 to 2 units/kg/day.
   Rapid acting insulins such as insulin glulisine should ideally be given 10 to 15 minutes before
   meals or immediately before meals to help reduce postprandial hyperglycemia and nocturnal
   hypoglycemia 3 , Type 2 diabetes mellitus: Insulin therapy should be initiated in pediatric patients presenting
   with ketosis/ketoacidosis and/or the patients HbA1c is greater than 5%. After initial treat-
   ment, insulin may be weaned off over 2 to 6 weeks by decreasing the insulin dose 30% to 50%
   with each increase in the metformin dose. If glycemic goals are not met after initial treatment
   and the addition of a glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) agonist or empagliflozin, consider adding
   an intermediate/long-acting insulin. The starting dose of basal insulin is typically 25 to 5
   units/kg and the dose is titrated based on results of self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG)
   3 ,4
          .

   Pediatric FDA Approved Indication:
   Insulin glulisine is indicated to improve glycemic control in pediatric patients with diabetes
   mellitus 5

   Administration
   IV route 1
    ●   Dilute with normal saline only.
    ●   Concentration of 05 to 1 unit/mL using polyvinyl chloride infusion bags.
    ●   Do not administer insulin mixtures intravenously.

SubQ route 1
    ●   If mixing with NPH human insulin in the same syringe, draw insulin glulisine first then NPH
        insulin and use immediately after mixing.
    ●   Insulin glulisine should be administered by subQ injection into the abdominal wall, thigh, or
        deltoid muscle.
    ●   Rotate injection site between each injection.

   Continuous SubQ infusion pump: 1
    ●   Do not mix or dilute with any other insulin when used in an insulin infusion pump.
    ●   Should be administered into the abdominal wall.
    ●   Rotate infusion sited within the same region.
    ●   Insulin glulisine in the reservoir and the infusion sets should be changed at least every 48
        hours.
    ●   Should not be exposed to temperatures greater than 6°F (37°C).
   .', 'The most commonly reported adverse effects in pediatric and adolescent patients were na-
   sopharyngitis (9%), upper respiratory tract infection (8.3%), headache (6.9%), and hypoglycemic
   seizure (6.1%) Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   Type 1 Diabetes Mellitus

   •Routinely assess glycemic control to determine treatment effectiveness. Monitor HbA1c at
   least twice yearly in a patient who is well-controlled, every 3 months if not meeting treatment
   goals or when there is a change of therapy, or more frequently as clinically warranted. An
   HbA1c goal of less than 7% is appropriate for many children and adolescents, but HbA1c goals
   may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to each patient 4/7
•Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) multiple times a day (6 to 10 times a day via
   meter or continuous glucose monitoring [CGM]), including prior to meals, snacks, at bedtime,
   and as needed to assist in meeting goals of therapy. Real-time or intermittently scanned CGM
   should be offered as soon as possible to children on multiple daily injections or insulin pump
   therapy Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus

   •Monitor HbA1c every 3 months if possible. A reasonable HbA1c goal for most patients is less
   than 5%, but HbA1c goals may be more or less stringent and should be individualized to
   each patient 6 ,4

   •Perform self-monitoring of blood glucose (SMBG) as needed to assist in meeting goals of ther-
   apy; SMBG should be individualized to each patient 6

   Toxic Monitoring

   •Monitor for blood glucose and potassium in patients receiving IV administration •Closely monitor blood glucose, especially in renally or hepatically impaired patients. Monitor
   blood glucose closely during times of stress, illness, or changes in coadministered medication
   or meal patterns •Observe patient for signs or symptoms of heart failure when receiving concomitant peroxi-
   some proliferator activated receptor-gamma agonists', '•Monitor for blood glucose and potassium in patients receiving IV administration •Closely monitor blood glucose, especially in renally or hepatically impaired patients. Monitor
Insulin stimulates the uptake of glucose by skeletal tissue and fat, and inhibits hepatic glucose
production. Insulin also enhances protein synthesis and inhibits lipolysis and proteolysis Cmax: 83 microunits/mL', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Insulin glulisine is a rapid-acting insulin analog produced by recombinant DNA technology. Like
   other insulins and insulin analogs, the primary activity is regulation of glucose metabolism.
   Insulin stimulates the uptake of glucose by skeletal tissue and fat, and inhibits hepatic glucose
   production. Insulin also enhances protein synthesis and inhibits lipolysis and proteolysis Cmax: 83 microunits/mL
   Following subQ administration of insulin glulisine 15 units/kg, the peak concentration was 83
   microunits/mL (range, 40 to 131 microunits/mL) compared with 50 microunits/mL (range, 35 to
   71 microunits/mL) with regular human insulin Tmax: 60 minutes

Following subQ administration of insulin glulisine 15 units/kg, the median time to maximum
   concentration was 60 minutes (range, 40 to 120 minutes) compared with 120 minutes (range,
   60 to 239 minutes) for regular human insulin Half-life

   IV route: 13 minutes compared with 17 minutes for regular human insulin SubQ route: 42 minutes compared with 86 minutes for regular human insulin Pediatric: There were no relative difference in pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamic be-
   tween insulin glulisine and regular human insulin in pediatric patients (7 to 16 years of age,
   n=20) and healthy adult subjects and adults with type 1 diabetes'),
