   Rinse mouth with water without swallowing after each dose •Use a spacer or a valve holding chamber in younger patients or patients with poor inhaler
   technique', 'Most common adverse events (3% or greater) are nasopharyngitis, sinusitis, and headache 5/10
Black Box Warning
   Long-acting beta(2)-adrenergic agonists (LABA), such as formoterol, one of the active ingredi-
   ents in mometasone furoate/formoterol fumarate, increase the risk of asthma-related death.
   Data from a large placebo-controlled United States study that compared the safety of another
   long-acting beta(2)-adrenergic agonist (salmeterol) or placebo added to usual asthma therapy
   (12 years and older; mean age 39 years) 9 showed an increase in asthma-related deaths in pa-
   tients receiving salmeterol. This finding with salmeterol is considered a class effect of the
   LABA, including formoterol. Currently available data are inadequate to determine whether
   concurrent use of inhaled corticosteroids or other long-term asthma control drugs mitigates
   the increased risk of asthma-related death from LABA. Available data from controlled clinical
   trials suggest that LABA increase the risk of asthma-related hospitalization in pediatric and
   adolescent patients. Therefore, when treating patients with asthma, mometasone
   furoate/formoterol fumarate should only be used for patients not adequately controlled on a
   long-term asthma control medication, such as an inhaled corticosteroid or whose disease
   severity clearly warrants initiation of treatment with both an inhaled corticosteroid and LABA.
   Once asthma control is achieved and maintained, assess the patient at regular intervals and
   step down therapy (e.g., discontinue mometasone furoate/formoterol fumarate) if possible
   without loss of asthma control, and maintain the patient on a long-term asthma control medi-
   cation, such as an inhaled corticosteroid. Do not use mometasone furoate/formoterol fu-
   marate for patients whose asthma is adequately controlled on low or medium dose inhaled
   corticosteroids In a meta-analysis (110 studies; n=60,954 patients 4 years through greater than 65 years of
   age), there was an overall higher risk for the composite event (asthma-related deaths, intuba-
   tions, and hospitalizations) associated with LABA use compared with no LABA use (overall inci-
   dence difference of 3 events per 1000 patient-years; 95% CI, 2 to 3) for all age groups.
   The highest risk was seen in children 4 to 11 years of age (30.4 events per 1000 patient-years;
   95% CI, 7 to 1; p=0.02). This result was consistent regardless of inhaled corticosteroid use;
   however, data indicated that providing ICS and LABA as a single inhaler may decrease the risk.
   There were 9807 pediatric patients included in the meta-analysis, and the pediatric composite
   consisted primarily of asthma-related hospitalizations. Asthma-related death/intubations were
   sparse in the 4- to 17-year age groups (no LABA = 4 and LABA = 2) Monitoring
   Assess patient periodically for adequate asthma control and step down therapy if possible 1 ,4 .
   Monitor pulmonary function tests including prebronchodilator FEV1/FVC and pre- and post-
   bronchodilator FEV. Monitor severity and frequency of asthma exacerbations 1 Monitor

growth status (eg, stadiometry) periodically in children receiving long-term corticosteroid treat-
   ment. Assess bone mineral density initially and periodically during therapy in high risk patients', '', 'Kidney

