   Renal impairment: Dosage adjustments are unnecessary in patients with renal failure.
   Reduction of unbound clearance of valproate is 27% in patients with a CrCl of less than 10
   mL/minute 13,14 ; however, hemodialysis typically decreases concentrations by about 20%.
   Monitoring total concentration may be misleading in patients with renal failure because pro-
   tein binding is significantly reduced Hepatic impairment: Do not administer valproic acid or divalproex sodium to patients with
   hepatic disease or significant hepatic insufficiency. Liver disease impairs the capacity to elimi-
   nate valproate. In one study, the clearance of free valproate was decreased by 50% in patients
   with cirrhosis and by 16% in patients with acute hepatitis. Patients with liver disease have de-
   creased albumin concentrations and larger unbound fractions by a 2- to 6-fold increase.
   Monitoring of total concentrations may be misleading since free concentrations may be signifi-
   cantly elevated in patients with hepatic disease, with total concentrations appearing to be nor-
   mal 13,14,15 .
   Hemodialysis: Dosage supplementation is not required in patients following hemodialysis,
   peritoneal dialysis, or continuous arteriovenous hemofiltration Hemodialysis typically re-
   duces concentrations by about 20% 13 , 14 , 15 , but a 27% reduction in the unbound clearance of

valproate has been reported in patients with a CrCl of less than 10 mL/minute. Monitoring to-
   tal concentration may be misleading in patients with renal failure because protein binding is
