   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Laboratory Monitoring
   Therapeutic Drug Monitoring

   • Anticonvulsant or mood stabilizer: 50 to 100 mcg/mL (346 to 693 mcmol/L) 43
   • In a consensus guideline, the use therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) with valproic acid is
   rated as 1, strongly recommended for dose titration and for special indications. The highest

probability of response or remission can be expected at drug concentrations within the re-
   ported therapeutic reference range • The therapeutic reference range for valproic acid when used as a mood stabilizer or anticon-
   vulsant is 50 to 100 mcg/mL, with a laboratory alert level of 120 mcg/mL; however, 120
   mcg/mL has been clinically tolerated in acute mania. Reported therapeutic reference ranges
   are established. Controlled clinical trials have shown beneficial effects of TDM. Reports on de-
   creased tolerability or intoxications exist • Monitor valproic acid plasma concentrations periodically to assist in achieving satisfactory
   clinical response, especially during the early course of therapy and with the addition or discon-
   tinuation of concomitant antiepileptic drugs Samples for valproic acid levels should be
   drawn before the morning dose due to its short half-life to insure proper interpretation of con-
   centration 44

   Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Measurements of 25-hydroxy-vitamin D levels should be assessed regularly in children to de-
   tect early osteopenia • Perform liver function tests prior to the initiation of therapy and at frequent intervals there-
   after, especially during the first 6 months of therapy, in children under 2 years of age, those re-
   ceiving multiple anticonvulsant agents, or comorbid congenital metabolic disorders, severe
   seizure disorder with mental retardation, hereditary mitochondrial disease, and organic brain
   disease • Screen patients for mutations of the mitochondrial DNA Polymerase Gamma (POLG) gene
   prior to therapy initiation in accordance with clinical practice • Measure complete blood counts and perform coagulation tests before and during therapy at
   periodic intervals, especially in pregnant patients and prior to planned surgeries • Monitor plasma ammonia levels 15 , even if free valproic acid level is within the safety range • Measure unbound valproic acid levels in patients with hypoalbuminemia, rather than total
   valproic acid to more accurately predict neurologic adverse events. When laboratory measure-
   ment of unbound concentration is not available, derived formulas to estimate the unbound
   concentration may underestimate the normalized total concentration. Accuracy of derived for-
   mulas is variable, and does not consider other situations that may affect valproic acid-albumin
   binding such as drug interactions, hypertriglyceridemia, hyperbilirubinemia, and renal or he-
   patic dysfunction. Derived formulas may not apply to ICU patients. The optimal therapeutic
   range of unbound valproic acid concentration is not determined 13/25
