   with late infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis type 2 (also known as tripeptidyl peptidase 1
   deficiency) Administration
   Preparation
   Thaw cerliponase alfa and intraventricular electrolytes vials at room temperature for 60 min-
   utes; do not thaw or warm any other way and do not re-freeze. Do not shake vials. Cerliponase
   alfa vials may contain naturally occurring thin translucent fibers or opaque particles 2

   Unopened thawed vials may be stored in the refrigerator (2 to 8 degrees C) for 24 hours.
   Labeled syringes containing product may be stored in the refrigerator (2 to 8 degrees C) for up
   to 4 hours Administration 1
    ●   Cerliponase alfa
    ●   Prior to each infusion, inspect the scalp for signs of intraventricular access device leakage,
        failure or potential infection
    ●   Administer via surgically implanted intraventricular access device (Codman(R) Holter
        Rickham Reservoir and Codman(R) ventricular catheter); initiate first infusion no sooner
        than 5 to 7 days after implantation
    ●   Check patency of intraventricular access device prior to each dose by withdrawing 5 to 1
        mL of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF); send fluid for culture; do not return CSF to access device
    ●   Use an infusion set with a 2 micron inline filter
    ●   Withdraw 10 mL of cerliponase alfa into the provided 20-mL sterile syringe; do not dilute
        and do not mix with any other drug
    ●   Administer cerliponase alfa via B Braun Perfusor(R) Space Infusion Pump System.
    ●   Infuse at a rate of 5 mL/hour; set occlusion alarm to a pressure of 281 mmHg or less.
    ●   Do not administer as a bolus; do not administer manually.
    ●   Intraventricular electrolytes
    ●   Withdraw 2 mL of intraventricular electrolytes into provided 20-mL sterile syringe; discard
        any unused portion.
    ●   Administer intraventricular electrolytes via infusion pump (B Braun Perfusor(R) Space
        Infusion Pump System)

