   Renal and Hepatic Dose Adjustments

   Change in pediatric dose should not be made based solely on weight measurement during a single
   visit as weight may fluctuate There are no specific dose adjustments for pediatric patients. However for laboratory abnormalities
   the following recommendations for adults with rheumatoid arthritis or giant cell arteritis may be
   followed:
   Renal impairment: No dose adjustment is required in patients with mild to moderate renal
   impairment; no studies have been done in patients with moderate to severe renal impairment Hepatic impairment or active hepatic disease: Use not recommended 2
   Liver enzyme abnormalities
   • Persistent elevations of greater than 1 to 3x ULN: Consider dosage modification or inter-
   ruption until enzymes levels have normalized 2

• Elevations of greater than 3 up to 5x ULN: Hold dosing until levels have fallen to less than
   3x ULN, then follow the recommendations for levels of 1 to 3 times the ULN

   • Persistent increases greater than 3x the ULN or for any elevation greater than 5x the
   ULN: Discontinue therapy • If appropriate, dose modify or stop concomitant methotrexate and/or other medications and
   hold tocilizumab-aazg dosing until the clinical situation has been evaluated. In PJIA and SJIA the
   decision to discontinue therapy for a laboratory abnormality should be based upon the medi-
   cal assessment of the individual patient Other Dose Adjustments

   Serious infection: Hold tocilizumab-aazg until the infection is controlled Hematologic toxicity

