   In infants and toddlers with congenital heart disease, a heart rate of less than 80 bpm oc-
   curred in 5% of dexmedeTOMIDine 5 mcg/kg intranasal group compared with 8% of pen-
   tobarbital 5 mg/kg oral group (RR, 1; 95% CI, 3 to 5); heart rates less than 60 bpm were
   not observed in either group. Hypotension (lowest systolic blood pressure of less than 70
   mmHg) occurred in 19% of patients in the dexmedeTOMIDine group and 9% of patients in the
   pentobarbital group. Median ages (interquartile range) for dexmedeTOMIDine were 8 months
   (6 to 25 months) in the 3 to 12 month group and 17 months (15 to 20 months) in the 13 to
   24 month group, and 9 months (7.5 to 11 months) in the 3 to 12 month group and 17 months
   (14 to 20 months) in the 13 to 24 month group for pentobarbital The incidence of bradycardia was 3% with dexmedeTOMIDine as the only anesthetic agent in a
   meta-analysis (n=2835 patients; mean, 21 months of age). The mean decrease in heart rate
   from baseline was 26 beats/min Transient hypertension following loading doses has
   been reported Seventeen intubated hemodynamically stable patients (0.15 to 1 years of
   age, median age, 6 years) in the intensive care unit were administered dexmedeTOMIDine 7 mcg/kg/hr for 6 to 24 hours. A sustained (more than 2 hours) decrease in heart rate of
   greater than 20% was seen in 35% of patients, and a sustained increase in blood pressure
   (greater than 20%) was seen in 29% of patients while a sustained greater than 20% decrease in
   systolic blood pressure was seen in 12% of patients. No arrhythmias were detected while on
   continuous electrocardiographic monitoring Following intranasal administration of
   dexmedeTOMIDine, maximum mean reductions in systolic blood pressure were 9% and 7% and maximum mean reductions in heart rate were 1% and 1% for doses of 1
   mcg/kg and 2 mcg/kg, respectively 7/17
QT Interval Effects: During sevoflurane anesthesia, dexmedeTOMIDine 3 mcg/kg IV short-
   ened the QTc interval (-7.8 milliseconds) 2 minutes after administration compared with contin-
   ued QTc prolongation (+10.2 milliseconds) in the control group in a prospective, observational
   case-control study of 50 children 1 to 16 years of age (mean 9 years). A significant transient
   decrease in heart rate was observed with dexmedeTOMIDine, but no intervention was re-
   quired. None of the children had pre-existing cardiac conduction disorders, pre-existing ar-
   rhythmias, or pre-existing prolonged QT syndrome Neurological Effects: Transient neurologic abnormalities (eg, agitation, pupil change, abnor-
   mal movements) has been reported Respiratory Effects: Decreased oxygen saturation has been reported In infants and tod-
   dlers with congenital heart disease, desaturation occurred in 5% of dexmedeTOMIDine 5
   mcg/kg intranasal group compared with 7% of pentobarbital 5 mg/kg oral group. Median ages
   (interquartile range) for dexmedeTOMIDine were 8 months (6 to 25 months) in the 3 to 12
   month group and 17 months (15 to 20 months) in the 13 to 24 month group, and 9 months
   (7.5 to 11 months) in the 3 to 12 month group and 17 months (14 to 20 months) in the 13 to 24
   month group for pentobarbital Withdrawal symptoms of rebound hypertension (12% vs 2%) and rebound tachycardia (8% vs
   0%) were reported more frequently in the dexmedeTOMIDine group compared with conven-
   tional sedative agents in critically ill children with heart disease receiving continuous infusion
   for prolonged periods (96 hours or more; median duration 196 hours), in a retrospective re-
   view (n=52) 47

