   Monitoring
   • Assess level of sedation. Continuously monitor blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate,
   and oxygen saturation 30,49 . Frequently check head position to assure airway patency • Assessment of the level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients is recom-
   mended, using the Comfort-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation
   Scale 29

   • All pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation should be assigned a target depth of
   sedation and evaluated at least once daily with a validated sedation assessment tool 29', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: dexmedeTOMIDine, the S-enantiomer of medetomidine, is a relatively
   selective alpha2 adrenergic agonist with sedative effects Concentrations: In 20 infants (postnatal age, 4 to 203 days; postmenstrual age, 33 to 61
   weeks) administered dexmedeTOMIDine IV 1 to 5 mcg/kg/hr (some received boluses of 1
   to 3 mcg/kg), the median concentration was 68 ng/mL (0.07 to 44 ng/mL). Adverse effects
   were not associated with concentrations Distribution: Protein binding approximately 94%; lower values with hepatic impairment In
   20 infants (postnatal age, 4 to 203 days; postmenstrual age, 33 to 61 weeks), the median Vd

was 5 L/kg In pediatric patients, average volume of distribution (Vd) was 1 L/kg 55
   Metabolism: Metabolized almost entirely by the liver, with minimal unchanged drug found in
   urine and feces. Glucuronidation and CYP2A6-mediated metabolism are the primary pathways
   of elimination 30

   Clearance: In 20 infants (postnatal age, 4 to 203 days; postmenstrual age, 33 to 61 weeks), the
   median Cl was 51 L/kg/hr In pediatric patients, clearance (Cl) average was 8 L/kg/hr 55 ;
   infants greater than 12 months of age and young children have higher Cl rates Half-life: Mean terminal half-life is approximately 2 hours Intranasal Administration
   Time of onset and Duration: In children (n=100; age 1 to 12 years), the median time for onset
