   tion with centrally acting anticholinergic agents when oral therapy is not possible or con-
   traindicated; as an antihistamine for the amelioration of allergic reactions to blood or plasma;
   as an adjunct to EPINEPHrine for the treatment of anaphylactic reactions after the acute reac-
   tion has been controlled; and in other uncomplicated allergic conditions of the immediate type
   when oral therapy is not possible or contraindicated Oral

   DiphenhydrAMINE for oral use is indicated in children for active and prophylactic treatment of
   motion sickness; in mild cases of parkinsonism (including drug-induced) and other cases of
   parkinsonism (including drug-induced) in combination with centrally acting anticholinergic
   agents; as an antihistamine for the improvement of allergic reactions to blood or plasma, der-
   matographism, allergic conjunctivitis due to food, and other uncomplicated allergic conditions;
   as an adjunct to epinephrine (or other standard measures) for the treatment of anaphylactic

reactions after the acute reactions have been controlled, and for the treatment of insomnia in
   children 12 years or older 2 ,10 . Over-the-counter diphenhydrAMINE oral formulations are com-
   monly used for allergic rhinitis and for the relief of symptoms due to the common cold 6 ,3 ,4 ,5 .

   Topical
   Topical diphenhydrAMINE is indicated for the temporary relief of itching and pain associated
   with insect bites, minor burns, sunburn, minor cutes and scrapes, minor skin irritations, and
   rashes due to poison ivy or poison sumac 11 , 12 , 13 , 14 , 15 , Administration
   IM

   • Administer undiluted (50 mg/mL) by deep IM injection • Local necrosis has been associated with SUBQ or intradermal use of the injectable formula-
   tion IV

   • Administer IV push over 5 minutes or infuse over 15 minutes undiluted at a concentration of
   50 mg/mL, or dilute in D5W or NS if necessary; give at maximum rate not to exceed 25 mg/min
   7 , Oral

   • Do not break the capsules or softgels .

   Topical

