   Guidelines: Intensive insulin therapy, either by multiple subQ injections of prandial and basal
   insulin or continuous subQ infusion, is the mainstay of treatment. Prandial insulin daily re-
   quirements are approximately 55% to 70% of the total daily dose, while basal insulin accounts
   for 30% to 45% of the total daily dose. Prepubertal children typically require 7 to 1
   units/kg/day and during puberty, the insulin requirements may rise to 1 to 2 units/kg/day 7 ,2 .

   In a 52-week, open-label, randomized controlled trial in pediatric type 1 diabetic patients aged
   2 to 16 years, those receiving insulin detemir once or twice daily (n=177; baseline HbA1c 4%)
   had similar increases in HbA1c compared with those receiving twice-daily NPH insulin (n=170;
   baseline HbA1c 4%). When stratified by age (2 to 5 years (n=80) and 6 to 16 years (n=258)),
   similar results were seen . All patients received bolus insulin aspart before each meal. HbA1c

increases of 3% and 2% from baseline were observed in the insulin detemir and NPH in-
   sulin groups, respectively (mean treatment difference, 1% (95% CI, -0.1% to 4%)). Fasting
   plasma glucose levels were reduced by a mean of 10 mg/dL in patients receiving insulin de-
   temir (baseline mean fasting glucose level, 135 mg/dL) while no mean change was seen in pa-
   tients receiving NPH insulin (baseline mean fasting glucose level, 141 mg/dL). The total daily in-
   sulin dose was 35 units and 34 units for insulin detemir and NPH insulin, respectively In a non-blinded, randomized, controlled clinical study, pediatric patients (6 to 17 years old)
   with type 1 diabetes were treated for 26 weeks with a basal-bolus insulin regimen. Insulin de-
   temir provided a decrease in hemoglobin A1c (HbA1c) similar to neutral protamine insulin
   (NPH) and had a greater reduction in fasting plasma glucose in pediatric patients. Insulin de-
   temir (n=232) and NPH insulin (n=115) were administered once or twice daily (bedtime only or
   morning and bedtime) according to each patient''s pretrial dose regimen. All patients received
   bolus insulin aspart before each meal. The insulin detemir treated patients had a decrease in
   HbA1c similar to those patients treated with NPH human insulin (mean change from baseline
   -0.7% vs -0.8%, respectively, (95% CI -0.1% to 3% for treatment difference)). Patients in the in-
   sulin detemir group experienced a greater reduction from baseline in fasting plasma glucose
   compared with patients receiving NPH (adjusted mean change from baseline -39 mg/dL vs -21
   mg/dL, respectively) Type 2 diabetes mellitus: Insulin therapy should be initiated in pediatric patients presenting
   with ketosis/ketoacidosis and/or the patients HbA1c is greater than 5%. After initial treat-
   ment, insulin may be weaned off over 2 to 6 weeks by decreasing the insulin dose 30% to 50%
   with each increase in the metformin dose. If glycemic goals are not met after initial treatment
