pentobarbital, potassium chloride, procainamide, ranitidine, sodium bicarbonate, and sodium
   nitroprusside.

   Terminal Injection Site Incompatibility
   Phenytoin.

   Monitoring
   Continuous monitoring of EKG, heart rate, and blood pressure. Observe for worsening of
   seizure activity. Measuring blood concentrations is not clinically useful except when accumula-
   tion is suspected. Therapeutic drug concentration (antiarrhythmic treatment) is 5 to 6 mg/L,
   with toxicity associated with concentrations greater than 9 mg/L', 'Nasal Discomfort; Midazolam-Induced', 'Pharmacology
   The mode of action for lidocaine as an anticonvulsant drug is thought to be related a specific
   membrane sealing or stabilizing effect on the CNS that restricts the movement of sodium and
   potassium across the membrane, thereby decreasing membrane excitability. Age-dependent
   kinetics. Distributes rapidly into the brain, with onset of action seen within several minutes.
   Volume of distribution is approximately 3 L/kg in adults, and 5-fold higher in neonates.
   Free drug fraction in term neonates is approximately twice that found in older children be-
   cause of significantly reduced protein binding by alpha 1-acid glycoprotein. Lidocaine is metab-
   olized in the liver into 2 active metabolites: monoethylglycinexylidide (MEGX) and glycinxylidide
   (GX). Approximately 20% is excreted unchanged in the urine. The half-life is 1 to 8 hours 3 ,23,7 .'),
