   commonly reported adverse reactions in a clinical trial of preschool children with severe
   ADHD (n=183) included decreased appetite (7% to 14%), emotional outburst (10% to 15%), and
   weight loss (4% to 7%) In another clinical trail of children 3 to 5 years of age,
   methylphenidate was associated with significantly more mild adverse events than placebo,
   particularly decreased appetite and insomnia. No significant differences were observed in
   moderate or severe adverse events across groups, and no serious adverse events were attrib-
   uted to the intervention Priapism:Cases of painful and prolonged penile erections (ie, more than 4 hours) and pri-
   apism have been reported with methylphenidate use following dose increases, with longer
   than usual dosing intervals, and after temporary or permanent drug withdrawal. Some cases
   required surgical intervention. Inform male patients/caregivers of the signs and symptoms of
   priapism and the need for immediate medical evaluation if the condition occurs
   29,64,65,66,67,68
                         .
   In an FDA analysis, 15 cases of priapism associated with methylphenidate were reported over
   a 15-year period, with the majority of affected patients under age 18 (median age, 5 years;
   range, 8 to 33 years). Some cases required inpatient care, including surgical treatment in 2 pa-
   tients (ie, shunt placement, needle aspiration of the corpus cavernosum). Priapism developed
   following methylphenidate withdrawal in 4 cases. Priapism resolved after the drug was
   restarted in some patients Extended-Release Capsule (Adhansia XR™): The most commonly reported adverse effects in
   patients 12 to 17 years (summary of all dose levels) were decreased appetite (20%), insomnia
   (6%), initial insomnia (5%), decreased weight (7%), upper abdominal pain (4%), nausea (6%),

and dizziness, dry mouth, and vomiting (3% each). Adverse events occurring in greater than 5%
   of patients 6 to 12 years of age were decreased appetite (35%), upper abdominal pain (15%),
   affect lability (13%), nausea or vomiting (13%), weight decreased (12%), insomnia (10%), irri-
   tability (10%), headache (10%), and heart rate increased (5%) Extended-Release Capsule (Ritalin® LA and Metadate® CD): The most commonly reported
   adverse effects in patients 6 to 15 years of age in the clinical trial involving Metadate® CD
   (n=188) were headache (12%), abdominal pain (7%), anorexia (9%), and insomnia (5%) In the
   clinical trial involving Ritalin® LA (n=195), the most commonly reported adverse effects in pa-
   tients 6 to 12 years of age were of the same events as the Metadate® CD trial with incidence
   greater than 5% each Extended-Release Capsule (Aptensio XR™): The most commonly reported adverse effects in
   pediatric patients 6 to 17 years of age were headache (10.9%), insomnia (9.8%), upper abdomi-
   nal pain (8.2%), decreased appetite (4.9%), nausea (3.8%), vomiting (3.8%), and dizziness (2.2%) Extended-Release Disintegrating Tablet (Cotempla XR-ODT: The most commonly reported
   (≥2%) adverse effects in children, adolescents, and adults were decreased appetite, decreased
   weight, nausea, abdominal pain, dyspepsia, dry mouth, vomiting, insomnia, anxiety, nervous-
   ness, restlessness, affect lability, agitation, irritability, dizziness, vertigo, tremor, blurred vision,
   increased blood pressure, increased heart rate, tachycardia, palpitations, hyperhidrosis, and
