angle glaucoma • Evaluate family history for Tourette''s syndrome before initiating therapy and monitor for the', 'Pharmacology
   Methylphenidate is a mild CNS stimulant; the drug has similar pharmacological properties as
   the amphetamines, with predominantly central activity and minimal effects on the cardiovas-
   cular system. Methylphenidate is thought to activate the brainstem arousal system, cortex,
   and subcortical structures including the thalamus to produce its stimulant effect. The mecha-
   nism by which methylphenidate exerts its behavioral effects in children has not been deter-
   mined, however, the activity of this racemic mixture has been shown to reside with the d-iso-
   mer 36,42,30,33,25,28,21,35,31,22 .

   Following oral administration of methylphenidate, absorption is rapid and complete Tmax
   is 5 to 5 hours Protein binding is approximately 15%. Stereoselective metabolism in the
   liver to essentially inactive metabolites, which are excreted in the urine Average half-life 5
   hours over the usual dosing range for the immediate-release product Preschool children
   (n=14) had a higher systemic exposure and slower clearance than young school-age children
   (n=9) in a small comparative PK study of immediate-release methylphenidate. Other parame-
   ters were similar across these age groups Methylphenidate immediate-release (IR) dosage forms provide a rapid onset; however, one of
   its main disadvantages is the short duration of action, requiring 2 to 3 times per day adminis-
   tration. This may place the patient at an increased risk for breakthrough symptoms between
   doses and medication noncompliance. The following longer acting methylphenidate prepara-
   tions were then developed to overcome the disadvantages 37 :

   Ritalin® SR tablet and Metadate ER tablet; the tablet formulation was one of the earlier
   longer acting preparation developed as an attempt to increase methylphenidate duration of
   action; however, pharmacokinetic studies showed this formulation may have been less effec-

tive than methylphenidate IR for the treatment of ADHD as many patients receiving these
   products once daily may not have symptom control into the afternoon hours 24,37 .
   Ritalin® LA capsule and Metadate® CD capsule; the capsule formulation has an equivalent
   efficacy relative to twice daily methylphenidate IR and may be effective for 8 to 9 hours. These
   2 products appear to produce more improvement during the morning hours compared with
   Concerta® osmotic tablet. Also commonly used in patients with swallowing difficulties as the
   capsule may be opened and sprinkled on certain food for administration Concerta® osmotic tablet has an equivalent efficacy relative to 3 times daily
   methylphenidate IR and may be effective for 10 to 12 hours. Compared with the capsule for-
   mulation, Concerta® appears to produce more improvement in the later part of the day,
   which may help cover the 4- or 5-hour post school period. Not recommended in patients with
   swallowing difficulties as tampering with the tablet will affect the delivery system and subse-
   quent duration of action Daytrana® transdermal patch has an equivalent efficacy relative to 3 times daily
   methylphenidate IR and may be effective for 11 to 12 hours. Due to an onset of effect at 2
   hours after patch application, some patients may require a dose of methylphenidate IR before
   school to improve morning benefit. Wear time of the patch may be shortened to alleviate late-
   day adverse events Transdermal application of methylphenidate results in less first pass effect than oral adminis-
   tration and consequently, the mg/kg dose of transdermal methylphenidate is lower compared
   to oral dosages There was moderate accumulation seen with multiple-dose vs single-dose
   pharmacokinetic analysis Following a 1- or 7-day administration of Daytrana®, the Cmax
   and AUC of d-methylphenidate were approximately 50% lower in adolescents compared with
   children Jornay PM™: The relative bioavailability of Jornay PM (given once a day) compared to the same
   daily dose of a methylphenidate immediate-release oral product (given 3 times a day) in adults
   is 9%. The initial absorption of methylphenidate into plasma is delayed such that no more
   than 5% of total drug is available within the first 10 hours after dosing. After the lag period, the
   absorption of methylphenidate occurs in a single peak with a median Tmax 0 hours, fol-
   lowed by a gradual decline throughout the rest of the day Quillivant™ XR oral suspension: Relative bioavailability compared to methylphenidate IR oral
   solution is 95%. Tmax occurs at 4 hours in children and 2 hours in adolescents. Following a sin-
   gle dose of 60 mg, plasma concentrations in children (9 to 12 years old) are approximately
   twice that observed in adolescents and adults 16/25
