Preparation
   • Initiate treatment in a healthcare setting. Once therapy has been safely established after at
   least 3 doses, administration by the patient (if 12 years or older) or caregiver may be consid-
   ered after careful assessment of risk for anaphylaxis and mitigation strategies • Store in the original carton refrigerated at 36 to 46 degrees F (2 to 8 degrees C). Do not freeze
   or use if syringe/autoinjector was frozen. Protect from direct sunlight • May be removed from and placed back in the refrigerator if needed. The total combined time
   out of refrigerator may not be more than 7 days. Do not use if exposed to temperatures above
   77 degrees F (25 degrees C) • Determine the number of prefilled syringes required for the dose (available as 75 mg/0.5 mL
   [yellow needle shield/plunger rod] and 150 mg/mL [blue needle shield/plunger rod]) • Remove carton(s) from refrigerator and place on a clean, flat surface. Allow the carton(s) to
   sit for 20 minutes to warm to room temperature. Leave the prefilled syringe in the carton(s) to
   protect from light. Do not speed up warming process (eg, microwave, warm water) • Do not shake • The syringe plunger stopper and needle cap are not made with natural rubber latex • Carefully remove from carton by gripping the syringe body. Do not touch the plunger rod or
   needle cap when removing the prefilled syringe from carton • The liquid in the syringe should be clear to opalescent and colorless to pale brownish-yellow.
   Do not use if cloudy, discolored, or contains particles. The syringe may have 1 or more small
   air bubbles; this is normal', 'Common, 12 years or older, asthma (n=738): Arthralgia (8%), general pain (7%), leg pain
   (4%), fatigue (3%), dizziness (3%), fracture (2%), arm pain (2%), pruritus (2%), dermatitis (2%),
   and earache (2%). Injection site reactions occurred in 45% (any severity) and 12% (severe) of
   patients who received Omalizumab-igec. Most occurred within 1 hour of injection and lasted
   for less than 8 days Common, 6 to 12 years of age, greater than 3%: Nasopharyngitis, headache, pyrexia, upper
   abdominal pain, pharyngitis streptococcal, otitis media, viral gastroenteritis, arthropod bite,
   and epistaxis Black Box Warning
   Anaphylaxis
   Anaphylaxis, presenting as bronchospasm, hypotension, syncope, urticaria, and/or an-
   gioedema of the throat or tongue, has been reported to occur after administration of omal-
   izumab products. Anaphylaxis has occurred as early as after the first dose of omalizumab
   products, but also has occurred beyond 1 year after beginning regularly administered treat-
   ment. Because of the risk of anaphylaxis, initiate omalizumab-igec therapy in a healthcare set-
   ting and closely observe patients for an appropriate period of time after omalizumab-igec ad-
   ministration. Health care providers administering omalizumab-igec should be prepared to
   manage anaphylaxis that can be life-threatening. Inform patients of the signs and symptoms

of anaphylaxis and instruct them to seek immediate medical care should symptoms occur.
   Selection of patients for self-administration of omalizumab-igec should be based on criteria to
   mitigate risk from anaphylaxis Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Evaluate baseline serum total IgE levels to use in combination with body weight to determine
   dose and dosing frequency. Total IgE levels will rise with treatment and remain elevated for 1
   year after discontinuation of therapy. Retesting levels during treatment cannot be used for
   dose determination. If therapy is interrupted for 1 year or longer, retest total serum IgE levels • In patients with allergic asthma or nasal polyps, monitor for significant changes in body
