   Note: Prefilled syringes are for subQ use when not experiencing a flare 1
   Loading dose: 600 mg subQ, followed by 300 mg subQ 4 weeks later and every 4 weeks there-
   after 1
   Initiating or reinitiating treatment after a flare: Four weeks after treatment with IV
   spesolimab-sbzo, 300 mg subQ every 4 weeks; a subQ loading dose is not required 1

   Generalized Pustular Psoriasis(Experiencing a Flare)

   12 years or older and 40 kg or more
   Loading dose: 900 mg IV infusion over 90 minutes as a single administration; if generalized
   pustular psoriasis flare symptoms persist, an additional 900 mg IV infusion over 90 minutes
   may be administered 1 week after the initial dose 1

   Dose Adjustment
   Renal impairment: No formal study of the effect of renal impairment on the pharmacokinet-
   ics of spesolimab-sbzo was conducted; as a monoclonal antibody, it is not expected to un-
   dergo renal elimination 1/6
Hepatic impairment: No formal study of the effect of hepatic impairment on the pharmacoki-
   netics of spesolimab-sbzo was conducted; as a monoclonal antibody, it is not expected to un-
   dergo hepatic elimination Other Adjustments
   Anaphylaxis or serious hypersensitivity reaction: Discontinue use immediately and initiate
   appropriate treatment Infusion-related reaction (mild or moderate): Stop infusion and consider appropriate medi-
   cal therapy; upon resolution the infusion may be restarted at a slower infusion rate with grad-
   ual increase to complete the infusion Infection: If a patient develops a clinically important active infection, discontinue therapy until
   the infection resolves or is adequately treated 1', 'Spesolimab-sbzo is indicated to treat generalized pustular psoriasis (GPP) in adults and pedi-
   atric patients aged 12 years or older and weighing at least 40 kg 1

   Administration
   Missed dose: If a dose is missed, administer the dose as soon as possible then resume dosing
   at the regular scheduled time 1

   IV

   • Do not mix spesolimab-sbzo solution with other medicinal products • Administer as a continuous IV infusion through an IV line with a sterile, nonpyrogenic, low
   protein binding-in-line filter (pore size, 2 micron) over 90 minutes • If a preexisting IV line is used, flush with sterile NS before and at the end of infusion. No
   other infusion should be administered in parallel via the same IV access • If the infusion is slowed or temporarily stopped, the total infusion time (including stop time)
   should not exceed 180 minutes • No incompatibilities have been observed with infusion sets of polyvinylchloride, polyethylene,
   polypropylene, polybutadiene and polyurethane, and in-line filter membranes of polyethersul-
   fone (neutral and positively charged) and positively charged polyamide 2/6
SubQ

   • Prefilled syringes are for subQ use when not experiencing a flare 1
   • Administer subQ in the upper thighs or abdomen. Do not inject into areas where the skin is
   tender, bruised, erythematous, indurated, or scarred • Choose a different injection site for each injection, at least 1 inch away from the other injec-
   tion sites. Alternate between the upper thigh and abdomen injection sites for each complete
   dose • For a complete 300 mg dose, two 150 mg/mL prefilled syringes are required to be injected,
   one right after the other', 'Common, generalized pustular psoriasis flare, adult, IV: Asthenia and fatigue, nausea and
