   Inhalation: Acetylcysteine exerts its mucolytic action through its free sulfhydryl group, which
   opens the disulfide linkage in mucus and lowers its viscosity. This action increases with in-
   creasing pH and is most significant at pH 7 to The mucolytic action of acetylcysteine is not
   affected by the presence of DNA Systemic: Acetylcysteine may protect against acetaminophen overdose-induced hepatotoxic-
   ity by maintaining or restoring hepatic concentrations of glutathione, or by acting as an alter-
   nate substrate for conjugation with reactive acetaminophen metabolites 3 ,16 . Glutathione is
   required to inactivate an intermediate metabolite of acetaminophen that is thought to be hep-
   atotoxic. In acetaminophen overdose, excessive quantities of this metabolite are formed be-
   cause the primary metabolic pathways (glucuronide and sulfate conjugation) become satu-
   rated. CYP2E1 then metabolizes a larger fraction of the ingested dose to form an increased
   amount of the toxic metabolite Metabolism of acetylcysteine is believed to form cysteine and disulfides (N,N-diacetylcysteine
   and N-acetylcysteine), with further metabolism of cysteine to form glutathione and other me-
   tabolites. Acetylcysteine steady-state Vd and protein binding were 47 L/kg and 66% to 87%,
   respectively. Urinary recovery was 13% to 38% within 24 hours of a single oral dose. Mean to-
   tal body clearance was 11 L/hr/kg with an estimated renal clearance of 30% of the total.
   Mean half-life was 6 hours 3 , 17/20
