   lites: 17-BMP, 21-BMP, and beclomethasone. Beclomethasone metabolism is similar in adults
   and pediatric patients. Mainly excreted in feces; less than 10% of drug and metabolites are re-
   nally excreted 18,19,7 .

   Oral Inhalation route: Time to peak concentration is 5 hours for beclomethasone dipropi-
   onate and 7 hours for beclomethasone-17-monopropionate (17-BMP), active metabolite, af-
   ter oral inhalation of beclomethasone dipropionate. Protein binding of 17-BMP is approxi-
   mately 94% to 96% over concentration of 1000 to 5000 picograms/milliliter. Mean elimination
   half-life is 8 hours for 17-BMP. In one study of pediatric patients (mean age, 10 years; n=17),
   Cmax of beclomethasone-17-monopropionate (17-BMP) was 787 picograms/milliliter (pg/mL)
   at 6 hours after inhalation of 160 mcg. Approximately twice the systemic exposure to 17-
   BMP was reported with the administration of chlorofluorocarbon-beclomethasone with a
   large-volume spacer compared with hydrofluoroalkane-beclomethasone without a spacer in
   another study of pediatric patients (mean age, 12 years; n=14). In children younger than 5
   years, the amount of beclomethasone delivered through an Optichamber and Aerochamber
   Plus® was significantly reduced with increasing wait times of 5 to 10 seconds between actua-
