   cations include hypercalcemia associated with renal impairment, hypercalciuria, urolithiasis,
   and nephrocalcinosis Monitoring
   Monitor serum calcium, serum vitamin D (25-OH-D), and parathyroid hormone levels as long
   as the patient requires calcium or vitamin D supplementation: weekly measurements while es-
   tablishing treatment regimen; quarterly for stable patients. Assess serum phosphorus and
   magnesium levels, and urine calcium when indicated 7 ,8 ,4 ,5 . Assess gastrointestinal tolerance.

   Corrected Calcium Formula (Hypoalbuminemia)

   The formula below is a rough estimate of the corrected calcium level in patients with hypoal-
   buminemia. In cases where true hypocalcemia is suspected, an ionized calcium level should be
   obtained for accurate diagnosis Corrected calcium (mg/dL)= serum calcium (mg/dL) + 8 {4 -serum albumin (g/dL)}', 'Absorption of calcium carbonate is decreased by H2 blockers and proton-pump inhibitors (re-', 'Pharmacology
   Absorption of calcium administered orally is approximately 50%. Absorption takes place
   throughout the small intestine, and is primarily regulated by 1,25-dihydroxy vitamin D.
   Excreted in the urine. Calcium carbonate significantly reduces the absorption of levothyroxine
   by forming insoluble complexes; separate administration of the 2 drugs by 4 hours.
   Absorption of calcium carbonate is decreased by H2 blockers and proton-pump inhibitors (re-
   quires acidic environment for absorption); calcium citrate is recommended for patients on
   these drugs. Clinical signs suggestive of hypocalcemia in children include seizures, muscle
   cramps, tetany, refractory hypotension, wheezing, dyspnea, dysphagia, abdominal pain, and
   biliary colic 8 ,2 ,4 ,5 .'),
