   • Birth to 6 months: 4 mcg/day
   • 7 to 12 months: 5 mcg/day
   • 1 to 3 years: 9 mcg/day
   • 4 to 8 years: 2 mcg/day
   • 9 to 13 years: 8 mcg/day
   • 14 to 18 years: 4 mcg/day; 6 mcg/day during pregnancy; 8 mcg/day while breastfeeding

   Vitamin B12 Deficiency
   Breastfeeding infants with vitamin B12-deficient mothers

   • Dosage: 250 to 1000 mcg IM once daily for 1 week, then once weekly until recovery; also treat
   mother for vitamin B12 deficiency to enrich breast milk 3
   • Concomitant supplementation: Orally supplement infant with 1 to 2 mcg/day of vitamin B12 or
   vitamin B12-enriched formula or food Younger children, malabsorption:

   • Initial dosage: 1000 mcg IM once daily for 2 to 7 days 3 ,4

   • Maintenance dosage: 100 mcg IM once monthly OR 500 to 2000 mcg orally once daily 3

   • Duration: Lifelong 3 ,4

   Older children, mild malabsorption:

• IM:1000 mcg IM once daily or every other day for 1 week, then weekly for 4 to 8 weeks, then
   once monthly 3

   • Oral: 500 to 1000 mcg orally once daily 3

   • Duration: Lifelong 3 , 4

   • Older children, severe malabsorption:
   Initial dosage: 1000 mcg IM once daily or every other day for 1 week, then weekly for 4 to 8
   weeks 3

   • Maintenance dosage: 100 mcg IM once monthly OR 1000 to 2000 mcg orally once daily 3

   • Duration: Lifelong 3 , 4', 'Vitamin B12 Deficiency
   Vitamin B12 deficiency can be caused by dietary deficiencies (eg, vegan or vegetarian diets) or
   conditions that lead to vitamin B12 malabsorption (eg, autoimmune gastritis/pernicious ane-
   mia, gastrointestinal disorders and surgeries, use of metformin, proton pump inhibitors, or
   H2-blockers). Deficiency caused by malabsorption usually requires lifelong supplementation
   with IM or high-dose oral vitamin B12. Infants may be born with vitamin B12 deficiency or de-
   velop deficiency if exclusively breast-fed when mothers are vitamin B12 deficient; treatment
   and supplementation of both the infant and the mother are necessary 3 ,4 .

   FDA Approved Indications:
   Cyanocobalamin injection is indicated for the treatment of vitamin B12 deficiencies due to
   malabsorption Administration
   Injection

   • For IM or subQ use only; do not give IV Oral

   • Time-release formulations: Preferably take with a meal. Do not take within 2 hours of other
   medications 2/7
• Sublingual formulations: Place tablet under tongue for 30 seconds before swallowing', 'Common: Itching, transient diarrhea 5

   Monitoring
   Diagnosis: Experts suggest confirming a vitamin B12 deficiency diagnosis by measuring serum
   methylmalonic acid levels if the serum vitamin B12 level is 150 to 399 picograms/milliliter
   (pg/mL) 2
   • Serum vitamin B12 levels lower than 200 to 250 (pg/mL) are generally defined as subnormal,
   but have a sensitivity of 65% to 95% and specificity of 50% to 60% for detecting vitamin B12 de-
   ficiency; they can also be problematic when anti-intrinsic factor antibodies are present.
   Sensitivity is 90% for serum levels less than 350 pg/mL. 2 ,3
   • Serum methylmalonic acid is the most sensitive marker and levels greater than 400
   nanomol/L have a sensitivity of 98% for detecting deficiency; however, levels may also be ele-
   vated with renal insufficiency 2 ,3

   • Serum homocysteine levels may also indicate vitamin B12 deficiency but are also elevated
   with folate deficiency and renal insufficiency 2 , Therapeutic Monitoring
   Measure hematocrit, reticulocyte count, vitamin B12, folate, and iron levels prior to treatment
   initiation 8 ,1 ,5 ; consider the potential for drug interactions to interfere with vitamin B12 and
   folate diagnostic blood assays Injection formulation

   • Monitor hematocrit and reticulocyte counts daily from the fifth to seventh days of therapy
   and then frequently until the hematocrit is normal; repeat determinations of iron and folic acid
   may reveal a complicating illness that might inhibit the response of the marrow 5
   • Monitor serum potassium closely during the first 48 hours of the initial treatment of patients
   with pernicious anemia 5', 'mia, gastrointestinal disorders and surgeries, use of metformin, proton pump inhibitors, or
initiation 8 ,1 ,5 ; consider the potential for drug interactions to interfere with vitamin B12 and
may reveal a complicating illness that might inhibit the response of the marrow 5', 'Renal: 50% to 98% of the dose is excreted in the urine after IM or subQ doses of 100 mcg or
   1000 mcg 5'),
