   Parent compound: 3 hours 5', 'Crohn''s disease; maintenance of remission
   Prior to initiation: Test for thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) by genotype or enzymatic ac-
   tivity to guide dosing (guideline dosing) 1 ,2 , and do not use thiopurines in patients who are ho-
   mozygous recessive or have extremely low TPMT activity 3

   Normal TPMT metabolism: 1 to 5 mg/kg orally once daily; may initiate therapy at the full
   dose without the need for gradual dose increase (guideline dosage) 2

   Ulcerative colitis; maintenance of remission
   Prior to initiation: Consider thiopurine methyltransferase (TPMT) genotype or phenotype
   testing, and do not initiate in children with homozygous mutants for TPMT or very low TPMT
   activity (guideline dosing) 4
   Normal TPMT metabolism: 1 to 5 mg/kg orally once daily; maximal effect may not develop
   until 10 to 12 weeks of treatment. Consider measuring metabolites 6-thioguanine (6-TGN; opti-
   mal range, 235 to 450 pmol/8 x 10(8) RBCs) and 6-methylmercaptopurine ribonucleotides (6-
   MMP; optimal, less than 6700 pmol/8 x 10(8) RBCs) to inform dosage adjustments; cutoff val-
   ues may vary between methods (guideline dosage) 4

   Concomitant medication: May continue 5-aminosalicylic acid, at least initially, to inhibit TPMT
   and increase 6-TGN (guideline dosage) 4
   Concomitant medication (patients with hyperactive TPMT resulting in high 6-MMP and
   low 6-TGN): Reduce azathioprine to 25% to 30% of initial dose plus allopurinol 50 mg orally
   once daily (weight less than 30 kg) or 100 mg once daily (30 kg or greater; maximum, 5 mg/kg)
   may be considered in suitably experienced units with close patient monitoring (guideline
   dosage) 4
   Discontinuation of therapy: Consider alternative treatment in patients with active disease
   despite an adequate 6-TGN level after at least 12 weeks of treatment (guideline dosage) 4

   Withdrawal: In patients with sustained clinical remission after at least 1 year of treatment and
   complete mucosal healing (and preferably also histological remission) withdrawal may be con-

sidered; 5-aminosalicylic acid may be helpful in maintaining remission, especially in 5-aminos-
   alicylic acid-naïve patients (guideline dosage) Dosage adjustments
   Renal impairment
   Impairment: Consider initiating at the low end of the dosing range in renally impaired pa-
   tients 5 ,6 , or increase the dosing interval to 36 to 48 hours in patients with CrCl less than 50
   mL/min Hepatic impairment

   Impairment: Consider dosage reduction 6 or initiate at the low end of the dosing range and
   monitor closely for toxicity Other adjustments

   Concomitant use with allopurinol: Reduce mercaptopurine to one-third to one-quarter of
   usual dose Concomitant use with aminosalicylates derivatives: Use the lowest possible dose of each
   drug Excessive hematological toxicity: Maintain absolute neutrophil count at a desirable level by
   reducing the dose Heterozygous thiopurine S-methyltransferase (TPMT) or nucleotide diphosphatase
   (NUDT15) enzyme deficiency: Reduce dosage based on tolerability; most patients tolerate
   usual doses; heterozygous for both TPMT and NUDT15 may require more substantial dosage
   reductions 5 , 8
   Homozygous TPMT or NUDT15 enzyme deficiency: Reduce initial dosage; patients typically
   require 10% or less of the usual dosage 5 ,8
   Thiopurine Methyltransferase (TPMT) and/or Nucleotide Diphosphatase (NUDT15)
