                                                              munosuppressive therapy.', 'Crohn''s disease; maintenance of remission
   Guidelines: Suggested uses for thiopurines (azaTHIOprine, 6-mercaptopurine) in pediatric pa-
   tients with Crohn disease include maintenance of steroid-free remission in patients at risk for
   poor disease outcome 2 and maintenance of remission in female patients (conditional recom-
   mendation, low quality evidence) Use to maintain remission in male patients was neither
   suggested or rejected due to safety concerns, including the risk for lymphomas, and lack of
   consensus. Although risk for lymphoma is elevated for both genders, it is higher in males and
   risk/benefit analysis of alternative methotrexate therapy might favor a thiopurine in females
   due to teratogenicity concerns with methotrexate. Thiopurine monotherapy should not be
   used to induce remission in Crohn disease 1 ,2 .
   In newly-diagnosed pediatric patients with moderate-to-severe Crohn disease, the addition of
   mercaptopurine (prodrug of azaTHIOprine) to corticosteroid therapy compared with placebo
   plus corticosteroid therapy did not significantly improve remission rates but did significantly
   reduce relapse rates and corticosteroid use in an 18-month randomized trial Subsequent
   observational studies have reported lower steroid-free remission rates with thiopurine use
   13,14,15

   A retrospective review of patients with moderate to severe active Crohn disease (n=105; 6 to
   17 years of age) illustrated that azaTHIOprine, at a mean dose of approximately 2 mg/kg/day,

maintained a steroid-free remission rate of 70%, 46%, 39%, and 36% at 6, 12, 18, and 24
   months, respectively, when administered following therapy to induce disease remission. There
   was a trend for greater efficacy in the groups who received early azaTHIOprine therapy and
   those with elective ileocecal or ileocolonic resection prior to azaTHIOprine use A second
   retrospective review examined azaTHIOprine use in patients with newly diagnosed Crohn dis-
   ease (n=88; mean age 3 years). During a mean follow-up period of 2 years, azaTHIOprine
   was used in approximately 80% of patients. These patients had a longer duration of remission
   and a larger proportion of them remained corticosteroid free, as compared to patients who
   did not receive azaTHIOprine Results from the ImproveCareNow Network, a quality improvement collaborative for pediatric
   IBD, demonstrated improved outcomes for patients with Crohn disease (CD) and ulcerative
   colitis (UC) through implementation of a standard process for disease management (n=1188;
   mean age 6 years). Thiopurine S-methyltransferase (TPMT) levels were measured prior to
   treatment to aid selection of an initial dose. The proportion of patients with inactive disease in-
   creased for patients with both CD and UC, and the proportion of patients with CD who re-
   quired prednisone was decreased. The proportion of patients with both CD and UC who were
   in remission at last visit showed a positive trend over time Ulcerative colitis; maintenance of remission: Thiopurines (azaTHIOprine, 6-mercaptop-
   urine) should be used to maintain remission in children who are corticosteroid-dependent or
   relapsing at least twice per year despite optimal 5-aminosalicylic acid (5-ASA) treatment or in 5-
   ASA-intolerant patients. Consider thiopurines after discharge from an acute severe colitis
   episode. Thiopurines should not be used for induction of remission. It is reasonable to con-
   tinue 5-ASA therapy with thiopurines, at least initially. Measure thiopurine metabolites in pa-
   tients with incomplete response on stable thiopurine dosage, patients who present with
   leukopenia or elevated transaminases, or if poor compliance is suspected. A switch between
   azathioprine and 6-mercaptopurine may be considered in patients who develop flu-like or
   acute gastrointestinal symptoms Results from the ImproveCareNow Network, a quality improvement collaborative for pediatric
   IBD, demonstrated improved outcomes for patients with Crohn disease (CD) and ulcerative
   colitis (UC) through implementation of a standard process for disease management (n=1188;
   mean age 6 years). Thiopurine S-methyltransferase (TPMT) levels were measured prior to
   treatment to aid selection of an initial dose. The proportion of patients with inactive disease in-
   creased for patients with both CD and UC, and the proportion of patients with CD who re-
   quired prednisone was decreased. The proportion of patients with both CD and UC who were
   in remission at last visit showed a positive trend over time 9/17
Administration
   NIOSH Guidance: For full NIOSH and safe handling guidance, see Special Considerations.

   Suspension

   •Give consistently with or without food •Two oral dispensing syringes (1 mL and 5 mL) are provided. Oral syringes are intended for
   multiple doses. Wash with warm soapy water, move plunger up and down several times while
   submerged, rinse well, and allow to dry completely between doses •Missed dose: Continue with the next scheduled dose 5

   Preparation -
   Oral Suspension

   • Shake suspension vigorously for at least 30 seconds prior to dispensing to ensure adequate
   mixing, and use within 8 weeks of opening Extemporaneous Oral Compounds
   Note: A 20 mg/mL oral suspension is commercially available.

   10 mg/mL suspension (100 mL) with SyrSpend SF PH4: 10

   • Crush 20 mercaptopurine 50 mg tablets with a mortar and pestle to a fine powder.

   • Add a small amount of SyrSpend SF PH4 to powder and mix to form a paste.
   • Add SyrSpend SF PH4 in approximately geometric portions almost to volume and mix thor-
   oughly after each addition.

   • Bring to final volume of 100 mL with SyrSpend SF PH4 and mix well.
   • Package in low-actinic, light-resistant prescription bottles and label with "Shake well."

