   induction 10 , 11 , 12 , 13 , Maximum 10 mg/dose. A dose of 5 mg/kg has been used in studies
   enrolling tonsillectomy patients 10,15 . A two-dose regimen, 25 mg/kg/dose IV before anes-
   thetic induction and 25 mg/kg/dose IV 1 hour postoperatively, has been used successfully', 'Intravenous:
   To facilitate small bowel intubation when the tube does not pass the pylorus with conventional
   maneuvers Administration
   IV bolus: Administer single doses slowly over 5 minutes; do not exceed at rate of 10 mg (undi-
   luted) over 1 to 2 minutes 6

   Intermittent IV infusion: Dilute to 2 mg/mL in D5W or NS and infuse over a minimum of 15
   minutes Oral solution, tablets, and orally disintegrating tablets (ODT): Administer on empty stom-
   ach at least 30 minutes before eating and at bedtime. Handle ODT with dry hands; place ODT
   on the tongue for about 1 minute without liquid; discard ODT if broken or crumbled while han-
   dling and remove a new tablet', 'Dystonic reactions and extrapyramidal symptoms are seen frequently at higher doses and
   with prolonged use. Children are more susceptible than adults, with incidences as high as 25%
   reported 29,33,3 ,34,35,36 . Tardive dyskinesia has been reported in children 37,38 . Rare cases of
   neuroleptic malignant syndrome have been reported. Drowsiness is common and may espe-
   cially occur with rapid IV administration Black Box Warning
   Tardive Dyskinesia

   Treatment with metoclopramide can cause tardive dyskinesia, a serious movement disorder
   that is often irreversible. The risk of developing tardive dyskinesia increases with duration of
   treatment and total cumulative dose. Metoclopramide therapy should be discontinued in pa-
   tients who develop signs or symptoms of tardive dyskinesia. There is no known treatment for
   tardive dyskinesia. In some patients, symptoms may lessen or resolve after metoclopramide
   treatment is stopped. Treatment with metoclopramide for longer than 12 weeks should be
   avoided in all but rare cases where therapeutic benefit is thought to outweigh the risk of devel-
   oping tardive dyskinesia 2 ,32 .

   Solution Compatibility
   D5W and NS.

   Terminal Injection Site Compatibility
