   long-term therapy (greater than 12 weeks) Monitor for fluid retention or volume overload in patients with hepatic impairment', 'nesia and other extrapyramidal symptoms, as well as a risk of methemoglobinemia in neonates Chemotherapy-Induced nausea and vomiting, adjunct for breakthrough: 1 to 2
Measure gastric residuals. Observe for increased irritability or vomiting. Monitor for
long-term therapy (greater than 12 weeks) Monitor for fluid retention or volume overload in patients with hepatic impairment Pharmacology', 'Pharmacology
   Derivative of procainamide. Exact mode of action is unknown; however, metoclopramide has
   both dopamine-receptor blocking activity and peripheral cholinergic effects. Well absorbed
   from GI tract. Onset of action is 1 to 3 minutes for IV, 10 to 15 minutes for IM, and 30 to 60
   minutes for oral. Duration of action is 1 to 2 hours. Lipid-soluble, large volume of distribution
   (about 3 L/kg in children). Protein binding is approximately 30%. Variable first-pass metabolism
   by liver. Significant fraction excreted unchanged in urine (85%). Half-life in children is 1 to 5
   hours; prolonged in patients with renal failure 6 ,29,39,40 .'),
