   Administration
   Administer with food 3 ,4 , ideally breakfast and dinner Do not administer concomitantly with
   antacids containing magnesium trisilicate 6 , 3 , Preparation
   Extemporaneous oral compound
   Note: A 5 mg/mL oral suspension is commercially available.

2 mg/mL Suspension (300 mL) with SyrSpend SF PH4® 8 :
   • Triturate 600 mg nitrofurantoin active pharmaceutical ingredient to a fine powder using a
   mortar and pestle.
   • Add a small amount of SyrSpend SF PH4® to wet the powder and mix into a fine paste.

   • Add SyrSpend SF PH4® in geometric proportions almost to volume, mixing thoroughly after
   each addition.
   • Add sufficient SyrSpend SF PH4® to bring volume to 300 mL, then mix well.

   • Label the container with "Shake well before using."
   • Stable for 90 days at room temperature (20 to 25°C) or under refrigeration (2 to 8°C) when
   packaged in low-actinic light-resistant prescription bottles.', 'Common adverse effects include anorexia, abdominal discomfort, diarrhea, dizziness, fever,
   fatigue, headache, nausea and vomiting, pruritus, and urticaria 19,20,21,22 .

   Monitoring
   Obtain urine specimens for culture and susceptibility testing prior to and following therapy.
   Monitor pulmonary function in patients treated for 6 months or longer. Periodically monitor
   for changes in biochemical tests for signs of liver injury. Monitor renal function with long-term
   therapy 6 ,3 ,4 .', 'inhibiting vital biochemical processes such as protein synthesis, aerobic energy metabolism,', 'Pharmacology
   Nitrofurantoin is bactericidal in the urine at therapeutic doses. Through its reactive intermedi-
   ates, it inactivates and alters bacterial ribosomal proteins and other large molecules, thereby
   inhibiting vital biochemical processes such as protein synthesis, aerobic energy metabolism,
   DNA synthesis, RNA synthesis, and cell wall synthesis. The broad-based nature of mechanism
   of action helps to reduce its development of bacterial resistance. Nitrofurantoin is dialyzable.
   Bioavailability is increased with food. Following administration, approximately 20% to 43% is
   recovered from the urine unchanged over 24 hours. Highly soluble in urine, which may be the
   reason for brown color in urine. Blood concentrations at therapeutic dosage are usually
   low 6 , 3 , Furadantin® contains nitrofurantoin and is readily absorb and rapidly excreted in the urine.
   Macrodantin® contains nitrofurantoin macrocrystals and is a larger crystal form of
   Furadantin®. The absorption of Macrodantin® is slower and its excretion is less than
   Furadantin®. Macrobid® contains 25% of nitrofurantoin macrocrystals, which has slower dis-
   solution and absorption than nitrofurantoin monohydrate, and 75% of nitrofurantoin monohy-
   drate powder, which forms a gel matrix that releases nitrofurantoin over time upon exposure
   to gastric and intestinal fluids. Extent and rate of urinary excretion of Macrodantin® and
   Macrobid® are similar 6 ,3 ,4 .'),
