   Monitoring
   Monitor for lack of efficacy or clinical signs of toxicity. Frequent monitoring of serum theo-
   phylline levels is recommended for children less than 1 year of age. Obtain serum levels at 24-
   hour intervals during infusion. In general, maintaining average serum theophylline concentra-
   tions between 10 and 15 mcg/mL achieves the most therapeutic benefit while minimizing the
   risk of serious adverse events. Toxicity associated with serum levels greater than 20 mcg/mL
   1 ,5
          .', 'Monitor for lack of efficacy or clinical signs of toxicity. Frequent monitoring of serum theo-', 'Pharmacology
   Relaxes the smooth muscle in the airways (bronchodilation effects) and suppresses the re-
   sponse of the airways to stimuli (non-bronchodilator prophylactic effects). May increase di-
   aphragmatic contractility. Cerebral blood flow is acutely decreased following IV bolus dose.
   Renal effects include diuresis and increased urinary calcium excretion. Stimulates gastric acid
   secretion and may cause gastroesophageal reflux. Cardiac output is increased due to higher
   sensitivity to catecholamines. Approximately 40% bound to proteins (primarily albumin).
   Primarily metabolized (90% of the dose in children greater than 1 year of age). Elimination in
   children less than 3 months of age is primarily as unchanged drug, although significant inter-
   conversion to caffeine occurs; however, beyond 3 months of age, caffeine accumulation is clin-
   ically insignificant. Half-life is 20 hours in premature neonates with postnatal age 25 to 57
   days, 11 hours in term infants with postnatal age 3 to 30 weeks, and 5 hours in children 1 to
   17 years of age. Theophylline metabolism and clearance mature to adult values by 1 year of
   age, remains constant until about 9 years, and then slowly decreases by about 50% to adult
   values at 16 years of age. Aminophylline salt is 9% theophylline. Theophylline administered
   orally is approximately 80% bioavailable; therefore, no dosage adjustment is necessary when
   changing from IV aminophylline to oral theophylline 1 ,5 .'),
