Anidulafungin, a semi-synthetic echinocandin, is a non-competitive inhibitor of beta-(1,3)-D-
the development of drug resistance may be possible. No hepatic metabolism; not a substrate,
inducer, or inhibitor of CYP450. Undergoes slow chemical degradation at physiologic tempera-', 'Pharmacology
   Anidulafungin, a semi-synthetic echinocandin, is a non-competitive inhibitor of beta-(1,3)-D-
   glucan synthase; this enzyme is responsible for formation of the polysaccharide, beta-(1,3)-glu-
   can, an essential fungal cell wall component. Anidulafungin is most active (MIC90 in mcg/mL)
   against Candida albicans (0.06), C glabrata (0.12), C tropicalis (0.06), and C krusei (0.12) isolates,
   but less potent against C parapsilosis (2) and C guilliermondii (2). It has demonstrated activity
   against the biofilms of C. albicans and C. parapsilosis. The minimum effective concentration 90
   against Aspergillus fumigatus is 008 mcg/mL 10 , Candida isolates with reduced susceptibility
   to anidulafungin have been reported. The clinical relevance of these reports is unknown, but
   the development of drug resistance may be possible. No hepatic metabolism; not a substrate,
   inducer, or inhibitor of CYP450. Undergoes slow chemical degradation at physiologic tempera-
   ture and pH to a ring-opened peptide (inactive). In a single-dose study, less than 1% was recov-
   ered in urine and approximately 30% was recovered in the feces over 9 days, of which less
   than 10% was intact anidulafungin. Not removed by hemodialysis In a pharmacokinetic and safety study following 3 to 5 days of anidulafungin (n=15; age 2 days
   to 2 years), infants (n=7) demonstrated a median weight-adjusted clearance of 015 L/kg/hr
   (range 005 to 027), median half-life of 33 hours (range 30 to 173), and median exposure of 7 mcg*hr/mL (54.8 to 278) In a pharmacokinetic study of immunocompromised, neutropenic pediatric and adolescent pa-
   tients who received daily doses of anidulafungin 75 mg/kg or 5 mg/kg (n=25; age range 2 to
   17 years), drug exposure was proportional to the dose received. Mean steady-state values for
   clearance, volume of distribution, and half-life were 018 L/hr/kg, 54 L/kg, and 9 hours,
   respectively, for the lower dose, and 016 L/hr/kg, 43 L/kg, and 9 hours, respectively, for
   the high-dose group. Trough concentrations were 5 to 3 mg/mL for the lower dose and 1 to 5
   mg/mL for the high-dose group'),
