   Warning: Increased Mortality in Elderly Patients with Dementia-Related Psychosis and
   Suicidal Thoughts and Behaviors

   Elderly patients with dementia-related psychosis treated with antipsychotic drugs are at an in-
   creased risk of death. Brexpiprazole is not approved for the treatment of patients with demen-
   tia-related psychosis without agitation associated with dementia due to Alzheimer’s disease.

   Antidepressants increased the risk of suicidal thoughts and behaviors in pediatric patients and
   young adult patients in short-term studies. Closely monitor all antidepressant-treated patients
   for clinical worsening, and for emergence of suicidal thoughts and behaviors. The safety and

effectiveness of brexpiprazole have not been established in pediatric patients with major de-
   pressive disorder Monitoring
   Therapeutic Drug Monitoring

   Psychosis: 40 to 140 nanograms/milliliter (ng/mL) 5

   • In a consensus guideline, the use of therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM) with brexpiprazole
   was rated as 3, useful, specifically for special indications or problem solving. TDM can be used
   to control whether drug concentrations are in accordance with the dose-related reference
   range. Clinical improvement may be attained by dose increase in non-responders who display
   low drug concentrations • The antipsychotic therapeutic reference range for brexpiprazole is 40 to 140 ng/mL, with a
   laboratory alert level of 280 ng/mL. Therapeutic reference ranges were computed from drug
   concentrations at approved doses. Drug concentrations related to medication effects either
   are not yet available or are based on retrospective analyses of TDM data, single case reports
   or non-systematic clinical experience Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Assess clinical response and tolerability periodically • Assess need for continued therapy periodically Toxic Monitoring
   • Perform CBC with differential frequently during the first few months following therapy initia-
   tion in patients with preexisting low WBC or absolute neutrophil count, or a history of drug-in-
   duced leukopenia or neutropenia • Measure fasting plasma glucose at baseline, at week 12, then annually thereafter, or more
