• Monitor for signs of propylene glycol toxicity (hemolysis, hyperosmolarity with anion gap aci-
epoxide/carBAMazepine ratio and increasing age). Induces its own metabolism (autoinduction', 'Pharmacology
   The mechanism of action of carBAMazepine remains unknown 2 although in rats and mice, it
   appears to exert its anticonvulsant properties by reducing the polysynaptic response and
   blocking post-tetanic potentiation. The principal metabolite, carBAMazepine-10,11-epoxide
   demonstrated anticonvulsant activity in several in vivo animal models. Suspension, tablets,
   and extended-release (XR) tablets deliver equivalent drug amounts to systemic circulation,
   with suspension absorbed the fastest and XR tablets absorbed the slowest (89% bioavailability
   compared with suspension). Suspension delivers higher peak and lower trough compared to
   conventional tablet. Peak level at 5 hours for suspension, 4 to 5 hours for conventional
   tablets, 3 to 12 hours for XR tablets, and 4 to 8 hours for XR capsules. Plasma protein binding
   is approximately 76%. Metabolized in the liver to carBAMazepine-10,11-epoxide. This occurs
   more rapidly in children than in adults (inverse relationship between carBAMazepine-10,11-
   epoxide/carBAMazepine ratio and increasing age). Induces its own metabolism (autoinduction
   complete after 3 to 5 weeks after fixed dosing regimen). Initial half-life of 25 to 65 hours, de-
   creasing to 12 to 17 hours with repeated dosing regimen. Approximately 72% of a radiolabeled
   dose was recovered in the urine and 28% recovered in the feces 1 ,3 ,36 .'),
