   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Physical Monitoring

   Use of a peripheral nerve stimulator is recommended to assess drug response, need for addi-
   tional doses, and recovery During continuous infusion, assess neuromuscular blockade at least every 24 hours with tran-
   scutaneous electrical stimulation of the ulnar nerve using train-of-four pattern stimulation 1 ,5 ,
   especially in patients with neuromuscular disease 1 , Train-of-four (TOF) monitoring with clinical assessment, rather than TOF monitoring alone, is
   recommended in critically ill pediatric patients in the ICU 6/9
Toxic Physical Monitoring

   Monitor for signs/symptoms of malignant hyperthermia 1 ,3 .', 'Monitor for signs/symptoms of malignant hyperthermia 1 ,3 . Pharmacology
shorter duration of cisatracurium-induced neuromuscular block compared with adults.', 'Pharmacology
   Cisatracurium besylate, the R-cis, R''-cis isomer of atracurium (representing approximately 15%
   of the atracurium mixture), is a nondepolarizing neuromuscular blocking agent of intermedi-
   ate onset and duration of action 11,12 . Time of onset and duration of effect correlate with dose
   over the range of 1 to 2 mg/kg 4 , and pediatric patients have a faster time to onset and a
   shorter duration of cisatracurium-induced neuromuscular block compared with adults.
   Metabolism is dependent on Hofmann degradation and not renally or hepatically eliminated.
   Clearance is higher in healthy pediatric patients (5.89 mL/min/kg) compared with adults (4.57
   mL/min/kg) 11,12 . Clearance is significantly reduced in patients undergoing hypothermia Elimination half-life is approximately 22 minutes 11,12 .'),
