   Monitoring
   Toxic Monitoring

   • Monitor for elevated sodium and decreased potassium levels • Monitor for signs and symptoms of adrenal insufficiency after corticosteroid withdrawal,
   Cushing syndrome, and hyperglycemia during therapy • Monitor for the development of infection • In a patient with unexplained diarrhea or who has spent time in the tropics, exclude latent or
   active amebiasis before initiating corticosteroid therapy • Monitor blood pressure • Monitor for signs and symptoms of thromboembolism • Monitor intraocular pressure in patients receiving steroid therapy for greater than 6 weeks • Monitor for changes in bone density with chronic use • Monitor for signs and symptoms of behavioral and mood disturbances • Carefully monitor patients with frequent measurements of weight, height, peptic ulcers, and
   cataracts • Monitor the linear growth of pediatric patients treated with corticosteroids • Ophthalmic suspension use: Monitor intraocular pressure during long-term (10 day or
   longer) therapy 32
