metabolized in the liver, primarily by the CYP3A4 enzyme system Amprenavir is a CYP3A4 in-
ducer, inhibitor, and substrate 1 , and there are many drug interactions with fosamprenavir in-', 'Pharmacology
   Fosamprenavir is a prodrug that is rapidly hydrolyzed to amprenavir by cellular phosphatases
   in the gut epithelium as it is absorbed. Amprenavir binds to the active site of HIV-1 protease,
   preventing the processing of viral gag and gag-pol polyprotein precursors. This interference re-
   sults in formation of immature, noninfectious viral particles. Amprenavir Tmax following oral
   administration is 5 to 4 hours (median, 5 hours). Food has no effect on the bioavailability.
   Protein binding is approximately 90% and is primarily to alpha1-acid glycoprotein. Extensively
   metabolized in the liver, primarily by the CYP3A4 enzyme system Amprenavir is a CYP3A4 in-
   ducer, inhibitor, and substrate 1 , and there are many drug interactions with fosamprenavir in-
   volving these enzymes. Renal excretion of unchanged drug is minimal (less than 1%). Renal
   and fecal excretion of metabolites is 14% and 75%, respectively. Half-life is approximately 7
   hours. Pharmacokinetic studies (n=212; 4 weeks to 18 years of age) of fosamprenavir demon-
   strated that amprenavir apparent clearance decreased with increasing weight, suggesting that
   younger children require higher mg/kg dosing of fosamprenavir. The studies also showed that
   AUC and Cmin were generally lower in children younger than 6 months of age when compared
   with adults at the dose recommended for protease-experienced patients; therefore, twice-

daily dosing of fosamprenavir alone or in combination with ritonavir in treatment-experienced
   patients less than 6 months of age is not recommended'),
