  Pediatric Case Reports

  Drug reaction with eosinophilia and systemic symptoms (DRESS): MetronNIDAZOLE-induced DRESS with par-
  vovirus B19 reactivation was reported in a 10-year-old girl following treatment for a para-pharyngeal abscess. Seven
  days after initiating treatment with cefotaxime, vancomycin, and IV metroNIDAZOLE, the patient developed a diffuse
  pruritic, erythematous rash covering 80% of her body, along with a fever, facial edema, and lymphadenopathy. The pa-
  tient had a tonsillectomy five years prior and no previous history of drug hypersensitivity. She had not been taking any
  medications before hospitalization. Laboratory tests showed eosinophilia (800/mm(3)), mild liver cytolysis (AST, 60
  units/L; ALT, 55 units/L), elevated gamma-glutamyl transferase (90 units/L), and a mild increase in C-reactive protein
  (11 mg/L). Serologies for Chlamydia trachomatis, Mycoplasma, hepatitis viruses, cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus,
  and human-herpes virus 6 were negative. However, parvovirus IgM and IgG were positive, indicating reactivation of
  parvovirus B19. A skin biopsy showed spongiosis and mild lymphocytic infiltration, findings that are commonly but not
  exclusively associated with DRESS. The application of the Registry of Severe Cutaneous Adverse Reaction (RegiSCAR)
  criteria for DRESS resulted in a score of 5, suggesting a probable case, while the Naranjo score for metroNIDAZOLE was
  3, indicating a possible case. Initially, vancomycin was discontinued due to its higher association with DRESS based on
  available literature; however, the patient''s symptoms progressively worsened. On hospital day 10, metroNIDAZOLE
  was discontinued, and vancomycin was reintroduced. Symptoms improved 3 days after discontinuing metroNIDAZOLE
  along with treatment using topical betamethasone 05% and cetirizine 10 mg/day. The facial swelling, lymphadenopa-
  thy, and rash resolved without the need for systemic corticosteroids. Complete recovery was observed by hospital day
  17, and the patient was discharged on amoxicillin/clavulanic acid for five days. At a follow-up one month later the pa-
  tient was asymptomatic with no recurrence of rash, and a skin patch test for metroNIDAZOLE 5 months later was neg-
  ative Serum sickness-like reaction: A 16-year-old girl developed nausea, vomiting, and diarrhea while on a mission trip in
  Guatemala and was prescribed ciprofloxacin and secnidazole. After retuning to the United States 4 days later, her
  symptoms persisted and she discontinued ciprofloxacin and secnidazole and was started on metroNIDAZOLE 750 mg
  orally 3 times per day. On day eight of metroNIDAZOLE therapy, the patient developed hives and swelling of the face.
  The next day her symptoms progressed and she developed arthralgia, arthritis, generalized rash, itching and swelling
  of the hands and feet. A stool culture confirmed Cryptosporidium, whereupon metroNIDAZOLE was discontinued and
  nitazoxanide was started. Due to the patient''s severe symptoms, she was brought to the emergency department and

received methylprednisolone and diphenhydramine. The patient''s symptoms resolved throughout her stay and she
  was discharged after a 4-day hospital stay. The patient was challenged with ciprofloxacin 40 days after discharge. Upon
  completion of the course and no signs or symptoms of a reaction, ciprofloxacin was ruled out as the cause of the
  serum sickness-like reaction (SSLR) the patient experienced before. An evaluation using the Naranjo probability scale
  for adverse drug reactions provided a score of 5, indicating that metroNIDAZOLE was the probable cause of SSLR Black Box Warning
  Potential for Carcinogenicity

  MetroNIDAZOLE has been shown to be carcinogenic in mice and rats. Unnecessary use of the drug should be avoided.
  Its use should be reserved for the conditions described in the Indications and Usage section of the package insert
