   trointestinal disorders Monitor patients for polyomavirus associated nephropathy and cytomegalovirus infections, in-
   cluding identification of at risk patients Monitor patients with prior hepatitis B virus (HBV) or hepatitis C virus (HCV) infection closely
   for clinical signs of active hepatitis B or C or reactivation of either HBV or HCV Carefully observe renal posttransplant patients with delayed renal graft function for increased
   adverse events Therapeutic Drug Monitoring
   Controversies exist regarding the utility of mycophenolate therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)
   for graft outcome and patient survival. General use of TDM is not recommended based on the
   limited data correlating MPA exposure with efficacy and/or toxicity. TDM may be useful in the
   following situations: dual immunosuppressive therapy, reduced-dosage CNI therapy, CNI
   switch or withdrawal, transplant recipients with high immunologic risk, delayed graft function
   (renal, hepatic, bowel), altered gastrointestinal/hepatic/renal function, cystic fibrosis, drug in-
   teractions, and noncompliance Monitoring of mycophenolate levels is recommended in kid-
   ney transplant recipients The preferred parameter for TDM is MPA-AUC, which is most
   strongly correlated with graft rejection 14,10,68 . MPA target exposure used by most transplant
   centers is 30 to 60 mg x hr/L 5 ,10,14,68 . Because AUC determination is not feasible in most clini-
   cal settings, however, limited sampling strategies for monitoring MPA have been developed.
   These limited sampling strategies involve obtaining mycophenolate concentrations at 3 time
   points during the first 2 hours after dosing. The equations to predict the MPA-AUC are as fol-
   lows 10 , 14 :
   Concomitant Cyclosporine: AUC0–12 = 6 + 3 (C0) + 54 (C0.5) + 15 (C2). This equation
   has been validated with a low prediction error of 7%.

Concomitant Tacrolimus or no Calcineurin Inhibitor: AUC0–12 = 10 + 95 (C0) + 24 (C0.5)
   + 01 (C2)

   where, C0= trough concentration; C0.5= concentration 30 minutes postdose; C2= concentra-
   tion 2 hours postdose
   Nephrotic Syndrome: Monitor AUC during treatment to target an AUC greater than 50 mcg x
   hr/mL. MPA AUCs greater than 50 mcg x hr/mL may help reduce relapse rates 21

   Trough Concentrations

   A pre-dose trough level is of limited use for mycophenolate therapeutic drug monitoring (TDM)
   due to the variability of the second MPA peak level. Trough levels, however, may be useful to
   detect changes in drug exposure over time. A therapeutic MPA trough level of 0 to 5 mg/L
   (HPLC) 10,14 or 3 to 5 mg/L (EMIT) has been suggested for pediatric patients 14,68 . A target
   MPA trough level of greater than 2 to 3 mg/L has been suggested in heart transplant recipients
   (adult data) when mycophenolate is combined with tacrolimus. In 1 study (n=44) of pediatric
   heart transplant recipients, a target MPA trough level greater than 3 mg/L (EMIT) was used', 'then once a month through the first year of therapy Monitor for neutropenia (absolute neutrophil count less than 3 x 10 3/mcL) 14/27
