OXcarbazepine. In vitro studies indicate an MHD-induced blockade of voltage-sensitive sodium
channels, resulting in stabilization of hyperexcited neuronal membranes, inhibition of repeti-
prolonged to 19 hours in patients with renal impairment (CrCl less than 30 mL/min) Drug Interaction: A pharmacokinetic study of drug interactions with OXcarbazepine revealed', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: OXcarbazepine is rapidly reduced to its active metabolite, 10-monohy-
   droxy derivative (MHD), which is primarily responsible for the therapeutic effects of
   OXcarbazepine. In vitro studies indicate an MHD-induced blockade of voltage-sensitive sodium
   channels, resulting in stabilization of hyperexcited neuronal membranes, inhibition of repeti-
   tive neuronal discharges, and diminution of propagation of synaptic impulses. Approximately
   40% of MHD is bound to serum proteins, predominantly albumin. Rapidly metabolized in the
   liver. More than 95% of immediate-release OXcarbazepine appears in urine, with less than 1%
   as unchanged drug and less than 4% appears in feces 3 ,1 .

   Absorption: Food does not affect the rate or extent of absorption of the immediate-release
   formulation of OXcarbazepine; however, single-dose of extended-release 600 mg tablet with a
   high fat meal (800 to 1000 calories) produced peak plasma concentrations of MHD approxi-
   mately 60% higher and occurred 2 hours earlier compared with administration under fasted
   conditions, therefore, extended-release formulation should be given on an empty stomach AUC: The younger and lower in weight, the faster the weight-adjusted clearance for MHD. Due
   to increased clearance, in children age 2 to less than 4 years, the AUC of MHD is about one-half
   that of adults receiving a similar weight-adjusted dose. In children age 4 to 12 years receiving a
   similar weight-adjusted dose, the MHD AUC is about three-quarters that of adults. The weight-
   adjusted MHD clearance in children 13 years and older is expected to reach that of adults Half-life: Half-life of OXcarbazepine is approximately 2 hours for immediate-release, 7 to 11
   hours for extended-release, and 9 to 11 hours for MHD The half-life of OXcarbazepine is
   prolonged to 19 hours in patients with renal impairment (CrCl less than 30 mL/min) Drug Interaction: A pharmacokinetic study of drug interactions with OXcarbazepine revealed
   that carbamazepine, phenobarbital and phenytoin increase the apparent clearance of MHD by
   31% to 35%; clearance was even higher (by 74% to 80%) when a combination of 2 of these
   drugs were used. Levels of carbamazepine were lower (15%) and phenobarbital levels were
   higher (14%) when used in combination with OXcarbazepine; there was a trend toward lower
   levels for lamotrigine as well'),
