   take the missed dose and resume with the next scheduled dose', 'Common, any dose, age 10 to 93 years: Nasopharyngitis (8.3%), arthralgia (6.9%), diarrhea
   (6.8%), pain in extremity (6%), and urinary tract infection (5.7%) 1

The safety and tolerability profile of atorvastatin 10 to 20 mg daily, as an adjunct to diet to re-
   duce total cholesterol, LDL-C, and apo B levels, was generally similar to that of placebo in a 26-
   week controlled study of pediatric patients age 10 to 17 years Statins were well tolerated and adverse events were mostly unrelated to statin therapy in a
   meta-analysis of 9 studies in 1191 pediatric patients (mean age, 3 +/- 5 years; mean BMI, 3 +/- 7 kg/m(2) based on 6 studies) with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.
   Treatment-related adverse events were reported in 0% to 15%. Myalgia that resolved during
   continuation of treatment was reported in 2 studies (2.7% of patients receiving rosuvastatin
   and 1% receiving pitavastatin). There were no reports of life-threatening adverse events, and
   permanent discontinuation due to adverse events was 0% in 4 studies and 7% to 9% in 3
   studies. There was no significant difference compared with placebo in transaminase and crea-
   tine kinase levels, and there was no effect on growth or sexual development Long-term safety of statins in children, especially with regard to growth and sexual develop-
   ment, is unknown Monitoring
