azaTHIOprine is an imidazolyl derivative of 6-mercaptopurine and acts as an immunosuppres-
   sive antimetabolite. Well absorbed following oral administration. Protein binding is 30%.
   Extensively and rapidly metabolized to 6-mercaptopurine (6-MP). Further metabolism occurs
   through 3 competitive pathways, one of which is an activation pathway 25,5 ,20 . Once activated,
   6-MP forms 6-thioguanine nucleotides (6-TGN), which mediate immunosuppression through
   inhibition of lymphocyte proliferation; the incorporation of 6-TGN into cellular DNA causes cy-
   totoxicity The mean time to 90% steady-state accumulation for 6-TGN is 8 days, which
   may explain the delayed therapeutic response seen with azaTHIOprine One of the 2 inactivation pathways, by S-methyltransferase (TPMT), demonstrates genetic poly-
   morphism; approximately 10% of Caucasians and African-Americans are heterozygous and
   have intermediate TPMT activity, while 3% are homozygous and have low to no TPMT activity.
   Relative or absolute deficiency of this enzyme increases the risk of azaTHIOprine toxicity at
   usual doses; measurement of TPMT activity can identify those at risk. The other inactivation
   pathway, by xanthine oxidase, is inhibited by allopurinol 25,5 ,20 .'),
