   Literature
   • In a Cochrane Review that included 14 studies on azithromycin use for cystic fibrosis
   (N=1467, adults and pediatrics), it was reported that azithromycin improves forced expiratory
   volume (FEV1) predicted compared to placebo at 6 months (3.97% mean difference, 95% CI 74 to 19), although no difference was detected at 3 months (2.7% mean difference, 95% CI
   -0.12 to 52) or 12 months (-0.13% difference, 95% CI -4.96 to 7). Pulmonary exacerbations
   were reportedly lower in patients receiving azithromycin at 1, 3, 6, and 12 months. In addition,
   all studies found a longer time to pulmonary exacerbation in the azithromycin group com-
   pared to the placebo group (HR 61, 95% CI 5 to 75). Mild adverse events were common
   amongst both groups; nausea and wheezing were the most common adverse effect reported
   with azithromycin • In the COMBAT CF trial, azithromycin was ineffective at reducing structural lung damage, as
   assessed on chest computed tomography (CT) scans, in children up to 36 months old when
   starting therapy at 3 to 6 months old (N=130). At 36 months, 88% of the azithromycin group
   and 94% of the placebo group had bronchiectasis (OR 49, 95% CI 12 to 0) and total airway
   diseases did not differ between groups (-0.02% difference, 95% CI -0.59 to 56). However, chil-
   dren treated with azithromycin spent less time in the hospital for pulmonary exacerbations
   each year (mean difference -6.3 days), had fewer days of IV antibiotics each year (0.2 days for
   azithromycin vs 7 days for placebo, per year), and fewer courses of inhaled or oral antibiotics
   each year. The effect of azithromycin on pulmonary exacerbations was only significant in the
   first year of life The authors of the study suggested the absence of differences between
   treatment groups on CT scan may have been due to the relative insensitivity of the scoring al-
   gorithm used (Perth-Rotterdam Annotated Grid Morphometric for Cystic Fibrosis) to quantify
   small differences between individuals in lung structure. A post-hoc analysis of the COMBAT-CF

CT scans (n=228 scans) using two artificial intelligence methods to measure bronchus and
   artery dimensions, as well as low attenuation regions indicating trapped air, found that
   azithromycin-treated infants had a significant reduction in bronchial wall thickness at 36
   months compared to the placebo group. There was also a significant reduction in arterial di-
   ameter in the azithromycin group, indicating a positive effect of azithromycin on small airway
   disease and associated lung perfusion. There was no significant difference in low attenuation
   regions at 36 months between the two groups • The time to pulmonary exacerbation was reduced by 44% (HR, 56; 95% CI, 37 to 83) with
   azithromycin compared with placebo in children (6 months to 18 years) with cystic fibrosis and
   early Pseudomonas aeruginosa infection in a randomized trial (n=221). All children were
   treated with standard tobramycin inhalation solution. The number of patients who experi-
   enced at least 1 exacerbation was no different between azithromycin (39.1%) and placebo
   (52.3%) for a difference of -13.2% (95% CI, -25.7% to 0%) over a median follow-up time of 8
   months. Time to Pseudomonas aeruginosa recurrence was also not different (HR, 1; 95% CI, 64 to 55). Patient weight increased by 27 kg more for azithromycin compared with
   placebo; no improvements were observed in linear growth or lung function. No temporary or
   permanent sensorineural hearing loss was observed. Abnormal electrocardiograms were no
   different between the azithromycin group (0.9%) and placebo group (6.3%). Azithromycin
