   Cystic fibrosis;M. abscessusinfection: Recommendations for treatment of M. abscessus infec-
   tions in cystic fibrosis patients include the completion of an intensive phase of therapy follow-
   ing by a continuation phase. For the intensive phase, azithromycin is recommended as the pre-
   ferred macrolide antibiotic in combination with amikacin and one or more of the following:
   tigecycline, imipenem, or cefoxitin. The duration of the intensive phase of treatment is 3 to 12
   weeks and is determined by the severity of the infection, the response to treatment, and the
   tolerability of the regimen. Following the completion of the intensive phase, antimicrobials for
   the continuation phase include oral azithromycin and inhaled amikacin in combination with 2
   to 3 of the following oral antibiotics: minocycline, clofazimine, moxifloxacin, and linezolid. The
   optimal duration of therapy in this patient population is unknown, but is recommended that
   treatment be continued until repeat sputum cultures are negative for 12 months. Patients who
   do not achieve negative cultures while on therapy may still benefit from continuing or repeat-
   ing courses of treatment Cystic fibrosis;M. avium complex infection: Recommendations for treatment of clar-
   ithromycin-sensitive M. avium complex include a daily antibiotic regimen with azithromycin, ri-
   fAMPin, and ethambutol. Patients with a high bacterial load may benefit from an initial course
   of IV amikacin or streptomycin for 1 to 3 months in addition to the three-drug regimen. The
   optimal duration of therapy in this patient population is unknown, but is recommended that
   treatment be continued until repeat sputum cultures are negative for 12 months. Patients who
   do not achieve negative cultures while on therapy may still benefit from continuing or repeat-
   ing courses of treatment Hematopoietic stem cell and solid organ transplant recipients: Azithromycin is a preferred
   antimicrobial as apart of a multi-drug regimen in the treatment of nontuberculous mycobacte-
   rial infections in solid organ transplant patients due to M. avium complex, M. kansasii, M. mar-
   inum, M. haemophilum, M. xenopi, M. abscessus, or M. chelonae. M. fortuitum isolates contain an
   inducible erythromycin methylase gene; use macrolides with caution. Therapy with two drugs
   is standard. Three or more agents may be required when the infection is life-threatening, the
   organism burden is high, or the infection is with a rapid growing mycobacteria (M. fortuitum, M.
   chelonae, or M. abscessus) prior to species identification and susceptibility testing. No con-

trolled trials are available in this patient population to guide treatment duration but it is gener-
   ally continued for months to years. Treatment duration is shortest for cutaneous infections
   with M. marinum and longest for lung infections with any species. For pulmonary infections,
   treatment should be continued until repeat sputum cultures are negative for 12 months; how-
   ever, a fixed duration of 12 months may be adequate for M. kansasii infections rather than 12
   months after culture conversion 47,12 .

