   with the use of macrolides than with the use of amoxicillin and amoxicillin-clavulanate (RR 31; 95% CI, 07 to 6) Amoxicillin verus other antibiotic agents: In a nationwide cohort study of pediatric patients
   6 months to 12 years of age (N=1,051,007) with uncomplicated AOM, the combined outcome
   of treatment failure (defined as another antibiotic filled within 2 to 14 days) and recurrence
   (defined as another antibiotic filled within 15 to 30 days) was lower with amoxicillin (1.7%)
   compared with amoxicillin-clavulanate (11.3%), cefdinir (10%), or azithromycin (9.8%).
   Treatment failure or recurrence occurred in 5% of children overall and occurred more often
   in children 6 months to less than 2 years of age. Amoxicillin (56.6%) was the most frequently
   prescribed antibiotic, followed by cefdinir (20.6%), amoxicillin-clavulanate (13.5%), and
   azithromycin (9.3%). Long durations of antibiotics (10 days) were prescribed to 93% of patients,

but the odds of treatment failure or recurrence remained low for children who were pre-
   scribed either a short (5 to 7 days) or long duration of antibiotics Otitis media with effusion: Antibiotics are not recommended for asymptomatic otitis media
   with effusion Pertussis; treatment and postexposure prophylaxis: Macrolide antibiotics are the pre-
   ferred agents for patients 1 month or older. Trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole is an alternative
   for patients 2 months or older. Postexposure prophylaxis should be offered based on the in-
   fectiousness of the index patient and the intensity of the exposure, the potential conse-
   quences of severe pertussis in the contact, and the possibility for secondary exposure of per-
   sons at high risk. Postexposure prophylaxis is indicated for infants less than 12 months of age
   and for pregnant women in their third trimester. Prophylaxis should be started within 21 days
   of onset of cough in the index patient Post-exposure prophylaxis against pertussis in
   hematopoietic cell transplant patients should be provided regardless of vaccination status Pneumonia, community-acquired (CAP): Azithromycin is the preferred antibiotic for empiric
   therapy in outpatients and inpatients with presumed atypical pneumonia. Azithromycin is the
   preferred intravenous antibiotic for serious CAP (hospitalized) in patients 3 months and older
   with suspected or confirmed M pneumoniae, Chlamydia trachomatis or Chlamydophila pneumo-
