   mechanisms of action is recommended to optimize efficacy and to reduce the risk of antibiotic
   resistance. Topical agents with strong recommendations include topical retinoids, benzoyl per-
   oxide, and topical antibiotics. Agents with conditional recommendations included clas-
   coterone, salicylic acid, and azelaic acid. A conditional recommendation is made for clas-
   coterone based on high current cost of treatment that may impact equitable acne treatment
   access In 2 randomized controlled trials (N=1440), in patients aged 9 years or older with moderate to
   severe facial acne, clascoterone cream was associated with significantly higher treatment suc-
   cess compared with vehicle cream. Patients treated with clascoterone cream achieved
   Investigator Global Assessment (IGA) success (a 2-point reduction in IGA compared to baseline
   and an IGA score of clear or almost clear) compared with vehicle cream (Study 1, 4% vs 9%;
   Study 2, 3% vs 5%) at week There was a significant reduction in absolute noninflam-
   matory lesions from baseline to -19.4 with clascoterone treatment in both studies compared
   with -13 and -10.8 with vehicle, respectively, as well as a significant reduction in inflammatory
   lesions from baseline to -19.3 and -20 vs -15.5 and -12.6 with vehicle, respectively.

Approximately 83% of patients had an IGA score of 3 (moderate), a mean inflammatory lesion
   count of 4, and a mean non-inflammatory lesion count of 4 at baseline. Adverse event
