   Black Box Warning
   Hematologic Malignancy
   Hematologic malignancy, including life-threatening cases of myelodysplastic syndrome, has oc-
   curred in patients treated with elivaldogene autotemcel. Patients have been diagnosed be-

tween 14 months and 5 years after elivaldogene autotemcel administration, and the cancers
   appear to be the result of the elivaldogene autotemcel lentiviral vector, Lenti-D, integration in
   proto-oncogenes. Monitor patients closely for evidence of malignancy through complete blood
   counts at least every 6 months and through assessments for evidence for clonal expansion or
   predominance at least twice in the first year and annually thereafter; consider bone marrow
   evaluations as clinically indicated Monitoring
   Toxic Laboratory Monitoring
   • Consider baseline hematologic testing (eg, CBC with differential, hematopathology review of
   peripheral blood smear, bone marrow biopsy [core and aspirate] with flow cytometry, conven-
   tional karyotyping, and next generation sequencing with a molecular panel appropriate for age
   and including coverage for gene mutations) and testing for germline mutations that are associ-
   ated with hematologic malignancy • Monitor patients life-long and closely for evidence of hematologic malignancy through CBC
   with differential at least every 6 months and through assessments for evidence for evidence
   for clonal expansion or predominance at least twice a year in the first year and annually there-
   after for the first 15 years after treatment; consider bone marrow evaluations as clinically indi-
   cated. If hematologic malignancy is detected, contact bluebird bio at 1-833-999-6378 for re-
   porting and to obtain instructions on collection of samples for further testing • Consider additional testing (eg, more frequent CBC with differential, integration site analysis,
   bone marrow studies, and gene expression studies) and expert consultation in patients with
   delayed or failed engraftment of platelets or other cell lines; or new or prolonged cytopenias;
   or the presence of clonal expansion or predominance for evidence of hematologic malignancy • Monitor blood counts for prolonged cytopenias until normalization Monitor for thrombocytopenia according to standard guidelines • Monitor platelet count frequently until platelet engraftment and recovery are achieved • Perform blood cell count determination and other appropriate testing whenever clinical
   symptoms suggestive of bleeding arise • Monitor neutrophil count until engraftment has been achieved • Screen for hepatitis B virus, hepatitis C virus, HIV 1 and 2, and Human T-lymphotropic virus 1
   and 2 in accordance with clinical guidelines before collection of cells for manufacturing 5/8
• Assess patients for hepatic impairment to ensure hematopoietic stem cell transplantation
