liver by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 isoenzymes. Polymorphism may be displayed with the CYP2C19', 'Pharmacology
   Esomeprazole is the S-isomer of omeprazole (racemic mixture of both R- and S- isomers),
   which inhibits gastric acid secretion by inhibition of hydrogen-potassium ATPase, the enzyme
   responsible for the final step in the secretion of hydrochloric acid by the gastric parietal cell
   ("proton pump"). Inhibition of gastric acid secretion is dose-related up to 20 to 40 mg daily.
   Antisecretory activity persists for greater than 24 hours 9 ,7 . Maximum effect may take 3 to 5
   days to achieve Bioavailability is approximately 90%; food decreases bioavailability com-
   pared with fasting state Protein binding is approximately 97%. 9 ,7 . Following oral administra-
   tion, peak concentrations are reached at approximately 3 hours in infants less than 1 year of
   age 9 , 5 to 2 hours in older infants and children 40 , and 8 to 2 hours in adolescents Cmax and AUC increase proportionally as dose increases 40 , 41 , although the magnitude and
   variability are less in infants older than 12 months of age Extensively metabolized in the
   liver by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 isoenzymes. Polymorphism may be displayed with the CYP2C19
   isoenzyme leading to poor metabolizers of the drug. Excreted in the urine as unchanged drug
   (less than 1%) and inactive metabolites (80%); some inactive metabolites are also eliminated in
   the feces 9 ,7 . Following repeated daily oral doses, mean half-life is approximately 7 to 9
   hours in infants and children 13 , 40 , and 8 to 2 hours in adolescents 41 ; half-life appears to
   be dose- dependent across all age groups 13,41,40 . . In adults, the elimination half-life of intra-
   venous esomeprazole is approximately 1 to 4 hours and is similarly prolonged with in-
   creasing dose 8/13'),
