   z', 'Gaucher Disease, Type 1
   2 years of age or older: Initial, 30 to 60 unit/kg IV once every 2 weeks. Usual maintenance
   dose, 15 to 60 unit/kg IV once every 2 weeks, after having established an initial satisfactory
   clinical response 1 ,2 .', 'Long-term enzyme replacement therapy for patients 2 years of age and older with confirmed
   Type 1 Gaucher disease that results in anemia, thrombocytopenia, bone disease, hep-
   atomegaly, and/or splenomegaly Administration
   Administer by IV infusion over 1 to 2 hours. If slight flocculation (thin translucent fibers) ap-
   pears after dilution, solution may be filtered through an in-line low protein-binding 2 micron
   filter during administration. Individual infusion doses may be slightly increased or decreased
   to avoid discarding partially used vials; however, monthly administered dosage should not be
   substantially altered', 'The most common adverse events reported in children 2 to 12 years of age included dyspnea,
   fever, nausea, flushing, vomiting, and coughing. The most common adverse events reported in
   adolescents older than 12 years of age included headache, pruritus, and rash Approximately
   5% of patients have histamine-mediated symptoms, which can be managed by decreasing the
   infusion rate or administration of an antihistamine and/or glucocorticoid Monitoring
   Baseline assessments include laboratory parameters (hemoglobin, platelet count, acid phos-
   phatase, angiotensin convertin enzyme, chitotriosidase), organ volume parameters (liver and
   spleen), and skeletal parameters (radiography of entire femora, DEXA scan of spine and hips),
   and quality of life. Monitor laboratory parameters every 3 months and with dosage changes.
   Monitor organ volumes and skeletal parameters every 12 months and with dosage changes.
   Frequency of monitoring may be decreased in patients who have reached clinical goals and
   are without change in dose. Quality of life should be assessed every 6 months 3/5
Periodically monitor for IgG antibody formation during first year of treatment. Evaluate pa-
   tients having respiratory symptoms in the absence of fever for pulmonary hypertension', 'Periodically monitor for IgG antibody formation during first year of treatment. Evaluate pa-', 'Pharmacology
   Gaucher disease is characterized by a deficiency of beta-glucocerebrosidase activity, resulting
   in accumulation of glucocerebroside with clinical manifestations of severe anemia, thrombocy-
   topenia, progressive hepatospenomegaly, bone disease (eg, marrow infiltration, chronic pain,
   periodic painful bone crises, osteonecrosis, osteopenia/osteoporosis, pathologic fractures),
   growth retardation, and delayed puberty 1 ,3 . Imiglucerase is an analogue of the human en-
   zyme beta-glucocerebrosidase, produced by recombinant DNA using mammalian cell culture
   (Chinese hamster ovary), and differs from placental glucocerebrosidase by one amino acid at
   position 495 where histidine is substituted for arginine. It is a lysosomal glycoprotein that cat-
   alyzes the hydrolytic cleavage of the glycolipid glucocerebroside, which allows for normal
   metabolic clearance of glucose and ceramide. Steady-state enzymatic activity is achieved by 30
   minutes during 1-hour IV infusions of imiglucerase 5, 15, 30, and 60 unit/kg. Following infu-
   sion, Vd corrected for weight ranges from 09 to 15 L/kg (0.12 +/- 02 L/kg) , plasma clear-
   ance ranges from 8 to 3 mL/min/kg (mean, 5 +/- 4 mL/min/kg), and half-life ranges
   from 6 to 4 minutes'),
