   • Stable for up to 224 days at 23 to 25°C.', 'Similar adverse events have been reported in pediatric patients compared with adults. Hepatic
   toxicity, including fatal cases, has been reported in pediatric patients with juvenile rheumatoid
   arthritis. Severe anaphylactic reactions, including fatalities, have been reported. Should not be
   used in patients with a previous reaction to aspirin or other NSAIDs. Renal toxicity, hyper-
   kalemia, fluid retention/edema, and hypertension have been reported. May cause serious skin
   reactions such as exfoliative dermatitis, Stevens-Johnson syndrome, and toxic epidermal
   necrolysis, which can be fatal. Visual effects including corneal deposits and retinal distur-
   bances, including those of the macular, have occurred. Blurred vision may result from these
   visual disturbances. Elevated liver tests may occur in up to 15% of patients. Anemia has been
   reported. May aggravate CNS conditions such as depression or other psychiatric disturbances,
   epilepsy, and Parkinsonism. Nausea with or without vomiting, dyspepsia, headache, and dizzi-
   ness are common Black Box Warning
   NSAIDs cause an increased risk of serious cardiovascular thrombotic events, including myocar-
   dial infarction and stroke, which can be fatal. This risk may occur early in treatment and may
   increase with duration of use. Indomethacin is contraindicated in the setting of CABG surgery.
   NSAIDs can also cause an increased risk of serious gastrointestinal (GI) adverse events, includ-
   ing bleeding, ulceration, and perforation of the stomach or intestines, which can be fatal.
   These events can occur at any time during use and without warning symptoms. Elderly pa-
   tients and patients with a prior history of peptic ulcer disease and/or GI bleeding are at a
   greater risk for serious GI events 21,27 .

   Monitoring
   Assess sign/symptoms of gastrointestinal bleeding. Monitor blood pressure closely in hyper-
   tensive patients during initiation and throughout therapy. Monitor renal function and serum
   electrolytes during therapy. Periodic ophthalmic exams should be performed in patients on
   long-term therapy to assess for any visual disturbances (corneal deposits and retinal distur-
   bances). CBC (including platelets) and liver function tests should be obtained periodically', 'tipyretic effects by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandin. Decreases cerebral, renal, and', 'Pharmacology

Mechanism of action: Indomethacin is a nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drug that has an un-
   known mechanism of action. However, the drug exerts antiinflammatory, analgesic, and an-
   tipyretic effects by inhibiting the synthesis of prostaglandin. Decreases cerebral, renal, and
   gastrointestinal blood flow.

   Oral capsules completely bioavailable with 90% absorbed within 4 hours. Peak concentration
   reached at 2 hours. Suspension bioequivalent to capsules. Absorption rate faster with supposi-
   tories, so similar bioavailability is expected. Protein binding is approximately 99%. Metabolized
   in the liver to inactive compounds and excreted in the urine (60%; 26% as indomethacin and its
   glucuronide) and feces (33%; 5% unchanged drug). Serum half-life is approximately 5 hours'),
