        tamine, methylergometrine (methylergonovine)), irinotecan, lurasidone, oral midazolam, pi-
        mozide, triazolam, felodipine, nisoldipine, ivabradine, ranolazine, eplerenone, cisapride,
        naloxegol, lomitapide, lovastatin, simvastatin, avanafil, ticagrelor, finerenone, voclosporin.
        In addition, coadministration with colchicine, fesoterodine and solifenacin is contraindicated
        in subjects with varying degrees of renal or hepatic impairment, and coadministration with
        eliglustat is contraindicated in subjects that are poor or intermediate metabolizers of
        CYP2D6 and in subjects taking strong or moderate CYP2D6 inhibitors. Coadministration
        with venetoclax is contraindicated in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)/small
        lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL) during the dose initiation and ramp-up phase of venetoclax.
        Coadministration with itraconazole can cause elevated plasma concentrations of these
        drugs and may increase or prolong both the pharmacologic effects and/or adverse reac-
        tions to these drugs. For example, increased plasma concentrations of some of these drugs
        can lead to QT prolongation and ventricular tachyarrhythmias including occurrences of tor-
        sades de pointes, a potentially fatal arrhythmia.

   Solution
   Congestive Heart Failure and Cardiac Effects: If signs or symptoms of congestive heart failure oc-
   cur during administration of itraconazole oral capsules, discontinue administration. When itra-
   conazole was administered intravenously to dogs and healthy human volunteers, negative in-
   otropic effects were seen.
   Drug Interactions: Coadministration of a number of CYP3A4 substrates are contraindicated with
   itraconazole oral solution: Methadone, disopyramide, dofetilide, dronedarone, quinidine,
   isavuconazole, ergot alkaloids (such as dihydroergotamine, ergometrine (ergonovine), ergo-
   tamine, methylergometrine (methylergonovine)), irinotecan, lurasidone, oral midazolam, pi-
   mozide, triazolam, felodipine, nisoldipine, ivabradine, ranolazine, eplerenone, cisapride, nalox-

egol, lomitapide, lovastatin, simvastatin, avanafil, ticagrelor, finerenone, voclosporin.
   Coadministration with colchicine, fesoterodine and solifenacin is contraindicated in subjects
   with varying degrees of renal or hepatic impairment. Coadministration with eliglustat is con-
   traindicated in subjects that are poor or intermediate metabolizers of CYP2D6 and in subjects
   taking strong or moderate CYP2D6 inhibitors. Coadministration with venetoclax is contraindi-
   cated in patients with chronic lymphocytic leukemia (CLL)/small lymphocytic lymphoma (SLL)
   during the dose initiation and ramp-up phase of venetoclax. Coadministration with itracona-
   zole can cause elevated plasma concentrations of these drugs and may increase or prolong
   both the pharmacologic effects and/or adverse reactions to these drugs. For example, in-
   creased plasma concentrations of some of these drugs can lead to QT prolongation and ven-
   tricular tachyarrhythmias including occurrences of torsades de pointes, a potentially fatal ar-
   rhythmia Monitoring
   Monitor hepatic enzyme levels before therapy is started, then at least at 1, 2, and 4 weeks, and
   then every 3 months during therapy Discontinue therapy if hepatotoxicity is suspected.
   Evaluate patients for signs and symptoms of congestive heart failure, especially in patients
   with preexisting risk factors Evaluate fungal cultures and other relevant laboratory evidence
   (wet mount, histopathology, serology) prior to itraconazole therapy initiation to identify fungal
   organism Optimal serum itraconazole concentrations are unknown, but trough concentrations greater
   than 5 mcg/mL 40 and random or trough concentrations greater than 1 mcg/mL 4 ,5 have
   been recommended. At least 1 to 2 weeks of itraconazole therapy are recommended before
   measuring serum concentrations in patients with blastomycosis, histoplasmosis, and sporotri-
   chosis 7 , 4 , 5 and within the first 4 to 7 days after starting itraconazole when treating aspergillo-
   sis In bone marrow transplantation patients or cystic fibrosis patients, trough concentra-
   tions greater than 5 mg/L (0.5 mcg/mL) but less than 2 mg/L (2 mcg/mL) can be achieved with
   5 mg/kg/day divided every 12 hours for treatment and prophylaxis For the treatment of histoplasmosis in children, monitor antigen levels during and for 12
   months after therapy for relapse; persistent low-level antigenuria may not be a reason to pro-
   long treatment in patients who have completed appropriate therapy and have no evidence of
   active infection For candidemia, monitor blood cultures daily or every other day until Candida is cleared 21/27
and capsules in pediatric patients Metabolism is predominately by the CYP3A4 system, and
there are many drug interactions with itraconazole involving this enzyme The pharmacoki-', 'Pharmacology
   Itraconazole is a synthetic triazole antifungal agent that exerts its effect by blocking the C-
   demethylation step of CYP-dependent synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal
   cell membrane 1 ,2 . The capsule formulation depends on an acidic environment for optimal ab-
   sorption. In patients with relative or absolute achlorhydria, the AUC increased by 75% when
   the capsule was administered with cola The absorption of the solution is optimal under fast-
   ing conditions The oral bioavailability of the oral solution was up to 37% 48 to 60% 10 higher
   than the capsule, whereas, other pharmacokinetic parameters were similar between solution
   and capsules in pediatric patients Metabolism is predominately by the CYP3A4 system, and
   there are many drug interactions with itraconazole involving this enzyme The pharmacoki-
   netics in pediatrics, 5 years and older, are similar to adults. Nonlinear disposition with half-
   lives of 59 hours and 104 hours for itraconazole and 56 hours and 169 hours for its active me-
   tabolite, hydroxyitraconazole, at 5 mg/kg/dose orally once daily and 5 mg/kg/dose orally
   twice daily dosing, respectively, were demonstrated in pediatric patients. There were no phar-
   macokinetic differences between the 5 to less than 13 years age group and the 13 to 18 years
   age group There are a lack of pharmacokinetic data in children less than 2 years old, but
   one study demonstrated that 6 months to 2 years of age children tend to have more frequent
   low Cmin compared with older children when receiving 5 mg/kg/dose once daily In neu-
   tropenic children (2 to 12 years of age) receiving 5 mg/kg/dose every 12 hours of itraconazole
   oral solution for prophylaxis, trough concentrations of greater than 5 mcg/mL were achieved
   within 5 days in 6 to 12 years of age children and 15 days in 2 to 5 years of age children and
   trough concentrations of greater than 25 mcg/mL within 3 days in 6 to 12 years of age chil-
   dren and 5 days in 2 to 5 years of age children In cystic fibrosis and bone marrow trans-
   plantation pediatric patients, the bioavailability of the oral solution was 2-fold greater than the
   oral capsules. The study also demonstrated that 5 mg/kg/dose every 12 hours of the oral solu-
   tion would be needed to achieve a target Cmin of 5 mg/L (0.5 mcg/mL) in cystic fibrosis and
   bone marrow transplantation pediatric patients'),
