   mellitus.', 'muscle relaxation due to myosin phosphorylation inhibition and reduced intracellular ionic cal-', 'Pharmacology
   Levalbuterol tartrate is a relatively selective beta2-adrenergic receptor agonist which leads to
   adenylate cyclase activation and an increase in cyclic-3'', 5''-adenosine monophosphate (cyclic
   AMP) intracellular concentration upon beta2-adrenergic receptor activation on airway smooth
   muscle. Increase in cyclic AMP is associated with protein kinase A activation and subsequent
   muscle relaxation due to myosin phosphorylation inhibition and reduced intracellular ionic cal-
   cium concentrations. Smooth muscles of all airways are relaxed, from the trachea to the termi-
   nal bronchioles. Preferentially metabolized in the GI tract by SULT1 A3 (sulfotransferase), with
   elimination of parent compound and primary metabolites in the urine. Less than 20% of drug
   is detected in the feces 8 ,3 .

