   Five (5)-Breath Dosimeter Dosing Method in Patients 5 Years or Older

   Administer the diluent (the same diluent used to reconstitute the methacholine powder), using
   a 5-breath dosimeter, prior to administering the methacholine dose(s) to obtain post-diluent
   forced expiratory volume in 1 second (FEV1) values Administration of the Diluent to Obtain Post-Diluent FEV1 Value 1
   Using a 3 mL syringe and needle, draw up 2 to 3 mL of 9% saline OR 9% saline with 4%
   phenol and dispense into the nebulizer using a sterile bacterial-retentive filter (porosity 22
   micrometers) ●   The nebulizer should be held upright with the mouthpiece in patient''s mouth. A nose clip
        should be in place while inhaling from the nebulizer.
    ●   At the end of exhalation during tidal breathing, instruct patient to inhale slowly and deeply
        through the mouthpiece. Trigger the dosimeter soon after oral inhalation begins.

●   Have patient continue to inhale slowly (approximately 5 seconds to complete inhalation)
        and hold breath at total lung capacity for another 5 seconds.
    ●   Repeat previous step for a total of 5 inspiratory capacity inhalations, taking no more than 2
        minutes to perform 5 inhalations.
    ●   Perform spirometry and measure the FEV1 30 and 90 seconds after the fifth inhalation from
        the nebulizer to obtain the post-diluent FEV1 values. If the FEV1 value is not of acceptable
        quality, repeat the procedure. If the post-diluent FEV1 falls by 20% or greater from baseline
        FEV1, do not give further inhalations. If the post-diluent FEV1 falls by less than 20% from
        baseline FEV1 proceed with methacholine challenge test.
   Administration of Methacholine Challenge Testing

   Using a 3 mL syringe and needle, draw up the recommended methacholine oral inhalation
   concentration using the quadrupling dose method and dispense into the nebulizer using a
   sterile bacterial-retentive filter (porosity 22 μm) Table 1: Provocholine Concentration 0625 mg/mL (Solution 5) 25 mg/mL (Solution 4)

        1 mg/mL (Solution 3)

        4 mg/mL (Solution 2)

        16 mg/mL (Solution 1)

        Provocholine® Prescribing Information November 2019

    ●   Repeat same steps used for administration of diluent for each methacholine concentration,
        emptying the nebulizer between each concentration.
    ●   To keep the cumulative effect of methacholine relatively constant, the time interval between
        the commencement of 2 subsequent concentrations should be kept to 5 minutes.
    ●   Stop dosing if the FEV1 has fallen by 20% or more from the post-diluent FEV1 or the highest
        methacholine dose (16 mg/mL) has been administered (whichever comes first).
    ●   Do not administer additional methacholine concentrations if severe bronchoconstric-
        tion occurs.

●   After completion of test, administer an inhaled beta-agonist to expedite the return of the
        FEV1 to within 90% of baseline and relieve any discomfort.
    ●   Wait 10 minutes and measure FEV1 and Vital Capacity.
    ●   Patients should not be allowed to leave the laboratory until their FEV1 has returned to
        within 90% of baseline.

   Two (2)-Minute Tidal Breathing Dosing Method in Patients 5 Years or Older
   Administer the diluent (the same diluent used to reconstitute the methacholine powder) prior
   to administering the methacholine doses to obtain post-diluent FEV1 values Administer the diluent and the methacholine oral inhalation dose(s) using the English Wright
   nebulizer or other suitable nebulizers as long as the device output and particle size are charac-
   terized Administration of the Diluent to Obtain Post-Diluent FEV1 Value 1
   Using a 3 mL syringe and needle, draw up 2 to 3 mL of 9% saline OR 9% saline with 4%
   phenol and dispense into the nebulizer using a sterile bacterial-retentive filter (porosity 22
   micrometers) ●   Instruct the patient to relax and breathe the aerosol quietly (tidal breathing) for 2 minutes
        of inhalation time.
    ●   Place the face mask loosely over the nose and mouth or the mouthpiece in the mouth (with
        a nose clip) of the patient.
    ●   Instruct patient to hold nebulizer to avoid warming the solution. Nebulizer should be kept
        upright and vertical.
    ●   Start the nebulizer by adjusting the flow meter so that the nebulizer is operating at the cali-
        brated output (0.13 mL/minute time for the English Wright nebulizer). Start the stopwatch
        immediately.
    ●   After exactly 2 minutes, turn off the flow meter, remove mask (or the mouthpiece from the
        mouth), and discard any remaining solution.
    ●   Perform spirometry and measure the FEV1 30 and 90 seconds after the end of the inhala-
        tion to obtain the post-diluent FEV1. If the FEV1 value is not acceptable, repeat the proce-
        dure. If the post-diluent FEV1 falls by 20% or more from baseline FEV1, do not give further in-
        halations. If the post-diluent FEV1 falls by less than 20% from baseline FEV1 proceed with a
        methacholine challenge test.

