Pharmacokinetic Studies
   A pharmacokinetic study (n=11) in pediatric patients with inflammatory bowel disease demon-
   strated the bioavailability for oral methotrexate was 84%+/- 38% (range 29 to 158) with respect
   to subQ route. The mean AUC (0 to infinity) was 8 +/- 3 micromole x hr/L and 4 +/- 3 mi-
   cromole x hr/L after subcutaneous and oral doses, respectively, of methotrexate 5 to 40
   mg/m2 (median dose 17 mg/m2) The AUC was 38 +/- 65 micromole x hr/L and 99 +/- 34 micromole x hr/L after subQ and oral doses, respectively, of methotrexate 5 mg/m2
   twice weekly and 22 +/- 9 micromole x hr/L and 93 +/- 88 micromole x hr/L after subQ and
   oral doses, respectively, of 40 mg/m2 twice weekly when administered to 6 children with acute
   lymphoblastic leukemia A pharmacokinetic study in 13 pediatric patients with rheumatoid
   arthritis demonstrated that dose normalized AUC increased with age (p=0.03; τ=0.44) and was
   lower than that in adults. The dose normalized AUC of its metabolite (7-OHMTX) was constant
   with age. Children require higher doses of methotrexate compared with adults, possibly due
   to more extensive metabolism and/or lower bioavailability compared with adults'),
