   sessed by blinded independent central review and partial response was defined as a 20% or
   greater reduction in PN volume by volumetric MRI analysis. The median time to onset of re-
   sponse was 9 months (range, 1 to 8 months). Duration of response (DOR) of at least 12
   months was reported in 90% of pediatric patients and DOR of at least 24 months was reported
   in 48%. Median age of the pediatric population was 10 years (range, 2 to 17 years); dosage was
   mirdametinib 2 mg/m(2) orally twice daily for the first 21 days of each 28-day cycle until dis-
   ease progression or unacceptable toxicity. FDA Approved Pediatric Indication

   Mirdametinib is indicated for the treatment of adult and pediatric patients 2 years or older
   with neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1) who have symptomatic plexiform neurofibromas (PN) not
   amenable to complete resection 1

   Administration
   • Swallow capsule whole with or without food; swallow 1 capsule at a time. Do not open, break,
   or chew. Do not administer to patients unable to swallow whole • Swallow intact tablets whole with or without food; swallow 1 tablet at a time. For patients un-
   able to swallow whole tablets, prepare tablets for oral suspension • Administer oral suspension immediately from a dosing cup or oral syringe. Add an additional
   5 to 10 mL of drinking water to resuspend any remaining particles and administer to ensure
   entire dose is taken. Discard suspension if not administered within 30 minutes of preparation • Missed dose: Do not take an additional dose; take the next scheduled dose at the prescribed
   time • Vomiting: Do not take an additional dose if vomiting occurs after administration. Take the
   next scheduled dose at the prescribed time Preparation, tablet for oral suspension

   • For suspension, place prescribed number of tablets in a dosing cup containing 5 to 10 mL of
   drinking water and gently swirl water and tablets until fully dispersed. After approximately 2 to
   4 minutes, a white, cloudy suspension should form 4/7', 'Common, pediatric (greater than 25%): Rash, diarrhea, musculoskeletal pain, abdominal
