   Common: Electrocardiographic QT prolongation (7%), diarrhea (2%), and vomiting (0.3%) were
   the most common drug-related adverse effects in a randomized, double-blind study (n=301).
   The median age was 13 years (0.25 to 17 years). The dosage of moxifloxacin was dependent on
   age and weight; 4 to 5 mg/kg/dose IV/oral twice daily up to a maximum of 400 mg/dose for the
   treatment of complicated intra-abdominal infections Most common adverse effects were nausea, diarrhea, headache, and dizziness in adult pa-
   tients Cardiovascular: Electrocardiographic confirmed QT prolongation occurred in 7% of patients
   (95% CI, 4% to 3%; n=301) in the moxifloxacin IV group compared with 3% (95% CI, 2%
   to 7%; n=150) in the comparator group (ertapenem IV and amoxicillin/clavulanate oral) in a
   randomized, double-blind study. No morbidity or mortality due to moxifloxacin was reported.
   The median age was 13 years (0.25 to 17 years). The dosage of moxifloxacin was dependent on
   age and weight; 4 to 5 mg/kg/dose IV/oral twice daily up to a maximum of 400 mg/dose for the
   treatment of complicated intra-abdominal infections Out of 300 courses of oral or IV moxifloxacin corrected QT prolongation (6%) occurred in a ret-
   rospective study (221 pediatric patients; 5 months to 9 years of age). No clinical cardiac
   events occurred Hepatic: Out of 300 courses of oral or IV moxifloxacin, transaminase concentration increase
   (2.7%) and increased bilirubin concentration (1%) occurred in a retrospective study (221 pedi-
   atric patients; 5 months to 9 years of age) Musculoskeletal: Musculoskeletal adverse effects occurred in 3% in the moxifloxacin IV/oral
   group compared with 3% in the comparator group (ertapenem IV and amoxicillin/clavulanate
   oral) in a randomized, double-blind study (n=451). However, the investigators determined
   none of the events were drug related. Effects were typically mild and started 3 weeks to 1 year
   after the start of moxifloxacin. The median age was 13 years (0.25 to 17 years). The dosage of
   moxifloxacin was dependent on age and weight; 4 to 5 mg/kg/dose IV/oral twice daily up to
   MAX 400 mg/dose for the treatment of complicated intra-abdominal infections Black Box Warning

Fluoroquinolones, including moxifloxacin, are associated with disabling and potentially irre-
   versible serious adverse reactions that have occurred together, including tendinitis and tendon
   rupture, peripheral neuropathy, and CNS effects. Discontinue moxifloxacin and avoid use of
   fluoroquinolones in patients with these serious adverse reactions. Reserve use of moxifloxacin
   for patients with no alternative treatment options for acute bacterial sinusitis or acute bacte-
   rial exacerbation of chronic bronchitis. Fluoroquinolones, including moxifloxacin, may exacer-
   bate muscle weakness in persons with myasthenia gravis. Avoid in patients with known history
   of myasthenia gravis Solution Compatibility
   NS, sterile water for injection, D5W, D10W, lactated ringer''s for injection 22

   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring (TB Treatment)
   • Monitor for clinical (eg, decrease in cough and systemic symptoms, increase in weight), radio-
   logic, and bacteriologic response to treatment 14
   • Obtain sputum culture prior to initiation, then monthly during treatment. If cultures remain
