   mended for patients 3 months or older with uncontrolled atopic dermatitis refractory to mois-
   turization. Once-daily dosing is preferred over twice daily dosing. TCIs may be used in combi-
   nation with topical corticosteroids (TCS) although there is low certainty in the evidence for a
   modest benefit than using either agent alone. Clinicians should consider patient values and
   preferences when choosing treatment. For instance, using TCS for the general body and TCIs
   for sensitive areas (eg, face, folds) or that TCS generally come in larger dispensing sizes com-
   pared to TCIs Pediatric FDA Approved Indication:
   Pimecrolimus is indicated as second-line therapy for the short-term and non-continuous treat-
   ment of mild-to-moderate atopic dermatitis in non-immuncompromised pediatric patients 2
   years or older. It is considered a second-line agent when there is an inadequate response to
   other topical prescription agents or other treatments are unadvisable . Reexamine and con-
   firm diagnosis if signs and symptoms do not improve after 6 weeks of treatment with pime-
   crolimus Administration
   Apply a thin layer to the affected areas, limiting application only to the areas of involvement Do not use with occlusive dressings Avoid long term use 1', 'The most commonly reported adverse effects were application site burning, headache, na-
