   Heterozygous Familial Hypercholesterolemia

   Statin therapy for 6 to 96 weeks significantly improved LDL-C and other lipid parameters (ex-
   cept triglycerides) and was well-tolerated in a metaanalysis of 9 studies in 1191 pediatric pa-
   tients (mean age, 3 +/- 5 years; mean BMI, 3 +/- 7 kg/m(2) based on 6 studies) with
   heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. At the end of treatment, the observed differences
   in lipid parameters for statins vs placebo were: Total cholesterol (9 studies) -79.3 mg/dL (95%
   CI, -93.9 to -64.7 mg/dL); LDL-C (9 studies) -78.5 mg/dL (95% CI, -93.2 to -63.8 mg/dL);
   Triglycerides (8 studies) -4.8 mg/dL (95% CI, -9.7 to 1 mg/dL); Apolipoprotein B (8 studies)
   -49.6 mg/dL (95% CI, -60.4 to -38.8 mg/dL); HDL-C (8 studies) 2 mg/dL (95% CI, 08 to 29
   mg/dL). In subgroup analysis, statins showed a dose-dependent effect on achievement of the
   LDL-C target. Important differences among studies may affect the validity of the total choles-
   terol, LDL-C, and apolipoprotein B results. Lovastatin 10 to 40 mg/day was given in 2 studies,
   pravastatin 10 to 40 mg/day in 2 studies, simvastatin 10 to 40 mg/day in 2 studies, pitavastatin
   1 to 4 mg/day in 1 study, atorvastatin 10 to 20 mg/day in 1 study, and rosuvastatin 5 to 20
   mg/day in 1 study FDA Approved Pediatric Indication
   Pitavastatin (Livalo®) is indicated to reduce elevated total cholesterol, LDL-C, and apolipopro-
   tein B levels in pediatric patients 8 years or older with heterozygous familial hypercholes-
   terolemia 2/6
Limitations of Use

   The effect of pitavastatin on cardiovascular morbidity and mortality has not been determined Administration
   Pitavastatin may be taken at any time of day, with or without food', 'Statins were well tolerated and adverse events were mostly unrelated to statin therapy in a
   meta-analysis of 9 studies in 1191 pediatric patients (mean age, 3 +/- 5 years; mean BMI, 3 +/- 7 kg/m(2) based on 6 studies) with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia.
   Treatment-related adverse events were reported in 0% to 15%. Myalgia that resolved during
   continuation of treatment was reported in 2 studies (2.7% of patients receiving rosuvastatin
   and 1% receiving pitavastatin). There were no reports of life-threatening adverse events, and
   permanent discontinuation due to adverse events was 0% in 4 studies and 7% to 9% in 3
   studies. There was no significant difference compared with placebo in transaminase and crea-
   tine kinase levels, and there was no effect on growth or sexual development Common, adult: Myalgia, back pain, diarrhea, constipation and pain in extremity 1

   The safety profile was similar to that observed in the adult population Monitoring
