   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Drug Monitoring
   Target trough concentrations are 7 mg/L or greater for prophylaxis of invasive Aspergillus (IA)
   23,24
        and greater than 1 mg/L for the treatment of suspected or documented IA when the sus-
   pension is administered. Obtain concentrations after at least 5 days of therapy and repeat as
   necessary. When using the IV or tablets it may not be necessary to monitor posaconazole con-
   centration; however, consider for elevated MICs, lack of response, or unexplained toxicity For Candida species, no specific posaconazole target concentration has been identified For
   mucormycosis, consider a trough serum concentrations of 7 to 1 mg/L 16

   Toxic Laboratory Monitoring
   • Monitor for electrolyte disturbances, especially potassium, magnesium, and calcium, before
   and during therapy 25

   • Monitor liver function prior to and during treatment. If abnormal liver function tests occur,
   evaluate for the development of more severe hepatic injury • Closely monitor serum creatinine in patients with moderate to severe renal impairment (esti-
   mated GFR less than 50 mL/min) receiving IV injection • Frequently monitor tacrolimus and cyclosporine whole blood trough concentrations during
   therapy and at discontinuation of treatment 10/22
Toxic Physical Monitoring

   • Prior to initiation of therapy with powder for delayed-release oral suspension in pediatric pa-
   tients, obtain a history of hereditary fructose intolerance (HFI) symptoms (nausea, vomiting,
   abdominal pain) with sorbitol/fructose/sucrose exposure since a diagnosis of HFI may not yet
   be established and use of powder for delayed-release oral suspension is contraindicated in pe-
   diatric patients with HFI • In patients receiving delayed-release tablet, oral suspension, or powder for delayed-release
   oral suspension, closely monitor for breakthrough fungal infection in patients with severe re-
   nal impairment, severe diarrhea or vomiting, in patients who cannot eat a full meal (or tolerate
   an oral nutritional supplement or an acidic carbonated beverage and who do not have the op-
   tion of treatment with posaconazole delayed-release tablet), and in patients administered
   posaconazole by NG tube 1 ,2 .

   • Closely monitor for breakthrough fungal infections, particularly in patients with severe renal
   impairment', '• Monitor for electrolyte disturbances, especially potassium, magnesium, and calcium, before
