   • Indicated as an immunosuppressant and for the treatment of a variety of allergic and inflam-
   matory disorders Administration
   Ophthalmic

   • Shake well prior to use 13,14 .

   Oral

   • Administer with food to minimize GI irritation • Oral tablets, disintegrating: prednisoLONE orally disintegrating tablets should not be re-
   moved from blister packet until just prior to administration. They can either be swallowed

whole or allowed to dissolve in the mouth with or without water. Do not cut, split, break, or
   use partial tablets', 'Oral, short-term use

   • Adverse effects with short-term use include reversible abnormalities in glucose metabolism,
   increased appetite, fluid retention, weight gain, mood alteration, hypertension, peptic ulcer,
   and rarely aseptic necrosis • Bradycardia: In pediatric patients with severe Kawasaki disease, 1% developed bradycar-
   dia with prednisoLONE 2 mg/kg/day IV or orally plus IV immunoglobulin (n=134) vs 1% with
   IV immunoglobulin alone (n=42). Bradycardia resolved in 59 of 106 patients (55.7%) by the
   time of hospital discharge. No serious sequelae, such as a need for vasopressor, resuscitation,
   or admission to the intensive care unit occurred Oral, long-term use

   • Adverse effects with long-term use include adrenal axis suppression, growth suppression,
   dermal thinning, hypertension, diabetes, Cushing''s syndrome, cataracts, muscle weakness,
   and rarely immune function impairment • Decreased linear growth rate: Corticosteroids have been associated with a decreased rate
   of growth in children especially with high-dose, long-term treatment. Corticosteroids decrease

the quantity of growth hormone released and also decrease the sensitivity of tissues to its ef-
   fect. The effect on growth suppression may be minimized by using the lowest effective dose of
   the corticosteroid and using alternate day therapy, if possible. In patients deficient in growth
   hormone, human growth hormone may also increase growth rate 96,97,98 .
   • Neurodevelopmental outcomes: PrednisoLONE and methylPREDNISolone use for more
   than 28 days was associated with lower Bayley Scales of Infant Development-Third Edition
   (BSID-III) motor scores (adjusted mean difference -7.9, 95% CI -14.2 to -0.9). After adjusting for
   predetermined variables (small for gestational age, respiratory support at 14 days, maternal
   education, and serious adverse events), length of exposure was no longer associated with mo-
   tor outcomes. There was also no linear association between exposure duration and BSID-III
   scores at corrected age 2 years (n=54) Ophthalmic use
   • Glaucoma with optic nerve damage, visual acuity and field defects, posterior subcapsular
   cataract formation, secondary ocular infections, delayed wound healing, and perforation of the
   globe. Stinging or burning may occur 89 , Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Asthma: Monitor FEV1 or PEF to assess response to treatment • Autoimmune hepatitis (AIH): Monitor serum ALT, AST, and IgG levels to assess response to
   treatment Normalization of autoantibodies levels is also indicative of efficacy • Immune thrombocytopenia: Monitor platelet count to assess response to treatment 23 , Toxic Monitoring
   • Monitor growth status (eg, stadiometry) periodically in children receiving long-term corticos-
   teroid treatment • Monitor routine laboratory studies, including serum potassium and fasting blood glucose, at
   regular intervals during prolonged therapy • Monitor blood pressure and body weight at regular intervals during prolonged therapy • Monitor signs and symptoms of hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis suppression with
   chronic use • Monitor signs and symptoms of Cushing''s syndrome with chronic use 20/33
• Monitor signs and symptoms of hyperglycemia with chronic use • Monitor for the development of infection • Monitor bone density in patients on long term corticosteroid therapy • Monitor intraocular pressure if steroid therapy is continued for more than 6 weeks • Screen hepatitis B infection before initiating immunosuppressive treatment. For patients who
   show evidence of hepatitis B infection, recommend consultation with physicians with expertise
   in managing hepatitis B regarding monitoring and consideration for hepatitis B antiviral ther-
   apy • Closely monitor for the reactivation of tuberculosis • Ophthalmic: Routinely monitor intraocular pressure if duration of use is 10 days or longer
