   Supraventricular tachycardia, adenosine-refractory (PALS guidelines):
   Loading dose: 15 mg/kg IV or intraosseous over 30 to 60 minutes followed by a continuous in-
   fusion; maximum loading dose, 100 mg 4 ,5

   Continuous IV infusion: 20 to 80 mcg/kg/min; maximum 2000 mg per 24 hours 4 , 5', 'Supraventricular tachycardia (SVT)

   PALS guidelines: Procainamide may be considered to treat SVT in pediatric patients with car-
   diopulmonary compromise who are unresponsive to vagal maneuvers and adenosine Comparison with amiodarone: A small retrospective study (N=40 episodes of SVT) reported
   full treatment success (immediate conversion within 1 hour without further recurrences) in
   50% of the 14 episodes of SVT treated with procainamide and 15% of the 26 episodes treated
   with amiodarone. Amiodarone was switched to procainamide in 8 patients, with 4 achieving
   full success after conversion. Most patients were also given other antiarrhythmic medications
   before and during the study. Age of included patients ranged from 1 day to 19 years (median,
   34 days), with 54% younger than 30 days. Congenital heart disease was present in 65% SVT
   subtypes included were orthodronic reciprocating tachycardia (ORT), intra-atrial reentrant
   tachycardia (IART), and ectipic atrial tachycardia (EAT).. Median initial amiodarone dose was 5
   mg/kg (range, 1 to 10 mg/kg), followed by a continuous infusion of 5 mcg/kg/min (range, 5 to

15 mcg/kg/min) in 54% of amiodarone-treated patients. Median procainamide initial dose was
   10 mg/kg (range, 5 to 10 mg/kg) followed by a continuous infusion of 20 mcg/kg/min (range,
   10 to 40 mcg/kg/min) in 79% of procainamide-treated patients Junctional tachycardia:In a study of pediatric patients 1 day or older with postoperative junc-
   tional tachycardia, combined hypothermia plus procainamide was effective in 80% of treated
   subjects (n=30) compared with efficacy rates of 32% with hypothermia alone (n=38) and 22%
   with procainamide alone (n=9). Mean procainamide level for the hypothermia plus pro-
   cainamide group was 6 mcg/mL. Efficacy was defined as achieving a heart rate less than 170
   beats per minute within 2 hours of initiation with no heart rate rebound to greater than 170
   beats per minute. Procainamide was given as a bolus dose of 5 to 15 mg/kg over 15 to 30 min-
   utes followed by a continuous infusion of 20 to 80 mcg/kg/min; target serum procainamide
   level was 4 to 10 mcg/mL Junctional tachycardia: Procainamide monotherapy was safe and effective for rapid rate con-
   trol of postoperative junctional tachycardia (JT) occurring in children following surgical repair
   of congenital heart defects. Two procainamide dosing protocols were used. In protocol A (n=5),
   following a loading dose of 3 mg/kg IV over 20 minutes, a continuous infusion was initiated at a
   rate of 20 mcg/kg/min and increased in 10 mcg/kg increments every 30 min, up to 40 to 120
   mcg/kg/min, until the heart rate decreased below 180 beats/min. In protocol B (n=12), after a
   higher loading dose of 10 mg/kg over 20 min, a continuous infusion was initiated at 20
   mcg/kg/min and increased, in 10 mcg/kg increments every 10 to 15 min, up to 40 to 120
   mcg/kg/min, until the heart rate decreased below 180 beats/min. Procainamide decreased JT
   rates in all patients; however, the response was significantly faster with protocol B. The mean
   time to decrease JT rates below 180 beats/min was 2 hours in protocol B compared with 4
   hours in protocol A. With protocol A, 3 of 5 patients required additional boluses during the first
   hour of therapy whereas none of the patients in protocol B required additional boluses. The
   mean procainamide infusion rate required to maintain JT rates below 180 beats/min was 4
   mcg/kg/min . Procainamide and NAPA levels did not correlate with success in lowering JT rates
   below 180 beats/min. In addition, the procainamide infusion rate did not correlate with the
   time to decrease JT rates below 180 beats/min. The duration of procainamide infusions ranged
   from 28 to 120 hours. No proarrhythmia, bradycardia, or significant hypotension occurred in
   any patient Administration
   Intravenous/Intraosseous: Administer loading dose over 30 to 60 minutes 4 at a concentra-
   tion of 20 mg/mL. For continuous infusion, administer at a concentration of 2 to 4 mg/mL 2/6', 'Cardiovascular:
   Serious disturbances of cardiac rhythm such as ventricular asystole or fibrillation are more
   common with IV administration of procainamide than with IM administration. If evidence of
   QRS widening of more than 25% or marked prolongation of the Q-T interval occurs, concern
   for overdosage is appropriate, and interruption of the infusion is advisable if a 50% increase
   occurs Transient but severe lowering of blood pressure has been reported, particularly during IV ad-
   ministration Black Box Warning
   The use of procainamide hydrochloride as well as other antiarrhythmic agents should be re-
   served for patients with life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias. The prolonged administration
   of procainamide often leads to the development of a positive ANA test, with or without symp-
   toms of a lupus erythematosus-like syndrome. Agranulocytosis, bone marrow depression,
   neutropenia, hypoplastic anemia, and thrombocytopenia in patients receiving procainamide
   hydrochloride have been reported (in adults), some of which were fatal. Discontinue pro-
   cainamide if hematologic disorders are identified Solution Compatibility
   D5W (conflicting data), 45% NaCl, and NS.

   Solution Incompatibility
   D5W (conflicting data) 11,12 .

Terminal Injection Site Compatibility
   Amiodarone, dobutamine, famotidine, flumazenil, heparin, hydrocortisone, lidocaine,
   netilmicin, ranitidine, remifentanil, and sodium nitroprusside.

   Terminal Injection Site Incompatibility
   Esmolol, milrinone, and phenytoin.

   Monitoring
   Continuous monitoring of the ECG, blood pressure, and heart rate is recommended. It is rec-
   ommended that CBC with differential and platelet counts be obtained at weekly intervals for
   the first three months of therapy, and periodically thereafter, or if signs of infection are appar-
   ent. Monitor antinuclear antibody (ANA) titer and renal function periodically during therapy Measure procainamide and N-acetyl procainamide (NAPA) concentrations at 2, 12, and 24
   hours after starting the loading dose infusion, and every 24 hours during therapy Therapeutic concentrations:

   Procainamide: 4 to 10 mcg/mL, NAPA: 6 to 20 mcg/mL 8 , 9 , Sum of procainamide and NAPA: 10 to 30 mcg/mL Increasing frequency of toxicity associated with procainamide levels greater than 10 mcg/mL', '', 'Pharmacology
   Procainamide is a class IA antiarrhythmic agent that increases the effective refractory period of
   the atria and the ventricles of the heart. Onset of action occurs within minutes of starting the
   intravenous loading dose. Distribution half-life of approximately 10 minutes. Volume of distri-
   bution averages 2 L/kg in children. Metabolized primarily in the liver to N-acetylprocainamide
   (NAPA), an active metabolite. The rate of acetylation is primarily genetically determined in
   adults and children, with slow acetylators metabolizing 16% to 21% of the dose and fast acety-
   lators metabolizing 24% to 33% of the dose. Half-life is approximately 7 hours in children.
   Renal function is a significant determinant of procainamide clearance 8 , 7 , 4/6'),
