      month or longer treatment lapse                      tensive phase plus continuation phase)', 'Initial treatment of active tuberculosis in combination with other antituberculous agents Administration
   • Fixed-dose combination dosage forms are preferred when available to decrease pill burden
   3,2

   • Directly observed therapy (DOT) is generally recommended in all cases, especially with inter-
   mittent (not daily) dosage regimens Preparation
   Extemporaneous oral compound using SyrSpend SF PH4

100 mg/mL suspension (300 mL) 8
   • Place 30 grams of pyrazinamide active pharmaceutical ingredient (API) in a mortar and tritu-
   rate to a fine powder using a pestle.
   • Add a small amount of SyrSpend SF PH4 to wet the powder and mix into a fine paste.
   • Add SyrSpend SF PH4, in geometric portions almost to volume; mix thoroughly after each
   addition.
   • Add enough SyrSpend SF PH4 to container to bring final volume to 300 mL; mix well.
   • Label the container with "Shake well before using."
   • Stable for 90 days at room temperature (20 to 25°C) or under refrigeration (2 to 8°C).

   Extemporaneous oral compound using simple syrup

   100 mg/mL suspension (100 mL) 9

   • Triturate twenty 500-mg tablets of pyrazinamide to a fine powder using a mortar and pestle.
   • Add a small amount of simple syrup to wet the powder and mix into a fine paste.
   • Transfer to a graduate, rinse mortar with simple syrup, and transfer again.

   • Add enough simple syrup to container to bring final volume to 100 mL.
   • Label the container with "Shake well before using."
   • Stable for 60 days at room temperature or under refrigeration; protect from light.

   A pyrazinamide 10 mg/mL suspension may be prepared by triturating three 500-mg tablets,
   adding 10 mL of vehicle (1:1 mixture of Ora-Sweet and Ora-Plus, 1:1 mixture of Ora-Sweet SF
   and Ora-Plus, or cherry syrup (cherry syrup concentrate diluted 1:4 with simple syrup) to form
   a paste, then adding the vehicle in geometric proportions to a final volume of 150 mL.
   Suspension is stable for 60 days at room temperature or under refrigeration; shake well and
   protect from light', 'Common: Nausea, vomiting, anorexia, mild arthralgia and myalgia, hyperuricemia, increased
   liver enzyme levels 11

   Serious: Hepatotoxicity; may appear at any time during treatments and is dose-related 11

   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring
   • Obtain acid-fast bacilli (AFB) smear and culture from sputum at least monthly until 2 consecu-
   tive culture specimens are negative. In patients with positive AFB smears upon diagnosis, ob-
   tain follow-up smears more frequently (eg, every 2 weeks until two consecutive specimens are
   negative) • In cases of known or suspected drug resistance, perform in vitro susceptibility tests with re-
   cent cultures of M. tuberculosis against pyrazinamide and the usual primary drugs using a ref-
   erence laboratory capable of performing these studies • Perform chest x-rays after 2 to 3 months of treatment in patients with negative initial cultures
   and at end of treatment. In patients with positive cultures at diagnosis, repeat chest x-rays af-
   ter 2 months of treatment and at completion of treatment are useful but not required. Toxic Monitoring

• Monitor liver function tests, especially AST and ALT, prior to starting therapy, periodically dur-
   ing treatment, and if any clinical signs or symptoms occur during therapy, especially in patients
   with preexisting liver disease or those at increased risk for drug-related hepatitis (eg, alcohol
   abusers) • Monitor serum uric acid levels prior to starting therapy, periodically during treatment, and if
   any clinical signs or symptoms occur during therapy • Monitor for symptoms and clinical signs of liver injury; especially in patients with preexisting
   liver disease or those at increased risk for drug-related hepatitis (eg, alcohol abusers) Therapeutic Drug Monitoring

   Peak adult pyrazinamide concentration target is at least 20 mcg/mL in adults when measured
   2 hours after a dose of pyrazinamide Consider monitoring TB regimen drug levels in the following situations 5 :

   • Poor response despite adherence and susceptibility
   • Severe GI abnormalities such as short bowel syndrome, chronic diarrhea with malabsorption,
   severe gastroparesis
   • Drug-drug interactions
   • Impaired renal clearance
   • HIV coinfection

   • Diabetes
   • Use of second-line drugs
   Some experts use therapeutic drug monitoring for all patients with resistant TB early in treat-
   ment to identify poor responses earlier', 'any clinical signs or symptoms occur during therapy • Monitor for symptoms and clinical signs of liver injury; especially in patients with preexisting
• Drug-drug interactions', '• Liver: Hydrolyzed to major active metabolite, pyrazinoic acid 11
   • Metabolites: Pyrazinoic acid, active. Hydroxylated to main excretory product of 5-hydrox-
   ypyrazinoic acid 11

   Elimination

   • Renal excretion: 70% of a dose is excreted in the urine within 24 hours 11

   • Total body clearance: 15 L/h/kg in infants 13

   Half-life: 9 to 10 hours; prolonged with impaired renal or hepatic function 11

