   Distribution: Widely distributed into body tissues; protein binding is approximately 58% Metabolism: Metabolized extensively in the liver by cytochrome P450 enzymes CYP2C19,
   CYP2C9, and CYP3A4. CYP2C19 displays polymorphism which leads to higher exposure in pa-
   tients who are poor or heterozygous extensive metabolizers of this enzyme. The major metab-
   olite, N-oxide, has minimal antifungal activity Based on studies conducted in Caucasian and Japanese healthy subjects, poor metabolizers
   have approximately a 4-fold higher voriconazole exposure (AUC) compared with homozygous
   extensive metabolizers. Subjects who are heterozygous extensive metabolizers have an ap-
   proximate 2-fold increase in voriconazole exposure compared with homozygous extensive me-
   tabolizers Excretion: Less than 2% of a dose is eliminated unchanged in the urine Higher clearance in pediatric patients compared with adults has been demonstrated 50,48 .

   Half-life: Elimination half-life is dose dependent In pediatric patients 2 to 11 years of age receiving voriconazole 7 mg/kg IV every 12 hours, the
