   Daily dose: Evidence support higher doses (mg/kg) in pediatric patients compared with adults
   and in younger children compared with older children 23,46,51 . A pharmacokinetic study (n=48)
   demonstrated a 16 mg/kg/day IV dose in children 2 to 11 years of age provides similar
   voriconazole exposures to 8 mg/kg/day IV in adults Pharmacokinetic data revealed that

higher doses than 14 mg/kg/day IV were required in 25% of children (2 to 11 years of age) to
   achieve similar exposure seen in adults receiving recommended dosing regimens of 8
   mg/kg/day IV Doses of 20 to 32 mg/kg/day IV were required to achieve trough concentra-
   tions greater than 1 mg/L in some patients in a retrospective analysis of 6 infants and 10 chil-
   dren (median age 2 years (2.3 to 4 years)) Patients aged 6 years or younger needed a
   significantly higher median dose of oral voriconazole to achieve target concentrations (1 to 6
   mg/L) compared to older patient groups (17.8 vs 4 mg/kg/day, p less than 001) Median
   doses of 38 mg/kg/day (12 to 40 mg/kg/day) in children younger than 5 years and 15
   mg/kg/day (4 to 52 mg/kg/day) in children 5 years or older were necessary to achieve target
   trough concentrations of 1 to 5 mg/L in a prospective study (n=30) Children younger than
   3 years may require higher daily oral doses (17 mg/kg/day vs 8 mg/kg/day) compared with chil-
   dren older than 3 years to achieve concentrations of 1 to 5 mg/L Frequency: At 24 hours, voriconazole 9 mg/kg/dose IV every 8 hours achieved target concen-
   trations in 9% of simulated subjects compared with 9% of simulated subjects following 9
   mg/kg/dose IV every 12 hours. Among the various simulations, the regimen with improved
   early voriconazole exposure and minimization of toxic concentrations was 9 mg/kg IV every 8
   hours for up to 3 days. The pharmacokinetic model was derived from 23 pediatric patients (2
   to younger than 12 years) treated with prophylactic voriconazole after allogeneic hematopoi-
   etic stem cell transplantation followed by Monte Carlo-based simulations of various dose regi-
   mens with an attempt to achieve 1 to 6 mg/L voriconazole trough concentrations earlier in
