   ZOLMitriptan is a selective 5-hydroxytryptamine (5-HT1B/1D) receptor agonist, with effects on
   the intracranial blood vessels (including the arterio-venous anastomoses) and sensory nerves
   of the trigeminal system. It induces cranial vasoconstriction, inhibits the release of vasoactive
   neuropeptides, and reduces transmission in trigeminal pain pathways during a migraine attack
   when the vessels become dilated. Oral tablets are well absorbed. Tmax for the orally disinte-
   grating tablets was prolonged compared with the oral tablet (3 vs 5 hours). Mean absolute
   bioavailability is approximately 40%; food has no significant effect. Mean apparent Vd is 7 L/kg,
   and plasma protein binding is 25% (at 10 to 1000 nanograms/milliliter). The N-desmethyl active
   metabolite, which exhibits agonist activity at 5-HT1B/1D receptors with potency 2- to 6-fold
   greater than the parent compound, is present at about two-thirds the concentration of
   ZOLMitriptan. Following administration of radiolabeled ZOLMitriptan, total radioactivity recov-
   ered in urine and feces was 65% and 30%, respectively. Unchanged ZOLMitriptan (approxi-
   mately 8%) and indole acetic acid (31%), N-oxide (7%), and N-desmethyl (4%) metabolites were
   also recovered in the urine. Mean total plasma clearance is 5 mL/min/kg 2 ,3 .

   Oral tablet: In a comparison of pharmacokinetic parameters between adolescents and adults
   following oral administration of ZOLMitriptan 5 mg tablets (total n=39; adolescents, n=21),
   mean values for Cmax (9.7 vs 5 ng/mL) and mean AUC (51.4 vs 3 ng x hr/mL) were not sta-
   tistically different. Mean Cmax and AUC were higher in females than males in both groups,
   with girls having a Cmax of 1 ng/mL and boys 3 ng/mL; weight-adjusted AUC was 2 ng x
   hr/mL in girls and 2 ng x hr/mL in boys. Mean median Tmax was 5 hours in adolescents
   and 1 hour in adults, with a range of 5 to 4 hours for both groups. Mean half-life was shorter
   in adolescents (3.01 hours) than adults (3.75 hours), with no gender differences noted in ado-
   lescents. Systemic exposure to the active metabolite was higher in adolescents (Cmax 39% and
   AUC 36%) compared with adults 5/8
Nasal spray: Cmax occurs approximately 3 hours after dosing. Mean relative bioavailability
   102% compared with oral formulations. Following administration, ZOLMitriptan is detected in
   plasma within 5 minutes and the N-desmethyl metabolite is detected within 15 minutes. Vd is 4 +/- 3 L/kg. Mean elimination half-life is approximately 3 hours, which is similar to oral for-
   mulations. Half-lives did not vary for ZOLMitriptan and active metabolite following single (1
   day) or multiple (4 days) nasal doses. Mean total plasma clearance is 9 mL/min/kg'),
