monoamine oxidase A, and hydroxylation by CYP3A4 and CYP 2D6, with further oxidation to', 'Pharmacology
   Almotriptan malate is a selective agonist of 5-hydroxytryptamine receptors (5-HT1B/1D/1F) that
   are found on the extracerebral, intracranial blood vessels and sensory nerves of the trigeminal
   system. During a migraine attack, blood vessels become dilated and the trigeminal system is
   activated; almotriptan induces cranial vessel constriction, inhibits vasoactive neuropeptide re-
   lease, and reduces transmission in trigeminal pain pathways. Absolute bioavailability is ap-
   proximately 70%; peak plasma levels occur within 1 to 3 hours of administration. Absorption
   was similar in adolescents compared with adults, and was unaffected by food. Protein binding
   is approximately 35% and mean apparent Vd is approximately 180 to 200 L. The 2 primary
   routes of metabolism are oxidative deamination (approximately 27% of the dose) via
   monoamine oxidase A, and hydroxylation by CYP3A4 and CYP 2D6, with further oxidation to
   the gamma-aminobutyric acid derivative (approximately 12% of the dose). Flavin monooxyge-
   nase is a minor route of metabolism. Mean half-life is 3 to 4 hours. Of the total dose elimi-
   nated through the kidneys (approximately 75% of oral dose), approximately 40% is excreted

unchanged. Approximately 13% of the total dose is eliminated via the feces as both unchanged
   drug and metabolites Mean oral clearance was faster in adolescents (0.672 L/hr/kg) compared with that of adults
   (0.581 L/hr/kg) in a single dose, pharmacokinetic study (n=36; adolescents=18). Other parame-
   ters were similar between the 2 age groups, with peak levels at approximately 2 hours, half-life
   approximately 5 hours, and volume of distribution approximately 5 L/kg'),
