   In a single-center, pharmacokinetic and pharmacodynamic trial of ambrisentan in 16 subjects
   between 24 and 120 months old undergoing Fontan surgery for single ventricle heart defects, 5 mg of ambrisentan (n=13) or placebo (n=3) were administered for up to three doses start-
   ing on postoperative day number The dose adjusted Cmax and AUC were higher among the
   Fontan surgery subjects when compared with data from pulmonary arterial hypertension
   (PAH) patients, indicating possible reduced hepatic clearance in this population. Ambrisentan
   lowered pulmonary arterial pressures, pulmonary vascular resistance, and brain natriuretic
   peptide (BNP) but had no effect on weight-normalized chest tube drainage or hospital length
   of stay. No drug-related adverse events were observed. Simulations from the population phar-

macokinetic models determined a dose of 1- to 2-mg/kg/dose approximated the therapeu-
   tic exposure of a 5- or 10-mg dose in adult patients with PAH Pulmonary arterial hypertension (PAH)
   Guidelines: In children with PAH and negative acute vasoreactivity testing, a therapeutic strat-
   egy based on risk stratification and treatment response is recommended, extrapolated from
   that in adults but adapted for age. Oral therapy with either a phosphodiesterase type 5 in-
   hibitor (PDE5i; sildenafil or tadalafil) or endothelin receptor antagonist (ERA; bosentan or am-
   brisentan) is recommended in children with lower risk PAH. Oral therapy in combination with
   an inhaled prostacyclin may also be considered in lower risk PAH. The combination of a PDE5i
   or ERA with epoprostenol IV or treprostinil IV/subQ may be considered in those patients with
   higher risk PAH Long-term ambrisentan dosing: In a open-label extension study in pediatric PAH patients
   age 8 to less than 18 years of age (N=38), long-term ambrisentan dosing (median exposure, 5
   years), alone or in combination with other therapies, resulted in improvements in 6-minute
   walking distance (6MWD) and N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide (NT-proBNP) levels, as
   well as improved or unchanged WHO functional class. Most patients (89%) experienced at least
   one adverse event, most commonly upper respiratory tract infections (n=11), nasopharyngitis
   (n=9), headache (n=3), gastroenteritis (n=2), and anemia (n=2). Serious adverse events oc-
   curred in 55% of patients, including worsening PAH (n=3), acute cardiac failure, pneumonia, or
   anemia (n=2 each); no serious adverse events were considered related to ambrisentan.
   Anemia and hepatotoxicity adverse events were generally mild to moderate and did not result
   in dose adjustment Combination therapy with ambrisentan and tadalafil: In a retrospective review of patients
   18 years or younger with PAH (N=43; median age 3 years), combination therapy with am-
   brisentan and tadalafil was well tolerated and exhibited an acceptable safety profile. The me-
   dian medication dosage was ambrisentan 5 mg daily and tadalafil 20 mg daily. WHO functional
   class improved or was unchanged in 93% of patients. Improvements in exercise capacity,
   hemodynamics, and biomarkers were observed in all patients but only reached significance in
   the subgroup of patients previously treatment naïve or on monotherapy with a PED5i or ERA.
   Seven patients were hospitalized for worsening PAH, and three of those patients required fur-
   ther surgical intervention. Adverse events occurred in 65% of patients and included anemia
   (n=9), headaches (n=7), gastrointestinal symptoms (n=3), flushing (n=3), dizziness (n=2), epis-
   taxis (n=2), peripheral edema (n=1), and transaminitis (n=1). 6

