   Monitoring
   Therapeutic and Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   •No target range of activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) has been identified In stud-
   ies of thrombus treatment, a target range of 5 to 5 times baseline aPTT has been used 8 ,1 ,2

   •Monitor anticoagulant status more frequently in patients with renal impairment Thrombosis; Prophylaxis - Coronary intervention or cardiopulmonary bypass

   Therapeutic and Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Monitor activated clotting time (ACT) or ECT (if available) during coronary intervention or car-
   diopulmonary bypass 13,3 ,14,4 ,10,9 . The protocol for ACT measurement in a prospective study
   (n=110) in children receiving bivalirudin during cardiac catheterization was: ACT before dosing,
   within 5 minutes postbolus, at 30-minute intervals during procedure, just before infusion dis-
   continuation, and at 10 and 30 minutes after cessation of bivalirudin infusion from the sheath In a case report, a target ACT of greater than 400 seconds was used during cardiopulmonary
   bypass for effective anticoagulation Thrombosis; Prophylaxis - Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO)

   Therapeutic and Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Activated partial thromboplastin time (aPTT) was monitored during use of bivalirudin for
   thrombosis prophylaxis during ECMO and results were used to guide dose adjustments. The

protocol described in a retrospective study of children receiving bivalirudin during ECMO
