   Hepatic impairment in patients with Dravet Syndrome or Lennox-Gastaut Syndrome

   • Mild (Child-Pugh A): No dosage adjustment necessary 1

   • Moderate (Child-Pugh B): Initial, 25 mg/kg twice daily; maintenance, 5 mg/kg twice daily;
   maximum, 5 mg/kg twice daily 1
   • Severe (Child-Pugh C): Initial, 5 mg/kg twice daily; maintenance, 1 mg/kg twice daily; maxi-
   mum, 2 mg/kg twice daily 1
   Hepatic impairment in patients with Tuberous Sclerosis Complex

   • Mild (Child-Pugh A): No dosage adjustment necessary 2

   • Moderate (Child-Pugh B): Initial, 25 mg/kg twice daily; maintenance, 25 mg/kg twice daily
   2

   • Severe (Child-Pugh C): Initial, 5 mg/kg twice daily; maintenance, 5 mg/kg twice daily 2

   Other adjustments
   Hepatotoxicity

   • Signs or symptoms of hepatic dysfunction (eg, unexplained nausea, vomiting, right up-
   per quadrant abdominal pain, fatigue, anorexia, or jaundice and/or dark urine): Interrupt
   or discontinue treatment with cannabidiol, as appropriate 2
   • Transaminase level elevations greater than 3 times the ULN and bilirubin levels
   greater than 2 times the ULN or sustained transaminase elevations of greater than 5
   times the ULN: Discontinue therapy 2

   Hypersensitivity reaction: Discontinue therapy 2', 'Indicated for the treatment of seizures associated with Lennox-Gastaut syndrome, Dravet syn-
   drome, or tuberous sclerosis complex in patients 1 year or older Administration
   • May enterally administer via silicone feeding tubes (eg, nasogastric tube). Do not use with
   tubes made of polyvinyl chloride (PVC) or polyurethane, and avoid use of silicone nasogastric
   tubes with short lengths and narrow diameters (eg, less than 50 cm and less than 5 FR) • The recommended volume for flushing with room temperature drinking water after each
   dose is approximately 5 times the priming volume of the tube; flushing volume may need to
   be modified in patients with fluid restrictions 3
   • Food may affect cannabidiol levels; give consistently with regard to meals to reduce variabil-
   ity in cannabidiol plasma exposure. Coadministration with a high-fat/high-calorie meal in-
   creased Cmax by 5-fold, AUC by 4-fold, and reduced the total variability, compared with the
   fasted state in healthy volunteers • Use the provided calibrated measuring device to measure and deliver the prescribed dose • Discard any unused medication 12 weeks after first opening the bottle', 'Common, Lennox-Gastaut syndrome or Dravet syndrome: Infection (40% to 41%), somno-
   lence (23% to 25%), decreased appetite (16% to 22%), diarrhea (9% to 20%), fatigue (11% to
   12%), rash (7% to 13%), elevated transaminases (8% to 16%), and insomnia/sleep disorder,
   poor quality sleep (5% to 11%) Common, Tuberous Sclerosis Complex: Diarrhea (31%), transaminase elevations (25%), de-
   creased appetite (20%), somnolence (13%), pyrexia (19%), and vomiting (17%) 2

   Monitoring
   Laboratory

   Monitor serum transaminases (ALT and AST) and total bilirubin levels in all patients: 1
   ●   Prior to starting treatment
   ●   At 1, 3, and 6 months after initiation
   ●   Periodically thereafter or as clinically indicated.
   ●   Within 1 month following changes in dosage and addition of or changes in medications that
       are known to impact the liver.
   ●   Consider more frequent monitoring of serum transaminases and bilirubin in patients who
       are taking valproate or who have elevated liver enzymes at baseline.
   ●   If a patient develops clinical signs or symptoms suggestive of hepatic dysfunction (e.g., un-
       explained nausea, vomiting, right upper quadrant abdominal pain, fatigue, anorexia, or
       jaundice and/or dark urine) promptly measure serum transaminases and total bilirubin

