Coadministration with the potent CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 inducing agent rifampin resulted in a', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Cannabidiol is a cannabinoid that naturally occurs in the Cannabis sativa L. plant with an un-
   known mechanism for its anticonvulsant effect. It does not interact with cannabinoid receptors Absorption: Coadministration of cannabidiol with a high-fat/high-calorie meal increased Cmax
   5-fold and AUC 4-fold, and reduced the total variability, compared with the fasted state in
   healthy volunteers 1

   Tmax: 5 to 5 hours at steady state Distribution: Protein binding was greater than 94% 1
   Volume of distribution: The apparent Vd of cannabidiol was 20,963 to 42,849 L in healthy vol-
   unteers Metabolism: Metabolized in liver (primarily) and GIT by CYP2C19 and CYP3A4 enzymes and
   UGT1A7, UGT1A9, and UGT2B7 isoforms. 7-OH-CBD metabolite is active. After repeat dosing,
   7-OH-CBD has a 38% lower AUC than the parent drug. The 7-OH-CBD metabolite is converted
   to 7-COOH-CBD (inactive), which has an approximately 40-fold higher AUC than the parent
   drug Cannabidiol is an inhibitor of CYP2C8, CYP2C9, CYP2C19, UGT1A9, and UGT2B7; consider a
   dose reduction of substrates of these metabolic enzymes when coadministered as clinically
   appropriate. Cannabinol is also a weak inhibitor of CYP1A2 Cannabidiol may induce or inhibit CYP2B6 at clinically relevant concentrations; consider adjust-
   ing dosage of CYP2B6 substrates when coadministered as clinically appropriate Cannabidiol is a substrate of CYP3A4, CYP2C19, UGT1A7, UGT1A9, and UGT2B7 Coadministration of cannabidiol with the potent inhibitor of CYP3A4, itraconazole, increased
   exposure by less than 10% for cannabidiol and less than 20% for 7-OH-CBD and 7-COOH-CBD
   for both AUC and Cmax. The effects of the potent CYP2C19 inhibitor fluconazole were slightly
   more marked, but still considered not clinically meaningful (cannabidiol increased by 22% and
   24% for AUC and Cmax, respectively; 7-OH-CBD decreased by 28% and 41% for AUC and Cmax;
   7-COOH-CBD decreased by 33% and 48% for AUC and Cmax) 6/7
Coadministration with the potent CYP3A4 and CYP2C19 inducing agent rifampin resulted in a
   decrease in cannabidiol exposure of 32% and 34% for AUC and Cmax. There were moderate
   changes in exposure to the active metabolite (7-OH-CBD decreased by 63% and 67% for AUC
   and Cmax, 7-COOH-CBD decreased by 48% for AUC, whereas there was no change in Cmax) Excretion: Cannabidiol is excreted in feces and has minor renal clearance Half-life: 56 to 61 hours after twice-daily dosing for 7 days in healthy volunteers'),
