Acinetobacter baumannii infection, carbapenem-resistant: Guidance from the Infectious
   Disease Society of America (IDSA) recommends a sulbactam-containing agent in combination
   with a second agent for the treatment of infections caused by CRAB. Sulbactam/durlobactam
   in combination with imipenem/cilastatin or meropenem is preferred; polymyxin B, high-dose
   minocycline, tigecycline, or cefiderocol may be substituted for imipenem/cilastatin or
   meropenem. If sulbactam/durlobactam is unavailable, high-dose ampicillin/sulbactam in com-
   bination with polymyxin B, high-dose minocycline, tigecycline, or cefiderocol is an alternative. If
   prolonged therapy is necessary (eg, osteomyelitis), the second agent may be discontinued af-
   ter clinical improvement occurs and the sulbactam-containing agent continued as monother-
   apy for the rest of the duration of therapy. Nebulized antibiotics are not recommended 1 ,3 .

