CYP2D6, to multiple unidentified metabolites. Des-ciclesonide is primarily excreted in the feces', 'Pharmacology
   Ciclesonide is a pro-drug that is enzymatically hydrolyzed by esterases in the lung and nasal
   mucosa to des-ciclesonide, the pharmacologically active metabolite, that has anti-inflamma-
   tory and inhibitory activities against various mediators (eg, histamine, eicosanoids, leuko-
   trienes, and cytokines) and cell types (mast cells, eosinophils, basophils, lymphocytes,
   macrophages, and neutrophils) involved in the asthmatic response 16 and allergic inflamma-

tion The oral bioavailability of both ciclesonide and des-ciclesonide is less than 1% due to
   low gastrointestinal absorption and high first-pass metabolism 16,4 ,17 . Lung deposition is ap-
   proximately 52%, without the use of a spacer Serum concentrations of des-ciclesonide
   were below 45 pg/mL in pediatric patients between 6 and 11 years of age Based on a com-
   parative pharmacokinetics study of inhalation (1280 mcg) and intravenous (800 mcg) adminis-
   tration of ciclesonide, the absolute bioavailability of ciclesonide was 22% and the relative sys-
   temic exposure to des-ciclesonide was 63% Both ciclesonide and des-ciclesonide are highly
   bound to human plasma proteins (99% or higher), with 1% or less of unbound drug detected in
   the systemic circulation. Vd of ciclesonide and des-ciclesonide were approximately 9 L/kg
   and 1 L/kg, respectively, following intravenous administration of 800 mcg of ciclesonide.
   Des-ciclesonide is metabolized in the liver by CYP3A4 isoenzymes, and to a lesser extent by
   CYP2D6, to multiple unidentified metabolites. Des-ciclesonide is primarily excreted in the feces
   (66%) and in the urine (20% or less). The elimination half-life of des-ciclesonide is approxi-
   mately 6 to 7 hours 16 , 4 ,'),
