   crease gastrointestinal absorption of some drugs 2/4', 'temic drug-metabolizing enzymes (eg, CYP450) 2', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Colesevelam hydrochloride is a lipid-lowering polymeric agent that exerts its action by actively
   binding to bile acids to prevent its reabsorption in the intestines. This action depletes serum
   bile acids, which activates cholesterol 7-alpha-hydroxylase to convert cholesterol into bile
   acids. Consequently, a demand for cholesterol in the liver is increased, resulting in increased
   HMG-CoA reductase transcription and activity, and increased number of LDL receptors. This
   compensatory action increases LDL-cholesterol clearance from the blood, resulting in lowered
   serum LDL-cholesterol level Absorption: Colesevelam is a hydrophilic and water-insoluble polymer that is not hydrolyzed
   by digestive enzymes and is not absorbed Distribution: Colesevelam is not absorbed and distribution is limited to the gastrointestinal
   tract Excretion: In healthy volunteers, an average of 05% of administered radioactivity from a sin-
   gle 14C-labeled colesevelam dose was excreted in the urine Metabolism: Colesevelam is not metabolized systemically and does not interfere with sys-
   temic drug-metabolizing enzymes (eg, CYP450) 2'),
