   tendencies, depression, confusion and disorientation with memory loss, hyperreflexia,
   dysarthria, paresthesia 4

   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring (TB)

• Monitor for clinical (eg, decrease in cough and systemic symptoms, increase in weight), radio-
   logic, and bacteriologic response to treatment 2
   • Obtain sputum culture prior to initiation, then monthly during treatment. If cultures remain
   positive after 3 months, repeat susceptibility testing Toxic Monitoring
   Monitor serum drug concentrations periodically to maintain blood levels less than 30 mcg/mL.
   Evaluate at least weekly in patients with renal dysfunction, in patients receiving a total daily
   dose greater than 500 mg, and in patients with signs and symptoms of toxicity • Monitor renal function periodically • Obtain hematologic panels periodically • Perform periodic liver function studies periodically • Assess neuropsychiatric status frequently Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TB)
   Target peak concentrations in adults are 20 to 35 mcg/mL; the risk of toxicity and neuropsychi-
   atric adverse events is increased with blood concentrations above 30 mcg/mL 4 ,2 .

   Measure peak concentration within the first 1 to 2 weeks of initiation and continue to monitor
   periodically throughout treatment Consider monitoring therapeutic drug concentrations in patients with the following conditions
   2
       :
   • Gastrointestinal problems that may increase the risk of malabsorption
   • Concurrent HIV infection

   • Impaired renal function

   • Diabetes

   Some experts use therapeutic drug monitoring for all patients with resistant TB early in treat-
   ment to identify poor responses earlier', '• Monitor for clinical (eg, decrease in cough and systemic symptoms, increase in weight), radio-', '• Renal excretion: 65% recovered in urine within 72 hours of a dose 4

