group of critically ill infants not on ECMO Fluconazole is a potent inhibitor of CYP2C9 and CYP2C19, and is a moderate inhibitor of
CYP3A4 Cancer in children (n=28), treated with approximately 10 mg/kg/day of fluconazole, was associ-', 'Pharmacology
   Water-soluble triazole antifungal agent. Well absorbed after oral administration (approxi-
   mately 90% bioavailability) with peak serum concentrations reached within 1 to 2 hours. Less
   than 12% protein binding. Good penetration into CSF after both oral and IV administration.
   Larger volume of distribution and more rapid clearance in children compared with adults
   (higher doses needed). Primarily excreted unchanged in the urine (80%). Serum half-life in chil-
   dren is approximately 15 to 18 hours. Hemodialysis reduces plasma concentrations by approx-
   imately 50% 2 ,30,31 . Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) does not significantly re-
   move fluconazole in infants (n=10; median age, 19 days (1 to 113 days)) on fluconazole 25
   mg/kg/week for prophylaxis. The mean fluconazole extraction across the oxygenator was -2%.
   The volume of distribution was 50% to 90% higher but clearance was similar to a comparator
