   city (gastrointestinal (diarrhea, nausea, vomiting), hematologic (leukopenia, thrombocytope-
   nia), and hepatic (hepatitis)) 7 , Bone marrow depression (leukopenia, thrombocytopenia, pancytopenia, agranulocytosis),
   which may be irreversible and fatal, has been reported. Use caution in patients at risk for bone
   marrow depression (immunosuppressed, hematologic disease). Other adverse events re-
   ported include cardiotoxicity, rash, diarrhea, increased liver enzymes, and hepatic failure.
   Should only be used in combination with other antifungals (amphotericin B, fluconazole) be-
   cause of the risk of rapid emergence of resistance of Candida and Cryptococcus isolates to
   flucytosine 7 ,12 .

   Black Box Warning

Use with extreme caution in patients with impaired renal function. Close monitoring of hema-
   tologic, renal and hepatic status of all patients is essential. These instructions should be thor-
   oughly reviewed before administration of flucytosine Monitoring
   Desired serum concentration levels range between 40 and 80 mcg/mL 1 ,2 ,10 . Monitoring of
   serum concentrations is recommended, especially in patients with renal impairment. Prior to
   treatment initiation, electrolytes, hematologic, and renal function should be assessed. Close
   monitoring of hematologic, renal, and hepatic status of all patients is essential during therapy
   1,7
         .', '', 'Pharmacology
   Flucytosine is well absorbed orally (78% to 89%). Mean time to peak concentration in pediatric
   patients is 5 (+/- 3) hours. Transformed rapidly within fungal cells to fluorouracil, which in-
   terferes with DNA and RNA synthesis. Excellent penetration into CSF and body tissues. Protein
   binding low (3% to 4%). Renal elimination of unchanged drug is approximately 90%, propor-
   tional to GFR. Serum half-life in adults is 3 to 5 hours if renal function is normal, but 30 to 250
   hours if renal impairment is present. Median half-life in infants (n=3) was 4 hours. Resistance
   develops frequently if used alone. Synergistic with amphotericin even if treating resistant
   strain. Removed by hemodialysis 7 ,12 .'),
