   Extended-release capsule: Administer as a single daily dose at bedtime. Swallow whole; do
   not crush or chew Immediate-release tablet: Administer as a single daily dose at bedtime; doses larger than 50
   mg/day should be divided into 2 doses 2 with or without food 2 ,', 'Adverse events reported more in fluvoxaMINE-treated patients compared with placebo-
   treated patients during pediatric trials (n=248) were abdominal pain (49% vs 28%), agitation
   (12.3% vs 2%), asthenia (26.3% vs 9%), decreased appetite (16% vs 6%), diarrhea (15.8% vs 1%), dyspepsia (14% vs 4%), hyperkinesia (12.3% to 27% vs 2% to 12%), insomnia (29.8%
   vs 5%), muscle or joint pain (17% vs 8%), nausea (19% vs 14%), somnolence (10.5% to 21% vs 6% to 15%), sore throat (16% vs 9%), tiredness or fatigue (17% vs 8%), and vomiting (19% vs
   9%) 13,1 . Additional adverse events reported in adults, which are also similarly seen in pedi-
   atric patients, were nervousness, sweating, and tremor. Cough increase, dysmenorrhea, ecchy-
   mosis, emotional lability, epistaxis, manic reaction, rash, sinusitis, and weight decrease were
   each reported in 2 or more pediatric patients (and more frequently than with placebo), but
   were not observed in adults 2 , Black Box Warning

Antidepressants increased the risk compared to placebo of suicidal thinking and behavior (sui-
   cidality) in children, adolescents, and young adults in short-term studies of major depressive
   disorder (MDD) and other psychiatric disorders. Anyone considering the use of fluvoxamine
   maleate or any other antidepressant in a child, adolescent, or young adult must balance this
   risk with the clinical need. Short-term studies did not show an increase in the risk of suicidality
   with antidepressants compared to placebo in adults beyond age 24; there was a reduction in
   risk with antidepressants compared to placebo in adults aged 65 and older. Depression and
   certain other psychiatric disorders are themselves associated with increases in the risk of sui-
   cide. Patients of all ages who are started on antidepressant therapy should be monitored ap-
   propriately and observed closely for clinical worsening, suicidality, or unusual changes in be-
   havior. Families and caregivers should be advised of the need for close observation and com-
   munication with the prescriber. FluvoxaMINE maleate is not approved for use in pediatric pa-
   tients except for obsessive-compulsive disorder Monitoring
   Monitor for clinical worsening, suicidality, and unusual changes in behavior, especially within
   the first few months of initiation and during dose increases and decreases. Monitor for sero-
   tonin syndrome during therapy and for withdrawal symptoms during drug discontinuation.
   Regularly monitor height and weight during long-term treatment. Periodically reassess need
   for continued treatment. Carefully monitor patients with controlled epilepsy for increased
   seizure frequency during use 2 ,4 .', 'Monitor for clinical worsening, suicidality, and unusual changes in behavior, especially within
the first few months of initiation and during dose increases and decreases. Monitor for sero-
tones series. The specific inhibition of serotonin reuptake in brain neurons is presumed to be', 'Pharmacology
   fluvoxaMINE maleate is a potent SSRI belonging to the 2-aminoethyl oxime ethers of aralkylke-
   tones series. The specific inhibition of serotonin reuptake in brain neurons is presumed to be
   responsible for the clinical effect in obsessive-compulsive disorder. fluvoxaMINE maleate had
   no significant affinity for histaminergic, alpha or beta adrenergic, muscarinic, or dopaminergic
   receptors based on in vitro studies. Bioavailability: 53% (oral, immediate-release tablet) and
   84% (oral, extended-release capsule). Food does not significantly affect absorption. Vd is 25
   L/kg; approximately 80% bound to plasma protein, primarily albumin. Extensively metabolized
   in the liver via oxidative demethylation and deamination. Average 94% of drug-related prod-
   ucts recovered in urine within 71 hours. Half-life at steady state is 6 hours 2 , 5/8
In a multiple-dose study of immediate-release fluvoxaMINE maleate tablets in children age 6 to
   11 years, adolescents age 12 to 17 years, and adults, children, especially females, experience
   higher exposure to fluvoxaMINE compared with adolescents and adults. Following oral admin-
   istration of 100 mg twice daily, children (n=10) exhibited a mean AUC of 1 nanograms x
   hour/milliliter/kg (ng x hr/m/kgL) compared with 9 ng x hr/mL/kg in adolescents (n=17).
   Following oral administration of 150 mg twice daily, adolescents (n=13) exhibited a mean AUC
