   in children older than 5 years of age (n=22) and in children 2 to 5 years of age (n=17). The phar-
   macokinetic parameters were similar between younger children who received the extempora-
   neous compounded 100 mg/mL solution (cherry syrup) and older children who received either
   the capsule or an extemporaneous compounded solution. The capsule and solution were nearly
   100% bioequivalent Cmax: 8 mcg/mL for the solution and capsule in children older than 5 years and 4
   mcg/mL for the solution in children 2 to 5 years of age Tmax: 86 hours for the solution and capsule in children older than 5 years and 57 for the
   solution in children 2 to 5 years of age AUCinfinity: 1 mcg X hr/mL for the solution and capsule in children older than 5 years and 5 mcg X hr/mL for the solution in children 2 to 5 years of age Half-life: 3 hours for the solution and capsule in children older than 5 years and 96 hours
   for the solution in children 2 to 5 years of age CL: 2 L x hr/kg for the solution and capsule in children older than 5 years and 234 L x hr/kg
   for the solution in children 2 to 5 years of age Absorption: Hydroxyurea reaches peak plasma concentrations within 1 to 4 hours of oral ad-
   ministration. Mean peak plasma concentrations and AUCs increase more than proportionally
   as the hydroxyurea dose increases Distribution: Hydroxyurea distributes throughout the body and has a volume of distribution
   approximating total body water; concentrates in leukocytes and erythrocytes Excretion: Approximately 40% of hydroxyurea was excreted in the urine 9/13
