Elimination

   Teriflunomide is eliminated by direct biliary excretion of unchanged drug and renal excretion
   of metabolites. Over a 21 day period, 5% of teriflunomide was excreted via feces compared
   with 6% via urine. An additional 1% of teriflunomide was recovered following an acceler-
   ated drug elimination procedure with cholestyramine Total body clearance of teriflunomide was 5 mL/hr The elimination of teriflunomide can be accelerated with administration of cholestyramine or
   activated charcoal. Without the use of an accelerated drug elimination procedure, it make take
   up to 2 years for teriflunomide to reach concentrations of less than 02 mg/L Half-Life

   Teriflunomide has a median half-life of 18 to 19 days. Therefore, a loading dose of 100 mg was
   administered for 3 days to facilitate rapid attainment of steady state plasma concentrations Concentration in Pediatric Patients: Teriflunomide steady-state concentrations were 9+/-20.4 mcg/mL, 30+/-19.3 mcg/mL, and 5+/-7.2 mcg/mL in patients weighing more
   than 40 kg, patients weighing 20 to 40 kg, and patients weighing less than 20 kg, respectively Population Pharmacokinetics in Pediatric: Teriflunomide was well described as exhibiting
   one-compartment model with first-order kinetics in a population pharmacokinetic study of 73
   pediatric patients (3 to 17 years of age). The apparent volume of distribution was 8 X
   (weight/40 kg)0.769 and the total oral clearance was 020 X (weight/40 kg)0.43 . Doses to
   achieve a steady concentration, which were comparable to the concentration in adults on 20
   mg/day, were 10 mg/day for children 10 to 20 kg, 15 mg/day for children 20 to 40 kg, and 20
   mg/day for children greater than 40 kg. In adults, leflunomide 20 mg/day attained terifluno-
   mide median concentration of 34 mg/L at steady state'),
