   lipodystrophy (non-HIV-related) and diabetes, insulin resistance, or hypertriglyceridemia (n=55;
   53% pediatric patients) treated with metreleptin had significantly improved HbA1C, triglyc-
   erides, LDL, total cholesterol, and liver function test (ie, AST and ALT) results at 4 months, with
   sustained benefits for 3 years of therapy. All patients had low fasting leptin levels (less than 8
   ng/mL and 12 ng/mL for males and females, respectively). Patients (mean age, 25 years; range,
   7 to 68 years) self-administered metreleptin subQ once or twice daily via a weight- and sex-
   based dosing protocol (mean dose, 75 mg in males; 55 mg in females). Patients with ele-
   vated HbA1C, triglycerides, and liver enzyme levels at baseline had a more marked response.
