   higher definite cure rate in the miltefosine treatment group compared with placebo for the
   treatment of cutaneous leishmaniasis. Eligible patients, 12 years or older with newly diagnosed
   or relapsing cutaneous leishmaniasis without mucosal involvement. The study demonstrated a
   66% definite cure rate (primary endpoint, defined as complete epithelialization of all lesions 6
   months after completion of therapy) in the miltefosine treatment group compared with 30% in
   the placebo group (between group difference, 8% (95% CI, 1% to 4%) 2 , 1/7
In patients 12 to 65 years from 2 areas of Brazil known epidemiologically to be prevalent of the
   infecting Leishmania braziliensis (Bahia, Brazil) and Leishmania guyanensis (Manaus, Brazil)
   species, treatment with oral miltefosine achieved definite cure (complete re-epithelialization of
   ulcer at 6 months after end of therapy) in 5% (27 of 40) and 85% (34 of 40) of patients, re-
   spectively Visceral leishmaniasis: In a randomized, open-label study (398 patients 12 years or older), a
   28-day course of oral miltefosine demonstrated non-inferiority to a 30-day course of IV am-
   photericin B for the treatment of visceral leishmaniasis in an area known to be endemic for the
   infecting Leishmania donovani species (Bahir, India). At the end of treatment, splenic aspirates
   were obtained from 293 of 299 patients in the miltefosine group and 98 of 99 in the ampho-
   tericin B group. One hundred percent of the aspirates were parasitologically negative, thus,
   the initial cure rate for both groups was 100%. At the 6-month follow up, 100 patients contin-
   ued to have signs and symptoms of visceral leishmaniasis; in 73 of these patients symptoms
   were attributed to other causes, the remaining 27 patients underwent splenic or bone marrow
   aspiration. Leishmania amastigotes were found in the aspirate of 9 (3%) patients treated with
   miltefosine, indicating relapse, and none of the patients treated with amphotericin. Final cure,
   defined as initial cure plus absence of signs and symptoms of visceral leishmaniasis at 6-
   month follow-up was 94% (95% CI, 91% to 97%) and 97% (95% CI, 91% to 99%) for miltefosine
   and amphotericin B subjects, respectively 2 ,4 .

   Short-course combination therapy: A combination of miltefosine for 7 days with a single-
   dose of liposomal amphotericin B, or a combination of miltefosine for 10 days with paro-
   momycin for 10 days worked as well as the standard 30 days of amphotericin B therapy for vis-
   ceral leishmaniasis in a study of 634 patients (6 years or older). Oral dosages of miltefosine
   were 5 mg/kg/day for children younger than 12 years, 50 mg once daily for body weights less
   than 25 kg, and 50 mg twice daily for body weights 25 kg or more 5
   Younger than 12 years: Doses of 5 to 5 mg/kg/day provided a clinical cure rate of 88% to
   90% in Indian children, 3 to 11 years of age, with visceral leishmaniasis in a dose range study
   (n=39) A dose of 5 mg/kg/day in 79 Indian children 3 to 10 years of age resulted in a final
   cure rate of 94% FDA approved indication: Miltefosine is indicated for the treatment in adults and adolescents
   12 years or older weighing 30 kg (66 lbs) or greater. For the following 1 :
    ●   Visceral leishmaniasis caused by Leishmania donovani species
    ●   Cutaneous leishmaniasis caused by Leishmania braziliensis, Leishmania guyanensis, and
        Leishmania panamensis species
    ●   Mucosal leishmaniasis caused by Leishmania braziliensis species

Limitations of Use 1
    ●   Leishmania species studied in clinical trials evaluating miltefosine were based on epidemio-
        logic data.
    ●   Clinical response to Leishmania species may vary by geographic location.
    ●   Efficacy to other Leishmania species has not been established.

