   The specific mechanism of action of miltefosine is unknown. Miltefosine antileishmanial activ-
   ity is suspected to be exerted by interaction with phospholipids and sterols, including mem-
   brane lipids, inhibition of mitochondrial CYP-c oxidase, and apoptosis-like cell death Mean Cmax, median Tmax, and mean AUC were 2 mcg/mL, 7 hours, and 636 mcg x hr/mL
   with 50 mg twice daily and 9 mcg/mL, 4 hours, and 486 mcg x hr/mL, respectively with 50
   mg 3 times daily. Absolute bioavailability has not specifically been determined; absorption ap-
   pears to continue throughout the dosing interval as maximal concentrations are reached just
   prior to next dose. Plasma protein binding is 98%. CYP450-mediated metabolism is negligible.
   Miltefosine undergoes a slow metabolic breakdown in human hepatocytes by phospholipase
   D-like cleavage into choline and a fatty alcohol-containing remnant. The fatty alcohol-contain-
   ing remnant is oxidized to palmitic acid then enters the fatty acid metabolism pathway.
   Oxidation is blocked in patients with Sjogren-Larsson syndrome. The miltefosine terminal half-
   live was 7 days'),
