   Oral

   Minocycline hydrochloride oral preparations (Minocin® capsules and suspension, minocycline
   hydrochloride [generic] tablets) are indicated in the treatment of asymptomatic carriers of
   Neisseria meningitidis and infections caused by Mycobacterium marinum. Reserve prophylactic
   use for Neisseria meningitidis carriers for situations in which the risk of meningococcal meningi-
   tis is high 3 , 2 , 4

   Oral, Extended-Release
   Minocycline hydrochloride extended-release (Minolira™, Solodyn®, and Ximino®) is indicated
   for the treatment of inflammatory lesions of nonnodular, moderate to severe acne vulgaris in
   patients 12 years or older 6 ,7 ,8 . Other minocycline forms (Minocin® capsules and suspension,
   minocycline hydrochloride [generic] tablets) may be useful adjunctive therapy for severe acne
   in patients older than 8 years 3 ,4 ,2 .

   Minocycline micronized topical foam (Amzeeq™) is indicated for the topical treatment of in-
   flammatory lesions of non-nodular moderate to severe acne vulgaris in patients 9 years or
   older Intravenous and Oral

   Minocycline hydrochloride IV (Minocin®) and oral preparations (Minocin® capsules and sus-
   pension, minocycline hydrochloride [generic] tablets) have been approved for the following in-
   fections due to susceptible isolates of the designated bacteria 3 ,2 ,4 ,5 :
    ●   Bartonellosis due Bartonella bacilliformis
    ●   Brucellosis due to Brucella species (in conjunction with streptomycin)
    ●   Campylobacter fetus infections caused by Campylobacter fetus
    ●   Cholera caused by Vibrio cholerae
