   6 years to less than 15 years of age: 5 mg orally as a single dose, prior to exercise 15 years or older: 10 mg orally as a single dose, prior to exercise', 'Allergic rhinitis: First line drug treatment includes an oral antihistamine (second generation
   preferred) or intranasal corticosteroid 7 ,8 . Patients with moderate to severe symptoms may
   require combination therapy with an oral antihistamine and an intranasal corticosteroid with
   low systemic absorption (eg, fluticasone) Oral rather than intranasal antihistamines are pre-
   ferred for routine use 8 , however, intranasal agents may help reduce nasal congestion Oral
   decongestants may be considered as rescue or as needed medication to combat nasal conges-
   tion, but should not be used on a regular basis. Intranasal decongestants may be considered
   in older children, but should only be used for 3 to 5 days Montelukast 4 mg daily and cetirizine 5 mg daily both reduced total rhinitis symptom scores,
   nasal airway resistance, percentage of eosinophils in nasal smears, and values from the
   Pediatric Rhinoconjunctivitis Quality of Life Questionnaire in a randomized, placebo-controlled
   trial in young children with perennial AR (n=60; age 2 to 6 years). Cetirizine was more effective
   than montelukast in the reduction of total rhinitis symptom scores and nasal itching, however,
   montelukast provided better night sleep quality. Neither drug reduced serum IgE or blood
   eosinophil counts Combination therapy with montelukast 5 mg and loratadine 10 mg daily
   produced a lower pulmonary symptom score and a smaller decrease in FEF 25-75 compared to
   loratadine alone in children with seasonal AR who received treatment during grass pollen sea-
   son in an open-label, cross-over clinical trial (n=32; mean age 10 years). There was no differ-
   ence between the two groups for nasal flow rates, nasal symptoms scores, quality of life
   scores or eye symptoms The addition of montelukast to fluticasone propionate aqueous
   nasal spray (FPANS) did not produce a substantial advantage over FPANS monotherapy in the
   reduction of nasal symptoms in a randomized, placebo-controlled trial of FPANS monotherapy
   compared to the addition of cetirizine, montelukast to FPANS or a combination of cetirizine
   and montelukast in adults and adolescents with seasonal AR (n=100; age 12 to 50 years) 2/12
Asthma: The type, amount and scheduling of medication is determined by the level of asthma
   severity or asthma control. For intermittent symptoms, a short-acting beta2-agonist (SABA),
   with use as needed, is recommended. For persistent asthma, inhaled corticosteroids (ICS) are
   preferred for daily, long-term control therapy; the dose is dependent on the frequency and/or
   severity of symptoms. Montelukast is an alternative for mild asthma and can be used as ad-
   junctive therapy in combination with ICS in pediatric patients younger than 12 years of age; a
   long-acting beta2-agonist is the preferred adjunctive therapy for adolescents. All asthmatic pa-
   tients should have a SABA available to treat acute exacerbations, regardless of long-term con-
   troller therapy Montelukast 5 mg did not improve measures of FEV1, beta agonist use, asthma scores (day or
   night), or peak expiratory flow rates (PEFR; AM or PM) when added to usual therapy in a ran-
   domized, placebo-controlled trial of children with mild to moderate asthma having docu-
   mented sensitivity to aeroallergens (n=421: mean age 8 years). Subgroup analyses did not
   reveal any differences between active and placebo treatment groups. When measured by in-
   vestigator and parent global asthma evaluations, however, montelukast was considered more
   effective than placebo Chronic idiopathic/spontaneous urticaria: The guideline for management of chronic ur-
   ticaria from the World Allergy Organization and other professional groups recommends
   nonsedating antihistamines as first line therapy, with dose escalation, up to 4 times the usual
   dose, if needed. If symptoms continue, a different nonsedating antihistamine or an addition of
   a leukotriene antagonist is recommended In a retrospective review of patients with chronic idiopathic urticaria (n=18; age 9 to 60 years),
   the addition of either montelukast or zafirlukast produced dramatic improvement in 10/18 pa-
   tients within 1 week, with resolution of symptoms within 1 month. The dose of montelukast
   ranged from 5 mg once daily to 10 mg twice daily In a second retrospective review (n=25;
   age 13 to 66 years), the addition of montelukast 10 mg once daily improved symptoms in 48%
   of patients; 11/25 saw no difference and 2/25 patients worsened Montelukast 10 mg once daily combined with desloratadine 5 mg was more effective than
   desloratadine alone in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled comparative trial in
   adolescents and adults with chronic urticaria (n=81). The combination produced greater im-
   provement in overall symptom scores, reduced the number and size of wheals, and reduced
   the mean pruritus score compared with desloratadine alone, however, the number of sepa-
   rate urticarial episodes was not different between the 2 active treatments Chronic urticaria: Montelukast 10 mg once daily was more effective than cetirizine 10 mg
   once daily in a randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled comparison trial in adolescents

and adults with chronic urticaria to aspirin and/or food additives (n=51; age 15 to 71 years).
   Montelukast produced a higher median percentage of symptom-free days for hive, itch and
   angioedema than cetirizine; it also resulted in less interference with sleep and fewer days re-
   quiring rescue medication Cystic fibrosis (CF): The Cystic Fibrosis Foundation concluded that there is insufficient evi-
   dence to recommend for or against the routine chronic use of leukotriene modifiers to im-
   prove lung function or reduce exacerbations in children 6 years or older 18 and children 2
   through 5 years of age Exercise-induced bronchoconstriction (EIB): An inhaled, short-acting beta2-agonist (SABA) is
   the most effective agent to protect against EIB when used prophylactically before exercise or
   after exercise to accelerate recovery of FEV1; attenuation or prevention of EIB is seen in most
   patients with use. Because daily use raises concerns for tolerance to its therapeutic effects and
   potential to worsen EIB severity, even when used in combination with an inhaled corticos-
   teroid (ICS), SABA are recommended for use only on an intermittent basis. Leukotriene in-
   hibitors (LTRA), such as montelukast, are effective with intermittent or daily use and do not
   lead to tolerance. Patients rarely achieve complete protection from EIB with montelukast, how-
   ever, with approximately half of patients achieving 30% to 80% attenuation of symptoms. Mast
   cell stabilizers (MCS) also attenuate EIB in approximately half of patients. Since LTRA and MCS
   do not have bronchodilator activity, neither can reverse airway obstruction once it occurs. EIB
   in patients with chronic asthma suggests inadequate control of underlying asthma; anti-in-
   flammatory therapy should be maximized until control is achieved ICS, alone or in combi-
   nation with other agents, are first line treatment for chronic asthma Montelukast monotherapy and budesonide plus montelukast produced greater improvement
   in AUC and maximum percent drop in FEV1 as compared to budesonide monotherapy or
   budesonide plus formoterol following a treadmill exercise challenge in a randomized, placebo-
   controlled, comparative trial in children with mild to moderate asthma (n=100; age 6 to 18
   years). All active treatments were more effective than placebo Regular treatment did not
   promote tolerance to its protective effect over a 4-week study period Henoch-Schönlein purpura (HSP), adjunct: Three months of adjunct montelukast improved
   symptoms, shortened length of hospital stay, and lowered some serum biomarkers (e.g. inter-
   leukin and eosinophil count) in 130 children with acute HSP, with or without nephritis, in a
   double-blind, placebo-controlled, randomized study. Oral doses were 4 mg/day and 5 mg/day
   for children 2 to 5 and 6 to 14 years of age, respectively 4/12
Obstructive sleep apnea (OSA): Montelukast improved symptoms related to OSA (eg, restless
   sleep, mouth breathing, and snoring) and reduced the obstructive apnea index (OAI), adenoid
   size, and time spent in stage 1 sleep in a 12-week, randomized, placebo-controlled trial of chil-
   dren with nonsevere OSA (n=46; age older than 2 and younger than 10 years). There was also a
