   Black Box Warning
   Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy

   Natalizumab increases the risk of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), an op-
   portunistic viral infection of the brain that usually leads to death or severe disability. Risk fac-
   tors for the development of PML include the presence of anti-JCV antibodies, duration of ther-
   apy, and prior use of immunosuppressants. These factors should be considered in the context
   of expected benefit when initiating and continuing treatment with natalizumab 4/9
• Healthcare professionals should monitor patients on natalizumab for any new sign or symp-
   tom that may be suggestive of PML. Natalizumab dosing should be withheld immediately at
   the first sign or symptom suggestive of PML. For diagnosis, an evaluation that includes a
   gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) scan of the brain and, when indi-
   cated, cerebrospinal fluid analysis for JC viral DNA are recommended • Because of the risk of PML, natalizumab is available only through a restricted program under
   a Risk Evaluation and Mitigation Strategy (REMS) called the TOUCH® Prescribing Program Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Evaluate the patient, as required by the TOUCH(R) Prescribing Program, 3 and 6 months after
   the first infusion, every 6 months during therapy, and for at least 6 months after discontinua-
   tion • A reduction in annualized relapse rate and a longer time to onset of a sustained increase in
   disability are indicative of efficacy.

   Toxic Monitoring

   • Assess anti-JC virus (JCV) antibodies using an analytically and clinically validated immunoas-
   say. However, anti-JCV antibody testing should not be used to diagnose progressive multifocal
   leukoencephalopathy (PML). Patients with a negative anti-JCV antibody test result should be
   retested periodically due to the risk of development of PML for a new JCV infection or a false
   negative test result. A patient with a positive anti-JCV antibody test at any time is anti-JCV anti-
   body positive regardless of the results of any prior or subsequent anti-JCV antibody testing. To
   avoid false-negative test results, do not test for anti-JCV antibodies within 2 weeks of plasma
   exchange or within 6 months of IV immunoglobulin • Serum antibodies to natalizumab may be tested in select patients, particularly if presence of
   antibodies is suspected due to infusion-related reactions. Repeat testing, 3 months after initial
   positive result, is recommended to confirm the presence of persistent antibodies. Consider an-
   tibody testing in patients who wish to recommence therapy following a dose interruption • Obtain a brain MRI scan in patients with multiple sclerosis (MS) prior to initiating therapy • Observe patients during all infusions for signs or symptoms consistent with a hypersensitiv-
   ity-type reaction. For the first 12 infusions, also observe for 1 hour after the infusion is com-
   plete. Use clinical judgement regarding the ongoing need for post-infusion monitoring for the
   13th and subsequent infusions if no hypersensitivity reactions have occurred 5/9
• Observe for signs/symptoms suggestive of progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy
   (PML) (eg, progressive weakness on one side of the body or clumsiness of limbs, disturbance
   of vision, and changed in thinking, memory, and orientation leading to confusion and person-
   ality changes) during treatment and for 6 months following discontinuation • Consider obtaining MRI scan periodically to evaluate for radiographic signs of PML and con-
   sider monitoring patients at high-risk of PML more frequently. MRI findings and the presence
   of JCV DNA in the cerebrospinal fluid in the absence of clinical signs or symptoms specific to
   PML, have been reported • Monitor for signs and symptoms of meningitis and encephalitis • Monitor patient for development of infection', 'PML, have been reported • Monitor for signs and symptoms of meningitis and encephalitis • Monitor patient for development of infection • Vd: 7 L for multiple sclerosis patients 1', '• Vd: 7 L for multiple sclerosis patients 1

