  (25°C/77°F) Extemporaneous oral compound

  Note: A 6 mg/mL oral suspension is available commercially. During emergency situations and when neither the oral-suspension or age-appro-
  priate strengths of capsules are available, an emergency supply of oral suspension may be compounded from the 75 mg capsules. The com-
  pounded suspension yields a 6 mg/mL concentration (same as commercially available 6 mg/mL suspension) and total volume adequate for 1 pa-
  tient for a 5-day course of treatment or a 10-day course of prophylaxis 6 mg/mL suspension 1

  • Determine dose, required number of 75 mg oseltamivir capsules, and total volume required for compounding. For a dose of 15 mg or less, 3
  capsules are required and total volume is 5 mL; for 30 mg, 6 capsules are required and total volume is 75 mL; for 45 mg, 8 capsules are re-
  quired and total volume is 100 mL; for 60 mg, 10 capsules are required and total volume is 125 mL; for 75 mg, 12 capsules are required and total
  volume is 150 mL. If the dose is between these doses, default to the next greater dose and volume.

  • Place specified amount of water into a polyethyleneterephthalate (PET) or glass bottle: 5 mL for 3 capsules; 5 mL for 6 capsules; 7 mL for 8
  capsules; 8 mL for 10 capsules; 10 mL for 12 capsules.
  • Transfer contents of required number of oseltamivir 75 mg capsules into the PET or glass bottle and gently swirl for at least 2 minutes to wet
  the powder.
  • Slowly add the specified volume of vehicle to the bottle: 5 mL for 3 capsules (total volume, 5 mL); 69 mL for 6 capsules (total volume, 75
  mL); 91 mL for 8 capsules (total volume, 100 mL); 115 mL for 10 capsules (total volume, 125 mL); 137 mL for 12 capsules (total volume, 150 mL).
  Vehicle options include cherry syrup, Ora-Sweet SF®, or simple syrup.

  • Close the bottle and shake well for 30 seconds to dissolve active drug.
  • Label with "Shake well before use."
  • Stable for 35 days when refrigerated (2 to 8°C/36 to 46°F) or for 5 days at room temperature.', 'Common (greater than 1%)
  2 weeks to less than 1 year: In a study of 135 infants receiving oseltamivir for treatment of influenza, the most common adverse events were
  vomiting (9%), diarrhea (7%), diaper rash (7%) 1 ,2 .

1 to 12 years: In a study of 859 pediatric patients receiving oseltamivir for treatment of influenza, the most common adverse effect was vomit-
  ing (1% or more). Vomiting (8%) was also the most common adverse effect in 148 pediatric patients receiving oseltamivir for influenza prophy-
  laxis 1 ,2 .
  13 years and older: In a study of 2646 patients receiving oseltamivir for treatment of influenza, the most common adverse effects were nausea
  (10%), vomiting (8%), and headache (2%). In a study of 1943 patients receiving oseltamivir for influenza prophylaxis, the most common adverse
  effects were headache (17%), nausea (8%), pain (4%), and vomiting (2%) 1 ,2 .

  Cough (13.2%), rhinitis (10%), pyrexia (12.1%), diarrhea (not reported (NR)), vomiting (NR), nasal congestion (3.5%), bronchitis (5.6%), and upper
  respiratory tract infection (1.5%) were observed in children 24 months or younger (median age, 12 months) treated with oseltamivir (n= 340) for
  flu-like symptoms in an observational study. A sample size of 3000 is required to detect uncommon adverse events In a retrospective case study (n=180) in infants less than 12 months of age, neurological events were not more common in infants treated with
  oseltamivir compared with those treated with amantadine or rimantadine Monitoring
  Closely monitor patients with influenza for neuropsychiatric symptoms or abnormal behavior 1 ,2 .', '', 'Renal: 99% 1 ,2

  Clearance, 2 weeks to less than 1 year of age: decreases with decreasing age 1 ,2
  Clearance, 1 year to less than 12 years: decreases with increasing age; younger subjects clear both the prodrug and the active metabolite faster
  than adult subjects 1 ,2

  Elimination Half Life

  Parent compound: 1 to 3 hours 1 ,2

  Oseltamivir carboxylate: 6 to 10 hours 1 ,2'),
