   Use of peripheral nerve stimulator is recommended to assess drug response, need for addi-
   tional doses, and recovery Train-of-four (TOF) monitoring with clinical assessment, rather than TOF monitoring alone, is
   recommended in critically ill pediatric patients in the ICU', '', 'Pharmacology
   Nondepolarizing muscle-relaxant that competitively antagonizes autonomic cholinergic recep-
   tors and also causes sympathetic stimulation. ED95 values greatest in children compared with
   infants and adolescents (mean of 93 mcg/kg in children compared with 66 mcg/kg in infants
   and 77 mcg/kg in adolescents). Onset of action is approximately 2 to 3 minutes depending on
   age (shortest in infants and longest in adolescents). Time to maximal effect of 7 minutes.
   Longer duration of action; 40 to 60 minutes for recovery time (dose and age dependent).
   Distribution half-life of approximately 6 minutes. Volume of distribution of approximately 2
   L/kg. Strong binding to gamma globulin and moderate binding to albumin. Approximately 13%
   unbound to plasma protein. Partially hydroxylated by the liver, 40% excreted unchanged in
   urine. Reversed by neostigmine in conjunction with atropine. Elimination half-life approxi-
   mately 100 minutes in children. Elimination half-life is doubled and clearance decreased by
   60% in patients with renal failure and/or hepatic/biliary tract disease (slow neuromuscular
   recovery) 1 , 13 , 5 , 6 , 14 , 7 , Factors affecting duration of neuromuscular blockade:
   Potentiation: Acidosis, hypothermia, neuromuscular disease, hepatic disease, renal failure,
   cardiovascular disease, younger age, aminoglycosides, hypermagnesemia, and hypokalemia.

   Antagonism: Alkalosis, epinephrine, and hyperkalemia 15,16,1 .'),
