   Optimal maintenance dose, timing, and duration have not been established. Week 1: 6 mg/kg/dose orally twice daily, at least 9 hours apart 1/18
Week 2: 1 mg/kg/dose orally twice daily Week 3: 7 mg/kg/dose orally twice daily for 6 months. Adjust the dose periodically as the
   child''s weight increases. May re-initiate if hemangiomas recur 2.3 to 4 mg/kg/day was rec-
   ommended (guideline dosing) Continuation of therapy until full involution of the lesion has occurred or until 1 year of age
   has been recommended 18 , typically continue until at least 8 to 12 months of age (treatment
   duration of 3 to 12 months) Recurrences have been reported with early discontinuation of
   therapy Tapering periods have ranged from 2 weeks to 1 month 20 , 21 , 22 , 23 , Dose adjustment: In infants with PHACE syndrome (posterior fossa defects, hemangiomas,
   cerebrovascular arterial anomalies, cardiovascular anomalies, and eye anomalies), especially
   in the presence of neurovascular anomalies, slowly titrate dose, use the lowest effective dose,
   and administer in 3 divided doses. Lower doses may also be necessary in patients with pro-
   gressive IH ulceration while receiving therapy and in patients who experience adverse effects
   (e.g. sleep disturbances) Long QT Syndrome (LQT1): 2 to 3 mg/kg/day orally 25,26 ; give immediate-release products in
   3 to 4 divided doses; give extended-release capsules in 1 or 2 divided doses 5 , Maximum
   dose, 240 to 320 mg/day 7

   Migraine Prophylaxis
   3 years of age and older:

   20 to 40 mg orally 3 times daily (guideline dosing) 27

   Supraventricular Tachyarrhythmias

   Initial: 5 to 1 mg/kg/day orally; give immediate-release products in 3 to 4 divided doses; give
   extended-release capsules in 1 or 2 divided doses 28 , 29 , 30 , 1 , Higher initial doses (2
   mg/kg/day) have also been used for the treatment of supraventricular tachycardias and long
   QT syndrome 32,33,34,35,36 .
   Titration and Maintenance: May increase by 1 mg/kg/day increments every 3 to 4 days up to
   a maximum of 6 mg/kg/day orally. Usual maintenance dose is 1 to 4 mg/kg/day; give immedi-
   ate-release products in 3 to 4 divided doses; give extended-release capsules in 1 or 2 divided
   doses 28,29,37,32,33,30,34,1 ,31 . Doses as high as 14 to 16 mg/kg/day have been used in children
   to control supraventricular tachycardias 29 , 38 , 35 , Tetralogy of Fallot, Prevention of Hypoxemic Spells: Initial, 1 mg/kg/day (0.25 mg/kg every 6
   hours) orally for first week; if treatment is ineffective, may titrate in increments of 1 mg/kg/day

to a maximum of 5 mg/kg/day, allowing 24 hours at each new dose. Treatment success was as-
   sociated with larger mean daily doses (2.57 vs 16 mg/kg) 39', 'Indicated for the treatment of proliferating infantile hemangioma requiring systemic therapy.
   Initiate treatment at age 5 weeks to 5 months. Safety and effectiveness for infantile heman-
   gioma have not been established in pediatric patients older than 1 year 6/18
Administration
   Intravenous: Administer 1 mg/mL concentration at a rate not to exceed 1 mg per minute Oral: Administer during or right after a feeding, skip the dose if the child is not eating or is
   vomiting Oral Extended Release: (Innopran XL®) Administered once daily at bedtime consistently ei-
   ther with or without food', 'Adverse effects are related to beta-receptor blockade. Significant reductions in myocardial
   function and hypotension resulting in cardiac compromise have been reported in association
   with beta-blockers, especially during IV administration in infants. May cause AV block.
   Bradycardia, fatigue, headache, dizziness, somnolence, diarrhea, anorexia, insomnia, personal-
   ity changes, and depression are the most common adverse effects reported in children.
   Bronchospasm may occur. Can mask symptoms of hypoglycemia in patients with diabetes; use
   with caution. Case reports of hypoglycemia occurring in children after therapeutic doses, in ab-
   sence of diabetes 2 , 67 , 68 , 1 , 38 , May mask certain clinical signs of hyperthyroidism. A with-
   drawal syndrome (nervousness, tachycardia, sweating, hypertension) has been associated with
   sudden cessation of the drug 2 ,67,68,1 ,38,72 .

   Infantile hemangioma: The most common (10% or more) adverse events in infants treated
   for infantile hemangioma were sleep disorders, aggravated respiratory tract infections, periph-
   eral coldness, agitation, diarrhea, somnolence, nightmare, irritability, decreased appetite, ab-
   dominal pain, and vomiting Asymptomatic and symptomatic hypoglycemia, requiring hospitalization, have been reported
   in infants receiving propranolol for the treatment of infantile hemangioma. Infants less than 3
   months of age are at increased risk 73 , 74 , Hypoglycemia including hypoglycemic seizures
   were typically associated with poor oral intake or concomitant infection Concomitant corti-
   costeroids increase the risk of hypoglycemia Within 2 hours the effect on heart rate and blood pressure were evident 9/18
Solution Compatibility
   D5W and NS.

   Terminal Injection Site Compatibility
   Alteplase, dobutamine, heparin, hydrocortisone succinate, linezolid, milrinone, morphine,
   potassium chloride, and propofol.

   Monitoring
   Continuous ECG monitoring should be done during acute treatment of arrhythmias and during
   IV therapy. Measure systemic blood pressure frequently. Monitor vital signs and measure
   blood glucose during initiation of treatment and after dosage changes. Assess for increased
   airway resistance 16,23,2 ,67,68 .
   When treating infantile hemangiomas, monitor heart rate and blood pressure for 2 hours after
   the first or increasing dose Perform a complete history and physical examination with par-
   ticular attention to cardiac and pulmonary systems. Electrocardiography may be considered,
   particularly in younger infants, those with a low heart rate, and those at risk of congenital
   heart disease (by examination or family history) Before treating large facial infantile he-
   mangioma with propranolol, assess for potential arteriopathy associated with PHACE syn-
   drome', 'therapy Tapering periods have ranged from 2 weeks to 1 month 20 , 21 , 22 , 23 , Dose adjustment: In infants with PHACE syndrome (posterior fossa defects, hemangiomas,
constriction (reduction of blood flow to the hemangioma), inhibition of angiogenesis (de-
creased expression of vascular endothelial growth factor and inhibition of tubulogenesis of en-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Propranolol is the most widely used nonselective β-adrenergic-recep-
   tor blocking agent. Possesses no other autonomic nervous system activity. Potential molecular
   mechanisms of action for propranolol in the treatment of infantile hemangioma include vaso-
   constriction (reduction of blood flow to the hemangioma), inhibition of angiogenesis (de-
   creased expression of vascular endothelial growth factor and inhibition of tubulogenesis of en-
   dothelial cells), and induction of apoptosis in endothelial cells 10/18
Peak serum concentration has reached approximately 1 to 4 hours after an oral dose.
   Propranolol undergoes significant first-pass hepatic metabolism, resulting in 25% bioavailabil-
   ity. Protein binding is approximately 90%. Volume of distribution is 4 to 5 L/kg. Extensively me-
   tabolized by aromatic hydroxylation, N-dealkylation, and direct glucuronidation to 4 major me-
   tabolites. Elimination is by renal excretion of metabolites. Elimination half-life is 3 to 4 hours in
   younger children, and 6 hours in older children. Serum half-life is prolonged in patients with
   liver disease 2 ,67,68,29 .
   At steady state in 23 infants (35 to 150 days of age) administered 4 mg/kg/day twice daily, the
   Tmax was 2 hours, clearance was 7 L/hr/kg in infants younger than 90 days and 3 L/hr/kg in
   infants older than 90 days, and half-life was 5 hours. Plasma propranolol concentrations in-
   creased in proportion to dose increases within the dose range of 2 mg/kg/day to 4
   mg/kg/day. The main metabolite is 4-OH-propranolol, which is about 5% of total plasma expo-
   sure of propranolol'),
