tine testing in patients taking long-term proton pump inhibitors Pharmacology
RABEprazole is a benzimidazole proton pump inhibitor. RABEprazole suppresses gastric acid
secretion by specific inhibition of the hydrogen-potassium adenosine triphosphatase (H+, K+-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   RABEprazole is a benzimidazole proton pump inhibitor. RABEprazole suppresses gastric acid
   secretion by specific inhibition of the hydrogen-potassium adenosine triphosphatase (H+, K+-
   ATPase) enzyme system found at the secretory surface of parietal cells. RABEprazole blocks
   the final step of gastric acid secretion. Since the H+, K+-ATPase enzyme system is regarded as
   the acid (proton) pump of the gastric mucosa, RABEprazole is known as a gastric acid pump in-
   hibitor. RABEprazole does not have anticholinergic or histamine H2-receptor antagonist prop-
   erties 2 ,1 .

   Tmax: 2 to 4 hours in pediatric patients 1 to 11 years of age Protein Binding: 3% bound to human plasma proteins 2 ,1
   Excretion: According to radiolabeled studies, 90% of an oral RABEprazole dose is eliminated in
   the urine, primarily as thioether carboxylic acid; its glucuronide, and mercapturic acid metabo-
   lites. No unchanged drug is recovered 2 ,1 .

   Half-life: 5 hours in pediatric patients 1 to 11 years of age 2 , 1
   Metabolism: RABEprazole is extensively metabolized to a thioether, a sulphone, and a
   desmethyl metabolite, none of which have significant antisecretory activity. The thioether me-
   tabolite is formed by the reduction of RABEprazole, while the sulphone metabolite is formed
   via CYP3A and desmethyl RABEprazole via CYP2C19 2 ,1 .'),
