Raltegravir is a HIV integrase strand transfer inhibitor (HIV-1 INSTI) Absorption: Peak concentration with a dose of 400 mg twice daily is reached approximately 3
ferase (UGT1A1)-mediated glucuronidation pathway Excretion: Fecal and renal excretion is approximately 51% and 32%, respectively Half-life: Elimination half-life is approximately 9 hours 1 ,2 .', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of Action

   Raltegravir is a HIV integrase strand transfer inhibitor (HIV-1 INSTI) Absorption: Peak concentration with a dose of 400 mg twice daily is reached approximately 3
   hours after dosing in the fasted state. With a dose of 1200 mg once daily, the median Tmax is
   about 5 to 2 hours in the fasted state.
   Bioavailability is greater for the chewable tablets and oral suspension compared with the
   400-mg film-coated tablets, and bioavailability is greater for the oral suspension compared

with the chewable tablets. Compared with the 400-mg formulation, the 600-mg film-coated
   tablet has higher bioavailability.
   In pediatric patients 25 kg and greater, the chewable tablet produces lower trough concentra-
   tions than the film-coated tablet, making the 400-mg film-coated tablet the preferred dosage
   form in patients who weigh at least 25 kg 1 ,2 .

   Protein binding: Approximately 83% is bound to plasma proteins Metabolism: Clearance is primarily through metabolism via a uridine diphosphate glucotrans-
   ferase (UGT1A1)-mediated glucuronidation pathway Excretion: Fecal and renal excretion is approximately 51% and 32%, respectively Half-life: Elimination half-life is approximately 9 hours 1 ,2 .'),
