   Missed Doses

   • Once-daily administration: Take the missed dose as soon as possible but only on the same
   day. If this is not possible, skip the dose and take the next scheduled dose. Do not take 2 doses
   to make up for a missed dose • Twice-daily administration: Take a missed morning dose as soon as possible. A missed morn-
   ing dose may be taken together with the evening dose. A missed evening dose can only be
   taken the same evening • Three-times-daily administration: If a dose is missed, skip the missed dose and take the next
   scheduled dose at the usual time without making up the missed dose 2/14
Switching between Anticoagulants

   • Switching from warfarin to rivaroxaban: Discontinue warfarin and start rivaroxaban as soon
   as the INR is below 5 1
   • Switching from rivaroxaban to warfarin: Continue rivaroxaban for at least 2 days after the
   first dose of warfarin. After 2 days of coadministration, obtain an INR before the next sched-
   uled rivaroxaban dose. Continue coadministration of rivaroxaban and warfarin until INR is at
   least INR testing may be done reliably 24 hours after the last rivaroxaban dose • Switching from rivaroxaban to a non-warfarin anticoagulant: Discontinue rivaroxaban and
   give the first dose of the other oral or parenteral anticoagulant at the time the next rivaroxa-
   ban dose would have been taken 1
   • Switching from non-warfarin anticoagulant to rivaroxaban: Start rivaroxaban 0 to 2 hours be-
   fore the next scheduled administration of the anticoagulant (eg, low molecular weight heparin
   on non-warfarin oral anticoagulant) and omit administration of the other anticoagulant 1
   • Switching from continuous infusion unfractionated heparin to rivaroxaban: Stop the infusion
   and start rivaroxaban at the same time Surgical/Procedure Management
   Surgical and other procedures: If required, discontinue rivaroxaban at least 24 hours prior to
   procedure and restart after the procedure as soon as adequate hemostasis is established, not-
   ing that the time to onset of therapeutic effect is short. If oral medication cannot be taken dur-
   ing or after surgical intervention, consider administering a parenteral anticoagulant. Weigh risk
   of increased bleeding against the urgency of the procedure Dosing Adjustments

