   the next scheduled IV dose, or administer the first IV dose at the time of the next scheduled
   oral dose Dosage Adjustment

   Hepatic impairment: No dosage adjustment necessary Renal impairment, CrCl 30 mL/min or greater: No dosage adjustment is required Renal impairment, CrCl less than 30 mL/min: IV formulation only is contraindicated, as hy-
   droxypropyl-beta-cyclodextrin contained in the injection is eliminated via glomerular filtration.
   No dose adjustment is required for the oral formulation', 'Mpox:

   Tecovirimat use has been reported in at least 18 cases, included 8 children under 5 years old,
   and to an infant 10 days old In 3 separate cases (age ranges: 6 to 12 months, 2 to 7 years, 14
   to 17 years), lesions had fully healed by the end of tecovirimat treatment. Complete healing of
   lesions was noted at days 7 and 8 in 2 cases. Tecovirimat was well tolerated in all 3 cases HIV Co-infection - Guidelines: Tecovirimat is recommended as first-line treatment for severe
   disease or at risk of severe disease as a result of mpox in patients with HIV co-infection. The
   decision to use oral or IV therapy is dependent on the severity of illness (eg, extent of other or-
   gan systems affected by mpox, presence of coalescing non-healing lesions), other comorbidi-
   ties that could contribute to greater severity of illness, expected adherence to the oral formu-
   lation, and gastrointestinal absorption capacity. Adjunct treatments that may be considered in-
   clude cidofovir, brincidofovir, or vaccinia immune globulin intravenous (VIGIV). For ocular
   mpox, administer tecovirimat and trifluridine for treatment Expanded Access Investigational New Drug (EA-IND)

   Tecovirimat is not FDA-approved for the treatment of mpox. Patients may only receive tecoviri-
   mat if they have laboratory-confirmed or suspected mpox and meet eligibility criteria. For sus-
   pected cases, If laboratory testing returns negative, treatment with tecovirimat should be sus-
   pended Detailed information regarding eligibility criteria can be found at


   Eligibility criteria: 6

   • Severely immunocompromised

   • Active skin conditions conferring a higher risk for disseminated infection
   • Pregnant or lactating, regardless of illness severity or underlying comorbidities

   • Younger than 18 years, regardless of illness severity or underlying comorbidities
   • Lesions affecting 25% or more of body surface that may be confluent, necrotic, or hemor-
   rhagic in appearance or cause sepsis

   • Disease resulting in airway compromise or affecting the nervous system

   • Cardiac or neurologic disease (eg, myocarditis, encephalitis)
   • Ocular or periorbital infection, regardless of the time since infection onset

   Smallpox: Efficacy is based on 2 animal models and a placebo-controlled pharmacokinetic and
   safety study in humans (N=452). Adverse effect rates and rates of discontinuation due to ad-
   verse effects were similar between tecovirimat and placebo 3/13
FDA Pediatric Approval: Tecovirimat is indicated for the treatment of human smallpox dis-
   ease caused by variola virus in pediatric patients weighing at least 3 kg Efficacy in humans has not been determined as adequate field trials have not been feasible,
   and introducing smallpox to human subjects is not ethical Efficacy may be reduced in immunocompromised patients Administration
   IV

   • Use only if patient is unable to take oral formulation or if gastrointestinal absorption capacity
   is altered; switch to oral therapy as soon as tolerated 3 ,4 .

   • Administer via IV infusion over 6 hours using a syringe pump Nasogastric
   • Mpox indication only: May administer via nasogastric tube per hospital policy for patients un-
   able to feed by mouth and with no evidence of gastrointestinal dysfunction Oral

   • Administer within 30 minutes of a full moderate- to high-fat meal If weight is 13 kg or less,
   follow all doses with a feeding • Smallpox indication only: If unable to swallow capsules, may open capsule and mix contents
   with 30 mL of liquid (eg, milk, chocolate milk) or soft food (eg, applesauce, yogurt). Administer
   within 30 minutes of preparation Preparation (IV)

   • Withdraw appropriate amount of solution for injection from vial, then dilute with 2 equal
   parts of either NS or D5W in a syringe of suitable size. Two syringes may be necessary de-
   pending on dose and syringe pump system. Do not use prefilled infusion bags for preparation
   or administration • Gently swirl syringe prior to inserting it into the syringe pump • Store diluted solution for up to 24 hours if refrigerated (2 to 8 degrees C) or for up to 4 hours
   if kept at room temperature (15 to 25 degrees C) • Vials are single-dose; do not reuse vial once it has been punctured 4/13
