asthma. In humans, pretreatment with single oral doses of zafirlukast inhibited bronchocon-', 'Pharmacology
   Zafirlukast is a selective and competitive receptor antagonist of the cysteinyl leukotrienes D4
   and E4. The cysteinyl leukotrienes, originally described as slow-reacting substances of anaphy-
   laxis, produce airway edema, smooth muscle constriction, and altered cellular activity associ-
   ated with the inflammatory process, all of which are associated with the pathophysiology of
   asthma. In humans, pretreatment with single oral doses of zafirlukast inhibited bronchocon-
   striction caused by sulfur dioxide and cold air and reduced the early- and late-phase reaction
   in patients with asthma caused by inhalation of various antigens, such as grass, cat dander,
   and ragweed. Zafirlukast reduced the increase in bronchial hyperresponsiveness to inhaled
   histamine that followed inhaled allergen challenge Rapidly absorbed following oral administration; peak plasma concentrations generally
   achieved within 3 hours. Absolute bioavailability unknown. Mean bioavailability reduced by ap-
   proximately 40% following administration with food. More than 99% plasma protein bound,
   predominantly albumin. Apparent steady-state Vd, approximately 70 L. Extensively metabo-
   lized in liver through hydroxylation via CYP2C9. Metabolites are 90 times less active than the
   LTD4 receptor antagonists. Apparent oral clearance, approximately 20 L/hr. Approximately

90% excreted in feces and 10% in urine. Mean terminal half-life, approximately 10 hours; mean
   plasma half-life, approximately 8 to 16 hours (both normal and asthmatic subjects) Pediatric: Following administration of a single 20-mg dose of zafirlukast to pediatric patients 5
   to 6 years of age (n=29) and 7 to 11 years of age (n=20), Cmax was 756 and 601
   nanograms/milliliter (ng/mL), AUC was 2458 and 2027 ng x hr/mL, Tmax was 1 and 5 hours,
   and apparent clearance (CL/f) was 2 and 4 L/hr. Greater systemic drug exposure was
   noted in children compared with adults administered an identical dose (weight-unadjusted
   CL/f: 7 to 11 years, 4 L/hr; 5 to 6 years, 2 L/hr). Multiple dosing in children did not change
   zafirlukast disposition'),
