   • The premixed injection (1.5 mg/mL and 8 mg/mL) is available as a single-use, ready-to-use,
   iso-osmotic solution in dextrose for intravenous IV administration. No further dilution is re-
   quired. Discard any unused portion after use • Whenever possible, administer through a central venous catheter dedicated to that purpose • Use an in-line filter during administration • Loading infusion: Directly infuse premixed injection (1.5 mg/mL) 25 over 10 24 to 60 minutes IV or Intraosseous Bolus Administration (cardiac arrest)

   Perfusing atrial or ventricular arrhythmia: Administer IV over 20 to 60 minutes Pulseless ventricular fibrillation/ventricular tachycardia: May be given undiluted via rapid
   IV bolus In two studies of adult patients, amiodarone was given peripherally as a rapid bo-
   lus with doses diluted to 10 to 15 mg/mL prior to administration 26,27 . In another adult study,
   amiodarone was given undiluted (50 mg/mL) as a rapid bolus into a vein located as centrally as
   possible (external jugular or antecubital vein), followed by a rapid bolus of approximately 200
   mL of Lactated Ringer''s solution. The use of undiluted amiodarone was not associated with
   significant hypotension or bradycardia in patients who achieved return of spontaneous circula-
   tion 28

   Oral Administration

   • Give consistently with regard to meals Preparation, Diluted Solution

   • Loading dose: Add 3 mL (150 mg) to 100 mL D5W for a concentration of 5 mg/mL • Maintenance infusion: Dilute with D5W to a final concentration of 1 to 6 mg/mL 3/17
• For infusions longer than 1 hour, do not exceed concentrations of 2 mg/mL, unless a central
   venous catheter is used • Infusions exceeding 2 hours must be administered in glass or polyolefin bottles containing
   D5W; do not use evacuated glass containers for admixing as incompatibility with a buffer in
   the container may cause precipitation • Amiodarone adsorbs to polyvinyl chloride (PVC) tubing, but all of the clinical experience has
   been with PVC tubing and the concentrations and rates of infusion provided reflect dosing in
   these studies • Amiodarone has been found to leach out plasticizers, including DEHP [di-(2-
   ethylhexyl)phthalate] from IV tubing (including PVC tubing). The degree of leaching increases
   when infusing amiodarone at higher concentrations and lower flow rates than provided in
   FDA-approved dosage and administration instructions. Polysorbate 80, a component of amio-
   darone injection, is also known to leach DEHP from PVC • Do not use plastic containers in series connections. Such use could result in air embolism due
   to residual air being drawn from the primary container before the administration of the fluid
   from the secondary container is complete • Amiodarone is stable at room temperature mixed in a D5W solution to concentrations of 1 to
   6 mg/mL for up to 2 hours in a polyvinyl chloride (PVC) container (less than 10% loss) and for
   up to 24 hours in a polyolefin or glass container (no loss) Preparation, Premixed Solution

   • The premixed injection (1.5 mg/mL and 8 mg/mL) is available as a single-use, ready-to-use,
   iso-osmotic solution in dextrose for intravenous IV administration. No further dilution is re-
   quired. Discard any unused portion after use • If the administration port protector is damaged, detached, or not present, discard the con-
   tainer as the solution path sterility may be compromised. Check for minute leaks prior to use
   by squeezing the bag firmly. If leaks are detected, discard solution as sterility may be impaired.
   Protect from light until ready to use Extravasation Management: Although not pediatric-specific, the following are recommenda-
   tions for extravasation of acidic or alkaline agents (amiodarone is acidic with a pH ranging
   from 5 to 5) 30 :
   ●   General:
   ●   Stop and disconnect infusion; do not remove the cannula or needle
   ●   Attempt to gently aspirate as much extravasated agent as possible; avoid manual pressure

●   Remove cannula or needle
   ●   Dry heat and elevation
   ●   Closely monitor for signs of coagulation and ischemia
   ●   Avoid attempt at pH neutralization (amiodarone - pH 5 to 5)
   ●   Monitor and consider the need for surgical management such as surgical flushing with nor-
       mal saline or debridement and excision of necrotic tissue (especially if pain persists for 1 to
       2 weeks). In cases of compartment syndrome, surgical decompression may be required
   ●   Refractory Events:
   ●   Hyaluronidase 15 units intradermally along injection site and edematous area. Give as five, 2 mL intradermal injections along extravasation site and edematous tissue.
   ●   Inadvertent Intraarterial Administration:
   ●   Leave inadvertent intraarterial line in place for diagnostics
   ●   Systemic heparin titrated to therapeutic anticoagulant effect.
   ●   Stellate ganglion block

