and its major metabolite may inhibit CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6, CYP3A, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, and
CYP2C8, as well as the transporters P-glycoprotein and organic cation transporter (OCT2).', 'Pharmacology
   Class III antiarrhythmic agent that is an iodinated benzofuran compound. Electrophysiologic
   activity is accomplished by prolonging the duration of the action potential and increasing the
   effective refractory period. Increases cardiac blood flow and decreases cardiac work and my-
   ocardial oxygen consumption. Low negative inotropic effect. Antiarrhythmic efficacy seen in
   most children during first week of therapy, with a mean of 1 days before response to treat-
   ment (peak at day 3). This is shorter than that seen in adults (metabolized more quickly in chil-
   dren). Oral absorption is variable with approximately 30% to 50% bioavailability. Widely distrib-
   uted and accumulates in tissues; serum levels can be detected for months. Highly protein
   bound (96%) in adults. Extensively metabolized to an active metabolite (N-desethylamiodarone
   (DEA)) by the cytochrome CYP3A isoenzyme system. Drug-drug interactions potentially occur
   when given in combination with drugs that inhibit cytochrome CYP3A: phenytoin, fospheny-
   toin, clarithromycin, erythromycin, azole antifungals (eg, fluconazole, ketoconazole, itracona-
   zole), protease inhibitors (eg, indinavir, ritonavir), class IA and class III antiarrhythmics (eg,
   quinidine, procainamide, sotalol) and cimetidine (amiodarone levels increase). Amiodarone
   and its major metabolite may inhibit CYP2C9, CYP2C19, CYP2D6, CYP3A, CYP2A6, CYP2B6, and
   CYP2C8, as well as the transporters P-glycoprotein and organic cation transporter (OCT2).
   Amiodarone prevents the elimination of digoxin resulting in high digoxin levels. Highly
   lipophilic drug resulting in extremely long half-life; half-life reported to be 26 to 107 days in
   adults (mean, 40 to 55 days). Contains 3% iodine by weight. Adheres to PVC tubing: low in-
   fusion rates may lead to reduced drug delivery during continuous infusions 50,51,42,34 .'),
