   Monitor blood pressure during dose titration and periodically thereafter', 'thereby inhibiting the contraction of these muscles. It exhibits a greater selectivity for vascular', 'Pharmacology
   amLODIPine besylate is a dihydropyridine long-acting calcium channel blocker that selectively
   blocks the movement of extracellular calcium ions into cardiac and vascular smooth muscle,
   thereby inhibiting the contraction of these muscles. It exhibits a greater selectivity for vascular
   smooth muscles compared with cardiac cells, and lowers blood pressure by reducing periph-
   eral vascular resistance by a direct action on vascular smooth muscle. It binds to the calcium
   channel receptor by slow association and disassociation, providing a gradual onset of action,
   and prolonged duration of antihypertensive effect (of approximately 24 hours). amLODIPine
   does not have an affect on the serum calcium concentrations. 6 ,7

   Absolute bioavailability: Between 64% to 90% with food having little effect on absorption 6 ,8 .
   Bioequivalence of the oral suspension prepared extemporaneously from the crushed tablets
   was not different from the commercial tablets in a bioavailability study of adult volunteers Tmax: 6 to 12 hours 6 ,8 ,17 .

   Protein binding: Approximately 93% 6 ,8 ,17 .

   Metabolism: Extensively metabolized by the liver with approximately 90% of amLODIPine con-
   verted to inactive metabolites 6 , 8 , Renal excretion: Approximately 60% as inactive metabolites and 10% as unchanged drug in
   the urine 6 ,8 ,17 .

   Fecal excretion: Approximately 20% to 25% Total body clearance (greater than 6 years of age):Weight adjusted clearance is similar to
   adults; however, clearance in children less than 6 years of age is much greater than older chil-
   dren Elimination half-life: Approximately 30 to 52 hours. Steady state is reached after 7 to 8 days
   of consecutive dosing 6 ,8 .'),
