Monitor blood pressure, heart rate, and respiratory rate for at least 6 hours after intravenous
   administration', 'Bumetanide is a loop diuretic with a similar mechanism of action to furosemide. Inhibits chlo-
ride reabsorption in the ascending limb of Henle''s loop and inhibits tubular sodium transport,
cate excellent oral bioavailability and significant hepatic metabolism (40%) via the cytochrome', 'Pharmacology
   Bumetanide is a loop diuretic with a similar mechanism of action to furosemide. Inhibits chlo-
   ride reabsorption in the ascending limb of Henle''s loop and inhibits tubular sodium transport,
   causing major loss of sodium and chloride. Increases urinary losses of potassium, calcium, and
   bicarbonate. Urine sodium losses are lower with bumetanide than furosemide, but urine cal-
   cium losses are higher. For diuretic potency, 1 mg of bumetanide is equivalent to approxi-
   mately 40 mg of furosemide. Highly protein bound (greater than 97%). Data from adults indi-
   cate excellent oral bioavailability and significant hepatic metabolism (40%) via the cytochrome
   CYP pathway; 45% eliminated renally as unchanged drug. After IV administration, onset of ac-
   tion is within minutes; peak diuresis is achieved by 1 hour with a duration of action of approxi-
   mately 4 hours. After oral administration, onset of diuresis occurs in 30 to 60 minutes with
   peak diuresis at 90 minutes, and the duration of action is 4 to 6 hours. Half-life is 1 to 2 hours
   in infants and children, but varies up to 15 hours, determined by age and disease state
   9 ,14,18,15,5 ,6
                      .'),
