   Calcitriol is the active form of vitamin D3 (cholecalciferol). The known sites of action are intes-
   tine, bone 2 , kidney, and parathyroid gland Vitamin D3 is metabolically activated in the liver
   and kidney prior to being an active calcium and phosphorous metabolism regulator 3 , Physiological daily production of calcitriol is 5 to 1 mcg. This may be higher during periods of
   increased bone synthesis Calcitriol is rapidly absorbed from the intestine; Tmax is 3 to 6 hours. Following multiple-dose
   administration, steady-state is reached within 7 days Two metabolic pathways have been identified for calcitriol: conversion to 1,24,25-(OH)3D3 and
   to calcitroic acid Calcitriol is 9% protein bound (alpha-globulin vitamin D binding protein). Enterohepatic re-
   cycling and biliary excretion of calcitriol occur. The metabolites of calcitriol are primarily ex-
   creted through feces. The half-life is 5 to 8 hours after a single oral dose. The pharmacologic
   actions of therapeutic doses of calcitriol is 3 to 5 days 3 ,2 .
