           .', 'Cefuroxime is a second generation cephalosporin that is bactericidal by inhibition of cell-wall', 'Pharmacology
   Cefuroxime is a second generation cephalosporin that is bactericidal by inhibition of cell-wall
   synthesis, against many gram-positive and a few gram-negative organisms. It is stable against
   many bacterial beta-lactamases 7 ,25 .

   Oral: Rapidly absorbed after oral administration. Absorption increased with food (37% to 52%
   for tablet). Peak concentrations reached in approximately 3 hours (oral suspension in chil-
   dren). Protein binding is approximately 50%. In adults, most of the drug (50%) is excreted un-
   changed in the urine within 12 hours; however, one study showed the amount recovered in
   the urine of 4 pediatric patients was less than 1% of the administered dose. Elimination half-
   life ranges from 4 to 9 hours in children. Half-life prolonged in patients with renal impair-
   ment. Oral suspension is not bioequivalent to oral tablets and is not interchangeable on a mg-
   per-mg basis. In clinical studies, the AUC and Cmax of the suspension were lower than those of
   the tablets (average of 9% and 29%, respectively) 7 , Injectable: Cmax reached in approximately 15 minutes after IV injection and 45 minutes
   (range, 15 to 60 minutes) after IM injection. Protein binding is approximately 50%. Penetrates
   well into the CSF. Vd is approximately 6 L/kg and elimination half-life is approximately 5
   hours in children 10/14'),
