  Monitoring
  Therapeutic Monitoring
  The goal of therapy is to reduce leukocyte cystine levels to less than 1 nanomole (nmol) half cystine/mg protein (based
  on the mixed leukocyte assay) Concentration values from different assays may not be interchangeable in a given individual. Interpret results based on
  specific assay used Immediate-Release
  Obtain blood samples 5 to 6 hours after dose. Monitor at least every 3 months unless patients are converted from cys-
  teamine hydrochloride or phosphocysteamine to cysteamine bitartrate; then, more frequent monitoring is needed Delayed-Release

  Obtain blood samples 12 hours after dose, prior to the next dose (ie, trough concentration) In patients 1 to younger than 6 years not previously treated with cysteamine, obtain measurements 2 weeks after initia-
  tion of therapy then continue during dosage titration until target WBC cystine concentration is achieved. Upon reaching
  therapeutic target, monitor monthly for 3 months, quarterly for 1 year, and then twice yearly, at a minimum 6/9
In patients 6 years or older not previously treated with cysteamine, obtain measurements after reaching the mainte-
  nance dose, then monthly for 3 months, then quarterly for 12 months, then at least twice per year, at a minimum, there-
  after In patients switching from immediate-release to delayed-release therapy, obtain measurements 2 weeks after initiation
  of delayed-release therapy, and continue monitoring if further dosage titration is required. Upon reaching the therapeu-
  tic target WBC cystine level, monitor quarterly for 6 months, and at least twice yearly thereafter Toxicity Monitoring

  Monitor white blood cell counts 5 ,2 .

  Obtain liver function tests 2 , particularly alkaline phosphatase Monitor for development of skin or bone lesions 5 ,2 .

