   The goal to maintain adequate organ perfusion directs the choice and dose of the vasoactive
   agent(s). Septic shock is a dynamic process; therefore, the agents and/or doses may need to
   be changed over time. The first-line inotropic agents recommended are DOPamine (5 to 9
   mcg/kg/min), DOBUTamine, or EPINEPHrine (0.05 to 3 mcg/kg/min). For vasopressor effects,

DOPamine (greater than 10 mcg/kg/min has vasopressor and inotropic effects), EPINEPHrine
   (greater than 3 mcg/kg/min), or norepinephrine is recommended with no preference for a
   specific agent. For vasodilator effects, initial treatment with an inotropic agent (such as
   EPINEPHrine or DOBUTamine) that lowers systemic vascular resistance (SVR) may be war-
   ranted for a patient with normotension, low cardiac output, and high SVR. Other vasodilators
   may be considered Administration
   • May administer by IV or IO route as a continuous infusion • Avoid bolus administration of the drug. Infusion into a large vein is preferred to minimize risk
   of tissue extravasation 1
   • Vials must be diluted prior to use in compatible diluent up to a concentration of 4000
   mcg/mL. Solutions containing DOBUTamine may exhibit a pink color which will increase with
   time due to oxidation of the drug. There is no significant loss of potency over 24 hours • Standard concentration: The recommended standard concentrations are 500, 1000, 2000,
   and 4000 mcg/mL Solution Preparation Calculations
   To calculate the AMOUNT of drug needed per defined final fluid volume:

   Desired final concentration (mg/mL) x defined final fluid volume (mL) = AMOUNT of drug to
   add to final infusion solution (mg).
   To calculate the VOLUME of drug needed per defined final fluid volume:

   *AMOUNT of drug to add (mg) ÷ drug (vial) concentration (mg/mL) = VOLUME of drug to add
   (mL)

   Example (for DOBUTamine): 30 mL of 2000 mcg/mL solution using DOBUTamine concentra-
   tion of 5 mg/mL.

   2000 mcg/mL = 2 mg/mL

   2 mg/mL x 30 mL = 60 mg DOBUTamine
   *60 mg ÷ 5 mg/mL = 8 mL of DOBUTamine

   Add 8 mL of DOBUTamine (12.5 mg/mL) to 2 mL of compatible solution (eg, D5 W) to yield
   30 mL of infusion solution with a concentration of 2000 mcg/mL.

