   mended for prophylaxis in high-risk pediatric patients undergoing major orthopedic or general
   surgery Has also been used successfully in thrombosis prophylaxis in pediatric cardiac
   catheterization 26 , and in at-risk patients with acute lymphoblastic leukemia Dosing Issues

   Once-daily prophylactic doses have been used in children 12,25,5 ,27 . Based on pharmacoki-
   netic data, doses of 1 mg/kg sub Q once daily in children 2 months or younger, and 5 mg/kg
   subQ once daily in children older than 2 months have been suggested for thromboprophylaxis In a pharmacokinetic study of children (N=126; median age 9 years) once-daily enoxa-
   parin for secondary thromboembolic prophylaxis resulted in median 24-hour trough levels
   above the desired anti-FXa activity level in over half of the subjects (53.2%). Once-daily dosing
   with frequent monitoring may be appropriate for some patients One pilot study success-
   fully used prophylactic doses of 1 mg/kg every 24 hours after initial treatment doses for chil-
   dren with deep vein thrombosis In another open label pilot study in children (n=80) with
   deep vein thrombosis, after a 2-week, twice-daily treatment period, patients received once-

daily enoxaparin dosing (1.5 mg/kg/day) or twice-daily dosing (1 mg/kg every 12 hours). No sig-
   nificant differences were found between the 2 groups for post-thrombotic syndrome, re-
   thrombosis, bleeding, or therapy-related death Another study comparing antithrombin (AT)
   supplementation plus enoxaparin with AT supplementation alone used prophylactic enoxa-
   parin doses of 1 mg/kg every 24 hours in children with acute lymphoblastic leukemia. The
   enoxaparin/AT group had no thromboembolisms (TE) while the AT-only group had nine cases
   of TE Treatment of venous thromboembolism 2 , 8 , 28 , 5 , 27 , Enoxaparin has been safely adminis-
   tered to children with deep vein thrombosis receiving chemotherapy, maintaining anticoagula-
   tion with minimal risks Dosing Issues

   Several retrospective studies have suggested that higher initial doses are required to more
   quickly achieve therapeutic anti-Xa levels and reduce the number of dosage adjustments. In
   one retrospective study in children (n=192), higher initial doses (1.7 mg/kg every 12 hours for
   children 3 months of age and younger; 2 mg/kg every 12 hours for children greater than 3
   months of age) achieved more rapid therapeutic anti-factor Xa levels resulting in fewer
   venipunctures, without an increase in adverse events, compared with standard doses.
   Treatment outcomes (resolution or reduction of thrombus) were not different between
   groups. The authors concluded a higher starting dose of 8 mg/kg every 12 hours for infants
   less than 3 months of age, 5 mg/kg every 12 hours for children 3 to 12 months of age, and 2 mg/kg every 12 hours for children 1 to 5 years of age may be considered Another retro-
   spective study (n=150) found that only 41% of patients attained therapeutic anti-Xa levels with
   initial dosing consistent with current standard treatment guidelines. The following doses were
   required to achieve a therapeutic anti-Xa level (dose given every 12 hours): less than one
   month of age, 8 mg/kg; one month to 1 year, 64 mg/kg; 1 to 6 years, 45 mg/kg; over 6
   years, 05 mg/kg A third retrospective study (n=140) also revealed that less than half of the
   population achieved therapeutic anti-Xa levels following the initial dose with the current stan-
   dard treatment guidelines. The following higher doses were required to achieve a therapeutic
   anti-Xa level (dose given every 12 hours): less than 2 months of age, 6 mg/kg; 2 months to 1
   year, 5 mg/kg; 1 to 5 years, 2 mg/kg; 6 to 16 years, 1 mg/kg In a retrospective study, whole-milligram enoxaparin dosing using insulin syringes (undiluted
   100 mg/mL; 1 mg enoxaparin = 01 mL = 1 unit) was associated with therapeutic anti-Xa levels
   and no reported dose measurement errors. The study included neonates, infants and children
   (n=514); 27% were infants less than 3 months of age (900 to 4700 g in weight). Five children

(less than 1%) had a supra-therapeutic initial anti-Xa level without hemorrhagic consequences.
   No patients needed decimal dosing to attain therapeutic levels Once-daily treatment doses have been studied in children. In a
   pharmacokinetic/pharmacodynamic study (n=14) investigating once-daily enoxaparin dosing
   for thromboembolism, higher doses (greater than the initial dose of 5 mg/kg/day) were re-
   quired to achieve therapeutic levels. Only one child achieved a therapeutic anti-factor Xa with
   the initial dose. Results showed lower total drug exposure with the use of once-daily dosing in
   children compared with adults. The study concluded that once-daily dosing may not be possi-
   ble in the pediatric population Advantages over standard unfractionated heparin:

   (1) May be given subcutaneously,

   (2) More predictable pharmacokinetics,

   (3) Minimal monitoring,
   (4) Dosing every 12 hours,

   (5) Less frequent bleeding complications One disadvantage is the inability to quickly and completely reverse its anticoagulant effects Call 1-800-NOCLOTS for case reporting and treatment guidance.

