   Enoxaparin is a low-molecular weight heparin that has considerably less activity against throm-
   bin than does standard heparin. Efficacy in newborns is decreased due to decreased thrombin
   generation; higher doses required. It is also much less likely to interfere with platelet function
   or cause osteoporosis. It activates antithrombin III, which progressively inactivates both throm-
   bin and factor Xa, key proteolytic enzymes in the formation of fibrinogen and activation of pro-
   thrombin. Bioavailability is almost 100% after subcutaneous administration, with peak activity
   approximately 4 hours later. Pharmacokinetic study reported a peak of 1 to 2 hours after IV
   administration with levels decreasing 6 to 8 hours after dose Metabolized in the liver by
   desulfation and/or depolymerization. Total renal excretion is 40% of the dose. The apparent
   half-life of anti-Xa activity is 4 to 5 hours. Clearance in children is faster compared with adults.
   Steady state achieved in 5 to 7 days. Decreased clearance in patients with renal impairment;
   dose adjustment recommended 22,14,32,10 .'),
