RNA-dependent and DNA-dependent DNA polymerase activities. Etravirine does not inhibit the', 'Pharmacology
   Etravirine is a non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitor (NNRTI) of human immunodefi-
   ciency virus type 1 (HIV-1). Etravirine binds directly to reverse transcriptase and blocks the
   RNA-dependent and DNA-dependent DNA polymerase activities. Etravirine does not inhibit the
   human DNA polymerases alpha, beta, or gamma Bioavailability is unknown, but Tmax is

reached in 5 to 4 hours. Systemic exposure was decreased by approximately 50% when ad-
   ministered under fasting conditions compared with after a meal. Highly protein bound, pri-
   marily to albumin (99.6%) and alpha 1-acid glycoprotein (97.66% to 02%) in vitro. Primarily
   metabolized by the liver via CYP3A, CYP2C9, and CYP2C19 enzymes. After administration of a
   single 800-mg dose of 14C-etravirine, 2% of the dose was recovered in the urine and 7%
   of the dose was recovered in the feces. Mean elimination half-life is approximately 41 +/- 20
   hours In a pharmacokinetic study performed in children and adolescents on a stable
   lopinavir/ritonavir-based regimen (n=23), an etravirine dose of 2 mg/kg twice daily produced
   mean drug exposures comparable to that seen with 200 mg twice daily in adults'),
