Fosinopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. ACE catalyzes the conversion of
angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor sub', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Fosinopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. ACE catalyzes the conversion of
   angiotensin I to the vasoconstrictor substance, angiotensin II. Inhibition of ACE decreases
   plasma angiotensin II and leads to decreased vasopressor activity and decreased aldosterone
   secretion. Its antihypertensive action is believed to be primarily due to suppression of renin-
   angiotensin-aldosterone system Absorption: 36%. The primary site of abso', 'Hypertension, chronic
   Weighing at least 50 kg: 5 to 10 mg orally once daily. Titrate every 2 to 4 weeks until blood
   pressure is controlled or adverse effects occur, up to Max 40 mg/day 1 ,2 .

   An appropriate dosage has not been determined in patients weighing less than 50 kg', 'Indicated for the treatment of hypertension in pediatric patients. Doses of 1 to 6 mg/kg
   were studied in children 6 to 16 years of age and shown to effectively reduce blood pressure Administration
   Administer with or without food', 'The adverse effect profile is similar to the profile in adults. The most commonly reported ad-
   verse effects in hypertensive adult patients were cough (2.2%), dizziness (1.6%), and
   nausea/vomiting (1.2%) Black Box Warning
   When pregnancy is detected, discontinue fosinopril as soon as possible. Drugs that act directly
   on the renin-angiotensin system can cause injury and death to the developing fetus Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   •Diabetes mellitus: The goal of treatment for patients with type 1 or 2 diabetes mellitus is less
   than 90th percentile for age, sex, and height, or in adolescents 13 years or greater, less than
   130/80 mmHg Toxic Monitoring

   •Monitor renal function during the first few weeks of therapy in patients with preexisting renal
   vascular disease including renal artery stenosis •Monitor potassium levels frequently in patients who are receiving concomitant thiazide di-
   uretics, potassium sparing diuretics (eg, spironolactone, amiloride, triamterene), or potassium
   supplements •Frequently monitor serum lithium concentrations with concomitant use of lithium •Consider monitoring white blood cell counts in patients with collagen-vascular disease, partic-
   ularly when associated with impaired renal function •Closely monitor blood pressure, renal function, and electrolytes in patients receiving concomi-
   tant agents that affect the renin-angiotensin system 3/5
•Closely observe patients for hypotension several hours following initial dosing in patients who
