   Administration site reactions, including erythema, bruising, edema and infusion site pain 41

   Adverse effects reported in literature

   Hypercalciuria and development of bone demineralization and renal calculi occur with long-
   term therapy. May displace bilirubin from albumin binding sites when given at high doses or
   for prolonged periods. Cholestatic jaundice and cholelithiasis have also been reported with
   loop diuretics (mainly in preterm infants receiving long-term total parenteral nutrition and
   furosemide therapy). Other adverse events include hypotension, fatigue, nausea, and muscle
   cramps 9 ,23,43,44,45 .

   Musculoskeletal

   In a retrospective cohort study (N=53,725), an association between furosemide use for a me-
   dian of 3 years and fractures was observed in children (younger than 12 years) with congenital
   heart disease, cardiomyopathy, or heart failure. The odds ratio was 9 (95% CI, 17 to 98)
   for the furosemide group (those who received furosemide prescriptions for at least 70% of the
   time during the first year) compared with no furosemide group Otic
   • Preterm infants who received furosemide 1 to 4 mg/kg/day for 28 days were not at greater
   risk of hearing loss compared with patients who received placebo. On adjusted analysis, the
   odds ratio (OR) for hearing loss was 58 (95% CI, 12 to 92) for infants who received low-
   dose furosemide (1 mg/kg IV or 2 mg/kg enteral every 24 hours; n=31) vs placebo (n=19), and
   the OR was 65 (95% CI, 13 to 2) for infants who received medium-dose furosemide (1
   mg/kg IV or 2 mg/kg enteral every 6 hours; n=30) vs placebo (n=19) 8/18
