   Furosemide is a potent diuretic which, if given in excessive amounts, can lead to profound di-
   uresis with water and electrolyte depletion. Therefore, careful medical supervision is required

and dose and dose schedule must be adjusted to the individual patient’s needs Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Monitor urine output and weight changes 12

   Toxic Monitoring

   • Monitor serum electrolytes (especially potassium), carbon dioxide, BUN, creatinine regularly
   for the first few months and periodically thereafter, especially in patients receiving higher
   doses for prolonged periods of time 12
   • Monitor renal function in pediatric patients, especially premature infants, to assess for poten-
   tial nephrocalcinosis or nephrolithiasis 12
   • Monitor blood and urine glucose periodically during treatment in patients with diabetes and
   patients suspected of having latent diabetes 12

   • Assess for emergence of blood dyscrasias 12

   • Assess for signs or symptoms of kidney damage 12

   • Assess for signs or symptoms of liver damage 12

   • Consider renal ultrasonography in premature infants as furosemide may precipitate
   nephrocalcinosis/nephrolithiasis 40
   • Monitor for urinary retention, particularly during initial treatment, in patients with severe
   symptoms of urinary retention (eg, bladder emptying disorders, prostatic hyperplasia, urethral
   narrowing) • Monitor for signs/symptoms of fluid/electrolyte imbalance 12', 'prematurity; monitoring recommended • Preterm infants who received furosemide 1 to 4 mg/kg/day for 28 days were not at greater
