   Population Pharmacokinetics

   A pharmacokinetic-pharmacodynamic model was developed to link furosemide dose to
   furosemide serum concentrations, renal function (creatinine clearance), and urine output, and
   then tested in patients less than 1 year old after cardiac surgery (N=18). The model was based
   on data from 17 pediatric patients ranging in age from 5 to 4 weeks with a mean weight
   of 5 kg. These patients were given an optional furosemide bolus dose, followed by a continu-
   ous furosemide infusion initiated at 05 to 5 mg/kg/hour IV, titrated in increments of 1
   mg/kg/hour every 24 hours. Simulations using this model suggest the most appropriate dosing
   regimen to be 1 or 2 mg/kg as a loading dose, then 2 mg/kg/hour as a continuous infusion.
   This dosing strategy was then tested in a follow-up study of 12 pediatric patients post cardiac
   surgery (age range, 5 to 35 weeks; mean weight, 7 kg). Subjects received a bolus dose of 1
   mg/kg, followed 6 hours later with a 1- or 2-mg/kg loading dose, then a 2 mg/kg/hr IV infu-
   sion. With this dosing strategy, an adequate and more controlled urine production was ob-
   tained post-cardiac surgery'),
