   until blood pressure is controlled or adverse effects occur, up to a maximum 6 mg/kg/day
   or 10 mg/day 2', 'Safety and effectiveness have not been established in pediatric patients', 'Adverse effects in 72 pediatric patients treated for hypertension were headache (n=2), flushing
   (n=2), dizziness (n=1), tachycardia (n=1), and unspecified (n=1) In adults, the most commonly reported adverse effects at all doses of isradipine were dizziness
   (7.3%), edema (7.2%), palpitations (4%), fatigue (3.9%), flushing (2.6%), nausea (1.8%), dyspnea
   (1.8%), tachycardia (1..5%), rash (1.5%), pollakiuria (1.5%), weakness (1.2%), and vomiting
   (1.1%). Serious adverse reactions reported in 5% to 1% of patients include atrial fibrillation,
   ventricular fibrillation, myocardial infarction, heart failure, syncope, paresthesia, transient is-
   chemic attack, stroke, leukopenia, elevated liver function tests, shortness of breath, hypoten-
   sion, and urticaria Monitoring
   Monitor blood pressure every 1 to 2 weeks until under control, then every 1 to 3 months in a
   non-acute situation 2/5', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Isradipine is a dihydropyridine calcium channel antagonist. It exerts its effect by binding to cal-
   cium with high affinity and specificity and blocking the flux of calcium into smooth and cardiac
   muscles. It also exhibits a peripheral vasodilation which results in some increase in cardiac
   output and reduced systemic vascular resistance Absorption: Isradipine is subject to extensive first pass metabolism with 90% to 95% absorp-
   tion and bioavailability 15% to 24%. In plasma, isradipine is detectable within 20 minutes of a
   single oral dose of isradipine 5 to 20 mg. Peak concentrations (1 ng/mL/mg) occurs within ap-
   proximately 5 hours of administration. Administering isradipine with food delays the peak
   concentration time by about 1 hour but there is no effect on the total bioavailability of isradip-
   ine Distribution: Volume of distribution is 3 L/kg Metabolism: Isradipine is completely metabolized prior to excretion and no unchanged drug
   is detected in urine. Six metabolites have been characterized in blood and urine with mono
   acids of the pyridine derivative and a cyclic lactone product accounting for more than 75% of
   the material identified. The Cmax and AUC are increased by 32% and 52%, respectively, in the
   presence of hepatic impairment Excretion: Total body clearance is 4 L/min. The AUC of isradipine is increased by 45% when
   mild renal impairment (30 to 80 mL/min) is present. In contrast, patients with severe renal fail-
   ure (CrCL less than 10 mL/min) experience a reduction in AUC by 20% to 50% compared to
   healthy volunteers Half-life: Isradipine has a biphasic elimination rate with an early half-life of 5 to 2 hours and
   a terminal half-life of 8 hours'),
