   dandruff, dry skin, and skin burning sensation in less than 1% of patients. Treated subjects
   were 6 months to 4 years of age (n=47), 4 to 12 years of age (n=179), 12 to 16 years of age
   (n=56), and 16 years or older (n=97) In two multisite, randomized, double-blind studies consisting of 765 patients 6 months or
   older, pruritus, excoriation, and erythema were reported as the most common adverse events
   with topical application, occurring at a rate less than 1%. Ocular irritation was noted in 7 pa-
   tients (2 from the ivermectin group) of which 4 of the 7 patients reported ocular irritation at
   baseline Contact and allergic dermatitis have been reported during postmarketing surveillance Monitoring
   Confirm eradication of the Strongyloides stercoralis nematode with at least 3 stool examinations
   over the 3 months following treatment. Patients with onchocerciasis and a history of signifi-
   cant exposure to Loa loa-endemic areas should be assessed prior to treatment for loiasis and
   followed carefully post-therapy', '', 'Pharmacology
   Ivermectin, a semisynthetic, anthelmintic agent, is active against many nematodes at various
   stages in the life cycle. Ivermectin is active against the tissue microfilariae of Onchocerca volvu-
   lus, but not against the adult form. Ivermectin is also active against Strongyloides stercoralis, but
   only in the intestinal stages Ivermectin binds selectively and with high affinity to glutamate-

gated chloride ion channels in invertebrate muscle and nerve cells of the microfilaria. This
   binding causes an increase in the permeability of the cell membrane to chloride ions and re-
   sults in hyperpolarization of the cell, leading to paralysis and death of the parasite. Ivermectin
   also is believed to act as an agonist of the neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA),
   thereby disrupting GABA-mediated CNS neurosynaptic transmission Half-life of ivermectin (oral) is approximately 18 hours. Tmax (oral) is approximately 4 hours.
   Ivermectin and/or its metabolites are excreted primarily in the feces over an estimated 12
   days; less than 1% of the dose is excreted in urine. Bioavailability increased approximately 5-
   fold following a high-fat meal Following a single application of ivermectin 5% topical lotion in a clinical study, the mean
   Cmax was 24 +/- 23 nanograms/milliliter (ng/mL) and the mean AUC was 7 +/- 2 hours
   x ng/mL among 20 lice-infested patients 6 months to 3 years of age'),
