  Discontinuation: A gradual withdrawal over at least 1 week is recommended 3 ,4 .', '• Lacosamide immediate-release is indicated as monotherapy or adjunctive therapy for partal-onset seizures in patients 1 month
  or older, and as adjunctive therapy for primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients 4 years or older • Lacosamide extended-release is indicated for the treatment of partial-onset seizures in pediatric patients weighing at least 50
  kg Administration
  Tablets

  • Give with or without food • Tablets should be swallowed whole with liquid; do not divide Capsules

  • Give with or without food • Extended-release capsules should be swallowed whole with liquid; do not open, chew, or crush 3

Oral Solution

  • Give with or without food • Use a calibrated measuring device for the oral solution • Discard unused oral solution 7 weeks after opening the bottle • Oral solution may be administered via a nasogastric tube or gastrostomy tube Intravenous Injection

  • May be administered IV without further dilution or mixed with NS, D5W, or LR 5

  • Preferably infuse over a period of 30 to 60 minutes; infusion durations less than 30 minutes are generally not recommended in
  pediatric patients • If diluted prior to infusion, do not store for longer than 4 hours at room temperature 5

  • Discard any unused portion 5', 'Common, partial-onset seizures, adjunctive therapy, adults: Dizziness (31%), headache (13%), nausea (11%), diplopia (11%),
  vomiting (9%), fatigue (9%), blurred vision (8%), ataxia (8%), somnolence (7%), and tremor (7%) 3 ,5 .
  Additional adverse reactions included nystagmus, balance disorder, vertigo, gait disturbance, diarrhea, contusion, skin lacera-
  tion, memory impairment, pruritus, depression, and asthenia Adverse reactions reported in pediatric clinical trials were similar to those seen in adult patients 3 ,5 .

  Common, primary generalized tonic-clonic seizures, adjunctive therapy, 4 years or older: Dizziness (23%), somnolence
  (17%), headache (14%), and nausea (10%) 4

  Additional adverse reactions include myoclonic epilepsy and acute worsening of seizures, including status epilepticus Monitoring
  Therapeutic Laboratory Monitoring

  • Monitoring lacosamide serum levels (reference range, 10 to 20 mg/L [40 to 80 mcmol/L]) may be clinically useful • Saliva can be a useful alternative for therapeutic drug monitoring as drug distributes into saliva at concentrations that reflect
  non-protein-bound concentration in plasma Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

  • Monitoring lacosamide serum levels (reference range, 10 to 20 mg/L [40 to 80 mcmol/L]) may be clinically useful • Saliva can be a useful alternative for therapeutic drug monitoring as drug distributes into saliva at concentrations that reflect
  non-protein-bound concentration in plasma Toxic Physical Monitoring
  • Obtain an ECG prior to initiation of treatment and following titration to maintenance dose in patients with known cardiac con-
  duction problems, those with severe cardiac disease, and cardiac sodium channelopathies, and with concomitant use of medica-
  tions that affect cardiac conduction 3 ,2 , particularly if lacosamide is administered IV • Assess for new onset or worsening depression, suicidality, or unusual changes in behavior • Closely observe patients with mild to moderate hepatic impairment for adverse reactions 2', 'duction problems, those with severe cardiac disease, and cardiac sodium channelopathies, and with concomitant use of medica-', 'The volume of distribution is approximately 6 L/kg (Vimpat®) 5 to to 67 L/kg (Motpoly XR) 3 and is close to the volume of total
  body water. Lacosamide is less than 15% bound to plasma proteins Elimination

  Lacosamide is primarily eliminated from systemic circulation by renal excretion and biotransformation Following oral administration, approximately 95% of radioactivity was recovered in the urine and less than 5% in the feces Half-life
