Lisinopril is a long-acting angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. Inhibition of ACE re-', 'Pharmacology
   Lisinopril is a long-acting angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor. Inhibition of ACE re-
   sults in decreased plasma angiotensin II which leads to decreased vasopressor activity and de-
   creased aldosterone secretion. Blood pressure lowering effect is thought to be mainly due to
   suppression of renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system. Lisinopril increases levels of bradykinin,
   a potent vasodilator. Peak concentrations reached in approximately 5 to 6 hours Bioavailability is approximately 25% with wide range of variability (6% to 60%); food does not
   affect absorption. Not bound to other serum proteins. Does not undergo any metabolism and
   is eliminated unchanged entirely in the urine. Elimination half-life is approximately 12 hours.
   Elimination is decreased in patients with renal impairment (CrCl less than 30 mL/min) Pediatric Patients

   In patients 6 to 16 years of age (N=29), steady state peak plasma concentrations occurred
   within 6 hours of administering lisinopril 1 to 2 mg/kg. The extent of absorption was ap-
   proximately 28% based on urinary recovery. The typical value of lisinopril clearance is 10 L/hr
   in children weighing 30 kg'),
