of metabolism is CYP2C8 and CYP3A4; minor isoenzymes are CYP2B6 and CYP2D6. Excretion', 'Pharmacology
   Loperamide slows intestinal motility and affects water and electrolyte movement through the
   bowel. Loperamide binds to the opiate receptor in the gut wall and inhibits the release of
   acetylcholine and prostaglandins, resulting in reduced peristalsis and increased intestinal tran-
   sit time. Loperamide also increases the tone of the anal sphincter which reduces incontinence
   and urgency In humans, loperamide prolongs transit time of intestinal contents and reduces the daily fecal
   volume, increases viscosity, increases bulk density, and diminishes fluid and electrolyte loss.
   Tolerance to antidiarrheal effects has not been reported The elimination half-life of loperamide is 8 hours (range 1 to 4). The major isoenzymes
   of metabolism is CYP2C8 and CYP3A4; minor isoenzymes are CYP2B6 and CYP2D6. Excretion
   of the metabolites is mainly through the feces'),
