   Lifestyle management, including diet and exercise, is the cornerstone of lipid management.
   When needed, any statin drug may be used as no comparative studies in pediatric patients are
   available. Statin therapy should be considered in children 10 years of age or older following a
   6-month trial of lifestyle management when LDL-C is 190 mg/dL or more; LDL-C is 160 to 189
   mg/dL with a positive family history OR 1 high-level risk factor OR 2 or more moderate-level
   risk factors; or when LDL-C is 130 to 159 mg/dL plus 2 high-level risk factors OR 1 high-level
   plus 2 or more moderate-level risk factors OR clinical cardiovascular disease. It is recom-
   mended that statin therapy in children younger than 10 years should be limited to patients
   with severe primary hyperlipidemia (homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, primary hy-
   pertriglyceridemia [triglycerides ≥ 500 mg/dL]), a high risk condition, or evident cardiovascular
   disease, all under the care of a lipid specialist. For children 8 to 9 years of age, statin therapy
   might be considered following a 6-month trial of lifestyle management when LDL-C is 190
