channel antagonist that selectively inhibits the transmembrane influx of calcium ions into car-
impairment (92% to 93%). Extensively metabolized via the CYP450 3A4 system. Approximately', 'Pharmacology
   NIFEdipine is a vasodilator with antianginal and antihypertensive effects. It is a slow-calcium
   channel antagonist that selectively inhibits the transmembrane influx of calcium ions into car-
   diac muscle and vascular smooth muscle which is dependent upon for the contractile process.
   Available in both immediate- and extended-release forms 17,18 . Bioavailability is approxi-
   mately 52% to 56% for both formulations Procardia® XL tablet is designed to provide
   NIFEdipine at a constant rate over 24 hours and does not appear to be affected by food 18 ;
   however, food prolongs the time to peak concentration and greatly increases peak plasma
   concentration of Adalat® CC (by 60%) 14 and of the immediate-release formulation Adalat®
   CC and immediate-release NIFEdipine should not be administered with food 14 , 17 , Onset of
   action is rapid and complete following oral administration of immediate-release dose; initial
   response is within 20 minutes, peak blood levels occur within 30 minutes, and duration of ac-
   tion is 4 to 8 hours 17,4 . For extended-release tablets, an initial peak occurs at 2 to 5 hours
   followed by a second, lower peak 6 to 12 hours after administration. Duration of extended-re-
   lease tablet is 24 hours; however, twice daily dosing may be warranted in hypertensive chil-
   dren Relative bioavailability is not different when the capsules are given orally and either
   swallowed whole, bitten and swallowed, or bitten and held sublingually; however, biting
   through the capsule prior to swallowing results in earlier plasma concentrations Highly pro-
   tein bound (approximately 90% to 96%), but may be reduced in patients with renal or hepatic
   impairment (92% to 93%). Extensively metabolized via the CYP450 3A4 system. Approximately
   20% to 30% is removed by first-pass metabolism. Greater than 90% is excreted in the urine as
   inactive metabolites, most occurring within the first 24 hours 4 ; only small amounts of drug
   (0.01%) are eliminated unchanged in the urine Elimination half-life is 7 to 4 hours and 7 to 3 hours for the immediate- and extended-release tablets, respectively, and may be
   longer in patients with chronic liver disease Elimination half-life of NIFEdipine administered
   as Adalat® CC is approximately 7 hours'),
