Monitor for signs or symptoms of anticholinergic CNS side effects (e.g., hallucinations, agita-
liver by CYP3A4 isoenzymes; inducers or inhibitors of this enzyme family may alter serum con-', 'Pharmacology
   oxyBUTYnin is a competitive antagonist of acetylcholine at postganglionic muscarinic recep-
   tors, which causes relaxation of bladder smooth muscle. In patients with involuntary bladder
   spasms, oxyBUTYnin increases maximum urinary bladder capacity and volume to first detru-
   sor contraction. It diminishes the frequency of detrusor muscle contractions and delays initial
   desire to void. Widely distributed following systemic absorption. Extensively metabolized in the

liver by CYP3A4 isoenzymes; inducers or inhibitors of this enzyme family may alter serum con-
   centrations of the parent drug. Half-life of 2 to 3 hours. Specific pharmacokinetic parameters,
   as well as time to therapeutic effect, are dependent on dosage form 32,30,8 ,20,9 .

   Immediate-release tablets and syrup. Bioavailability: 6%; increased by 25% when syrup ad-
   ministered with food. Tmax: 1 hour. Cmax of R-oxybutynin approximately 6 ng/mL 8 ,9 .

   Extended-release tablets. Relative bioavailability: greater than 150% compared with regular
   oxyBUTYnin; food has no effect on rate or extent of absorption. Tmax: 5 hours. Cmax of R-oxy-
   BUTYnin 7 ng/mL. At steady-state, stable serum concentrations are maintained for up to 24
   hours, with minimal fluctuations between peak and trough levels Transdermal patch. Following application of the first patch, oxyBUTYnin plasma concentra-
   tions rise for 24 to 48 hours, and are then maintained for up to 96 hours. Absorption is equiva-
   lent whether applied to the abdomen, buttocks, or hip. Steady-state conditions are reached
   during the second patch application, with average plasma concentrations between 3 and 4
   ng/mL (adult data) Intravesical administration results in rapid absorption and produces plasma levels higher than
   those achieved with oral administration Desethyloxybutynin (DEO), an active metabolite, is primarily responsible for the anticholinergic
   side effects associated with oxyBUTYnin. Oral dosage forms, which undergo hepatic and in-
   testinal metabolism, produce higher DEO:oxyBUTYnin ratios than transdermal dosage forms,
   which have minimal presystemic metabolism. Intravesical administration produces ratios that
   are between those of oral and transdermal products. As a result, transdermal products have
   the potential for the least anticholinergic side effects 32 , 16 , Overall, tolerability to adverse ef-
   fects of marketed products, from best to worst, appears to be transdermal gel, transdermal
   patch, extended-release tablet, and immediate-release tablet (and liquid)'),
