   Black Box Warning
   Warning

   Physicians should completely familiarize themselves with the complete contents of this leaflet
   before prescribing primaquine phosphate 5

   Monitoring
   Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   •Perform glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) testing prior to initiating treatment •Monitor baseline hematocrit and hemoglobin prior to initiating treatment and closely during
   treatment (eg, at day 3 and 8) if decision is made to administer primaquine to patients with
   mild to moderate G6PD deficiency, patients with an unknown G6PD status, or where G6PD
   testing is unavailable •Consider monitoring blood cell counts and hemoglobin determinations during treatment in
   G6PD normal patients •Perform a pregnancy test in sexually active females of reproductive potential prior to initiat-
   ing treatment Toxic Physical Monitoring
   •Closely observe patients with a previous idiosyncratic reaction to primaquine phosphate
   (manifested as hemolytic anemia, methemoglobinemia, or leukopenia), personal or family his-
   tory of hemolytic anemia, or nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide methemoglobin reductase de-
   ficiency •Monitor ECG during use in patients with cardiac disease, long QT syndrome, a history of ven-
   tricular arrhythmias, uncorrected hypokalemia or hypomagnesemia, bradycardia, or with con-
   comitant use of QT interval prolonging drugs 4/6', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Primaquine phosphate is an 8-aminoquinolone compound which elimi-
   nates tissue (exoerythrocytic) infection. It prevents the development of the blood (erythrocytic)
   forms of the parasite which are responsible for relapses in vivax malaria. Primaquine phos-
   phate is also active against gametocytes of Plasmodium falciparum'),
