   Mechanism of action

   Rilonacept is a targeted inhibitor of interleukin-1 (IL-1) that reduces inflammation in cryopyrin-
   associated periodic syndromes (CAPS), deficiency of IL-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA), and recur-
   rent pericarditis. Rilonacept acts as a decoy receptor that binds both IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta
   and prevents its interaction with cell surface receptors. It also binds IL-1 receptor antagonist
   (IL-1ra). Inflammation in CAPS is associated with mutations in the NLRP-3 gene, which result in
   excessive release of activated IL-1 beta. DIRA is an auto-inflammatory disorder caused by loss
   of function mutations in the IL1RN gene, which encodes IL-1ra, resulting in unopposed signal-
   ing of the proinflammatory cytokines IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta through the IL-1 receptor. IL-1 is
   a key cytokine that mediates the pathophysiology of may inflammatory processes, and has
   been implicated as a causative factor in pericarditis Cmin: The mean Cmin was 20 mcg/mL (3.6 to 33 mcg/mL) in pediatric patients, which was sim-
   ilar to adult trough concentrations (mean 24 mcg/mL (7 to 56 mcg/ml) 5/8
AUC: The median AUC0 to infinity was 1 mcg X days/mL in 66 children 1 to 18 years of age
   with active systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis. Rilonacept exposure was approximately 50%
   lower in children with serum albumin less than 4 g/dL compared with children with higher al-
   bumin concentrations; however, dose adjustments are unnecessary Steady-state trough: The mean steady-state trough ranged from 5 mg/mL to 74 mg/mL in
   pediatric patients (between 3 and 6 years and body weight between 7 kg and 9 kg) with
   deficiency of interleukin-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA). The trough levels for 4 pediatric patients
   measured at the end of the weekly dose interval (mean 20 mcg/mL) were similar to those ob-
   served in adult patients with cryopyrin-associated periodic syndromes CAPS. The mean steady-
   state trough was approximately 23 mcg/mL and appeared to be reached by approximately 2
   weeks in patients with recurrent pericarditis CL: The median CL was 557 L/day in 66 children 1 to 18 years of age with active systemic ju-
   venile idiopathic arthritis . Clearance increased with age and decreased with increased serum
   albumin Half-life, active systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis: The median half-life was 9 days in
   66 children 1 to 18 years of age with active systemic juvenile idiopathic arthritis. The half-life
   decreased with age Half-life, recurrent pericarditis: The half-life was approximately 7 days following a 320 mg
   loading dose and weekly subcutaneous doses of 160 mg for up to 36 weeks'),
