   Mechanism of action

   The exact mechanism of action is unknown. In vitro studies suggests that rufinamide, a triazole
   derivative, modulates the activity of sodium channels and subsequently prolongs the inactive
   state of the channel. The inactive state is prolonged by a delayed prepulse in cultured cortical
   neurons and limited sustained repetitive firing of sodium-dependent action potentials AUC: There was a 29% reduction in rufinamide AUC in patients undergoing dialysis 3 hours af-
   ter a rufinamide dose. In patients with creatinine clearance of less than 30 mL/min the phar-
   macokinetics of rufinamide was similar to healthy subjects. The oral suspension is bioequiva-
   lent to the oral tablets. The rate of absorption is slow and the extent of absorption is reduced
   as the dose is increased Bioavailability: The bioavailability was 85% after a single 600 mg dose of rufinamide under
   fed conditions, based on urinary excretion data. The bioavailability decreased with increasing
   doses both for single and multiple doses. The oral suspension is bioequivalent to the oral
   tablets Cmax: There was a 16% reduction in rufinamide Cmax in patients undergoing dialysis 3 hours
   after a rufinamide dose Distribution: Protein binding was 34% with the majority (27%) bound to albumin. Rufinamide
   was evenly distributed between plasma and erythrocytes. The apparent volume of distribution

(Vd) was 50 L at 3200 mg/day. Vd was dose-dependent and varies with body surface area Effects of food: After a single 400 mg dose of oral rufinamide taken with food in healthy vol-
   unteers, the extent of absorption increased by 34% and the Cmax increased by 56%. The Tmax
   remained unchanged Excretion: Renal excretion was the major route of elimination with 85% of a radiolabeled dose
   excreted in the urine. The acid metabolite, CGP 4792, accounts for a minimum of 66% of the
   dose eliminated in the urine. Two percent of the dose was excreted unchanged rufinamide Half-life: The half-life of rufinamide is 6 to 10 hours in healthy subjects and patients with
   epilepsy Metabolism: Rufinamide is extensively metabolized but has no active metabolites. Rufinamide
   is not dependent on the CYP450 system or glutathione for metabolism. Carboxylesterase(s)
   mediated hydrolysis of the carboxamide group to the acid derivative CGP 47292 is the primary
   route of metabolism. Rufinamide is a weak inhibitor of CYP2E1 and weak inducer of CYP3A4.
   Rufinamide does not inhibit any other CYP450 isoenzymes Tmax: Under both fed and fasted conditions, the Tmax was 4 to 6 hours'),
