   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Laboratory Monitoring
   Monitor whole blood trough concentrations at least 2 times (on separate days) during the first
   week of treatment, with concomitant use of CYP3A4 inducers or inhibitors, after dosage
   changes, after changes in concomitant CYP3A4 inducers or inhibitors, or with changes in renal
   or hepatic function The recommendation for whole blood trough concentration is 5 to 20 nanograms/mL (ng/mL)
   during months 1 to 12 in pediatric kidney, liver, and heart transplantation For lung trans-
   plantation, recommended trough concentrations are 10 to 20 ng/mL for weeks 1 to 2, then 10
   to 15 ng/mL for week 2 to month 12 For renal transplantation, target concentrations are 10
   to 20 ng/mL during month 1, and 5 to 15 ng/mL thereafter when used with basiliximab induc-
   tion In patients with severe hepatic impairment (Child Pugh score 10 or greater), close
   monitoring is recommended. Check local protocols for specific blood level targets.
   Nephrotic syndrome: Titrate to target a 12-hour trough of 5 to 10 nanograms/mL (6 to 12
   nanomol/L); maintain lowest level needed to maintain remission and avoid toxicity 13

   Ulcerative Colitis (Acute, Severe); Steroid Refractory: Titrate to target trough level of 10 to
   15 ng/mL initially; after remission, 5 to 7 ng/mL. Longer duration treatment using lower levels
   of 2 to 5 ng/mL have been reported 15

   Toxic Physical Monitoring
   Continuously monitor patients for signs or symptoms of anaphylaxis for at least the first 30
   minutes following the initiation of tacrolimus infusion and at frequent intervals thereafter Examine patients for skin changes 21/34
Monitor for neurologic symptoms Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   Monitor renal function , liver function, electrolytes (including serum potassium and magne-
   sium), fasting blood glucose, and blood pressure regularly 18,8 .

   Monitor for signs or symptoms of infection, including opportunistic infection (eg, activation of
   latent viral infection, including BK virus-associated nephropathy, which may lead to renal allo-
   graft loss) 27 , For patients who develop renal failure or clinical manifestations of ventricular dysfunction
   while on tacrolimus, consider performing an echocardiographic evaluation Monitor ECGs; periodically during treatment, especially in patients at risk for QT prolongation
   (concomitant use of other QT prolonging drugs or CYP3A inhibitors; electrolyte disturbances;
   congestive heart failure; or bradyarrhythmia)

