Tacrolimus (FK506) is a macrolide immunosuppressant that inhibits T-lymphocyte activation
through formation of a protein complex (FKBP-12) that inhibits the enzyme calcineurin.
Calcineurin is a key enzyme in the process of lymphokine formation; inhibition of this process
closporine (another calcineurin inhibitor) for immunosuppression Immediate-Release
Volume of distribution in children (2.6 L/kg) is approximately twice that of adults Extensively metabolized in the liver by demethylation and hydroxylation, primarily by CYP3A4', 'Pharmacology
   Tacrolimus (FK506) is a macrolide immunosuppressant that inhibits T-lymphocyte activation
   through formation of a protein complex (FKBP-12) that inhibits the enzyme calcineurin.
   Calcineurin is a key enzyme in the process of lymphokine formation; inhibition of this process
   suppresses T-lymphocyte activation 18,8 . Shown to be 50 to 200 times more potent than cy-
   closporine (another calcineurin inhibitor) for immunosuppression Immediate-Release

   Absorption is incomplete, with average bioavailability of 30% Bioavailability is decreased
   with food, so tacrolimus should be taken consistently with regards to meals Bioavailability,
   Cmax, and AUC are lower in African-American than in Caucasian or Latin-American patients Tmax occurs at 2 hours following capsule administration; Tmax is delayed (6 hours) and
   bioavailability is reduced (13%) with use of an extemporaneously compounded oral suspen-
   sion Strong correlation between AUC and trough levels 72 , but not with mg/kg dosage Highly protein bound (99%), primarily to albumin and alpha-1-acid glycoprotein, with a high
   association with erythrocytes The ratio of whole blood to plasma concentration averages 35
   (range 12 to 67).

Volume of distribution in children (2.6 L/kg) is approximately twice that of adults Extensively metabolized in the liver by demethylation and hydroxylation, primarily by CYP3A4
   enzyme subsystem. The major metabolite, 13-demethyl tacrolimus, has similar activity to the
   parent drug.
   Most drug is eliminated in the feces (greater than 90%); less than 1% of the drug is excreted
   unchanged in the urine. Clearance is more rapid in pediatric than adult patients 74,75 ; the rate
   is highest children younger than 6 years A higher mg/kg dose may be necessary in younger
   children 74,6 ,75 . Adolescents and adults, but not children, demonstrate a decrease in clearance
   over time 6 ,75 . Clearance is significantly decreased in patients with severe liver impairment Mean terminal elimination half-life is approximately 10 to 5 hours in children 8 , There is
   no difference in terminal half-life among African-American, Caucasian, and Latin-American pa-
   tients The clearance of tacrolimus was influenced by hematocrit, time after transplantation, and
   body weight; volume of distribution was influenced by body weight only in a population phar-
   macokinetic analysis of 42 pediatric (median age, 35 years (0.53 to 93 years) de novo, liv-
   ing donor liver transplant patients. The initial dose of immediate-release tacrolimus was 1
   mg/kg orally twice daily with dosage adjustments, starting 15 days post-transplantation, to tar-
   get concentrations of 5 to 15 ng/mL. Final model values, using blood concentrations during a
   1-year follow-up, were 253 L,115 L/day, and 314 L/day for volume of central compartment, ap-
   parent intercompartmental clearance, and maximal blood clearance Extended-Release
   Bioavailability is decreased with food, so tacrolimus extended-release should be taken con-
   sistently with regards to meals. Highly protein bound (99%), primarily to albumin and alpha-1-
   acid glycoprotein, with a high association with erythrocytes The pharmacokinetic parameters of extended-release tacrolimus (initial dose of 3
   mg/kg/day) were evaluated in 10 pediatric patients (4 to 15 years) with de novo kidney trans-
   plant 14 :
   AUC(24): 4 (Day 1) to 4 (Day 28) nanograms x hr/mL
   Cmax: 7 (Day 1) to 7 (Day 28) nanograms/mL
   Trough concentration at 24 hours after morning dose: 7 (Day 1) to 2 (Day 28)
   nanograms/mL
   Tmax: 1 to 2 hours.

The tacrolimus AUC(24), Cmax, and predose trough concentration are comparable upon con-
   version from immediate-release (Prograf) to extended-release capsules (Astagraf XL) on a 1:1
   (mg:mg) basis in stable pediatric kidney transplant patients (n=45) In a pharmacokinetic study (n=18), extended-release tacrolimus achieved bioequivalence to
   twice daily, immediate-release capsules when converted in a 1:1 ratio in pediatric patients (5 to
   13 years of age) 6 months after liver transplantation. Patients were stabilized on a tacrolimus-
   based regimen for at least 2 weeks before converting to extended-release. Steady state AUCs
   were bioequivalent between immediate-release and extended-release on days 7 and AUC
   and trough concentration were strongly correlated for both products Ointment

   Minimally absorbed after topical application (bioavailability of approximately 5%) 22 , Tacrolimus 03% ointment produces mean blood levels of less than 1 nanogram/milliliter in
   approximately 90% of children (age 2 to 15 years) 77 and 95% of infants (age 3 to 24 months)
   76
        ; similar levels are reported with 1% ointment in children (age 6 to 12 years) Systemic
   exposure increases as the size of the treatment area increases 22 , 76 , Systemic exposure de-
   creases as treatment continues and skin improves 22,76,77,78 . Peak concentrations achieved in
