   Renal Dose Adjustments
   Renal impairment: There are no data on dose adjustments in renally impaired pediatric pa-
   tients; however, in adult patients with a CrCl less than 70 mL/min/1.73 m2, one-half of the
   usual dose is recommended 1 ,14,15,2 .
   Hemodialysis: A supplemental dose may be required, depending on duration of dialysis pe-
   riod, clearance rate of dialysis system, and effective renal clearance of topiramate in the pa-
   tient 1 ,14,15,8 ,21 .

Other Dose Adjustments

   Concomitant enzyme-inducing antiepileptic drugs (EI AED): Higher doses of topiramate are
   recommended with concomitant use of EI AEDs (eg, carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbi-
   tal); up to double the usual dose may be required Coadministration with phenytoin may re-
   quire an adjustment of phenytoin to achieve optimal clinical outcomes. Similarly, addition or
   discontinuation of phenytoin and/or carbamazepine may require a dose adjustment of topira-
   mate 14,15 .

   Withdrawal of seizure medication: Withdrawal of antiepileptic drug (AED) therapy may be
   considered when a patient has been seizure free for at least 2 years. AEDs should be tapered
