  Monitoring
  Toxic Monitoring

  • To minimize the risk of ototoxicity, auditory function monitoring should be considered in patients receiving concomitant ototoxic drugs • Monitor renal function for nephrotoxicity 1 ,2 .
  • Periodic monitoring of white blood cell count should be done to screen for neutropenia in patients on prolonged therapy with vancomycin
  or those who are receiving concomitant drugs that may cause neutropenia • Monitor for infusion-related events, including hypotension and vancomycin infusion reaction 1 ,2 .

  Therapeutic Drug Monitoring

  AUC/MIC ratio
  Suspected or serious MRSA infections: In general, target a vancomycin AUC of 400 mg x hr/L, assuming vancomycin MIC of 1 mg/L
  (AUC/MIC ratio of 400); may potentially target a higher AUC of 600 mg x hr/L Begin therapeutic monitoring within 24 to 48 hours of vancomycin initiation for serious MRSA infections AUC-guided monitoring is recommended for all pediatric age groups, including neonates. Trough-only monitoring, with a target of 15 to 20
  mg/L, is no longer recommended in patients with serious infections due to MRSA Bayesian software programs for AUC monitoring with a trough concentration are recommended; however, 2 pharmacokinetic samples (ie,
  1 to 2 hours post infusion and at the end of the dosing interval) may improve accuracy and precision of AUC estimates compared with using
  only a trough concentration. Another approach is collection of 2 concentrations (obtained near the steady-state, post-distributional peak
  concentration at 1 to 2 hours after infusion and trough at end of dosing interval), preferably but not required during the same dosing inter-
  val (if possible) and utilizing first-order pharmacokinetic equations to estimate the AUC Vancomycin AUC less than 800 mg x hr/L and trough less than 15 mg/mL may reduce risk of acute kidney injury Obesity: Perform early and frequent therapeutic monitoring, guided by AUC 18/24
Dialysis: Obtain predialysis vancomycin serum concentrations not less than weekly; complete within 24 hours after initiating continuous re-
  nal replacement therapy Trough concentrations

  Trough-only monitoring, with a target of 15 to 20 mg/L, is no longer recommended in patients with serious infections due to MRSA A trough concentration range of 10 to 15 mg/L for less severe infections 48 and for children (older than neonates) with endocarditis is rec-
  ommended. In the presence of MRSA with MIC of greater than 1 mg/L or when there is a lack of microbiological response, then 15 to 20
  mg/L may be required The following recommendations are based on primarily adult data. Based on the pharmacodynamic properties of vancomycin and their
  presumed similarity among different age groups 49 , these recommendations may be applicable to pediatric patients.

  Many experts recommend a trough of 15 to 20 mg/L when treating MRSA bacteremia, infective endocarditis, osteomyelitis, meningi-
  tis, pneumonia, complicated skin and soft-tissue infections, or bone/joint infections 20,26,48,22 .
  For shunt infections, consider monitoring CSF vancomycin levels during therapy to assess drug concentrations (goal: trough, 5 to 10 mg/L)
  and potential drug accumulation 11,14,10 .

  Oral administration

  In patients with renal insufficiency, colitis, and/or inflammatory disorders of the intestinal mucosa, significant systemic absorption and
  serum vancomycin levels at therapeutic levels for systemic infection treatment have been reported after multiple oral doses of vancomycin;
  serum concentration monitoring may be appropriate in these patients', '●   Closely monitor for signs of coagulation and ischemia
or those who are receiving concomitant drugs that may cause neutropenia • Monitor for infusion-related events, including hypotension and vancomycin infusion reaction 1 ,2 .', 'Poorly absorbed orally. Diffusion into the lung and bone is variable. Minimal penetration into the CSF in absence of inflamed meninges.
  Protein binding is approximately 55% in adults. Mean volume of distribution is 3 to 9 L/kg in infants and 5 to 8 L/kg in children.
  Elimination is primarily by glomerular filtration (80% to 90% recovered unchanged in urine), with a small amount of hepatic metabolism.
  Higher clearance in pediatrics compared with adults (2 to 3 times higher). Mean half-life of 3 to 4 hours in infants and 2 to 3 hours in chil-
  dren. Not effectively removed by hemodialysis or peritoneal dialysis 31,53,9 ,58 . Following oral dosing, measurable serum concentrations
  may occur in patients with active C difficile-associated diarrhea and in those with renal impairment Special Populations
  • Cardiac surgical patients: An empiric dosage of 20 mg/kg/dose IV every 8 hours would likely achieve vancomycin AUC0 to 24:MIC ratio of
  greater than 400 (assuming a Staphylococcus aureus MIC of 1 mg/L) and a mean trough of 9 mg/L, based on a population pharmacoki-
  netic simulation using data from pediatric post-cardiac surgical patients. The median demographics of the population were 7 weeks ges-
  tational age, 31 years of age, 6 weeks postmenstrual age, and creatinine clearance of 84 mL/min/1.73 min2. Mean vancomycin dosage
  was 5 mg/kg/dose in the study population, and patients who had at least 1 measured vancomycin serum concentration were included in
  analysis; 578 vancomycin concentrations were analyzed in 261 patients 19/24
• Renal dysfunction and therapeutic hypothermia (TH): In a single center retrospective chart review of patients aged 30 days to 17 years
  who received at least 1 dose of vancomycin after cardiac arrest, vancomycin clearance was reduced by 80% and 56% in patients with
  glomerular filtration rates (GFR) of 20 and 40 mL/min/1.73 m2, respectively, compared with normal renal function (GFR of 90 mL/min/1.73
  m2). During TH, vancomycin clearance was reduced by 25% and 10% for temperatures of 32°C and 35°C, respectively. When TH and poor
  renal function were present, vancomycin clearance was reduced by up to 84% in a pharmacokinetic simulation. The median age was 43
  months (4 to 211 months; n=11) for the hypothermia group and 23 months (1.75 to 210 months; n=41) for the normothermia group • Cystic fibrosis (CF) patients: Vancomycin concentrations of 15 to 20 mg/L were achieved in 2% of 90 treatment courses after initial
  vancomycin dosage of 60 mg/kg/day divided every 6 hours in a retrospective study of 58 CF pediatric patients (11 months to 20 years of
  age). A mean dosage of 6+/-16.7 mg/kg/day (median 66 mg/kg/day; range, 60 to 120 mg/kg/day) was required for therapeutic concentra-
  tions (15 to 20 mg/L) over all treatment courses • Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO): Starting doses of 30 mg/kg/dose IV every 12 hours for infants, 25 mg/kg/dose IV every
  12 hours for children 2 to 12 years of age, and 30 mg/kg/dose IV every 24 hours for adolescents were predicted to achieve mean AUC val-
