   been receiving oral anticoagulants In observational studies of infants with early, classical, or late VKDB, single 1 to 10 mg IV doses
   were used 3 ,4 . Daily doses of 1 to 5 mg IV for a mean of 7 days has also been used Vitamin K antagonist-induced bleeding: 30 mcg/kg IV for excessively prolonged INR (usually
   greater than 8) and no significant bleeding. In the presence of significant bleeding, fresh frozen
   plasma, prothrombin complex concentrates, or recombinant factor VIIa is required Oral

   Cystic fibrosis, recommended daily intake: 7 , 8
   0 to 12 months of age: 3 to 5 mg/day orally.

   1 to 3 years of age: 3 to 5 mg/day orally.
   4 to 8 years of age: 3 to 5 mg/day orally.
   Greater than 8 years of age: 3 to 5 mg/day orally.

   Optimal supplementation of vitamin K in cystic fibrosis patients has not been established Cholestasis: 5 to 5 mg/day orally. Due to poor absorption in patients with cholestatic liver
   disease, intermittent parenteral vitamin K may be required 10,11 .

   Vitamin K deficiency bleeding, prophylaxis for early and late bleeding
   Oral (when IM route refused at birth)

IM is the preferred route 12,13 . Oral route is an acceptable alternative when the IM route is refused
   by the parents Avoid the oral route in infants who are premature, ill, or unable to take oral vita-
   min K; have cholestasis or impaired intestinal absorption; or were exposed to drugs that interfere
   with vitamin K metabolism through their mothers Guidelines Dosage

   2 mg orally at birth followed by 1 mg orally once weekly for 3 months 12,15

   OR 2 mg orally at birth followed by 2 mg orally at 4 to 6 days and at 4 to 6 weeks 12

   OR 2 mg orally at the first feeding followed by 2 mg orally at 2 to 4 weeks of age and 6 to 8
   weeks of age Adequate intake (AI)

   AIs may be used as goals for individual intake 16 :
