   Dobutamine and phenytoin.

   Monitoring
   Check prothrombin time/INR when treating clotting abnormalities; a minimum of 2 to 4 hours
   is needed for measurable improvement To ensure adequate supplementation in patients
   with vitamin K deficiency, monitor PIVKA-II (des-carboxylated prothrombin) levels as an efficacy
   endpoint 7 ,8 ,23 .', 'were used 3 ,4 . Daily doses of 1 to 5 mg IV for a mean of 7 days has also been used Vitamin K antagonist-induced bleeding: 30 mcg/kg IV for excessively prolonged INR (usually', 'Pharmacology
   Vitamin K is an essential cofactor in post-translational conversion of glutamyl residues to
   gamma carboxyglutamyl residues including prothrombin, osteocalcin, and other bone metab-
   olism-related proteins. Vitamin K1 (phytonadione) promotes formation of the following clotting
   factors in the liver: active prothrombin (factor II), proconvertin (factor VII), plasma thrombo-
   plastin component (factor IX), and Stuart factor (factor X). Vitamin K1 does not counteract the
   anticoagulant action of heparin. The onset of action after IV administration is within 1 to 2
   hours with hemorrhage usually controlled within 3 to 6 hours. A normal prothrombin level is
   frequently seen in 12 to 14 hours. The oral form is adequately absorbed form the GI tract only
   if bile salts are present, and generally exerts coagulation effect within 6 to 10 hours
   22 , 7 , 26 , 18 , 5/8'),
