   Uveitis, Refractory: Abatacept may be considered in patients with refractory uveitis However, pediatric data are limited. Uveitis inactivity was achieved in 11 patients (8 had a re-
   currence) and uveitis remained active in 10 patients with severe juvenile idiopathic arthritis-as-
   sociated uveitis refractory to corticosteroids and systemic immunosuppressants who were
   treated with abatacept in a retrospective study (n=21) Pediatric Approved FDA Indications

   Indicated as monotherapy or in combination with methotrexate for the treatment of moderate
   to severely active polyarticular juvenile idiopathic arthritis (JIA). The IV formulation is indicated
   in pediatric patients 6 years or older and the subQ formulation is indicated for those 2 years or
   older Indicated for the prophylaxis of acute graft versus host disease, in combination with a cal-
   cineurin inhibitor (CNI) and methotrexate, in pediatric patients 2 years or older undergoing
   hematopoietic stem cell transplantation (HSCT) from a matched or 1 allele-mismatched unre-
   lated donor 1

   Abatacept is indicated in pediatric patients 2 years or older for the treatment of active psoriatic
   arthritis (PsA) In pediatric patients 2 years or older, abatacept may be used as a subQ injection as monother-
   apy or concomitantly with methotrexate. IV administration is not approved for pediatric pa-
   tients with psoriatic arthritis Limitations of Use

