   An ACE inhibitor should be routinely used, unless there is a contraindication, for the treatment
   of symptomatic or asymptomatic left ventricular dysfunction. Consider the use for patients
   with Duchenne muscular dystrophy. ACE inhibitors are not recommended, but may be consid-
   ered in specific cases, for valve regurgitation or ventricular dysfunction due to single-ventricle
   congenital heart disease Captopril is considered the first choice ACE inhibitor for heart fail-
   ure in children younger than 5 years Afterload reduction in patients with congestive heart
   failure 28,29,30,9 ,31,32,5 .

   Congenital heart disease: Renin-angiotensin-adolsterone-system inhibitors may be consid-
   ered in pediatric patients with congenital heart disease and systemic left ventricular (LV) dys-
   function, especially in those with hypertension. In patients with systemic right ventricular (RV)
   dysfunction, these may be considered on a case-by-case basis Hypertension: Treatment of moderate to severe hypertension 10,11,20,22,21 .
   Guidelines: Pharmacological treatment should be initiated with an ACE inhibitor, an an-
   giotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), long-acting calcium-channel blocker, or a thiazide diuretic
   in children who remain hypertensive despite lifestyle modifications, have symptomatic hyper-
   tension or stage 2 hypertension without a modifiable risk factor, or have concomitant chronic
   kidney disease (CKD) or diabetes mellitus. In children with concomitant CKD, proteinuria, or di-
   abetes mellitus, an ACE inhibitor or ARB is recommended as the initial agent 2/9
Nephrotic Syndrome: Treatment of childhood nephrotic syndrome (steroid-resistant, steroid-
   dependent or frequently relapsing nephrotic syndrome) 34,18,19,35 . Treatment of
   congenital/early onset nephrotic syndrome 15,12,16,17,36 .

   Administration
   Administer 1 hour before meals; food decreases absorption Preparation

   Extemporaneous oral compound

   Captopril 1 mg/mL oral solution (100 mL) 24,25
   •Place two captopril 50-mg tablets in a graduated cylinder. Add 50 mL distilled water to dis-
   solve and disintegrate tablet; shake until dissolved.
   •Add 500 mg ascorbic acid (either one 500-mg tablet or 500 mg of injection) to mixture and al-
   low to dissolve.

   •Add distilled water to fill to final volume of 100 mL
   •Shake well before use; do not filter.

   •Stable for 56 days under refrigeration (4°C). Room temperature (22°C) stability depends on
   ascorbic acid formulation used. If ascorbic acid tablet was used, solution is stable for 28 days
   at room temperature; If ascorbic acid injection was used, solution is table for 14 days at room
   temperature.

