Mechanism of action: Captopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor that
thought to be caused by a combination of afterload reduction and long-term inhibition of salt', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Captopril is an angiotensin-converting enzyme (ACE) inhibitor that
   blocks the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II, a potent vasoconstrictor. It thereby de-
   creases plasma and tissue concentrations of angiotensin II and aldosterone, and increases
   plasma and tissue renin activity. Captopril also prevents the breakdown of bradykinin, a potent
   vasodilator. Vascular resistance is reduced without reflex tachycardia. Beneficial effects are
   thought to be caused by a combination of afterload reduction and long-term inhibition of salt
   and water retention Similar pharmacokinetics seen with infants and children compared with adults.

   Absorption: Rapid absorption after oral administration with peak concentration attained at
   approximately 1 hour (range, 30 to 120 minutes). Absorption reduced by 30% to 40% in the
   presence of food. Onset of action is 15 minutes after a dose, with peak effects seen at approxi-
   mately 90 minutes.

   Duration of action: Approximately 6 to 10 hours.

   Elimination: Most of the drug is eliminated in the kidney (95%) with 40% to 50% as unchanged
   drug.

   Half-life: Elimination half-life approximately 3 hours in infants with congestive heart failure. A
   lower mean half-life (1.5 hours; range, 98 to 3 hours) was reported in normotensive chil-
   dren with renal scarring. Decreased clearance in patients with renal impairment 23 , 41 , 42 ,'),
