   CloNIDine given epidurally is a centrally-acting analgesic. It produces dose-dependent analge-
   sia that is not antagonized by opioid antagonists. Its action is restricted to body areas inner-
   vated by the spinal segments where analgesic levels of cloNIDine are present. The analgesic ef-
   fect of cloNIDine is thought to occur through prevention of pain signal transmission to the
   brain at presynaptic and post-junctional alpha-2-adrenoreceptors in the spinal cord. After a
   700-mcg epidural dose, peak concentrations were reached in 19 (+/- 27) minutes with CSF peak
   levels reached in 26 (+/-11) minutes. Epidural cloNIDine is lipid soluble and widely distributed.
   Protein binding is 20% to 40% (mainly albumin). Metabolized via minor pathways to the major
   metabolite (p-hydroxy-cloNIDine), which accounts for less than 10% of unchanged drug in the
   urine. Primarily eliminated in the urine (72%) with 40% to 50% as unchanged drug. Elimination
   half-life is approximately 9 (+/- 2) hours after a 300-mcg dose and 22 (+/- 15) hours after a 700-
   mcg dose'),
