   Monitoring
   Continuous ECG monitoring and frequent blood pressure monitoring should be performed
   during continuous infusion therapy Monitor renal and hepatic function periodically during
   oral therapy Monitor blood pressure during dose titration and periodically during therapy.', 'liver via CYP450 3A enzymes. Protein binding is approximately 77% to 93%, with 35% to 40%', 'Pharmacology
   DilTIAZem, a benzothiazepine derivative, is a slow calcium channel blocker that blocks calcium
   ion influx during depolarization of cardiac and vascular smooth muscle (VSM). It decreases pe-
   ripheral vascular resistance and causes relaxation of the VSM resulting in a decrease of both
   systolic and diastolic blood pressure. No published pediatric pharmacokinetic data available,
   therefore, only adult data are reported. Following oral administration, dilTIAZem is well ab-
   sorbed from the GI tract and is subject to an extensive first-pass effect. Bioavailability is ap-
   proximately 40% for the immediate-release formulation 15 , and 93% to greater than 95% for
   extended-release formulations 9 ,17 . Bioavailability is not greatly affected by food. Onset for
   immediate-release formulations is 30 minutes with peak concentration reached in approxi-
   mately 2 to 4 hours. Peak response for extended-release formulations is 3 to 6 hours with
   peak concentration reached in approximately 6 to 11 hours. Metabolized extensively in the
   liver via CYP450 3A enzymes. Protein binding is approximately 77% to 93%, with 35% to 40%
   binding to albumin Approximately 95% of the dose is excreted (60% in the feces and 35% in
   the urine) within 72 hours 19 ; only about 2% to 4% of the drug is excreted unchanged in the
   urine. Elimination half-life is approximately 3 to 5 hours for the intravenous 16 and immedi-

ate-release oral formulations 15 , and 5 to 10 hours for the extended-release oral formulations Half-life is increased in patients with hepatic impairment'),
