Foscarnet is an organic analogue of pyrophosphate that inhibits replication of herpes viruses
(eg, cytomegalovirus (CMV), herpes simplex virus (HSV) types 1 and 2) by selective inhibition at', 'Pharmacology
   Foscarnet is an organic analogue of pyrophosphate that inhibits replication of herpes viruses
   (eg, cytomegalovirus (CMV), herpes simplex virus (HSV) types 1 and 2) by selective inhibition at
   the pyrophosphate binding site on virus-specific DNA polymerases. This action occurs at con-
   centrations that do not affect cellular DNA polymerases. Because it does not require activation
   by kinases, CMV strains resistant to ganciclovir and HSV strains resistant to acyclovir may be
   sensitive to foscarnet. Foscarnet also as the potential to chelate divalent ions, such as calcium
   and magnesium. Apparent volume of distribution 4 to 5 L/kg; drug accumulation noted in
   bone. Renal clearance comprises approximately 90% of the systemic clearance of 6 to 7 L/hr.
   Elimination half-life is dependent on renal function; mean values of 2, 3, 13, and 25 hours were
   reported in patients having mean CrCl of 108, 68, 34, and 20 mL/min, respectively 5'),
