  Linezolid is not indicated for the treatment of gram-negative infections. It is critical that specific gram-negative therapy be initiated immedi-
  ately if a concomitant gram-negative pathogen is documented or suspected The safety and efficacy of linezolid formulations given for longer than 28 days have not been evaluated in controlled clinical trials Administration
  IV: Give as an intermittent IV infusion over 30 to 120 minutes. Supplied as ready-to-use infusion bags (2 mg/mL); no further dilution is nec-
  essary Oral: May administer without regard to meals. Before administering oral suspension, gently mix by inverting bottle 3 to 5 times. Do not
  shake Preparation

  Oral suspension

  To reconstitute the oral suspension, add 123 mL distilled water (final concentration, 100 mg/5 mL) in 2 portions, shaking between each por-
  tion. After reconstitution, invert the bottle gently 3 to 5 times before each use but do NOT shake. Reconstituted solution should be used
  within 21 days when stored at room temperature 10/16', '•Reversible tooth and tongue discoloration has been reported in association with oral linezolid therapy 36,37 . The most common drug-re-
  lated adverse effects reported in clinical trials included diarrhea (3.8% to 7%), nausea (1.4% to 3%), vomiting (1.2% to 9%), and loose
  stools (1.2% to 9%) •Sideroblastic anemia and severe cutaneous adverse reactions such as toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome have
  been reported during postmarketing surveillance •In a randomized study of hospitalized pediatric patients (aged newborn to 11 years) with Gram-positive infections, fatalities occurred in 6%
  of linezolid-treated patients (n=215) compared with 3% of vancomycin-treated patients (n=101) Solution Compatibility
