  Monitoring
  Therapeutic Monitoring (TB Treatment)

  • Monitor for clinical (eg, decrease in cough and systemic symptoms, increase in weight), radiologic, and bacteriologic response to treat-
  ment • Obtain sputum culture prior to initiation, then monthly during treatment. If cultures remain positive after 3 months, repeat susceptibility
  testing Toxic Monitoring
  • Monitor CBC weekly, especially in patients receiving linezolid for longer than 2 weeks, those with myelosuppression, those receiving con-
  current myelosuppressive drugs, or those with a chronic infection who have received previous or concomitant antibiotic therapy • TB treatment: Obtain CBC with differential prior to initiation, every 2 weeks for 2 months, then every 4 weeks for the duration of linezolid
  therapy • TB treatment: Linezolid trough levels greater than 2 mcg/mL have been associated with an increased risk of toxicity • Monitor lactate concentrations in patients receiving extended courses of linezolid therapy or in patients with pre-existing hepatic or renal
  dysfunction • Monitor blood pressure in patients with uncontrolled hypertension, pheochromocytoma, thyrotoxicosis, and/or in patients receiving sym-
  pathomimetic agents, vasopressive agents, or dopaminergic agents • Patients receiving an extended course of therapy (eg, over 28 days) should be monitored for signs and symptoms of neuropathy • TB treatment: Test reflexes at baseline and consider regular pinprick testing to monitor for neuropathy • Monitor for signs and symptoms of serotonin syndrome (hyperpyrexia, hyperreflexia, and incoordination) in patients receiving concomi-
  tant serotonergic agents 12/16
• Visual function should be assessed in patients receiving long-term linezolid (3 months or greater) and in all patients experiencing visual
  impairment • TB treatment: Test visual acuity at baseline and throughout treatment duration using symbol charts or "tumbling Es"; visual tracking of ob-
  jects can be used for infants and toddlers Therapeutic Drug Monitoring (TB Treatment)

  Consider monitoring therapeutic drug concentrations in patients with the following conditions 13 :
  • Gastrointestinal problems that may increase the risk of malabsorption

  • Concurrent HIV infection
  • Impaired renal function

  • Diabetes

