Linezolid is an oxazolidinone agent that has a unique mechanism of inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis. It is usually bacteriostatic, al-
dation without cytochrome P450 induction. Nonrenal clearance accounts for 65% of the total clearance. Excreted in the urine as unchanged
spective study (n=23). Concomitant proton pump inhibitors or amiodarone were associated with an increased Cmin; conversely, concomi-', 'Pharmacology
  Linezolid is an oxazolidinone agent that has a unique mechanism of inhibition of bacterial protein synthesis. It is usually bacteriostatic, al-
  though it may be bactericidal against S. pneumoniae, B. fragilis, and C. perfringens. Resistance to linezolid has been reported for vancomycin-
  resistant E. faecium and methicillin-resistant S. aureus 41,42,43,44,45 . Outbreaks of linezolid-resistant S. aureus, S. epidermidis, and S. haemolyti-
  cus have been reported in adult ICU settings. The majority of patients had received linezolid previously 46,47,41 .

  Rapidly penetrates osteoarticular tissues and synovial fluid. Central nervous system concentrations were 70% of plasma concentrations in
  older patients with non-inflamed meninges. Completely and rapidly absorbed when administered orally to adults and children. Protein
  binding approximately 31%. Volume of distribution in children is approximately 6 to 7 L/kg after IV administration. Metabolized by oxi-
  dation without cytochrome P450 induction. Nonrenal clearance accounts for 65% of the total clearance. Excreted in the urine as unchanged
  drug (30%) and two inactive metabolites (10% to 40%). Eliminated by dialysis (30%). Clearance decreases with increasing age (5.4 mL/min/kg
  for infants, 8 mL/min/kg for children, and 1 mL/kg/min for adolescents after IV administration). Serum half-life in infants (29 days to less
  than 3 months), children (3 months to 11 years of age), and adolescents is approximately 8, 9, and 1 hours, respectively, after IV ad-
  ministration 4 ,48,49 .

  Standard linezolid dosages (10 mg/kg/dose every 8 hours for children 2 to 11 years of age and 600 mg every 12 hours for children 12 to 18
  years of age) resulted in suboptimal Cmin (less than 2 mg/L) linezolid concentrations in 50% and 4% of children, respectively in a retro-
  spective study (n=23). Concomitant proton pump inhibitors or amiodarone were associated with an increased Cmin; conversely, concomi-
