   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Physical Monitoring
   • Use of the COMFORT-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale
   are recommended to assess level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients 13
   • For all pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation, consider assigning a target depth
   of sedation using a validated sedation assessment tool at least once daily 13

   • Assess periodically for therapeutic usefulness with long-term use Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Oral: Monitor blood counts periodically in patients on long-term therapy • Oral: Monitor liver function tests periodically in patients on long-term therapy Toxic Physical Monitoring

   • Prior to initiation and throughout treatment, assess each patient’s risk for abuse, misuse, and
   addiction and monitor for signs and symptoms of abuse, misuse, and addiction 7 , 11/17
• Oral: Monitor for symptoms of upper gastrointestinal disease frequently in patients on long-
   term therapy • Injection: In patients treated for status epilepticus, monitor vital signs while on parenteral
   therapy. If respiratory depression occurs, monitor respiration status closely • Use of the COMFORT-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale
   are recommended to assess level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients 13
   The Withdrawal Assessment Tool-1 (WAT-1) or Sophia Observational Scale (SOS) are recom-
   mended for the assessment of iatrogenic withdrawal syndrome (IWS) due to opioid or benzo-
   diazepine withdrawal in critically ill pediatric patients in the ICU • For all pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation, consider assigning a target depth
   of sedation using a validated sedation assessment tool at least once daily 13

   • When discontinuing therapy in critically ill patients, use of either the Withdrawal Assessment
   Tool-1 or Sophia Observation Scale for the assessment of iatrogenic withdrawal syndrome is
   recommended. Routine screening is recommended after a shorter duration (3 to 5 days) when
   higher benzodiazepine doses are used To monitor for propylene glycol toxicity, consider monitoring osmol gap when higher lo-
   razepam infusion rates (e.g. 07 mg/kg/hr or greater) are used. This suggestion is based on a
   retrospective chart review of 21 pediatric patients (0.3 to 52 years of age) on continuous in-
   fusion lorazepam', 'Anticipatory chemotherapy-induced nausea and vomiting, prophylaxis: 04 to 08
