   Guidelines: The initial treatment for type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM) includes metFORMIN
   and/or insulin as determined by symptoms, severity of hyperglycemia, and presence/absence
   of ketosis/DKA. For children with HbA1c less than 5%, metFORMIN monotherapy with life-
   style changes is the treatment of choice; for children with HbA1c greater than or equal to 5%,
   insulin will be required initially. MetFORMIN should be started at the same time as insulin un-
   less acidosis is present. Transition to metFORMIN alone can usually be achieved over 2 to 6
   weeks by decreasing the insulin dose by 30% to 50% each time the metFORMIN dose is in-
   creased. If HbA1c goal is not met after initial treatment, consider adding a glucagon-like pep-
   tide-1 (GLP-1) agonist or empagliflozin 12 , In the first multicenter, randomized, placebo-controlled, 16-week trial in children with type 2
   diabetes mellitus (T2DM) (n=82; 10 to 17 years of age), metFORMIN titrated up to 2000 mg/day
   significantly reduced adjusted mean fasting plasma glucose (FPG) by 9 mg/dL; greater re-
   ductions were seen when baseline FPG values were 200 mg/dL or greater. Adjusted mean
   HbA1c was reduced by 2%. Nearly 85% of patients treated with metFORMIN achieved
   American Diabetes Association (ADA) goals for either FPG or HbA1c as compared with 22% of
   placebo-treated patients. Based upon convincing evidence of efficacy and lack of safety con-
   cerns from a planned interim analysis, the trial was terminated early at the recommendation
   of an independent Data and Safety Monitoring Board; subjects on placebo treatment were
   then switched to open-label metFORMIN 2/13
In the TODAY study (n=699; 10 to 17 years of age), children and adolescents with type 2 dia-
   betes mellitus (T2DM) initially achieved glycemic control with metFORMIN 1000 mg twice daily,
   and were then randomized to receive continued metFORMIN alone, metFORMIN plus rosiglita-
   zone 4 mg twice daily, or metFORMIN plus intensive lifestyle intervention. metFORMIN
   monotherapy produced durable glycemic control in nearly half of patients over a range of up
   to 60 months; it was least effective in non-Hispanic black patients compared to other racial or
   ethnic groups. The addition of rosiglitazone, but not lifestyle intervention, proved superior to
   metFORMIN therapy alone and was more effective in girls than boys. However, 39% of patients
   did not have long-term glycemic control with this combination. Increases in BMI were greatest
   for the metFORMIN plus rosiglitazone group and least for the metFORMIN plus intensive life-
   style intervention group. Based upon these results, the majority of pediatric patients with
   T2DM may require combination therapy, and perhaps insulin, within several years after diag-
   nosis 14,15 .
   Glimepiride was compared to metFORMIN (n=285; 8 to 17 years of age) and produced similar
