   treatment with metFORMIN delays or prevents the development of PCOS in SGA girls with PP Treatment of insulin resistance (IR) related to obesity.

   Lifestyle intervention (nutrition, exercise, and behavioral) is a necessary component for the
   treatment for IR in pediatric patients 25,26 . When lifestyle interventions fail, metFORMIN may
   be considered in obese type 2 diabetic children or obese girls with polycystic ovary syndrome
   and impaired glucose tolerance Two meta-analyses have examined the effect of metFORMIN on IR 27,28 ; the larger of these re-
   viewed 5 randomized controlled trials (total n=365; 6 to 19 years of age). metFORMIN 1000 to
   2000 mg/day, over at least 6 months, reduced BMI and IR (measured by homeostasis model
   assessment insulin resistance) in obese, hyperinsulinemic children, many from ethnic back-
   grounds with a high prevalence of metabolic syndrome. There was no effect on fasting glu-
   cose, HDL cholesterol, triglyceride levels, or blood pressure In contrast, a randomized,
   placebo-controlled trial demonstrated no difference between metFORMIN and placebo in re-
   ducing BMI or improving IR (n=86; mean age 8 years). The 70 patients who did not signifi-
