   Iatrogenic opioid withdrawal syndrome:
   Initial dose: 05 to 1 mg/kg orally every 6 hours and adjust dose by 05 mg/kg if necessary
   to control symptoms. After 24 to 48 hours, extend interval to 8 to 12 hours, followed by a 10 to
   14 day taper of the dose 6 ,7 ,8 ,9 .

   Iatrogenic withdrawal syndrome, Prevention - Weaning Protocols

   The following protocol has been used to prevent opioid withdrawal in patients receiving 7 to
   14 days of continuous infusion fentanyl 10,11 :

    ●   Calculate the 24-hour fentanyl dose and convert to an equipotent methadone dose (eg, (24-
        hour fentanyl dose (mg) x 100)/6 = initial daily methadone dose (mg)). Potency ratio of fen-
        tanyl to methadone = 100:1. Correction factor for methadone half-life = 2/18
●   Day 1, give initial daily methadone dose orally or IV in 4 divided doses every 6 hours for 24
        hours. Maximum 40 mg/day.
    ●   Day 2, give 80% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 3 divided doses every 8 hours for
        24 hours.
    ●   Day 3, give 60% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 3 divided doses every 8 hours for
        24 hours.
    ●   Day 4, give 40% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 2 divided doses every 12 hours for
        24 hours.
    ●   Day 5, give 20% of initial daily methadone dose orally one time.
    ●   Day 6, discontinue methadone.

   The following protocol has been used to prevent opioid withdrawal in patients receiving
   greater than 14 days of continuous infusion fentanyl 10,11 :
    ●   Calculate the 24-hour fentanyl dose and convert to an equipotent methadone dose (eg, (24-
        hour fentanyl dose (mg) x 100)/6 = initial daily methadone dose (mg)). Potency ratio of fen-
        tanyl to methadone = 100:1. Correction factor for methadone half-life = ●   Days 1 and 2, give initial daily methadone dose orally or IV in 4 divided doses every 6 hours
        for 48 hours. Maximum 40 mg/day.
    ●   Days 3 and 4, give 80% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 3 divided doses every 8
        hours for 48 hours.
    ●   Days 5 and 6, give 60% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 3 divided doses every 8
        hours for 48 hours.
    ●   Days 7 and 8, give 40% of initial daily methadone dose orally in 2 divided doses every 12
        hours for 48 hours.
    ●   Days 9 and 10, give 20% of initial daily methadone dose orally once a day for 48 hours.
    ●   Day 11, discontinue methadone.

   Note: The same protocols can be used in patients receiving continuous infusion morphine.
   Calculate the 24-hour morphine dose and convert to an equipotent methadone dose (eg, (24-
   hour morphine dose (mg) x 1)/6 = initial daily methadone dose (mg)). The potency ratio mor-
   phine to methadone = 1:1. Correction factor for methadone half-life = Follow the protocols
   above based on duration of continuous infusion morphine Concomitant medication: Consider prescribing naloxone based on the patient''s risk factors
