Routinely monitor for signs of misuse, abuse, and addiction during treatment. Monitor for', 'Pharmacology
   Long-acting narcotic analgesic. Analgesic potency 5 to 20 times greater than morphine. Oral
   bioavailability is high (80% to 90%). Analgesic effect 1 to 2 hours after administration, with
   peak plasma levels obtained in 2 to 4 hours. Duration of action of 6 to 24 hours. Highly protein
   bound (85% in adults). Metabolized extensively via hepatic N-demethylation through cy-
   tochrome P450 enzyme subsystem. Mean serum half-life of 19 hours in children (range, 4 to
   62 hours) and 44 hours in neonates (range, 8 to 62 hours). Half-life prolonged in patients
   with renal failure. Rifampin and phenytoin accelerate the metabolism of methadone and can
   precipitate withdrawal symptoms 3 , 27 , Factors to consider which differentiate methadone from other opioids 18,19,20 :
    ●   High inter-patient variability in absorption, metabolism, and relative analgesic potency
    ●   Duration of analgesic effect shorter (based on single-dose studies) than the plasma half-life
    ●   Steady-state plasma concentrations, and full analgesic effects, not attained until 3 or more
        days after initiation of dosing
    ●   Peak respiratory depressant effect occurs later and persists longer than peak analgesic
        effect
    ●   With repeated dosing, methadone is retained in the liver and slowly released, which pro-
        longs the duration of potential toxicity
    ●   Narrow therapeutic index, especially in combination with other drugs

●   High opioid tolerance does not eliminate the possibility of overdose with methadone'),
