   Other Dose Adjustments
   • Gastrointestinal (GI) effects in Niemann-pick type C disease, 4 years and older: Consider
   dosage reduction if intolerable GI effects persist despite intervention, and discontinue treat-
   ment in severe cases of GI disturbances that cannot be managed with dosage reduction, di-
   etary modification, and antidiarrheals (eg, loperamide) • Neurological deterioration in Niemann-pick type C disease, 4 years and older:
   Discontinuation should be considered if progressive neurological deterioration leading to an
   unacceptable quality of life occurs', 'Niemann-Pick disease, Type C with Neurological Manifestations
   Guideline recommendations
   • Offer miglustat therapy to all patients with Niemann-Pick type C disease (NPC) and neurologi-
   cal, psychiatric, or cognitive signs or symptoms at diagnosis. There are no curative treatments
   for NPC, but long-term miglustat therapy may stabilize the disease or reduce the rate of pro-
   gression. Clinical benefits may not be apparent for 6 months to 1 year and may take longer
   with slowly progressive disease (eg, adult-onset) • In patients younger than 4 years, more data are needed to determine the efficacy of miglus-
   tat • Do not initiate miglustat therapy in patients with a confirmed NPC diagnosis without neuro-
   logical, psychiatric, or cognitive signs or symptoms at diagnosis, as these manifestations may
   take a long time to develop or, in rare cases, may not develop • If there is severe neurological impairment at diagnosis, especially if patients are very young,
   miglustat is less likely to provide substantial benefit • In young patients with cholestatic disease, resolve systemic manifestations before starting
   miglustat; miglustat does not treat cholestatic symptoms 1

   • In patients with NPC and systemic symptoms or who were identified during sibling screening,
   monitor regularly for neurological symptoms and consider treatment at the first signs of neu-
   rological disease 1

12 Years and older

   • In a randomized trial of patients 12 years or older with Niemann-Pick type C disease (NPC),
   there was no significant difference with miglustat (n=20) versus standard care (n=9) in the pri-
   mary outcome of mean change from baseline to 12 months in horizontal saccadic eye move-
   ment (HSEM)-alpha (-0.431 vs +0.074 ms/deg; mean difference [MD], -0.518; 95% CI, -1.125 to 089). HSEM-alpha corresponds to the asymptotic peak velocity. In exploratory analysis that
   excluded patients taking benzodiazepines (5 vs 1), there was a significant difference in HSEM-
   alpha with miglustat versus standard care (-0.485 vs +0.234 ms/deg; MD, -0.718; 95% CI, -1.349
   vs -0.088). HSEM-beta increased in all patients; increases were smaller with miglustat but there
   was no significant difference compared with standard care (MD, -0.722 ms/deg; 95% CI, -7.781
   to 337). At 12 months, significantly more patients were able to swallow one-third of a cookie
   with miglustat versus standard care (85% vs 63%); miglustat increased the ability to swallow 5
   mL of water (60% to 85%) and 1 teaspoon of puree (85% to 95%) but standard care did not
   (75% for water and 88% for puree at both timepoints). The ability to swallow 1 teaspoon of soft
   lumps did not change with miglustat (80%) but decreased with standard care (75% to 63%).
   Standard ambulatory index with miglustat increased from 4 to 6 from baseline to last visit
   and with standard care from 9 to 6. Mini-mental status exam (MMSE) increased from 8
   to 24 with miglustat and decreased from 4 to 1 with standard care. In the miglustat
   group, auditory acuity was normal in 15 of 20 patients in the right ear and 16 of 20 in the left
   ear; at 12 months, normal auditory acuity was reported in 1 additional patient for the right and
   left ears. In the standard care group, all 9 patients had normal auditory acuity for the right and
   left ears at baseline and 2 of 9 had abnormal results at last assessment. There was high inter-
   patient variability in outcome parameters, especially those related to SEM. In the miglustat and
   standard care groups, respectively, the mean age was 4 and 9 years, and 45% and 56%
   were men • In a 12-month open-label extension phase, 15 patients completed 24 months of miglustat
   treatment. Among those completers, HSEM-alpha improved from baseline to 12 months (3.04
   to 57 ms/deg) and was stable compared with baseline at 24 months (3.27 ms/deg). HSEM-
   beta mildly deteriorated from 51 ms/deg at baseline to 98 ms/deg at 12 months and 85 ms/deg at 24 months. Compared with baseline, swallowing was improved or stable in
   86% of patients at 12 months and 79% to 93% of patients at 24 months, depending on sub-
   stance swallowed. Among 24-month completers (n=15), standard ambulation index (SAI)
   scores were 13 at baseline, 2 at 12 months, and 4 at 24 months (lower scores indicate
   better ambulation). Among 6 patients who completed 24 months, mean MMSE scores were 5 at baseline, 17 at 12 months, and 33 at 24 months • In an Italian observational study of patients with Niemann-Pick type C disease (NPC) who re-
   ceived miglustat (N=25), the mean composite severity score (MCSS) was stabilized or improved
   in 3 of 9 patients with juvenile NPC (older than 5 years up to 16 years). Two patients with early

infantile NPC (2 years old or younger) and 6 patients with late infantile NPC (older than 2 years
   up to 5 years) did not experience long-term improvement or stabilization, even if they re-
   sponded to treatment within the first 24 months. Two patients with visceral NPC who received
   miglustat for prevention of neurological manifestation did not develop any neurological sign or
   symptom after 60 and 84 months of treatment. Among the 25 patients, the mean age at miglu-
   stat initiation was 69 years, the mean age at NPC diagnosis was 73 years, and 9 were
   male 4 to 12 years old

   • In a French observational study of pediatric patients with NPC (n=20), key neurological pa-
   rameters on NPC disability scales were stabilized in 1 of 8 patients with early-infantile neuro-
   logical onset, stabilized or improved in 6 of 8 patients with late-infantile neurological onset,
   and improved in 1 of 3 patients with juvenile neurological onset; 1 patient with perinatal vis-
   ceral NPC was not evaluated. The median age at neurological disease onset was 3 years
