      Reference: Davis, 2017', 'It acts via selective phosphodiesterase III inhibition that leads to increased intracellular cyclic', 'Pharmacology
   Milrinone (bipyridine derivative of amrinone) improves cardiac output by enhancing myocar-
   dial contractility, enhancing myocardial diastolic relaxation and decreasing vascular resistance.
   It acts via selective phosphodiesterase III inhibition that leads to increased intracellular cyclic
   AMP, increased myocardial intracellular calcium, and increased reuptake of calcium after sys-
   tole. Vasodilatation is related to increased levels of cyclic GMP in vascular smooth muscle.
   Unlike catecholamines, myocardial oxygen consumption is not increased 12 , In one pharma-
   cokinetic study (n=20) in children, a mean blood pressure decrease of 12% and a mean cardiac
   index increase of 18% were noted after loading doses (50 mcg/kg) Can take 3 to 30 hours to
   reach steady state if no loading dose is used Volume of distribution approximately 9 L/kg
   in infants and 7 L/kg in children (greater than adults). Approximately 70% bound to plasma
   protein. Elimination is primarily via renal mechanisms. Faster clearance in children compared
   with infants (both greater than adults) 12,22 . Half-life is quite variable, ranging from 62 to 9 hours in one study (mean, 9 hours; median, 5 hours) Dose reduction recom-
   mended in patients with renal impairment Of the 220 blood levels analyzed, only 48% were within the therapeutic range for milrinone
   (100 to 300 ng/mL) in children younger than 2 years of age administered a median loading
   dose of 50 mcg/kg followed by 5 mcg/kg/min (interquartile range (IQR), 5 to 75
   mcg/kg/min). The median (IQR) was 82 ng/mL (46 to 185 ng/mL). Levels were drawn during 3
   time periods after the start of infusion (9 to 12 hours, 18 to 24 hours, and 40 to 48 hours) and
   again at the end of infusion 7/10'),
