   over 2 to 3 days Hypertension, chronic
   Diuretic and beta-blocker or other sympathetic nervous system suppressant should be used con-
   comitantly Younger than 12 years: Initial, 2 mg/kg/dose orally once daily. Dose may be increased by
   50% to 100% no sooner than every 3 days. Effective dose range: 25 to 1 mg/kg/day. Doses
   may be in single or divided doses 2 (1 to 3 times daily) maximum 50 mg/day 2
   12 years or older: Initial, 5 mg orally once daily. Dose may be increased to 10, 20 and 40 mg in
   single or divided doses 2 (1 to 3 times daily) Dose increases should be no sooner than every
   3 days. Effective dose range: 10 to 40 mg/day. maximum 100 mg/day 2', 'Indication is limited to the treatment of hypertension that is symptomatic or associated with
   target organ damage and is not manageable with maximum therapeutic doses of a diuretic
   plus 2 antihypertensive agents. Not recommended for milder degrees of hypertension Administration
   • Tablets may be halved Extemporaneous oral compound

   1 mg/mL Suspension (300 mL) 6
   • Triturate thirty 10-mg tablets of minoxidil to a fine powder using a mortar and pestle.
   • Measure 150 mL of Ora-Plus. Add a small amount of Ora-Plus to wet the powder and mix
   into a smooth, thick paste.
   • Add the remaining Ora-Plus to mixture via doubling; mix well until completely incorporated.

   • Add a small amount of Ora-Sweet SF and mix well; transfer to a graduated conical measur-
   ing cylinder.
   • Rinse mortar with small amounts of Ora-Sweet SF and transfer rinsings to cylinder until a fi-
   nal volume of 300 mL is reached. Mix well with a stirring rod, then transfer to a prescription
   bottle
   • Label the container with "Shake well before using."

   • Stable for 3 weeks at room temperature (25°C) or 24 weeks when refrigerated (4°C).', 'Common: Hypertrichosis without virilism (80%) and temporary edema (7%). Rashes, including
   severe reactions, have been reported Hypertrichosis: Develops within 3 to 6 weeks of therapy starting at the temples, between the
   eyebrows, and between the hairline and the eyebrows, or in the side-burn area of the upper
   lateral cheek. It then progresses to the back, arms, legs, and scalp. New hair growth stops after
   minoxidil is discontinued but 1 to 6 months may be required for restoration to baseline.

   Black Box Warning
   •Minoxidil tablets contain the powerful antihypertensive agent, minoxidil, which may produce
   serious adverse effects. It can cause pericardial effusion, occasionally progressing to tampon-
   ade, and angina pectoris may be exacerbated. Minoxidil should be reserved for hypertensive
   patients who do not respond adequately to maximum therapeutic doses of a diuretic and two
   other antihypertensive agents.
   •In experimental animals, minoxidil caused several kinds of myocardial lesions as well as other
   adverse cardiac effects.

•Minoxidil must be administered under close supervision, usually concomitantly with thera-
   peutic doses of a beta-adrenergic blocking agent to prevent tachycardia and increased my-
   ocardial workload. It must also usually be given with a diuretic, frequently one acting in the as-
   cending limb of the loop of Henle, to prevent serious fluid accumulation. Patients with malig-
   nant hypertension and those already receiving guanethidine should be hospitalized when mi-
   noxidil is first administered so that they can be monitored to avoid too rapid, or large ortho-
   static, decreases in blood pressure Monitoring
   • Closely monitor fluid and electrolyte balance and body weight • Observe closely for signs or symptoms of pericardial disorder (ie, pericardial effusion)', 'tion, minoxidil triggers sympathetic, vagal inhibitory, and renal homeostatic mechanisms.', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Minoxidil is a direct acting peripheral vasodilator used to reduce elevated systolic and diastolic
   blood pressure by decreasing peripheral vascular resistance. As a result of peripheral vasodila-
   tion, minoxidil triggers sympathetic, vagal inhibitory, and renal homeostatic mechanisms.
   Minoxidil does not interfere with vasomotor reflexes and therefore does not produce ortho-
   static hypotension Absorption: At least 90% absorbed in the GI tract. Maximum plasma levels are reached within
   the first hour and rapidly decline going forward Distribution: No protein binding. Does not cross the blood brain barrier Metabolism: 90% of minoxidil is metabolized. Predominantly metabolized by conjugation with
   glucuronic acid at the N-oxide position in the pyrimidine ring Excretion: Glomerular filtration rate approximates renal clearance. Minoxidil and metabolites
   can be removed by hemodialysis Half-life: 2 hours'),
