Phenytoin is an antiepileptic drug that inhibits the spread of seizure activity in the motor cor-
jury leading to increased dosage requirements. Potent inducer of cytochrome P450 enzyme', 'Pharmacology
   Phenytoin is an antiepileptic drug that inhibits the spread of seizure activity in the motor cor-
   tex. Through the promotion of sodium efflux from neurons, phenytoin possibly tends to stabi-
   lize the threshold against hyperexcitability caused by excessive stimulation or changes in envi-
   ronment that is capable of reducing membrane sodium gradient. Displays nonlinear (zero or-
   der), Michaelis-Menten kinetics (as dose increases, saturation of elimination mechanisms oc-
   cur leading to progressive accumulation of phenytoin). After IV administration, peak concen-

tration reached within 10 to 30 minutes. After oral administration, peak of 5 to 3 hours for
   suspension and tablets and 4 to 12 hours for capsules. Absorption increased when oral pheny-
   toin is given with food. Suspension has best absorption, followed by capsules, followed by
   tablets. Highly protein bound (90%); only free fraction can cross blood-brain barrier. Bilirubin
   displaces phenytoin from protein-binding sites, resulting in increased serum free phenytoin
   concentration. Higher free phenytoin levels in patients with decreased protein binding (hypoal-
   buminemia). Bioavailability in young infants (30% to 50%) appears to be less when compared
   with children and adults (greater than 90%). Primarily eliminated through hepatic metabolism.
   Altered protein binding and metabolism shown in critically ill children with traumatic brain in-
   jury leading to increased dosage requirements. Potent inducer of cytochrome P450 enzyme
   systems resulting in a reduction of serum levels of drugs metabolized by this system. Renal ex-
   cretion is negligible (1% to 5%). Serum half-life is approximately 10 to 70 hours, but is depen-
   dent on serum concentration 54,55,56,50,8 .

   The clearance, Vd, and bioavailability of phenytoin were 14 L/hr/70 kg, 447 L/70 kg, and 63%,
   respectively, in 1 month to 17 years of age (average age, 9 years) critically ill patients with
   serum albumin of 25 g/L or more in a population pharmacokinetic study (n=32). The mean un-
   bound phenytoin fraction was 12. During the study, the absolute change in serum albumin
   concentration was 26% (2% to 54%) in 70% of patients'),
