Ritonavir is an inhibitor of HIV-1 and HIV-2 protease 8 , and is as a pharmacokinetic enhancer of
other protease inhibitors (PI) in the pediatric population. Ritonavir acts by inhibiting the me-
primarily by CYP3A4 and, to a lesser extent, by CYP2D6. Due to its potent inhibition of CYP3A4
and CYP2D6, there are many drug interactions with ritonavir involving these enzymes.', 'Pharmacology
   Ritonavir is an inhibitor of HIV-1 and HIV-2 protease 8 , and is as a pharmacokinetic enhancer of
   other protease inhibitors (PI) in the pediatric population. Ritonavir acts by inhibiting the me-
   tabolism of the second (or boosted) PI in the regimen, thereby increasing the boosted PI
   plasma concentration Tmax is approximately 2 hours and 4 hours after dosing under fasting
   and non-fasting conditions, respectively, following 600-mg doses of oral solution. Food in-
   creases absorption; therefore, administer with food. Protein binding is 98% to 99%, primarily
   to human serum albumin and alpha-1 acid glycoprotein. Extensively metabolized in the liver,
   primarily by CYP3A4 and, to a lesser extent, by CYP2D6. Due to its potent inhibition of CYP3A4
   and CYP2D6, there are many drug interactions with ritonavir involving these enzymes.
   Approximately 3% (3.5% unchanged) and 4% (33.8% unchanged) of a dose (600 mg) ap-
   pears in the urine and feces, respectively. Oral clearance was 5 to 7 times faster in pediatric
   patients compared with adults, according to the PACTG Study 310 which evaluated the steady-
   state pharmacokinetics of 37 HIV-infected patients ages 2 to 14 receiving ritonavir doses rang-
   ing from 250 mg/m2 twice daily to 400 mg/m2 twice daily and PACTG Study 345 which evalu-
   ated 41 patients ages 1 month to 2 years receiving doses of 350 to 450 mg/m2 twice daily. The
   half-life is 3 to 5 hours'),
