small intestine into the gut lumen. Is a substrate for CYP3A4 and P-gp; drugs; inducers and in-', 'Pharmacology
   Sirolimus, a macrocyclic triene antibiotic, is an immunosuppressive agent which inhibits T-lym-
   phocyte activation and proliferation that occurs in response to antigenic and cytokine (inter-
   leukin (IL)-2, IL-4, and IL-15) stimulation by a mechanism that is distinct from that of other im-
   munosuppressants Sirolimus binds to the FK-binding protein 12 (FKBP12), and unlike
   tacrolimus, does not inhibit calcineurin activity. The complex binds to and inhibits the activa-
   tion of the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR), a main regulatory kinase. Sirolimus works
   at a later stage in the cell cycle and thus can be used with the calcineurin inhibitors, cy-
   closporine and tacrolimus, for a synergistic action It also inhibits antibody production.
   Bioavailability of the oral solution is approximately 14%. Bioavailability is approximately 27%
   higher with the tablets compared with the solution. Oral solution and tablets are not bioequiv-
   alent; however, bioequivalence has been demonstrated between the 2 mg-oral tablet formula-
   tion and a 2-mg dose of the oral solution Sirolimus exposure is higher when used concomi-
   tantly with cyclosporine compared with concomitant tacrolimus use or no calcineurin inhibitor
   use 11,40 . Peak concentrations reached within 1 to 2 hours after administration 8 ,41,12 . Highly
   protein bound (92%), primarily to serum albumin (97%), alpha1-acid glycoprotein, and lipopro-
   teins. Extensively metabolized by O-demethylation and/or hydroxylation in the liver and in-
   testinal wall to 7 major metabolites, and undergoes counter-transport from enterocytes in the

small intestine into the gut lumen. Is a substrate for CYP3A4 and P-gp; drugs; inducers and in-
   hibitors of these enzymes decrease and increase serum sirolimus concentrations, respectively.
   Most drug is eliminated in the feces (91%); urinary excretion is minimal (2.2%) Higher clear-
   ance rates have been reported in younger children (less than 6 years of age) than in older chil-
   dren and adolescents Elimination half-life in children averages 10 to 12 hours 12 , and may
   increase up to 20 hours several months after transplant Steady-state concentrations occur
   5 to 7 days after initiation of therapy or a dose change . Near steady-state concentrations will
   be reached within 1 day in most patients with administration of a loading dose of 3 times the
   maintenance dose There is a good correlation between trough levels and AUC, so measure-
   ment of trough levels is generally recommended for monitoring therapy'),
