  • Stabile for 30 days at controlled room temperature (23° to 27°C) or 90 days at controlled refrigeration (2 to 8°C).

  TiaGABine 1 mg/mL suspension 3 :
  • Grind the calculated quantity of tiaGABine tablets in a mortar and pestle.
  • Mix with a vehicle of either Simple syrup:1% Methylcellulose (6:1 ratio) or Ora-Sweet:Ora-Plus (1:1 ratio).
  • Transfer to plastic prescription bottle and add sufficient vehicle for a final tiaGABine concentration of 1 mg/mL.

  • Stability in Ora-Plus/Ora-Sweet: 10 weeks at 25°C or 13 weeks at 4°C.
  • Stability in Simple syrup/Methylcellulose 1%: 6 weeks at 25°C or 13 weeks at 4°C.', 'The most commonly reported adverse effects were dizziness (27%), asthenia (20%), somnolence (18%), nausea (11%),
  nervousness (10%), tremor (9%), diarrhea, (7%), vomiting (7%), abdominal pain (7%), pharyngitis (7%), difficulty concen-
  trating (6%), insomnia (6%), ataxia (5%), confusion (5%), pain (5%), and rash (5%) In children 2 to 17 years of age (n=52) on adjunctive tiaGABine, the most common adverse events were asthenia (19%),
  nervousness (19%), dizziness (17%), and somnolence (17%) Monitoring
  Monitor patients for new onset or worsening depression, suicidality, or unusual changes in mood and behavior Consider obtaining plasma concentrations of tiaGABine before and after changes are made to concomitant drugs that
  induce or inhibit hepatic metabolizing enzymes, though therapeutic concentrations have not been established', 'induce or inhibit hepatic metabolizing enzymes, though therapeutic concentrations have not been established Two metabolic pathways (thiophene ring oxidation and glucuronidation) occur for tiaGABine according to in vitro and in
vivo studies. Thiophene ring oxidation leads to the formation of 5-oxo-tiaGABine (inactive) CYP3A is likely the primary metabolizer for tiaGABine although contributions from CYP1A2, CYP2D6, and CYP2C19 are', 'Two metabolic pathways (thiophene ring oxidation and glucuronidation) occur for tiaGABine according to in vitro and in
  vivo studies. Thiophene ring oxidation leads to the formation of 5-oxo-tiaGABine (inactive) CYP3A is likely the primary metabolizer for tiaGABine although contributions from CYP1A2, CYP2D6, and CYP2C19 are
  also possible 3/4
When tiaGABine is taken without enzyme-inducing antiepileptic drugs (AEDs), then tiaGABine blood concentrations are
  about twice as high compared with tiaGABine with enzyme-inducing AEDs Elimination

  Approximately 2% of oral tiaGABine is excreted unchanged in the urine and feces. The majority of the metabolites are
  excreted in the urine (25%) and feces (63%). The mean systemic plasma clearance of tiaGABine is 109 mL/min. The ap-
  parent clearance per unit body surface area or kg were fairly similar in children (3 to 5 years of age) and adults taking
  enzyme-inducing agents Half-life: 2 to 5 hours in patients on enzyme inducing agents'),
