   Valsartan is an angiotensin receptor blocker which blocks the vasoconstrictor and aldosterone-
   secreting effects of angiotensin II by selectively blocking the binding of angiotensin II to the
   AT1 receptor in many tissues, such as vascular smooth muscle and the adrenal gland. Its ac-
   tion is independent of the pathways for angiotensin II synthesis. Does not inhibit angiotensin
   converting enzyme and therefore does not affect response to bradykinin like ACE inhibitors.
   Bioavailability of the tablet is approximately 25% (range, 10% to 35%); bioavailability of the ex-
   temporaneous suspension is 6 times greater than that of the tablet, therefore dose may
   have to be increased when suspension is replaced by a tablet. Food decreases bioavailability
   of the tablet or suspension by approximately 50%; however, valsartan may be taken with or
   without food 6 ,5 . Peak concentrations reached approximately 2 hours after administration of
   the oral suspension For equivalent doses, the oral solution has an 86% higher Cmax and
