serum calcium. Its antianginal effect is related to inhibition of coronary spasm, and relaxation', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action: Verapamil is an L-type calcium channel inhibitor (slow-channel blocker)
   that selectively blocks the transmembrane influx of calcium ions into arterial smooth muscles
   including conductile and contractile myocardial cells, without affecting the concentration of
   serum calcium. Its antianginal effect is related to inhibition of coronary spasm, and relaxation
   of main coronary artery and coronary arterioles Tmax: Peak concentrations reached in approximately 1 to 2 hours after oral administration.
   Bioavailability: Undergoes an extensive first-pass metabolism following oral administration
   with a bioavailability of only 20% to 35%.

   Protein Binding: Highly bound to plasma proteins (90%).
   Metabolism: Extensively metabolized in the liver.
   Excretion: Approximately 70% of a dose is excreted as metabolites in the urine and 16% or
   more in the feces within 5 days. About 3% to 4% eliminated unchanged in the urine Elimination half-life: Approximately 2 to 5 hours after IV administration 9 , and ranges from 8 to 4 hours after single dose oral administration; half-life increases after repetitive dosing
   from 5 to 12 hours. In patients with hepatic impairment, elimination half-life increases up to
   14 to 16 hours'),
