   Mechanism of Action:
   Anakinra is a recombinant form of nonglycosylated human interleukin-1 receptor antagonist,
   produced by using an                bacterial expression system. The recombinant com-
   pound differs from naturally-occurring nonglycosylated human interleukin-1 receptor antago-
   nist by addition of one N-terminal methionine. Anakinra blocks the biologic activity of inter-
   leukin-1 (IL-1) alpha and beta by competitively inhibiting IL-1 binding to the IL-1 type I receptor. Spontaneous mutations in the CIAS1/NLRP3 gene have been identified in patients with cryopy-
   rin-associated periodic syndromes such as neonatal-onset multisystem inflammatory disease
   (NOMID). CIAS1/NLRP3 encodes for cryopyrin, a component of the inflammasome. These
   spontaneous gene mutations lead to IL-1 beta secretion and increased systemic inflammation Deficiency of IL-1 receptor antagonist (DIRA) is an autoinflammatory disease that leads to the
   loss of secretion of IL-1 receptor antagonist (IL-1Ra), resulting in unopposed IL-1 alpha and IL-1
   beta pro-inflammatory signaling and systemic inflammation with skin and bone involvement Bioavailability: 95% 11

   Tmax: 3 to 7 hours in subjects with rheumatoid arthritis 11

   Half-life: 7 hours in patients with NOMID (n=12) 11

Pediatric Pharmacokinetic study
   A study of 87 pediatric patients (9 months to 21 years of age; 3 to 83 kg) determined that
   anakinra pharmacokinetics can be described as one-compartment model with linear absorp-
   tion and elimination. Clearance was estimated at 24 L/hr/70 kg, and apparent volume of dis-
   tribution was estimated to be 2 L/70 kg. Body weight and age were the only covariate to in-
   fluence pharmacokinetic parameters A target anakinra concentration of 4 mg/mL was determined to achieve a maximal reduction
   of C-reactive protein to 10 mg/L or less in 22 children (2.26 to 8 years of age) with systemic-
   onset juvenile idiopathic arthritis (sJIA) based on pharmacokinetic modelling. The proposed
   dosing regimen for sJIA was 3 mg/kg/day (MAX 9 mg/kg/day) for children less than 10 kg, 2
   mg/kg/day (MAX 4 mg/kg/day) for children 10 to 50 kg, and 100 mg/day (MAX 1 mg/kg/day)
   for children greater than 50 kg'),
