   Neuraxial (spinal or epidural) anesthesia

   • The optimal timing between administration of apixaban and neuraxial procedures is not
   known. Indwelling epidural or intrathecal catheters should not be removed earlier than 24
   hours after the last apixaban dose. The next dose should not be administered earlier than 5
   hours after the removal of the catheter. If traumatic puncture occurs, delay administration for
   48 hours 3/22
• Discontinuation of apixaban 3 days preoperatively is recommended in individuals undergo-
   ing neuraxial anesthesia or procedures or at least 72 hours prior to a surgical or nonsurgical
   procedure to minimize risk of epidural bleeding. This includes those undergoing epidural
   steroid injections and sympathetic ganglion blocks Perioperative management

   • Discontinue apixaban at least 48 hours prior to elective surgery or invasive procedures with a
   moderate or high risk of clinically significant bleeding. Discontinue apixaban at least 24 hours
   prior to elective surgery or invasive procedures with a low risk of bleeding or in which the
   bleeding is in a noncritical location or easily controlled. Anticoagulation bridging is usually not
   necessary during the 24 to 48 hours after stopping apixaban prior to the procedure. Restart
   apixaban after surgery or procedure when adequate hemostasis is established • Perioperative heparin bridging: Generally not recommended due to increased risk of bleed-
   ing; however, in patients undergoing a high-bleeding risk operation with a high risk of VTE (eg,
   major cancer surgery, spinal surgery, hip/knee replacement surgery), a low-dose prophylactic
   low molecular weight heparin (eg, dalteparin 5000 international units/day or enoxaparin 40
   mg/day) should be started within 24 hours postoperatively and continued for 2 to 3 days be-
   fore resumption of apixaban 7
