   are stable for 60 days and 90 days, respectively, when stored at room temperature (20 to 25°C)
   or refrigerated (2 to 8°C)', 'Common adverse events reported in children with the use of beta-blockers, including atenolol,
   are mild headache, fatigue 5 ,24,7 ,25 , difficulty concentrating, depression, sleep disturbance,
   and occasional rash Black Box Warning
   Following abrupt cessation of therapy with certain beta-blocking agents, exacerbations of
   angina pectoris and, in some cases, myocardial infarction and ventricular arrhythmias have oc-
   curred. As with other beta blockers, when discontinuation of atenolol is planned, patients
   should be carefully observed and advised to limit physical activity to a minimum. If the angina
   worsens or acute coronary insufficiency develops, it is recommended that atenolol be
   promptly reinstituted, at least temporarily. Patients should be warned against interruption or
   discontinuation of therapy without the physician''s advice Monitoring
   Monitor blood pressure and heart rate Monitor ECG periodically during treatment of ar-
   rhythmias Monitor renal function, particularly in patients with renal impairment Measure

blood glucose in patients with diabetes.', '', 'Pharmacology
   Atenolol is a synthetic beta(1)-selective (cardioselective) adrenoreceptor blocking agent with-
   out membrane stabilizing or intrinsic sympathomimetic activities. At higher doses, it also in-
   hibits beta(2)-adrenoreceptors that are primarily found in bronchial and vascular musculature.
   Absorption is rapid, and consistent with approximately 50% of an oral dose absorbed from the
   GI tract and the remainder excreted unchanged in the feces. Peak serum concentration is
   reached approximately 2 to 4 hours after an oral dose and continues for at least 24 hours fol-
   lowing a dose. Protein binding is approximately 6% to 16%. Undergoes little or no metabolism
   by the liver; elimination is primarily by renal excretion with approximately 50% excreted in the
   urine within 24 hours following oral administration In children, mean elimination half-life is
   approximately 5 hours 26 , which is shorter than in adults (6 to 7 hours) Serum half-life is
   prolonged in renally impaired patients with significant accumulation occurring when CrCl is be-
   low 35 mL/min'),
