       ; levofloxacin is also an option 6 , Acute otitis media (AOM): Amoxicillin is the preferred first-line treatment for initial or de-
   layed antibiotic treatment. Amoxicillin/clavulanate may be considered in patients who have re-
   ceived amoxicillin in the previous 30 days or who have the otitis-conjunctivitis syndrome. In
   penicillin allergic patients, treatment options are cefdinir, cefuroxime, cefpodoxime, or ceftri-
   axone; these cephalosporins are highly unlikely to be cross-reactive due to their distinct chemi-
   cal structures. For failure of initial antibiotic treatment (after a 48 to 72 hour course), the first-
   line treatment is amoxicillin/clavulanate for those initially treated with amoxicillin or a 3-day
   course of ceftriaxone in those initially treated with amoxicillin/clavulanate or oral third-genera-
   tion cephalosporins. An alternative is clindamycin with or without a third-generation
   cephalosporin. Importantly, clindamycin may not be effective for the multidrug-resistant
   serotypes of Streptococcus pneumoniae and lacks efficacy against Haemophilus influenzae 2/8
The American Academy of Pediatrics recommends antibiotics for children 6 months or older
   with severe signs or symptoms of unilateral or bilateral AOM or in children 6 months through
   23 months of age for bilateral AOM without severe signs or symptoms. Antibiotics or observa-
   tion with close follow-up is recommended in children 6 months to 23 months of age with non-
   severe unilateral AOM and in children 24 months or older with nonsevere, unilateral or bilat-
   eral AOM. The above recommendations apply to otherwise healthy children without comor-
   bidities that may alter the natural course of AOM Community-acquired pneumonia (CAP)

   Short-course therapy: Compared with a 10-day course, a 5-day course of amoxicillin,
   amoxicillin/clavulanate, or cefdinir was found to be a safe and effective treatment in young
   children with uncomplicated outpatient CAP who demonstrated early clinical improvement.
   Relatively healthy children younger than 6 years (mean age 7 months) with outpatient CAP
   who showed clinical improvement during the first 5 days of antibiotic therapy (N=380) were
   randomized to standard (10-day) or short (5-day) course of amoxicillin, amoxicillin/clavulanate,
   or cefdinir. Fewer than 10% of participants in both groups demonstrated inadequate clinical
   response or persistent symptoms. The short-course strategy was associated with a signifi-
   cantly lower frequency of antibiotic-resistant genes detected in throat swabs at the end of
   treatment Results of a meta-analysis found shorter courses of amoxicillin or cotrimoxazole
   non-inferior to longer courses among children 2 to 59 months old with non-severe CAP
   (N=11,143). A 3-day course of antibiotic treatment was non-inferior to a 5-day course for the
   outcome of treatment failure (RR, 01; 95% CI, 91 to 12), and a 5-day course was non-infe-
   rior to a 10-day course (RR, 87; 95% CI, 5 to 53) Group A streptococcal pharyngitis in patients with penicillin allergy. More narrow-spectrum
   cephalosporins (cephalexin or cefadroxil) are preferred 8 ,9 . A meta-analysis including 35 clini-
   cal trials found clinical and bacteriologic failure was less likely when a cephalosporin was used
   to treat group A streptococcal tonsillopharyngitis compared with penicillin Current guide-
   lines recommend penicillin V or amoxicillin as drugs of choice in patients with no penicillin al-
   lergy 8 , Otitis media with effusion: Antibiotics are not recommended for asymptomatic otitis media
   with effusion Urinary Tract Infections

