   Therapeutic Physical Monitoring

   • Periodically reassess the usefulness of the drug for the individual patient 4 ,5 .
   • It may be useful to monitor EEG in patients treated for status epilepticus and severe recur-
   rent convulsive seizures • Use of the COMFORT-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale
   are recommended to assess level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients 13

• For all pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation, consider assigning a target depth
   of sedation using a validated sedation assessment tool at least once daily 13

   • Assess periodically for therapeutic usefulness with long-term use Toxic Laboratory Monitoring

   • Monitor CBC and liver function tests during long-term therapy 4 , Toxic Physical Monitoring

   • Prior to initiation and throughout treatment, assess patient for risk of abuse, misuse, and ad-
   dition using a standardized screening tool, especially in at-risk patients. If a substance use dis-
   order is suspected, evaluate the patient and institute or refer them for early treatment, as ap-
   propriate 1 ,3 ,4 ,5 ,2 ,6 ,7 .
   • Monitor for the emergence or worsening of depression, suicidal thoughts or behavior, and/or
   any unusual changes in mood or behavior • Monitor neonates exposed to treatment during pregnancy or labor for signs of sedation and
   for signs of withdrawal • Use of the COMFORT-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale
   are recommended to assess level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients 13
   • For all pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation, consider assigning a target depth
   of sedation using a validated sedation assessment tool at least once daily 13

   • When discontinuing therapy in critically ill patients, use of either the Withdrawal Assessment
   Tool-1 or Sophia Observation Scale for the assessment of iatrogenic withdrawal syndrome is
   recommended. Routine screening is recommended after a shorter duration (3 to 5 days) when
   higher benzodiazepine doses are used', '50 , • Neutropenia and jaundice have occurred rarely • Abrupt withdrawal may induce agitation, irritability, tremor, twitching, nausea, seizures, in-
