   Surviving Sepsis Campaign guidelines: Vasoactive medications should be given to patients
   with septic shock after 40 to 60 mL/kg of fluid resuscitation if there is evidence of abnormal
   perfusion, or sooner if fluid overload develops or other concerns for fluid administration
   present. EPINEPHrine or norepinephrine are both recommended as first line agents for septic

shock. Selection of the vasoactive agent should be guided by clinician preference, individual
   patient physiology, and local system factors. DOPamine may be substituted for treatment if
   neither EPINEPHrine or norepinephrine are available Peripheral vs central IV access: No recommendation could be given in regards to starting va-
   soactive treatment through peripheral access, however, a common practice is administering a
   dilute concentration through a peripheral vein if central venous access is not readily available.
