   Continuous heart rate and intra-arterial blood pressure monitoring is preferable. Assess urine
   output, cardiac output, pulmonary wedge pressure, and peripheral perfusion frequently.
   Observe IV site closely for blanching and infiltration 13,3 .', '', 'Pharmacology
   Catecholamine that acts on dopaminergic, α-, and β-adrenergic receptors in the body. Onset of
   action is 2 minutes after IV administration, with duration of action of less than 10 minutes.
   Metabolized in the liver rapidly by both monoamine oxidase and catechol-O-methyl trans-
   ferase. Mean serum distribution and elimination half-lives are approximately 2 and 26 min-
   utes, respectively, but clearance is quite variable. Volume of distribution approximately 3 L/kg.
   Wide interpatient variability in pharmacokinetics and responses 3 ,19,20,21 . In one pharmacoki-
   netic study (n=17) in children, hepatic and renal impairment did not alter the pharmacokinetics
   of DOPamine DOPamine increases blood pressure primarily by increasing systemic vascular resistance via α-
   adrenergic effects. Inotropic effect diminished in neonates compared with older children due

to immaturity in norepinephrine stores. Has a positive chronotropic effect. Dose-dependent
   pharmacological effects. In adult studies at low doses (less than 6 mcg/kg/min), dopamine re-
   ceptors are stimulated resulting in vasodilation in renal, mesenteric, and cerebral vessels. At
   intermediate doses (6 to 10 mcg/kg/minute), β-receptors are stimulated resulting in inotropic
   response due to norepinephrine release and direct β1 stimulation. At high doses (greater than
   10 mcg/kg/minute), α-receptors are stimulated resulting in increase in blood pressure, heart
   rate, peripheral vascular resistance, and decrease in renal blood flow. Low dose DOPamine (2
   to 5 mcg/kg/minute) causes selective renal dilation and has been used to increase renal blood
   flow, urine output, and sodium excretion, but data are lacking in children 3 ,18 .'),
