   • Open blister package with scissors immediately prior to use • Place the unit in mouth between cheek and lower gum; occasionally move from one side of
   mouth to the other using the handle. Suck medication; do not chew. Consume one unit over
   15 minutes • Dispose of the handle in container out of reach of children; if dose unit is partially-consumed
   or any drug matrix remains on the handle, place under hot running water until all of the drug
   matrix is gone, then dispose out of reach of children; contact manufacturer or seek assistance
   from local DEA office for assistance with disposal Transdermal
   • Transdermal fentanyl should be applied to intact, nonirritated, and nonirradiated skin on a
   flat surface (chest, back, flank, or upper arm). In patients with cognitive impairment, the upper
   back is the preferred location to minimize the potential of inappropriate patch removal. Hair at

the application site should be clipped, not shaved. If necessary, the area should be cleansed
   with clear water (do not use soaps, alcohol, oils, lotions) and allowed to dry completely before
   application • Apply the fentanyl transdermal patch immediately following removal from the package. Place
   the patch firmly at the site of application and hold for 30 seconds with the palm of the hand to
   ensure complete contact, especially around the edges. If the patch does not adhere, the edges
   of the patch may be taped with first aid tape. If adhesion problems persist, the patch may be
   overlaid with transparent adhesive film dressing such as Bioclusive® or Tegaderm®. Each sys-
   tem provides analgesia for 72 hours. If the patch falls off before 72 hours of use, apply a new
   patch to a different skin site. If the patch accidentally comes into contact with the skin of the
   caregiver or any other person, wash skin immediately with soap and water • Patch should be worn continuously for up to 72 hours • Used patches should be folded with adhesive sides together and flushed down the toilet im-
   mediately after removal. Improper disposal has resulted in accidental exposures and deaths Alternative disposal methods may exist (eg, medicine take-back programs or a Drug
   Enforcement Agency-authorized collector) but due to risk of accidental ingestion, these prod-
   ucts must be stored safely and securely until disposal. If there is not a timely take-back pro-
   gram that accepts medicines containing controlled substances, the most effective way to im-
   mediately and completely eliminate the potential for harm is to remove these medicines from
   the home by flushing them down the sink or the toilet 64,65 .

   • Do not use if the seal is broken or if the patch has been cut, damaged, or changed in any way.
   This can expose the patient and caregiver to the contents of the patch and possible fatal over-
   dose • The application site and the surrounding area of the intact patch should not be exposed to
   direct external heat sources such as heating pads, electric blankets, heat or tanning lamps,
   saunas, hot tubs, and heated water beds. Avoid hot baths or sunbathing because higher tem-
   perature may increase fentanyl release from the system and possibly result in overdose and
