   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Monitoring

   • Assess pain control through age appropriate pain scale 3 ,4 ,60 .
   • Assess pain in critically ill pediatric patients via observational pain assessment tools rather
   than vital signs alone. Use of Faces, Legs, Activity, Cry, and Consolability (FLACC) or COMFORT-
   Behavior (COMFORT-B) scales is recommended in noncommunicative critically ill patients; in
   patients 6 years and older capable of communication, use of Visual Analog Scale, Numeric
   Rating Scale, Oucher Scale, or Wong-Baker Faces pain scale is recommended. Toxic Monitoring

   • Monitor patients closely for respiratory depression, especially within the first 24 to 72 hours
   of initiating therapy with Duragesic®, when serum concentrations from the initial patch will
   peak • Closely monitor patients with renal or hepatic impairment for respiratory or CNS depression,
   particularly with dosage increases 18/29
• Assess vital signs 3 ,4 ,60 .

   • Assess level of sedation 3 ,4 ,60 .

   • Postoperative monitoring with EEG when used with droperidol 3 , 4 , • Observe for abdominal distention, loss of bowel sounds, and muscle rigidity 3 ,4 ,60 .

   • Consider urine retention if output is decreased 3 ,4 ,60 .

   • Monitor patients with biliary tract disease, including acute pancreatitis for worsening symp-
   toms • Monitor at-risk ambulatory patients for signs of hypotension after dose initiation or titration • Routinely monitor for signs of misuse, abuse, and addiction when using the patch for chronic
   pain • Patients receiving long-term (5 days or more) analgesia and sedation in the intensive care set-
   ting should be assessed for opioid and/or benzodiazepine withdrawal signs and symptoms
   and managed accordingly, including gradual opioid withdrawal, supportive measures, and
   pharmacologic therapy (eg, methadone) 81 , Patients considered at risk for opioid with-
   drawal are those who have received at least 7 days of continuous infusion opioids or cumula-
   tive fentaNYL exposure greater than 2 mg/kg (greater than 50% predictive for withdrawal)
   83,84,85
          . A total fentaNYL dose greater than 5 mg/kg or a continuous infusion duration
   greater than 9 days was 100% predictive of withdrawal in a prospective case series of children
   ages 1 week to 22 months of age', 'Renal and Hepatic Dose Adjustments
