   Monitoring
   Therapeutic Physical Monitoring
   • Assessment of the level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients is recom-
   mended, using the Comfort-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation
   Scale 13
   • All pediatric patients requiring mechanical ventilation should be assigned a target depth of
   sedation and evaluated at least once daily with a validated sedation assessment tool 13

   Toxic Laboratory Monitoring
   • Obtain baseline liver function tests, including alkaline phosphatase and gamma glutamyl
   transferase, and at periodic intervals during treatment in patients who receive recurrent treat-
   ment Toxic Physical Monitoring
   • For sedation procedures, continuously monitor blood pressure, heart rate, respiratory rate,
   and oxygen saturation 28,44 .

   • Frequently check head position to assure airway patency • Continuously monitor cardiac function in patients found to have hypertension or cardiac de-
   compensation • Assessment of the level of sedation in mechanically ventilated pediatric patients is recom-
   mended, using the Comfort-B Scale, State Behavioral Scale, or Richmond Agitation-Sedation
   Scale 13
