   Monitoring
   • Therapeutic Monitoring
   • Assess pain in critically ill pediatric patients via observational pain assessment tools rather
   than vital signs alone. Use of Faces, Legs, Activity, Cry, and Consolability (FLACC) or COMFORT-
   Behavior (COMFORT-B) scales is recommended in noncommunicative critically ill patients; in
   patients 6 years and older capable of communication, use of Visual Analog Scale, Numeric
   Rating Scale, Oucher Scale, or Wong-Baker Faces pain scale is recommended. 17
   • During chronic therapy, especially for non-cancer-related pain, periodically reassess the con-
   tinued need for morphine • For infants and children 2 weeks to 18 years of age experiencing iatrogenic drug withdrawal
   syndrome, the Withdrawal Assessment Tool (WAT-1) may be used score may be used to moni-
   tor withdrawal severity Toxic Monitoring

• Monitor respiratory, sedation levels, and cardiovascular status closely 76,77 .
   • Monitor patient at least 24 hours after epidural or intrathecal administration for delayed res-
   piratory depression 76,77 .

   • Observe for abdominal distention, loss of bowel sounds, and muscle rigidity 76 , • Consider urine retention if output is decreased 76,77 .
   • Patients receiving long-term (5 days or more) analgesia and sedation in the intensive care set-
   ting should be assessed for opioid and/or benzodiazepine withdrawal signs and symptoms
   and managed accordingly, including gradual opioid withdrawal, supportive measures, and
   pharmacologic therapy (eg, methadone) 76,77 . Monitor severity of narcotic withdrawal symp-
   toms with use of an abstinence scoring method (eg, Finnegan, Lipsitz) 46 , 78 , 12 , 79 , Patients
   considered at risk for opioid withdrawal are those who have received at least 7 days of contin-
   uous infusion opioids or cumulative fentanyl exposure greater than 2 mg/kg (greater than 50%
   predictive for withdrawal) 43,81,82,82 . A total fentanyl dose greater than 5 mg/kg or a continu-
   ous infusion duration greater than 9 days was 100% predictive of withdrawal in a prospective
   case series of children ages 1 week to 22 months of age 83,27 .

   • Routinely monitor for signs of misuse, abuse, and addiction. • Monitor for worsened seizure control in patients with a seizure history • Monitor patients susceptible to the intracranial effects of carbon dioxide retention (eg, evi-
