modulation of inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid. Rapid onset of anesthe-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action poorly understood; sedative/anesthetic effects thought to occur through
   modulation of inhibitory neurotransmitter gamma-aminobutyric acid. Rapid onset of anesthe-
   sia following an IV bolus dose related to rapid equilibration between plasma and brain. Plasma
   levels fall rapidly due to both distribution and clearance. Body tissues, which equilibrate with
   plasma levels as a function of infusion rate and duration, become saturated over time. Steady-
   state propofol levels are generally proportional to rate of infusion. Prompt awakening (10 to 15
   minutes) occurs after discontinuation of infusions of less than 1 day. Because of significant tis-
   sue stores, there may be a prolonged time to awakening following infusions of longer duration
   (10 days of ICU sedation) unless there is daily evaluation of sedation and an appropriate reduc-
   tion in infusion rate Pharmacokinetic parameters best fit a three-compartment model, with
   highly variable values for steady-state Vd (3 to 74 L/kg), clearance (23 to 99 mL/min/kg), and
   terminal half-life (8 to 24 hours) reported across the pediatric age range studied Clearance
   occurs primarily through conjugation in the liver and subsequent elimination by the kidney 12/18'),
