   TraMADol is a centrally-acting synthetic opioid analgesic structurally related to codeine, with a
   dual mechanism of action. traMADol and its metabolite, O-desmethyl (M1), bind to opioid re-
   ceptors, and M1 has a 200-fold higher affinity to the opioid receptors. traMADol has also been
   shown to inhibit the uptake of norepinephrine and serotonin, suggesting that its anti-nocicep-
   tion activity is mediated by both opioid and nonopioid mechanisms. Bioavailability is approxi-
   mately 75%. Absorption is not affected by food Vd during steady state in children (mean age 5 years; range, 2 to 8 years) who received 1 mg/kg intravenously was 2 L and total body
   clearance measured was approximately 2 L/hr Protein binding is approximately 20%.
   Extensively metabolized by a number of pathways including CYP2D6 and CYP3A4, conjugation,
   N- and O-demethylation and glucuronidation or sulfation. Approximately 30% of dose excreted
   unchanged in the urine and 60% as metabolite. traMADol is not dialyzable; less than 7% re-
   moved over 4 hours. Elimination half-life is approximately 6 hours in children 30 and in-
   creases with hepatic impairment or renal impairment Immediate-release: Analgesia onset of action is approximately 1 hour after administration
   and reaches a peak in approximately 2 to 3 hours'),
