   Age is the most important factor in determining warfarin dose requirements in children, with
   an inverse correlation (higher mg/kg/day doses in younger children) shown in studies of war-
   farin in children 6 ,2 ,7 . In a prospective, cohort study (n=59) in children receiving warfarin, me-
   dian therapeutic warfarin doses were 26 mg/kg, 063 mg/kg, and 08 mg/kg for children 1
   to 5 years of age, 5 to 10 years of age, and 10 years and older, respectively In another
   prospective study (n=319) in children, mean warfarin maintenance doses were 33, 15, 13,
   and 09 mg/kg/day for children less than 1 year of age, 1 to 5 years of age, 6 to 13 years of
   age, and 13 years and older, respectively In a prospective study in children receiving warfarin, 79% of children achieved their target INR
   within 7 days with this protocol Maintenance doses of warfarin in children who have undergone a Fontan procedure have
   been shown to be 25% less than children with other congenital heart diseases Conversion From Heparin to Warfarin Therapy
   Conversion to warfarin may begin concurrently with heparin or may be delayed 3 to 6 days.
   Full dose heparin therapy and warfarin overlap for 4 to 5 days is recommended and until ther-
   apeutic INR is achieved. After this, heparin therapy may be discontinued. Due to the effect of
   heparin on the INR, an INR should not be obtained until at least 5 hours after the last bolus in-
   travenous dose of heparin, 4 hours after discontinuation of heparin infusion, and at least 24
   hours after the last subcutaneous dose of heparin 2/13
Conversion From Nonheparin Anticoagulation to Warfarin in Patients With Heparin-
   Induced Thrombocytopenia

   Warfarin therapy is not recommended until the platelet count has recovered to 150,000
   cells/mm3 9 ,10 . A loading dose of warfarin should not be used. Initiate therapy using the ex-
   pected maintenance dose of warfarin, with a maximum dose of 5 mg. Nonheparin anticoagula-
   tion should be continued until the platelet count has stabilized, the target INR is reached, and
   after a minimum overlap of 5 days between nonheparin anticoagulation and warfarin therapy Reversal of Excessive Anticoagulation

   There are limited data on vitamin K use for reversal of excessive anticoagulation in children.
   Nomograms for discontinuation and reversal versus discontinuation of warfarin alone are de-
   rived from adult studies In a prospective cohort study (n=89) in children receiving warfarin,
   conservative management by withholding a single warfarin dose without giving vitamin K, was
   safe and effective in children with INRs of 5 to After withholding 1 dose of warfarin, 89% of
   children had an INR of less than 5 the next day (mean INR, 3; range, 2 to 8). No significant
   bleeding or thrombotic events occurred in the month following the INR greater than 5 A
   suggested nomogram for treatment of excessive oral anticoagulation (usually INR greater than
   8) in children is as follows 12 :
    ●      No bleeding, rapid reversal needed, and need to continue oral anticoagulant: Vitamin
           K 5 to 2 mg IV or subQ
    ●      No bleeding, rapid reversal needed, and no need to continue oral anticoagulant:
           Vitamin K 2 to 5 mg IV or subQ
    ●      Significant bleeding (non life-threatening): Vitamin K 5 to 2 mg IV or subQ and 20
           mL/kg of fresh frozen plasma
    ●      Life-threatening bleeding: Vitamin K 5 mg IV and 20 mL/kg of fresh frozen plasma or 50
           units/kg of prothrombin complex concentrate
   A single vitamin K dose of 30 mcg/kg IV has also been used in children to lower the INR 5 ,', 'Not FDA approved in pediatric patients Administration
   NIOSH Guidance: For full NIOSH guidance, see Special Considerations.

   Oral: Foods/beverages may interact with warfarin due to the vitamin K content. A diet with
   consistent vitamin K intake should be given during warfarin therapy Some infant enteral for-
   mulas are high in vitamin K content which may necessitate a higher warfarin dose 12,3 .

Consider procedures for proper handling and disposal of potentially hazardous drugs Pharmacy and clinical personnel who are pregnant should avoid exposure to crushed or bro-
   ken tablets', 'Bleeding complications are the most common adverse event 8 ,12,3 . In a prospective study
   (n=115), minor bleeding (eg, bruising, nosebleeds, coffee ground emesis, microscopic hema-
   turia, heavy menses) occurred in 19% of children receiving warfarin Withholding warfarin
   alone (without vitamin K administration) was safe and effective for children with INRs of 5 to 8
   in a prospective cohort study (n=89) Other adverse reactions reported with warfarin in-
   clude urticaria, dermatitis, alopecia, tracheal or tracheobronchial calcification, hepatitis, and el-
   evated liver enzymes 8 ,20,21 . Tracheobronchial calcification has been associated with long-
   term warfarin therapy in children with prosthetic heart valves and has been reported in infants
   of mothers who received warfarin during pregnancy Black Box Warning

Warfarin can cause major or fatal bleeding. Regular monitoring of INR should be performed on
   all treated patients. Drugs, dietary changes, and other factors affect INR levels achieved with
   warfarin sodium therapy. Instruct patients about prevention measures to minimize risk of
   bleeding and to report signs and symptoms of bleeding Monitoring
   Monitor INR daily following the initial warfarin dose until the INR is stabilized to the therapeu-
   tic range; then periodically based on clinical need, generally every 1 to 4 weeks. Perform addi-
   tional INR testing when other warfarin products are interchanged with Coumadin(R) or when
   other drugs (including botanicals) are initiated, discontinued, have dosages changed, or are
   taken irregularly. Monitor INR more frequently in patients with high risk of bleeding, when
   starting or stopping other drugs (including those for short-term use), changing dosages of
   other drugs, and in patients with renal impairment After stabilization, monitor INR fre-
   quently in children to ensure target INR is achieved and maintained 8 , Children, especially in-
   fants, require more frequent INR measurements compared to adults 12,2 ,3 . In a prospective
   study (n=115) in children receiving warfarin, children required a mean of 4 INR measurements
   per month with 5 dose adjustments. Only a small number of children (n=7) could be man-
   aged safely with monthly INR measurements In another prospective study (n=319) in chil-
   dren, infants less than 1 year of age required a mean of 8 INR measurements per month com-
   pared to 6 INR measurements in children 1 to 6 years of age and 4 INR measurements in chil-
   dren 6 years of age and older Target therapeutic INR is 5 (range, 2 to 3) for most indications. For low-dose prophylaxis, tar-
