of vitamin K(1) epoxide, thus inhibiting synthesis of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors which
metabolized in the liver, primarily by cytochrome P450 hepatic microsomal enzymes, including
CYP2C9 (primary isoenzyme), CYP2C19, CYP2C8, CYP2C18, CYP1A2, and CYP3A4, to inactive hy-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of Action: Warfarin is an anticoagulant that works by blocking the regeneration
   of vitamin K(1) epoxide, thus inhibiting synthesis of vitamin K-dependent clotting factors which
   include factors 2, 7, 9 and 10, and the anticoagulant proteins C and S. It is a racemic mixture of
   2 optically active isomers (R(+) and S(-) isomers) with the S(-) isomer about 2 to 5 times more
   potent as an anticoagulant than the R(+) isomer.

   Pharmacokinetics: The initial anticoagulant effect of warfarin usually occurs within 24 hours
   after administration, with a peak effect occurring at 72 to 96 hours. The duration of action is
   following a single dose is 2 to 5 days. Peak concentration usually reached within 4 hours after
   administration Completely absorbed after oral administration. Food had no effect on the
   pharmacokinetics of warfarin Highly protein bound (99%) to plasma proteins. Extensively
   metabolized in the liver, primarily by cytochrome P450 hepatic microsomal enzymes, including
   CYP2C9 (primary isoenzyme), CYP2C19, CYP2C8, CYP2C18, CYP1A2, and CYP3A4, to inactive hy-
   droxylated metabolites and also by reductases to warfarin alcohols. Many drugs interact with
   warfarin through pharmacodynamic of pharmacokinetic mechanisms. Eliminated in the urine,
   mainly as metabolites. The terminal half-life is about 1 week with a mean effective half-life of
   40 hours (range, 20 to 60 hours). The half-life of the R-enantiomer of warfarin (range, 37 to 89
   hours) is longer than the half-life of the S-enantiomer (21 to 43 hours) 22 , 12 , CYP2C9 and VKORC1 polymorphisms influence patient response to warfarin and these pa-
   tients require lower doses. In adults, these polymorphisms account for 33% to 63% of variabil-
   ity in warfarin dosing However, in children, these polymorphisms play a much smaller role.
   In a prospective, cohort study (n=59) in children receiving warfarin, most dosing variability was
   due to age (28%); CYP2C9 and VKORC1 genetic variations only minimally affected dosing vari-
   ability (0.4% and 7%, respectively)'),
