metabolite are oxidatively metabolized by CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP3A4. Several metabolites', 'Pharmacology
   Zileuton is a selective inhibitor of 5-lipoxygenase, which is the enzyme that catalyzes the for-
   mation of leukotrienes from arachidonic acid. Specifically, it inhibits the formation of leuko-
   trienes LTC4, LTD4, and LTE4, which are the active constituents of slow-reacting substances of
   anaphylaxis, and LTB4, a substance that attracts neutrophils and eosinophils. Leukotrienes
   produce numerous biological effects, including augmentation of neutrophil and eosinophil mi-
   gration, neutrophil and monocyte aggregation, leukocyte adhesion, increased capillary perme-

ability, and smooth muscle contraction, effects which contribute to inflammation, edema, mu-
   cus secretion, and bronchoconstriction in asthmatic patients 2 ,1 .

   General: Apparent Vd approximately 2 L/kg; 93% plasma protein bound, primarily to albu-
   min with remainder bound to alpha-1-acid glycoprotein. Zileuton and its N-dehydroxylated
   metabolite are oxidatively metabolized by CYP1A2, CYP2C9, and CYP3A4. Several metabolites
   have been identified in human plasma and urine, including 2 O-glucuronide conjugates (major
   and active) and an N-dehydroxylated metabolite (inactive). Following oral administration, 5% and 2% of radiolabeled dose was recovered in urine and feces, respectively 2 ,1 .

   Extended-release tablet: Following a single 1200-mg dose with food in healthy adults (n=23),
   mean AUC was increased by 34%, Cmax was increased by 18%, and Tmax was prolonged from 1 to 3 hours. In another study (n=24 healthy adults), administration of multiple 1200-mg
   doses at steady-state with food increased AUC by 43% and Cmin by 170%, but had no effect on
   Cmax. Apparent oral clearance, 669 mL/min. Mean terminal half-life, 2 hours Immediate-release tablet: Rapidly absorbed upon oral administration (mean Tmax, 7
   hours; mean Cmax, 98 mcg/mL); absolute bioavailability is unknown. Administration with
   food increased Cmax by 27%, but did not affect AUC or Tmax. Oral clearance 7 ml/min/kg.
   Mean terminal half-life, 5 hours'),
