   perthermia, and alterations in acid-base balance, primarily respiratory alkalosis', 'Mechanism of action: The main mechanism of action of aspirin is through inhibition of pros-
hibitor against COX-1 over COX-2 Aspirin is a more potent inhibitor of both prostaglandin
effect of aspirin occurs by an irreversible inhibition of platelet cyclooxygenase. This enzyme in-
platelet shape change, aggregation, and the release reaction 33 , 34 , 35 , Therapeutic effect is dependent on dose used. Platelet inhibition occurs at lower doses (1 to 5', 'Pharmacology

Mechanism of action: The main mechanism of action of aspirin is through inhibition of pros-
   taglandin biosynthesis. Prostaglandins are produced from arachidonic acid via COX (cyclooxy-
   genase; also known as prostaglandin endoperoxide synthase). Aspirin is a more specific in-
   hibitor against COX-1 over COX-2 Aspirin is a more potent inhibitor of both prostaglandin
   synthesis and platelet aggregation than its other salicylic derivatives due to the acetyl group on
   the aspirin molecule, which inactivates cyclooxygenase via acetylation The antithrombotic
   effect of aspirin occurs by an irreversible inhibition of platelet cyclooxygenase. This enzyme in-
   hibition blocks the formation of thromboxane A2 from arachidonic acid which would reduce
   platelet shape change, aggregation, and the release reaction 33 , 34 , 35 , Therapeutic effect is dependent on dose used. Platelet inhibition occurs at lower doses (1 to 5
   mg/kg/day), analgesic and antipyretic effect at moderate doses (60 mg/kg/day) and anti-inflam-
   matory effect at high doses (80 to 100 mg/kg/day). Rapidly absorbed following oral administra-
   tion with peak concentration achieved in 2 hours. Rapidly hydrolyzed by esterases in the liver,
   intestine, and blood to salicylic acid. Has a low Vd and is extensively bound to albumin (80% to
   90%). Eliminated through hepatic metabolism and renal excretion with elimination pathways
   dependent on dose. At therapeutic doses, most elimination occurs through hepatic metabo-
   lism to 3 major metabolites (all inactive); less than 10% is excreted unchanged in the urine. At
   higher doses, when saturation of metabolic pathways occurs, renal excretion dominates with
   greater than 50% of unchanged salicylic acid eliminated in the urine. Renal excretion depen-
   dent on urinary pH (alkaline urine increases elimination). Elimination half-life is approximately
   2 to 3 hours at low dose and 12 hours at anti-inflammatory doses. In Kawasaki disease,
   bioavailability of aspirin is decreased and clearance is increased leading to lower drug concen-
   trations'),
