   Monitoring
   Obtain CBC with platelet counts at baseline and periodically during therapy. Monitor hemat-
   ocrit and perform tests for occult blood in the stool periodically during therapy. When treating
   thromboses, maintain a prolonged aPTT in a range corresponding to an anti-factor Xa level of 35 to 7 units/mL (usually equivalent to an aPTT of 60 to 85 seconds). Assess for signs of
   bleeding and thrombosis 1 ,3 ,30,2 . Recent studies have reported a lack of correlation between
   aPTT and anti-Xa levels and unfractionated heparin dose, and that these tests do not accu-
   rately monitor therapy in children. Studies have also shown invalidity in extrapolating the aPTT
   range in adults to children, due to age-related differences in baseline aPTT values (increased
   compared with adults). There are also data showing that there are significant age-related dif-
   ferences in the aPTT that correlates to an anti-factor Xa level of 35 to 7 units/mL. Many ex-
   perts prefer anti-factor Xa assays to monitor heparin therapy in children 1 ,31,32 .

