interacts with progenitor stem cells to increase red blood cell production. Production of en-', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta is an erythropoietin receptor activator that pos-
   sesses greater activity in vivo and a longer half-life compared to erythropoietin. A primary
   growth factor for erythroid development, erythropoietin is produced in the kidney and re-
   leased into the bloodstream in response to hypoxia. In responding to hypoxia, erythropoietin
   interacts with progenitor stem cells to increase red blood cell production. Production of en-
   dogenous erythropoietin is impaired in patients with chronic renal failure (CRF), and erythro-
   poietin deficiency is the primary cause of their anemia Immunogenicity: Neutralizing antibodies to methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta that
   cross-react with endogenous erythropoietin and other erythropoiesis-stimulating agents
   (ESAs) can result in pure red cell aplasia (PRCA) or severe anemia (with or without other cy-
   topenias). Compared to subQ administration, the IV route of administration may lessen the
   risk for development of antibodies to methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta. In 1789 pa-
   tients treated with methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta in clinical studies, antibody test-
   ing using an enzyme-linked immunosorbent assay (ELISA) was conducted at baseline and dur-
   ing treatment. Antibody development was not detected in any of the patients. ELISA testing
   was conducted in 104 pediatric patients. In the first study (NCT00717366), blood samples to
   measure anti-methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta and anti-EPO antibodies were col-
   lected on Week 1, Week 13, Week 21, and Week In the second study (NCT03552393), they
   were measured at Week 1, Week 9, Week 21, and Week No patients tested positive at any
   time during the first study. In the second study, 2 patients tested positive for anti-methoxy
   polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta and anti-EPO antibodies. For 1 patient, the Week 1 samples
   before the first methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta administration were already posi-
   tive. The second patient had a transient positive test at Week The transient presence of an-
   tibodies did not neutralize the response and was not associated with any adverse events.
   Because of the low occurrence of anti-drug antibodies, the effect of these antibodies on the
   pharmacokinetics, pharmacodynamics, safety, and/or effectiveness of methoxy polyethylene
   glycol-epoetin beta products is unknown AUC, pediatric: No differences compared with adults 2

   Peak concentration, pediatric: No differences compared with adults 2

   Bioavailability, pediatric, subQ: 67% 2

   Tmax: 72 hours (range, 24 to 192 hours), adults Cmax: 66 ng/mL (coefficient of variation, 150%) following the third IV dose of methoxy polyeth-
   ylene glycol-epoetin beta in pediatric patients with chronic kidney disease on dialysis (n=64).

Patients received a dose of methoxy polyethylene glycol-epoetin beta based upon a conver-
   sion factor from their prior erythropoiesis-stimulating agent dosage Vd: 61 mL/kg (coefficient of variation, 46%), adults; multiple dosing had no effect on Vd Clearance: 51 mL/hr/kg (coefficient of variation, 96%) following the third IV dose of methoxy
