   Sucrose administration provides a calming effect and reduces acute procedural pain in infants.
   The potential mechanism of these effects includes activation of the endogenous opioid system
   through taste receptors on the tip of the tongue. The time to maximal effect is approximately 2
   minutes and the duration of effect is approximately 5 to 10 minutes. The beneficial effects of
   sucrose can be improved by nonnutritive sucking 3 ,11 .', '2 mL of 12% to 30% sucrose solution 2 minutes prior to the procedure 1 ,2 ,3 ,4 ,5 ,6 ,7 .', 'Mild analgesia and behavioral comforting prior to painful procedures (eg, vaccination, heel
   lances) in infants 2 ,8 ,3 ,4 ,5 ,6 ,7 . Efficacy as a pain reliever appears to decrease with increasing
   age and maturation (first 6 months of life) with greatest benefit seen in infants up to 3 months
   of age 2 , 8 , 9 , 10 , However, a double-blind, randomized trial (n=114) in infants 15-month of age
   demonstrated a statistical improvement in duration of crying for sucrose 30% compared with
   placebo (18 vs 33 seconds) when administered 1 to 2 minutes before vaccination Administration
   Administer sucrose solution directly to the tongue 2 minutes prior to the painful procedure.
   For patients able to suck, a pacifier should be offered immediately after sucrose
   administration.

   Alternatively, a pacifier dipped in sucrose solution can be offered 2 minutes prior to the proce-
   dure 2 ,3 ,4 ,5 ,6 ,7 .', 'Sucrose 24% has an osmolarity of approximately 700 mOsm/L. The adverse effects of re-
