   most common dosage used in the literature 1 ,2 ,3 ,4 . Some retrospective studies used up to 6
   drops/day 1 ,2 . Duration for 3 months provided the best response for superficial IH. The gel for-
   mulation was preferred because of potentially less systemic absorption than the solution 5

   Ophthalmic
   Ocular hypertension or Open-angle glaucoma
   2 years or o', 'Indicated (Timoptic®-XE and Timoptic®) for the treatment of elevated intraocular pressure in
   pediatric patients 2 years or older with ocular hypertension or open-angle glaucoma 8 ,6 ,7 .

   Administration
   Solution

•Remove contact lenses prior to administration; wait 15 minutes following administration to
   reinsert 7

   •Use Timoptic® in Ocudose® immediately after opening and discard remainder Gel

   •Invert the closed container and shake once before each use •Instill other topically', 'Topical: Episodic bradycardia (less than 80 beats/min) was detected on a Holter monitor in 4
   (2 full-term and 2 preterm infants) out of 22 at-risk infants. The 2 full term infants (less than
   2,500 g at initiation) experienced rare, brief, asymptomatic episodes, usually at night, and un-
   related to timolol administration. The 2 preterm infants with histories of bradycardia experi-
   enced symptomatic bradycardia associated with topical timolol. Hypothermia and apnea were
   also experie', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Timolol maleate is a non-selective beta1 and beta2 adrenergic receptor blocking agent. Beta-
   adrenergic receptor blockade reduces cardiac output and when applied topically, has the ac-
   tion of reducing intraocular pressure. The precise mechanism of the ocular hypotensive action
   of timolol maleate is unknown; however, studies suggest that its predominant action may be
   related to reduced aqueous formation 6 , Onset of action for reduction of intr', 'Topical
   Infantile Hemangioma (IH)

   1 drop of 5% gel applied topically twice daily on non-mucuosal, nonulcerated IHs was the
   most common dosage used in the literature 1 ,2 ,3 ,4 . Some retrospective studies used up to 6
   drops/day 1 ,2 . Duration for 3 months provided the best response for superficial IH. The gel for-
   mulation was preferred because of potentially less systemic absorption than the solution 5

   Ophthalmic
   Ocular hypertension or Open-angle glaucoma
   2 years or older
   Timoptic®

   Initial: 1 drop of 25% solution instilled into affected eye(s) twice daily. If clinical response is
   not adequate, may increase to a MAX of 1 drop of 5% solution to affected eye(s) twice
   daily 6 ,7
   Maintenance: May decrease to 1 drop instilled into affected eye(s) once daily if intraocular
   pressure is maintained at a satisfactory level 6 ,7

   Timoptic®-XE 1 drop of 25% or 5% gel forming solution instilled into affected eye(s) once
   daily; evaluate after approximately 4 weeks of treatment; doses greater than 1 drop of 5%
   solution once daily not studied, may add concomitant therapy (other than another beta-adren-
   ergic blocker) if response not satisfactory after 4 weeks 8', 'Indicated (Timoptic®-XE and Timoptic®) for the treatment of elevated intraocular pressure in
   pediatric patients 2 years or older with ocular hypertension or open-angle glaucoma 8 ,6 ,7 .

   Administration
   Solution

•Remove contact lenses prior to administration; wait 15 minutes following administration to
   reinsert 7

   •Use Timoptic® in Ocudose® immediately after opening and discard remainder Gel

   •Invert the closed container and shake once before each use •Instill other topically applied ophthalmic agents at least 10 minutes prior to Timoptic®-XE', 'Topical: Episodic bradycardia (less than 80 beats/min) was detected on a Holter monitor in 4
   (2 full-term and 2 preterm infants) out of 22 at-risk infants. The 2 full term infants (less than
   2,500 g at initiation) experienced rare, brief, asymptomatic episodes, usually at night, and un-
   related to timolol administration. The 2 preterm infants with histories of bradycardia experi-
   enced symptomatic bradycardia associated with topical timolol. Hypothermia and apnea were
   also experienced in 1 preterm infant each 4/8
No cardiovascular adverse events were reported with topical timolol for infantile hemangioma
   in a retrospective study (n=731). Bronchospasm was reported in 3 infants 2
   Significant sleep disturbances was reported in 1 patient out of 279 patients treated with topical
   timolol for infantile hemangioma in a meta-analysis (9 studies) Ophthalmic: The most commonly reported adverse effects were burning and stinging upon
   instillation 6 , Monitoring
   Infantile Hemangiomas (IHs): Monitor temperature, blood pressure, and heart rate in pre-
   mature and low-birth weight infants with complicated IHs when starting and during therapy
   with topical timolol. Consider using a Holter monitor when starting timolol in infants younger
   than postmenstrual age of 44 weeks Ocular Hypertension or Open-angle Glaucoma: Evaluate intraocular pressure after approxi-
   mately 4 weeks of therapy 8 ,6 ,7 .', '', 'Pharmacology
   Mechanism of action

   Timolol maleate is a non-selective beta1 and beta2 adrenergic receptor blocking agent. Beta-
   adrenergic receptor blockade reduces cardiac output and when applied topically, has the ac-
   tion of reducing intraocular pressure. The precise mechanism of the ocular hypotensive action
   of timolol maleate is unknown; however, studies suggest that its predominant action may be
   related to reduced aqueous formation 6 , Onset of action for reduction of intraocular pressure was 30 minutes.

   Maximum effect usually occurs in 1 to 2 hours
   Duration of effect for significant lowering of intraocular pressure can be maintained for peri-
   ods as long as 24 hours with a single dose 6 ,7 .

   Topical administration of Ophthalmic Timolol: Timolol was detected in the urine of 20 of 24
   (83%) of infants treated for small proliferating infantile hemangiomas with timolol gel 5%

topically twice daily without occlusion. In 3 patients serum was tested and the timolol serum
   concentrations were 1, 18, and 16 ng/mL'),
(953, 'Senna', 'gastrointestinal', TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, 95, 'Constipation
   Oral Syrup

