   • 6 to less than 12 years: 5 to 5 mL (8.8 to 2 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 5 mL (13.2 mg) twice daily 2
   • 12 years or older: 10 to 15 mL (17.6 to 22 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dos', 'Constipation
   Guidelines: According to NASPGHAN guidelines, initial treatment of functional constipation in-
   cludes education, diary use (to evaluate dietary and fluid intake, especially in patients younger
   than 6 months), and oral treatment in patients 6 months or older. Oral medication or occa-
   sional suppositories may be started in patients younger than 6 months if initial treatment is in-
   effective. The recommended initial treatment of choice for fecal disimpaction is polyethyl', 'Reported adverse effects include idiosyncratic hepatitis, melanosis coli, hypertrophic os-
   teoarthropathy, analgesic nephropathy', '', '', 'Constipation
   Oral Syrup

   • 1 to less than 2 years: 25 mL to 5 mL (2.2 to 4 mg sennosides) orally once daily at bed-
   time; maximum of 5 mL or 8 mg per day 1
   • 2 to less than 6 years: 5 to 75 mL (4.4 to 6 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 75 mL (6.6 mg) once daily 2

   • 6 to less than 12 years: 5 to 5 mL (8.8 to 2 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 5 mL (13.2 mg) twice daily 2
   • 12 years or older: 10 to 15 mL (17.6 to 22 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 15
   mL (22 mg) twice daily 2
   Oral Tablets
   • 2 to less than 6 years: 5 tablet (4.3 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 1 tablet
   (8.6 mg) twice daily 3

   • 6 to less than 12 years: 1 tablet (8.6 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 2 tablets
   (17.2 mg) twice daily 3
   • 12 years or older: 2 tablets (17.2 mg sennosides) orally once daily; MAX dose, 4 tablets
   (34.4 mg) twice daily 3', 'Constipation
   Guidelines: According to NASPGHAN guidelines, initial treatment of functional constipation in-
   cludes education, diary use (to evaluate dietary and fluid intake, especially in patients younger
   than 6 months), and oral treatment in patients 6 months or older. Oral medication or occa-
   sional suppositories may be started in patients younger than 6 months if initial treatment is in-
   effective. The recommended initial treatment of choice for fecal disimpaction is polyethylene
   glycol (PEG) or enemas. For maintenance therapy, PEG with or without electrolytes is the first-

line treatment; if PEG is not available, the recommended treatment is lactulose. Other laxa-
   tives, including senna, may be considered as additional or second-line treatments Duration of therapy: Initial therapy for fecal disimpaction should be 2 to 6 days. Maintenance
   therapy should be continued for 2 months, or at least 1 month after a good response to ther-
   apy 4 ,1 . Medications should be weaned gradually and may need to be continued for months to
   years in some patients. Relapses are common and may be treated with maintenance or disim-
   paction therapy as needed 1

   Indications
   Used for relief of occasional constipation; a bowel movement is generally produced in 6 to 12
   hours 2 ,3 .

   Administration
   Administer at bedtime or as directed 2 ,3 .', 'Reported adverse effects include idiosyncratic hepatitis, melanosis coli, hypertrophic os-
   teoarthropathy, analgesic nephropathy', '', '');

